    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Deucalion, son of Prometheus, built a chest and embarked with his wife Pyrrha; the sea rose and drowned all else. When the waters fell, an oracle bade the pair to 'throw the bones of your mother behind you' \u2014 the bones of Earth, that is, stones."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "And at the last, Jupiter bade the seas retire; the god-given Triton blew on his shell-wrought trumpet; the sea had a shore again, the channels received the rivers, and the hills put forth their tops. Then Deucalion led the tears from Pyrrha and fell upon her husband, and both of them were at a loss how to re-people the desolate earth: They were the last of mankind, weeping at the prospect of being left too few. Yet this pair had understanding of the will of the god."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "They drew near to the temple of Themis, the daughter of Uranus, and leaning upon their head, kissing the cold pavement, they cried out: 'If the gods still rule, unfold by Thymia, or by whatever art the mortal race may be restored.' The goddess, moved, said: 'go beyond the roof of this temple; draw your garments loose, and throw behind your backs the bones of your great mother.' Then Pyrrha broke the silence first: 'Leave her bones unviolated', she said, 'I am not qualified to do the will of an offended mother, who gave me my bones.' And Pyrrha, after they had for a long time weighed the words in secret, begged, 'I begin to suspect our discernment is fallacious, and the oracles are just and advise no sacrilege; the earth is the great mother \u2014 the stones are the bones she bids us cast.' So they threw stones behind their backs. And every stone did grow \u2014 as successive \u2014 into a man, for those Deucalion had thrown; and into a woman, for those Pyrrha had thrown: and thus sprang up a race of hard-working men, hardened and patient, from the nature of the stone from which they came."
      }
    ],
