    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "All passages are condensed paraphrases of the Morris/Magnusson translation, chapters XIII-XVIII, in the collector's own words; the birds' counsel and Odin's disguise as an old bearded man follow the saga. Saga version, not the Poetic Edda; coordinates are a neutral spot in the Rhineland, the unnamed 'Gnita-heath' of the Germanic legend-cycle."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Regin, the smith who fostered him, told Sigurd the tale of his family: Hreidmar and his three sons \u2014 the smith Regin, the mighty Fafnir, and Otter the fisher \u2014 lived alongside the gods. When Odin, Hoenir, and Loki slew Otter in his beast-shape, Hreidmar demanded as ransom an otter-skin's weight of gold, forcing Odin to extort the whole hoard of the dwarf Andvari, whom Loki robbed of his magic ring. The dwarf cursed the gold, dooming it to destroy every owner; the doom soon fell, for Fafnir killed their father for the treasure and, transformed into a great dragon of malice and greed, brooded over his gold on the heath, while Regin nursed his claim on the next generation \u2014 the fostered hero Sigurd, son of Sigmund, owner of the reforged sword Gram."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "On the heath Sigurd saw the track of the worm marvellously great; Regin, shaking in fear, showed him where to dig. As Sigurd dug, an old bearded man appeared, advised him to make many pits for the running blood, then vanished \u2014 Odin in disguise. When the dragon crept down to drink, shaking the earth and snorting venom before him, Sigurd thrust his sword Gram up under Fafnir's left shoulder to the hilt. The dying worm lashed out with head and tail, smashing all that stood before him, and warned Sigurd: the same gold he had owned would be Sigurd's bane too. Regin bade the victor roast Fafnir's heart for him; tasting the dragon's blood on his finger, Sigurd Suddenly understood the speech of birds, and the wood-peckers' chatter warned him that Regin meant to beguile him \u2014 so he took the gold, struck off Regin's head, and rode on toward Hindfell, where the fire slept."
      }
    ]
