    language: "Ugaritic (Northwest Semitic, alphabetic cuneiform)",
    canonical_translation: "J. C. L. Gibson, Canaanite Myths and Legends, Edinburgh 1978 (copyright); Dennis Pardee, Stories from Ancient Canaan, 2nd ed., Louisville 2018 (copyright) \u2014 quoted as summary only",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Baal Cycle \u2014 six tablets found at Ugarit (Ras Shamra, Syria) in 1929-31 \u2014 is the fullest Canaanite narrative and the essential comparandum for biblical storm-theology.\n\nTABLET 1-2: BAAL VERSUS YAM. The sea-king Yam (Sea, 'Prince Yam, the beloved of El') tyrannizes the divine assembly and demands Baal delivered as his slave. The gods cower; even El counsels compliance. Baal seizes weapons and would kill the messengers in the assembly; Asherah, the mother of the gods, intervenes \u2014 'why do you kill the messengers?' \u2014 and pledges to secure Baal's freedom by persuading El to concede Yam's kingship in exchange for a palace? (In KTU 1.1, Asherah promises Yam gifts, including a palace, if he lets Baal live.) The craftsman-god Kothar-wa-Hasis forges two clubs and names them as spells: 'Yagrush (Chaser): chase Yam!' \u2014 the club flies and fails; 'Ayamur (Scatter): scatter Yam!' \u2014 Yam falls dead at Baal's feet. Baal proclaims his reign: 'surely Baal reigns over the gods'; Yam's scattered body is threshed (in some readings the sea's fishing aition: 'fish are his tribute').\n\nTABLET 3: ANAT'S VENGEANCE. Anat, Baal's sister, the virgin warrior, slaughters the men of the towns who wronged her guests (the gruesome interlude), and exchanges messages with Baal about her powers ('I have a word of the tree, the whisper of the stone... which no man knows'); Baal promises to reveal the secret if she brings word of the? \u2014 Anat, pacified, proposes the palace project.\n\nTABLET 4: THE PALACE ON SAPHON. Baal has no house like the other gods; Asherah goes to El, walks across his? and secures the grant: 'A house for Baal like his brothers' \u2014 Kothar builds on Mount Saphon with cedars from Lebanon and gold, silver, lapis. Kothar offers to install windows: Baal refuses \u2014 through a window Yam's agents (or demons) would come; he promises? finally concedes; windows are opened, and storm passes through them ('Baal opened windows in the house, a rift in the clouds') \u2014 the aition of the rain through the clouds. Baal celebrates, and the hymn of his triumph: 'Baal's word is secret? ' He copulates with a heifer (in some tellings his own? as bull), begets Magar ('the Victory') \u2014 and visits Mot.\n\nTABLET 5: MOT SWALLOWS BAAL. Mot, lord of death ('Divine Mot'), sits in the pit, his throat from earth to heaven ('one lip to the earth, one lip to the heaven'); Baal comes at his summons and is swallowed whole: 'Baal is dead... what will the people do?' El, on his throne, dreams; the rains stop; the world dries (the death of the storm-god is the death of rain). Anat buries Baal on Saphon, slaughters Mot's son? (in KTU 1.5, she 'seizes the son of Asherah? ' \u2014 she cuts Mot's? the rite: she kills 'Divine Mot's heir'? \u2014 standard reading: she buries Baal, slays his killers?), and mourns with Shapash, the sun-goddess, who collects the pieces.\n\nTABLET 6: THE DEATH OF MOT AND THE RETURN. Athtar, 'the Morning Star', takes Baal's empty throne \u2014 but he is too small for it ('his head did not reach its top'). Anat finds Mot, seizes him, and performs the ritual execution: 'with a blade she split him, with a winnowing-fork she burned him, with millstones she ground him, in the field she sowed him \u2014 the birds ate his remains' \u2014 the farmer-god's death sown like seed. Seven years pass (in some tellings seven years of famine? the text: after seven years, Mot returns alive, complaining that he was slain 'as if a kid? '); El dreams that Baal lives; Baal returns, defeats the restored Mot in seven-year combat ('as Baal seized Mot... the two? '), until Shapash, the sun, intervenes: 'how can you fight the Word of the Strongest? Mot, hear: Baal has thy word? \u2014 the sun parts them, and the cycle closes with Baal restored and El's blessing.\n\nFUNCTIONS. The cycle is the theology of the year: rain (Baal) versus drought (Mot) versus chaos-sea (Yam); it grounds Ugaritic kingship ideology (the palace of Saphon mirrors the royal temple), the cult of the ancestors and of Baal-Hadad at Ugarit, and \u2014 through biblical polemic (Baal and Asherah in Hosea, Elijah on Carmel) \u2014 the very vocabulary in which Hebrew monotheism defined itself.",
      zh: "\u5DF4\u529B\u795E\u8A71\u5708\u516D\u677F\uFF1A\u6D77\u738B\u96C5\u59C6\u66B4\u8650\u795E\u6703\uFF0C\u7D22\u5DF4\u529B\u70BA\u5974\uFF1B\u8AF8\u795E\u61FE\u670D\uFF0C\u963F\u745F\u62C9\u8AAA\u60C5\uFF1B\u5DE5\u5320\u795E\u79D1\u5854\u723E\u9444\u96D9\u6756\uFF0C\u5FF5\u5492\u800C\u6295\uFF1A\u300E\u8FFD\u8D95\u8005\uFF0C\u8FFD\u96C5\u59C6\uFF01\u300F\u300E\u9A45\u6563\u8005\uFF0C\u788E\u96C5\u59C6\uFF01\u300F\u96C5\u59C6\u6583\uFF0C\u5DF4\u529B\u7A31\u738B\u3002\u963F\u5A1C\u7279\u5C60\u57CE\u96EA\u6065\uFF0C\u8207\u5144\u8AD6\u795E\u529B\u4E4B\u79D8\u3002\u5BE1\u5A66\u963F\u745F\u62C9\u5F80\u8AAA\u57C3\u723E\uFF0C\u5DF4\u529B\u5F97\u8607\u9CF3\u5C71\u5EFA\u6BBF\uFF1A\u9ECE\u5DF4\u5AE9\u96EA\u677E\u3001\u91D1\u9280\u9752\u91D1\uFF1B\u79D1\u5854\u723E\u6B32\u958B\u7A97\uFF0C\u5DF4\u529B\u521D\u62D2\uFF0C\u7D42\u8A31\u4E4B\u2014\u2014\u96F2\u9699\u964D\u96E8\u4E4B\u7DE3\u8D77\u3002\u5DF4\u529B\u5316\u725B\u8207\u725D\u725B\u5408\uFF0C\u751F\u300E\u52DD\u5229\u300F\u3002\u5F8C\u8D74\u6B7B\u4EA1\u4E4B\u4E3B\u6469\u7279\u4E4B\u53EC\uFF0C\u88AB\u5176\u541E\u566C\uFF1A\u5176\u5589\u81EA\u5730\u81F3\u5929\uFF1B\u5DF4\u529B\u65E2\u4EA1\uFF0C\u96E8\u7D55\u5730\u67AF\u3002\u963F\u5A1C\u7279\u846C\u5DF4\u529B\u65BC\u8607\u9CF3\u5C71\uFF0C\u8207\u65E5\u795E\u6C99\u5E15\u4EC0\u540C\u54C0\u3002\u661F\u8FB0\u963F\u5854\u723E\u651D\u4F4D\u800C\u8EAB\u5C0F\u4E0D\u80FD\u767B\u3002\u963F\u5A1C\u7279\u64D2\u6469\u7279\uFF0C\u4EE5\u5200\u5206\u4E4B\u3001\u4EE5\u7C38\u7B95\u711A\u4E4B\u3001\u4EE5\u78E8\u7814\u4E4B\u3001\u65BC\u7530\u64AD\u4E4B\u3001\u98DB\u9CE5\u98DF\u4E4B\u2014\u2014\u6B7B\u795E\u5982\u7A2E\u88AB\u64AD\u3002\u4E03\u5E74\u5F8C\u6469\u7279\u5FA9\u751F\u7D22\u6230\uFF0C\u57C3\u723E\u5922\u5DF4\u529B\u7336\u5B58\uFF1B\u5DF4\u529B\u6B78\uFF0C\u8207\u6469\u7279\u4E03\u5E74\u4E4B\u9B25\uFF0C\u65E5\u795E\u6C99\u5E15\u4EC0\u6B62\u4E4B\uFF1A\u300E\u6C5D\u5B89\u80FD\u8207\u81F3\u5F37\u8005\u4E4B\u8A00\u722D\uFF1F\u300F\u5DF4\u529B\u5FA9\u4F4D\uFF0C\u57C3\u723E\u8CDC\u798F\u800C\u7D42\u3002\u529F\u80FD\uFF1A\u96E8\u65F1\u6D77\u4E09\u529B\u4E4B\u5E74\u74B0\u795E\u5B78\u3001\u738B\u6B0A\u795E\u5B78\uFF08\u8607\u9CF3\u5C71\u6BBF\u5373\u738B\u5EDF\u4E4B\u5F71\uFF09\u3001\u4EE5\u53CA\u5E0C\u4F2F\u4F86\u8056\u7D93\u5DF4\u529B\u8AD6\u6230\u4E4B\u8A5E\u5F59\u6E90\u3002"
    },
