    license: "Public domain (translation 1898)",
    summary: {
      en: "Shamed by Hector, Paris proposes single combat with Menelaus for Helen and her goods, the two armies swearing a peace to follow. Menelaus gains the upper hand, but Aphrodite spirits Paris away in a cloud, and the truce dissolves.",
      zh: "\u5E15\u91CC\u65AF\u53D7\u8D6B\u514B\u6258\u8033\u8CAC\u5099\uFF0C\u63D0\u51FA\u8207\u58A8\u6D85\u62C9\u4FC4\u65AF\u55AE\u7368\u6C7A\u9B25\uFF1A\u52DD\u8005\u5F97\u6D77\u502B\u8207\u5176\u8CA1\u7269\uFF0C\u5169\u8ECD\u7ACB\u8A93\u4F11\u6230\u3002\u58A8\u6D85\u62C9\u4FC4\u65AF\u7565\u4F54\u4E0A\u98A8\u4E4B\u969B\uFF0C\u963F\u8299\u6D1B\u72C4\u5FD2\u4EE5\u96F2\u9727\u6551\u8D70\u5E15\u91CC\u65AF\uFF0C\u8A93\u7D04\u5C31\u6B64\u4F5C\u5EE2\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "theophany",
      "combat-ordeal"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "As the two hosts closed, Paris came forward as champion in gleaming panther-skin, brandishing two spears; but when Menelaus stepped toward him he flinched back into the crowd, pale as a man who stumbles upon a serpent in a mountain glade. Hector caught him at once and taunted him: beauty and the lyre would not save a man who had stolen a wife from a warrior people and dragged his city into war.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 3 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Paris answered that the gods' gifts should not be disdained, and offered the way out of the war: he and Menelaus alone would fight for Helen and all her possessions, and whoever won would take her home; the rest would swear peace, the Trojans keeping their city, the Achaeans sailing away. Hector made the terms known, lambs were sacrificed, and old Priam himself came down to swear the oath with the Greeks, calling the gods to witness.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 3 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Alone between the armies, the two men armed and fought. Menelaus drove his spear through Paris's shield, his sword snapped against the Trojan's helmet, and then he seized the helmet strap and hauled Paris backward, the chin-strap cutting into his neck and strangling the duel. But Aphrodite snapped the strap from Menelaus's grip, caught Paris up in a thick mist, invisible, and set him down safely in his own bedchamber; then she went to Helen and commanded her to tend him. Menelaus strode the field shouting that the Trojans had broken the oath, but the peace by combat proved thinner than the borrowed plume of the man it was sworn for.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 3 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
