    period: "Composed c. 8th century BCE (Butler translation 1898)",
    source: {
      archive: "The Internet Classics Archive (MIT)",
      work: "Homer, Iliad, trans. Samuel Butler (1898), Book XVI",
      url: "https://classics.mit.edu/Homer/iliad.html"
    },
    license: "Public domain (translation 1898)",
    summary: {
      en: "Achilles lets his dear friend Patroclus lead the Myrmidons out in his own armour to drive the Trojans from the ships, on the strict understanding that he stop at the wall. Patroclus kills Zeus's own son Sarpedon, forgets himself, and is beaten down by Apollo, a young Trojan, and Hector in turn, prophesying Achilles' death with his last breath.",
      zh: "\u963F\u57FA\u7409\u65AF\u501F\u51FA\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u7532\u80C4\u8207\u9EBE\u4E0B\u5BC6\u723E\u5F4C\u6771\u4EBA\uFF0C\u51C6\u5E15\u7279\u7F85\u514B\u6D1B\u65AF\u51FA\u6230\u9010\u6575\uFF0C\u552F\u56B4\u4EE4\u6B62\u6B65\u65BC\u57CE\u7246\u3002\u5E15\u7279\u7F85\u514B\u6D1B\u65AF\u6BBA\u6B7B\u5B99\u65AF\u4E4B\u5B50\u85A9\u723E\u4F69\u51AC\uFF0C\u537B\u5FD8\u4E4E\u6240\u4EE5\u653B\u57CE\uFF0C\u5148\u88AB\u963F\u6CE2\u7F85\u9707\u843D\uFF0C\u518D\u906D\u6B50\u798F\u8033\u73BB\u65AF\u6697\u69CD\uFF0C\u7D42\u70BA\u8D6B\u514B\u6258\u8033\u6240\u6BBA\uFF1B\u81E8\u7D42\u9810\u8A00\u963F\u57FA\u7409\u65AF\u4EA6\u5C07\u6B7B\u65BC\u6B64\u9580\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "prophetic-vision"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Patroclus came in tears to Achilles, asking, if Achilles would not fight, to let him lead the Myrmidons wearing Achilles' armour, so that the Trojans at the ships might think Achilles had given up his rage. Achilles reproached the world that had brought him honour only through loss, and granted the prayer: take the armour; but his terms were strict, for he warned Patroclus neither to chase the enemy to Troy's wall, nor to give his glory to the whole host, lest one of the Olympians come against him and stand in Apollo's service, whom he loves too well.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 16 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Dressed as Achilles, Patroclus led out the dark-clad Myrmidons and swept the Trojans from the ships in one great onset. On the plain below, Zeus's dear son Sarpedon charged him, and the two fought in a whorl of spears while Zeus upon Ida debated in his heart whether to snatch his living son from death; and Hera talked him back with a promise that Sleep and Death, twin brothers, should carry Sarpedon home to Lycia. Patroclus's spear went first through the heart of the Lycian king, and a shower of stones and blood-drawn swords buried the fight.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 16 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "But Patroclus, three times charging upon the rise of Troy, was struck back on the third by Apollo, unseen, with a flat hand that turned his mind; and a young Trojan, Euphorbus, whose spear had not yet tasted war; wounded him between the shoulder-blades; and Hector, seeing his grey eyes go out with the blow, drove his own spear through Patroclus's belly at close quarters. The dying man prophesied of Hector that his own death was near at hand at the hands of Achilles and that Achilles would not come as this day's saviour.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 16 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "So Patroclus died, with the words that Achilles' anger would end in the death of Hector. Hector drew out his spear and boasted over his body, while a bitter fighting raged over the corpse, Greeks and Trojans surging around dying men for possession of the dead. Word flew to Achilles' tent: Antilochus, the young son of Nestor, carried the terrible news, and a cold despair came over the son of Peleus, who had not yet heard that the day of death he had traded with Zeus was Patroclus's.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 16-17 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
