    lat: 39.96,
    lon: 26.24,
    period: "Composed c. 8th century BCE (Butler translation 1898)",
    source: {
      archive: "The Internet Classics Archive (MIT)",
      work: "Homer, Iliad, trans. Samuel Butler (1898), Books XX-XXI",
      url: "https://classics.mit.edu/Homer/iliad.html"
    },
    license: "Public domain (translation 1898)",
    summary: {
      en: "Achilles returns to the field and the armies break apart, with the gods themselves meleeing: Athena against Ares, Poseidon against Apollo. When the river Scamander chokes with the dead he has thrown into it and rises against Achilles, Hephaestus burns the waters dry until the gods part them.",
      zh: "\u963F\u57FA\u7409\u65AF\u91CD\u8FD4\u6230\u5834\uFF0C\u5169\u9663\u5927\u4E82\uFF0C\u8AF8\u795E\u4EA6\u89AA\u81EA\u4EA4\u92D2\uFF1A\u96C5\u5178\u5A1C\u6230\u963F\u745E\u65AF\uFF0C\u6CE2\u585E\u9813\u5C0D\u963F\u6CE2\u7F85\u3002\u514B\u73CA\u6258\u65AF\u6CB3\u585E\u6EFF\u5C4D\u9AD4\u800C\u66B4\u6012\u53CD\u64CA\uFF0C\u8D6B\u83F2\u65AF\u6258\u65AF\u4EE5\u70C8\u7130\u7099\u4E7E\u6CB3\u6C34\uFF0C\u5B99\u65AF\u5728\u65C1\u770B\u6230\uFF0C\u6700\u5F8C\u7531\u795E\u4F7F\u8ABF\u505C\u7F77\u624B\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "theophany",
      "divine-smith"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Zeus gave the gods leave to go down, and the battle split into a war of gods and men: Athena came against Ares, Poseidon took Apollo's challenge, and Hera and Artemis matched voices. Achilles, terrible as before but stripped of mercy, drove through the Trojans in a fury of slaughter; troop after troop fell before him as they fled for the city gates, and among those he caught Priam's son Lycaon by the river bank, gripping and drowning him to doom beside the water.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 20-21 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "He threw so many into the river, cutting the young men's flanks and holding their heads under, that Scamander's own waters rebelled: the river-god rose in a boiling wave and called on Achilles to stop, or the whole plain would drown. Achilles answered that he would keep on until Hector paid. Then the river closed on him, wave upon wave pushing him from the bank, and he gripped an elm and cursed the day; the gods, in open irony, gave Hephaestus his wind, and fire poured into the water, boiling the streams, searing the elms and willows, until the river-god yielded and the fight on the dry plain resumed.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 21 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Aeneas alone Achilles had spared, lifted out of his danger by Poseidon, who reminded the immortals that the line of Dardanus was fated to survive and rule the Trojans; but the rest of Troy's sons went down. The gods, finished with their own melee, stood off as the day of Achilles' victory gathered weight; and Zeus upon Olympus watched, weighing in his mind how much of Achilles' fury the city could bear before the fates called it too soon.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Iliad, Book 20-21 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
