    lon: 22.49,
    period: "Composed c. 8th century BCE (Butler translation 1898)",
    source: {
      archive: "The Internet Classics Archive (MIT)",
      work: "Homer, Odyssey, trans. Samuel Butler (1898), Book XI",
      url: "https://classics.mit.edu/Homer/odyssey.html"
    },
    license: "Public domain (translation 1898)",
    summary: {
      en: "Sent by Circe, Odysseus crosses the ocean to the country of the Cimmerians, digs a pit, and makes the dead come to him with blood offerings. The prophet Tiresias foretells Poseidon's spite, warns against the cattle of the Sun, and promises homecoming and a strange second journey by land \u2014 while Odysseus talks with his mother, Agamemnon, Achilles, Ajax, and sees the famous torment of Tantalus and Sisyphus.",
      zh: "\u5949\u5580\u8033\u523B\u4E4B\u547D\uFF0C\u5967\u5FB7\u4FEE\u65AF\u6A6B\u6E21\u5927\u6D0B\u81F3\u8F9B\u6885\u91CC\u5B89\u4EBA\u4E4B\u5730\uFF0C\u6398\u6E9D\u796D\u8840\uFF0C\u4EE4\u4EA1\u9B42\u8FD1\u524D\u554F\u4E8B\u3002\u5148\u77E5\u63D0\u745E\u897F\u963F\u65AF\u9810\u8A00\u6CE2\u585E\u9813\u4E4B\u6012\u3001\u8B66\u544A\u52FF\u50B7\u592A\u967D\u795E\u725B\u7FA4\u3001\u4E26\u8A31\u8AFE\u6B78\u5BB6\u8207\u4E00\u5834\u5947\u7279\u7684\u9678\u4E0A\u4E8C\u6B21\u9060\u884C\u3002\u4ED6\u53C8\u9047\u6BCD\u89AA\u3001\u963F\u4F3D\u9580\u8FB2\u3001\u963F\u57FA\u7409\u65AF\u8207\u57C3\u963F\u65AF\u4E4B\u9B42\uFF0C\u76EE\u7779\u5766\u5854\u7F85\u65AF\u8207\u897F\u6558\u798F\u65AF\u7684\u9177\u5211\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "descent-underworld"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Circe told Odysseus the route: a sail with wind she promised to raise along the river of Ocean, past sirens, past where two seas meet, to the place where the Cimmerians live wrapped in mist, where the sun never shines either on his rising or his setting. There a trench was to be dug with a sword, and sacrificial offerings poured in: milk and honey, sweet wine, water, barley, then the blood of black sheep; and all the souls of the dead would flock to the trench until he kept them back with the sword, letting only the one he wanted drink first.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 11 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The soul of his lost comrade Elpenor, who had fallen headfirst off a roof on Circe's island drunk, came first, and Odysseus promised the body burial. Then his mother Anticleia, whom he had not known to be dead, and Tiresias, whom he had come for, drank from the blood and prophesied to him: Poseidon's trouble on him for the eye he blinded, and the persistence of his anger; a hard homecoming, then rest; then a second journey, an oar carried inland until he met a man who had never seen the sea, whom he must teach; and after the cattle of Helios, labor and a gentle old age in Ithaca.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 11 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "When he had heard Tiresias out, Odysseus, letting his mother drink from the blood, learned that his wife was faithful, his father a beggar in his own fields, and his house held by insolent men. Agamemnon poured out his grief at home's murder, telling Odysseus of his own homecoming to his wife-murdering nephew, and warning him never to trust a woman beyond words. Achilles said joylessly that he would rather be a poor farmhand living above ground than a great lord of the dead, and asked after his father and son; his grief lasted until time brought his own affairs to mind. Ajax, still angry over the contest of arms, turned away without a word when called. He saw, too, the punishment of Tantalus, standing forever in a pool with fruit above him that receded from every reach, and of Sisyphus, endlessly trundling his rock against the run of the hill.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 11 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "When Heracles' shade itself came, in the form of the hero's glory but with death's terror about it, Odysseus drew his sword and held back the rest of the dead, lest the dark souls come on him in crowds; and he would have seen more heroes still, but the thought of dawn rushed up and he left for the ship. The underworld was thus the third stage of Odysseus's own road \u2014 the death Circe had warned him of, made a place of speech about the life ahead.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 11 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
