    license: "Public domain (translation 1898)",
    summary: {
      en: "Odysseus strips off his disguise, names himself, and kills Antinous mid-draught with the first arrow. Shut in the hall by Telemachus, himself, the swineherd Eumaeus and the cowherd, he and his small band destroy every suitor; the disloyal maids are hanged and the go-between Melanthius hacked to pieces.",
      zh: "\u5967\u5FB7\u4FEE\u65AF\u9664\u53BB\u507D\u88DD\u3001\u81EA\u5831\u59D3\u540D\uFF0C\u7B2C\u4E00\u652F\u7BAD\u8CAB\u7A7F\u6B63\u8209\u676F\u7684\u5B89\u63D0\u8AFE\u4FC4\u65AF\u3002\u5FD2\u52D2\u99AC\u79D1\u65AF\u3001\u7267\u8C6C\u4EBA\u6B50\u9081\u4FC4\u65AF\u8207\u7267\u725B\u4EBA\u6590\u7F85\u63D0\u4FC4\u65AF\u5408\u529B\u95DC\u9589\u6BBF\u9580\uFF0C\u4ED6\u5011\u4EE5\u5BE1\u64CA\u773E\uFF0C\u9010\u4E00\u8A85\u6BBA\u6240\u6709\u6C42\u5A5A\u8005\uFF1B\u4E0D\u5FE0\u7684\u4F8D\u5973\u88AB\u8655\u4EE5\u7D5E\u5211\uFF0C\u544A\u5BC6\u7684\u7267\u7F8A\u5974\u88AB\u5241\u6210\u788E\u584A\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "theophany"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Odysseus flung off his rags and stood by the threshold of the great hall with the bow and a quiver, and poured out his shaft against the throat of the mid-drinking Antinous; the death drained his body of wine and the cup rang over the gold-worked floor. Eurymachus tried to shift the blame onto Antinous, dead man now, and spoke of making restitution \u2014 twenty talents of gold, indemnity from the people's storehold; but Odysseus answered that not even all his goods could restore them to the road of peace: they had treated his house with insolence and his wife with dishonor.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 22 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The suitors snatched what weapons lie there under their feet and backs against the walls; and Telemachus, slipping out to the store-room as his father had sent him, brought back helmets, shields, and two spears for them, his father and the loyal herdsmen. Then Eurymachus tried spiriting the others: let the rest be spared this house's ruin, the men had made them do it; and he, the son whom Artemis and Apollo had both pained, was killed by an arrow in the chest that the father did not always turn aside; and the goose-boy made his own escape; odysseus, on the other hand, saying that if a man took to running, he would not live that way in my house of death again.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 22 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The loyal herdsman Philoetius and the swineherd stood like horses at the door; Melanthius, the go-between traitor, snuck out with his twin sons; the young men caught him, bound his feet and arms behind, dragged him into the hall, and, with a claim of justice, cut off his nose and ears and groin. But Melantho's treachery served them: they managed to reach the weapons in the store-room; and while Odysseus was striking the suitors in splendid lines, Athene appeared in likeness on the lintel with her shield before all, and the suitors, seeing the aegis, died in snowfall as of a storm.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 22 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "The end was the maids: the traitresses were told to carry out the dead before bed, and then, gathered in the court, were hanged on the ship's cable in a thin line, their feet not reaching the ground: the sons of a people drawn in one breath. And after that Odysseus, his son, and the loyal herdsmen seared the hall with fire and sulphur, to cleanse the house of the vengeance and its blood.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 22 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
