    period: "Composed c. 8th century BCE (Butler translation 1898)",
    source: {
      archive: "The Internet Classics Archive (MIT)",
      work: "Homer, Odyssey, trans. Samuel Butler (1898), Books XIII-XVI",
      url: "https://classics.mit.edu/Homer/odyssey.html"
    },
    license: "Public domain (translation 1898)",
    summary: {
      en: "The Phaeacians set the sleeping Odysseus and his gifts down in Ithaca; he searches the whole land for his own treasury till Athena, in the disguise of a young shepherd, shows him the island and then tousles him into the appearance of an old beggar. He hides at the hut of the old swineherd Eumaeus, who tells him the misery of the house, hears Odysseus's false tales of Cretan voyages, and reunites him, in secret, with his son.",
      zh: "\u8CBB\u57C3\u514B\u65AF\u4EBA\u5C07\u6C89\u7761\u7684\u5967\u5FB7\u4FEE\u65AF\u8207\u539A\u79AE\u9001\u62B5\u4F0A\u85A9\u5361\uFF1B\u4ED6\u4E0D\u8FA8\u5BB6\u9109\uFF0C\u76F4\u5230\u96C5\u5178\u5A1C\u5316\u8EAB\u5C11\u5E74\u7267\u4EBA\u76F8\u8A8D\uFF0C\u518D\u628A\u4ED6\u7684\u6A23\u8C8C\u6539\u6210\u8001\u4E5E\u4E10\u3002\u4ED6\u5BC4\u8EAB\u8001\u7267\u8C6C\u4EBA\u6B50\u9081\u4FC4\u65AF\u7684\u68DA\u5C4B\uFF0C\u807D\u5176\u54ED\u8A34\u5BB6\u5B85\u4E4B\u82E6\uFF0C\u7DE8\u9020\u514B\u91CC\u7279\u5546\u65C5\u7684\u5047\u6545\u4E8B\uFF0C\u4E26\u5728\u68DA\u4E2D\u8207\u6B78\u4F86\u7684\u5152\u5B50\u7955\u5BC6\u76F8\u8A8D\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "transformation",
      "reunion"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The Phaeacians themselves steered the oared ship through night and sea and, bringing the drowsing hero in his sleep, set him and chests of gifts on the shore below a hollow olive, and rowed away. Odysseus woke in his own land and did not know it: everything looked changed. He cursed aloud, thinking he had been dumped in a strange country; and the shepherd who came toward him with his flock, at whom he spoke angrily, was Athena, and she won from him the admission he would not give himself: he did not know where he was.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 13 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Then the goddess showed herself, named the land, and struck him with her wand: his skin shrunk to an aged man; his fair brown hair was gone from his head and chin; his eyes were dimmed; and the suit of clothes he wore was a foul old wrapper. So disguised, he put his treasure in the cave of the nymphs, sealed by a great stone, and went up country, where the sound of grinding made him pause.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 13 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The swineherd's hut held four young dogs that rushed at the guest before the swineherd himself, Eumaeus, drove them off; and Odysseus, no longer asking who fed the pigs, learned that the man had served his master faithfully alone. Eumaeus killed and cooked two young pigs for the stranger with his own hands and set before him, filling his wine-cup and promising his first food to every comer, for the suitors had laid waste the swine this long while; and such was the pig-herding under Penelope's threats.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 14 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "Telemachus, warned by Athena, avoiding the ambush his father set for him, came to the hut. At Athena's suggestion the swineherd went to the old woman Eurycleia to share the news with Penelope, and Athena, taking the shape of a beautiful woman for a moment in the doorway that only Odysseus saw, brought her wand down and made the dogs fawn instead of snarl; then Odysseus told his son his name, and the two men, clutching one another with tears, put heads together in the dark to plan the ruin of the suitors.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 16 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "athena",
      "zeus",
      "eumaeus",
      "melanthius",
      "odysseus",
      "telemachus"
    ]
