    license: "Public domain (translation 1898)",
    summary: {
      en: "Telemachus visits Nestor at Pylos and Menelaus and Helen at Sparta. At Sparta, Menelaus recounts how he held the shape-shifting sea-elder Proteus, who revealed that Odysseus was alive on Calypso's island; meanwhile the suitors' ambush reads its last steps on Ithaca toward his own return.",
      zh: "\u5FD2\u52D2\u99AC\u79D1\u65AF\u5148\u8A2A\u76AE\u6D1B\u65AF\u7684\u6D85\u65AF\u6258\u8033\uFF0C\u518D\u5230\u65AF\u5DF4\u9054\u7684\u58A8\u6D85\u62C9\u4FC4\u65AF\u8207\u6D77\u502B\u5BAE\u4E2D\u3002\u6D77\u502B\u8B1B\u8FF0\u8001\u6D77\u795E\u666E\u7F85\u900F\u65AF\u7684\u795E\u529B\u66F4\u5F62\uFF0C\u800C\u58A8\u6D85\u62C9\u4FC4\u65AF\u6349\u4F4F\u4ED6\u5F8C\u5F97\u77E5\uFF1A\u5967\u5FB7\u4FEE\u65AF\u9084\u6D3B\u8457\uFF0C\u88AB\u5361\u5442\u666E\u7D22\u56F0\u5728\u9060\u65B9\u7684\u5CF6\u4E0A\u3002\u6B64\u6642\u6C42\u5A5A\u8005\u7684\u4F0F\u5175\u5DF2\u7D93\u4F48\u4E0B\uFF0C\u7B49\u4ED6\u8FD4\u62B5\u5BB6\u9580\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "oracle-divination"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "At Pylos Telemachus found Nestor sacrificing to Poseidon with his sons, and the old man received him in the spirit of good hosts: he could not know that the boy before him was Odysseus's, and Nestor told tales of the returns \u2014 of Agamemnon murdered at home by Aegisthus, and of the storm that scattered the fleet after the row with the gods at Troy. Nestor could not advise him by sea (he had none), but sent him off on his own son Pisistratus's chariot with the young man as companion to Lacedaemon.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 3 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "At Sparta, Menelaus and Helen welcomed the two young men into their house of bronze; while the young pair ate, Helen spoke to the guests in disguise, all of them was the son of Odysseus himself: she had known him at Troy by his likeness to his father, and spoke of how she had wept at Odysseus's patience. Menelaus, pouring for his friend beneath the cold water's regret, told them when the two had retired to a room: he learned on his return from Egypt the hard way: he had been stuck by contrary winds on the island of Pharos; and it was there that he had been visited by the old sea-elder.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 4 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Menelaus told how, with Proteus's daughter's help, he and companions wrapped themselves in seal-skins on the sand and waited until noontide, when Proteus came out of the sea to count his seals and lay down among them. Set upon and held, the old sea-god changed from shape to shape \u2014 a lion, a serpent, a leopard, a boar, water, a tree \u2014 until Menelaus still soaked and held and would not let go, so that at last the shape-sifter gave the true words he sought. This was the news Menelaus carried home to Telemachus: that Odysseus was alive on the island of the nymph Calypso, without ship or men to bring him home.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Homer, Odyssey, Book 4 (Butler 1898); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
