    motifs: [
      "divine-marriage",
      "miraculous-birth",
      "demigod"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Fair-cheeked Demeter lay with the hero Iasion in a thrice-ploughed fallow field in the fruitful land of Crete, and bore Plutus, the kindly god who walks the whole earth and the back of the sea; the man who meets him and takes him by the hand is enriched, for Wealth brims his hands with substance. And golden Aphrodite's daughter Harmonia, wed to Cadmus, bore Ino, Semele, fair-cheeked Agave, Autonoe, and Polydorus, in the fair-meadowed halls of Thebes.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Theogony, lines ca. 963-980 (Evelyn-White 1914); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "And Eos bore to Tithonus the bronze-helmeted Memnon, king of the Aethiopians, and lord Emathion; and to Cephalus a shining son, mighty Phaethon, a god among mortal men: while he was still a delicate youth in tender flower, laughter-loving Aphrodite seized him up and made him her nightly watchman of sacred temples, a shining guardian. And by the plan of the immortal gods the strong son of Aeson, Jason, bore off from Aeetes' halls the daughter of Zeus-honoured Aeetes \u2014 after the many grievous tasks set him by the wild king \u2014 and brought her home to Iolcus by night, on the swift ship, with rosy-cheeked Medea, child of golden Aphrodite's force upon Idyia's blood-line, and wed her: she bore Medus, whom the centaur Chiron reared in the mountains.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Theogony, lines ca. 984-1000 (Evelyn-White 1914); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The daughters of the Old Man of the Sea came last: subtle-minded Psamathe bore to Aeacus the great Phocus; and silver-footed Thetis, wedding Peleus, bore the lion-hearted Achilles, the scatterer of ranks. And Cyprus-born Cytherea lay with Anchises on the peaks of windy Ida with its many glens, and bore Aeneas. Circe, child of Helios, bore to the enduring Odysseus Agrius and Latinus, lordly rulers of the far Tyrrhenian isles; and Calypso, nymph of Ogygia, bore him Nausithous and Nausinous: and so the line of heroes was braided into the dynasty of the gods.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Theogony, lines ca. 1010-1018 (Evelyn-White 1914); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
