      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Sea's old man Phorcys wed the deep Ceto, and their children glittered at the edge of the world: the grey-haired Graiae, sisters born with grey from the womb, and the Gorgons who dwell beyond glorious Ocean at the edge of night where the clear-voiced Hesperides keep house \u2014 Sthenno and Euryale, immortal and ageless forever, and Medusa alone of the three was mortal.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Theogony, lines ca. 270-281 (Evelyn-White 1914); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The dark-haired Poseidon had lain with Medusa in a soft meadow among the spring flowers; and so when the hero Perseus cut off her head, the Gorgon's death was also a birth-opening: out of her neck came the great Chrysaor and the foal Pegasus, the one named for the gold sword he bore, the other born by the water's edge.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Theogony, lines ca. 277-285 (Evelyn-White 1914); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Pegasus left the earth, mother of flocks, and flew up to the immortals: he dwells thereafter in the house of Zeus, bringing to his king the well-woven thunder and lightning that the god hurls. Chrysaor, in turn, married Callirrhoe, daughter of Ocean, and she bore him the triple-headed Geryon, whom Heracles would one day kill for his shambling cattle on the far island of Erytheia.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Theogony, lines ca. 283-295 (Evelyn-White 1914); not verbatim."
      }
