    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "When Odin, Vili and Ve slew Ymir, so much blood rushed from the giant's wounds that all his kin drowned in it - the first flood of the world. Only one household escaped: Bergelmir with his wife climbed onto a wooden trough (ludr) and floated the deluge, and from the two of them descend all the rime-giants. A Norse counterpart to Deucalion and the biblical flood, the story explains why the giants survived to menace the gods, and makes the enemies of order itself a remnant people saved by water at the making of the world.",
      zh: "\u5967\u4E01\u3001\u7DAD\u5229\u8207\u5049\u8A85\u6BBA\u5C24\u5F4C\u723E\u6642\uFF0C\u5DE8\u4EBA\u8840\u6E67\u6210\u6ED4\u5929\u6D2A\u6C34\uFF0C\u6DF9\u6C92\u5176\u65CF\u4EBA\u2014\u2014\u6B64\u70BA\u4E16\u4E0A\u7B2C\u4E00\u5834\u6D2A\u6C34\u3002\u552F\u6709\u8C9D\u683C\u723E\u7C73\u723E\u592B\u5A66\u767B\u4E0A\u6728\u81FC\uFF08ludr\uFF09\u6F02\u65BC\u6D2A\u6C34\u4E4B\u4E0A\uFF0C\u5016\u514D\u65BC\u96E3\uFF0C\u5176\u5F8C\u4EE3\u5373\u6240\u6709\u971C\u5DE8\u4EBA\u3002\u6B64\u795E\u8A71\u8207\u4E1F\u5361\u5229\u7FC1\u53CA\u8056\u7D93\u6D2A\u6C34\u5E73\u884C\uFF0C\u65E2\u89E3\u91CB\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4F55\u4EE5\u5B58\u7E8C\u4EE5\u5A01\u8105\u8AF8\u795E\uFF0C\u4EA6\u4F7F\u79E9\u5E8F\u7684\u4EC7\u6575\u6210\u70BA\u5728\u4E16\u754C\u8A95\u751F\u4E4B\u969B\u7531\u6C34\u6551\u8D77\u7684\u5B51\u907A\u4E4B\u65CF\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Gylfaginning via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "The making of the world is inseparable from an act of drowning. In Vafthrudnismal - a wisdom-contest in which Odin wagers his head against the ancient giant Vafthrudnir - Odin asks the oldest question he knows: whence came Aurgelmir's kin, the rime-giants? The giant answers that the eldest of years is Bergelmir, and that when Ymir was slain, the one 'on the ludr' floated the waters of the deluge; on the ludr - the trough or mill-vat, the timber of a primitive mill - Bergelmir and his wife went forth, and from them are all the giants come.\n\nSnorri turns the fragment into narrative. The blood of Ymir poured out so abundantly that it flooded everything: his sons Odin, Vili and Ve had no purpose in it other than the making of the world, and the flood was a by-product of the killing - the first catastrophe was collateral damage of the first act of order. Only Bergelmir and his wife climbed into the ludr and were saved; Snorri adds pointedly that from them a new race of giants is come, wherefore it is said in the old songs that the giants are a very ancient race - older in lineage than the gods themselves even though younger in the world.\n\nThe tale carries a dense set of cultural functions. It establishes a Germanic flood myth parallel to Deucalion and Pyrrha in Greece and to the deluge of Genesis and Mesopotamian literature - a theme motif-collectors codified as A1010, the world-flood and the surviving pair. It explains the structure of Norse enmity: the giants are not an original evil but a survivor-population, refugees of a cosmic crime committed by the gods, which grounds the moral ambiguity of the entire myth-world. And it defines the giants genealogically as a people - 'from them are come' - so that the war between gods and giants is framed as an ethnic myth of two nations, not a battle of good and evil.\n\nScholars have debated the meaning of the ludr: a mill-trough (Vafthrudnismal elsewhere places Bergelmir's mill in Jotunheim), a cradle-like boat, or a raft; all readings keep the paradox that the ark of the giants' salvation is a humble domestic implement of wood. Vafthrudnismal's phrasing also makes Bergelmir 'the eldest of years', a being as primordial as Ymir himself, so that the flood-remnant is nearly as old as the cosmos. Snorri's use of the poem is so close that his Gylfaginning paragraph is effectively a prose translation of st. 35 - a rare window into how the learned Icelandic compilers mined eddic verse for narrative.\n\nThe flood is never allowed its full closure: giants will fill the world again until Ragnarok, when the deluge's logic returns - the sea rising, the sun darkening, the survivors once more reduced to a pair. The myth thus forms a bracket around Norse time: from a flood at creation to a drowning at the end, with the remnant-pair the constant of both."
    },
