    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "In the first days the gods met on the plain of Idavoll and founded temples and high places, laid forges and made tools, played at tables, and lacked nothing - gold was counted wealth enough, until three giant-maidens came out of Jotunheim with burdens of that too-precious metal. The passage is a lost-age myth and, simultaneously, a template: at the world's renewal the surviving gods will find again on the same plain the golden game-pieces in the grass.",
      zh: "\u592A\u521D\u4E4B\u6642\uFF0C\u8AF8\u795E\u6703\u65BC\u4F0A\u9054\u5E73\u539F\uFF0C\u5EFA\u795E\u6BBF\u8207\u9AD8\u5EA7\uFF0C\u7F6E\u935B\u7210\u3001\u88FD\u5DE5\u5177\uFF0C\u64F2\u9AB0\u5C0D\u5F08\uFF0C\u4E00\u7121\u6240\u7F3A\u2014\u2014\u5F7C\u6642\u9EC3\u91D1\u53EA\u88AB\u7A31\u4F5C\u8DB3\u5920\u7684\u8CA1\u5BCC\uFF0C\u76F4\u5230\u4E09\u4F4D\u5DE8\u4EBA\u65CF\u5C11\u5973\u81EA\u7D04\u9813\u6D77\u59C6\u800C\u4F86\uFF0C\u5E36\u8457\u90A3\u904E\u5206\u8CB4\u91CD\u7684\u91D1\u5C6C\u3002\u6B64\u6BB5\u65E2\u662F\u5931\u843D\u6642\u4EE3\u7684\u795E\u8A71\uFF0C\u4E5F\u662F\u4E00\u500B\u6A21\u677F\uFF1A\u4E16\u754C\u66F4\u65B0\u4E4B\u65E5\uFF0C\u5016\u5B58\u8AF8\u795E\u5C07\u5728\u4F0A\u9054\u5E73\u539F\u7684\u8349\u9593\u91CD\u65B0\u62FE\u8D77\u9EC3\u91D1\u68CB\u5B50\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Voluspa's golden age is six short lines with an architecture of institutions. The gods went to their judgment-seats, the holy gods held council on the plain of Idavoll, and there they founded temples and high places - shrines; with tools, tongs and forges they laid; they made wealth, smithied ore, shaped tongs and made tools; and at tables they played at chess, gleeful, they lacked nothing - no gold, till there came three giant-maidens, mighty-mighty, out of Jotunheim.\n\nThe passage compresses a whole cultural program: religion (temples and high seats), law (judgment-seats, councils), technology (forges, tools, smithied wealth), and leisure (the board-game). Its grammar of origin matches Greek accounts of the Olympian order after the Titanomachy - the founding of divine society is told as the founding of institutions. What ruins the age is not a fall from virtue but an import: gold, arriving in the hands of three giant-women, becomes the metal of greed and later the subject of the Andvari curse and the whole Niflung cycle; gold is the first transgression of the gods' economy.\n\nThe golden age functions as a temporal anchor in a myth-cycle that knows decline: it is the positive pole against which the first war (the Aesir-Vanir war), the killing of Baldr, and Ragnarok are measured. And in a uniquely Norse move, the age is scheduled for restoration: at the renewal after Ragnarok, the surviving gods again find on Idavoll the wondrous golden game-pieces in the grass - the same chess-pieces of the first age lying where the old world fell. The plain of Idavoll is thus both the site of the first founding and of the second, a palindrome of institutional memory.\n\nFor the motif database the record demonstrates a structural rule of Norse myth: institutions are founded by council, and ruins are remembered as recoverable board-games. No other European corpus makes its paradise a meeting-place where law and craft are invented, then lost, then refound with the selfsame objects.\n\nThe gold-maidens' link completes the doctrine: the three giant-women arriving 'with burdens of gold' connect the golden age's end to the whole Niflung-cycle - the metal that ends paradise becomes the curse-metal of the heroic corpus, the first import the gods' economy cannot digest. And the restoration-template completes the record: at the renewal the surviving gods find again the golden game-pieces 'in the grass' on Idavoll - the same plain, the same objects - so the golden age is not lost but stored, recoverable by literal rediscovery. The record thus anchors the lost-age family: paradise as a meeting-place where law and craft are invented, lost and refound with the selfsame game-pieces."
    },
    functions: [
      "Founding myth of divine institutions: temples, law-courts, forges, leisure",
      "Decline-myth engine: the arrival of giant-women and gold begins the economy of greed",
      "Restoration template: post-Ragnarok renewal on the same plain with the same game-pieces",
      "Temporal anchor for measuring all later mythic decline"
    ],
