    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923; Laing, Heimskringla 1844)",
      work: "Voluspa st. 21-26; Snorri, Ynglinga saga chs. 1-4; critical ed. Dronke (1997)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/heim/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Laing 1844); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The first war in the world began when the witch-woman Gullveig was burned three times and reborn three times in Odin's hall, and the Vanir - whom the wise gods had cheated of their pledge, Freyja - broke the peace. Shield against spear, the war staled to a stalemate, and the two divine nations made peace by spitting into a vat: from the mingled spittle was made Kvasir, wisest of all, and hostages Njord, Freyr and Freyja came to live among the Aesir. The war explains why the Norse pantheon has two houses, why sacrifice and magic are split between them, and why the gods' counsel, Mimir, paid for the treaty with his head.",
      zh: "\u4E16\u9593\u7B2C\u4E00\u5834\u6230\u722D\u6E90\u65BC\u5973\u5DEB\u53E4\u723E\u7DAD\u683C\u2014\u2014\u5979\u5728\u5967\u4E01\u7684\u5927\u5EF3\u88AB\u711A\u71D2\u4E09\u6B21\uFF0C\u53C8\u4E09\u6B21\u91CD\u751F\uFF1B\u88AB\u300C\u777F\u667A\u8AF8\u795E\u300D\u4EE5\u4EBA\u8CEA\u8299\u857E\u96C5\u6B3A\u779E\u7684\u83EF\u7D0D\u65CF\u9042\u80CC\u68C4\u8A93\u7D04\u3002\u76FE\u8207\u77DB\u76F8\u6297\uFF0C\u6230\u4E8B\u81A0\u8457\uFF0C\u5169\u500B\u795E\u65CF\u6700\u7D42\u4EE5\u553E\u6DB2\u532F\u5165\u5DE8\u7515\u7DE0\u7D50\u548C\u5E73\uFF1A\u6DF7\u5408\u7684\u553E\u6DB2\u8A95\u751F\u4E86\u5168\u667A\u7684\u514B\u74E6\u5E0C\u723E\uFF0C\u800C\u4EBA\u8CEA\u5C3C\u5967\u5FB7\u53CA\u5176\u5B50\u5973\u5247\u524D\u4F86\u963F\u85A9\u795E\u65CF\u5B9A\u5C45\u3002\u9019\u5834\u6230\u722D\u89E3\u91CB\u4E86\u5317\u6B50\u795E\u8B5C\u4F55\u4EE5\u6709\u5169\u500B\u5BB6\u65CF\u3001\u796D\u7940\u8207\u9B54\u6CD5\u4F55\u4EE5\u5206\u5C6C\u5169\u65CF\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u8AF8\u795E\u7684\u8B00\u4E3B\u5BC6\u7C73\u723E\u4F55\u4EE5\u70BA\u689D\u7D04\u4ED8\u51FA\u9996\u7D1A\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Ynglinga saga via Laing 1844",
    narrative: {
      en: "The opening of Voluspa's war-fragment is a trial: she remembers the first war in the world, when Gullveig they pierced with spears and burned thrice - thrice burned, thrice reborn, ever anew she lives. Her name, Gold-drink or Gold-drunkenness, marks what the war was about; the sibyl then names her Heid, a witch in every house they saw her, teaching seid, the magic of prophecy, always delighting in evil. Then the war proper: the Vanir, whom the wise gods had overreached with oaths, broke their pledges - for Freyja had been given as a pledge and came back cheated, says Loki in Lokasenna, who claims he was there when it happened.\n\nThe prose tradition, Ynglinga saga, makes the casus belli explicit: Odin, leader of the Aesir in their Asiatic migration, cast his spear over the Vanir and began the first war. The Vanir defended their land so fiercely that the Aesir could not prevail, and peace was made on these terms: each side spat into a vat, and the mingled spittle - because no lie can come from it - was shaped into a man, Kvasir, wisest of all; and as hostages the Vanir sent Njord with Freyr and Freyja, while the Aesir sent Hoenir and Mimir to the Vanir. The exchange turns sour: Hoenir is handsome but dependent, answering every question 'let others decide', so the Vanir, discovering they were cheated, cut off Mimir's head and sent it back. Odin embalmed it with herbs, sang spells over it, and thereby gained the head that counsels him - wisdom preserved from an act of diplomatic atrocity.\n\nThe myth's cultural functions are heavy. It explains the composite pantheon: two houses, one of sky- and war-gods (Aesir) and one of sea-, wealth- and fertility-powers (Vanir), merged after an ethnic war - the mythic memory, some scholars think, of an actual merger of cults. It founds the ritual economy: hostageship and oath-fines, the spitting vat as the origin of the peace-oath, and Freyja's arrival as the introduction of seid magic into Asgard (the Vanir hostages bring the arts of prosperity and prophecy; the Aesir bring spear-luck). And it seeds the mead of poetry, for Kvasir, born of the peace, is killed by dwarfs whose brewing of his blood becomes the mead Odin will steal - the first war's peace-child turns into the very substance of skaldic art.\n\nThe war-fragment also preserves a motif of medieval politics: hostage-exchange as institution. In saga Iceland and viking Scandinavia, peace was sealed by fosterage and hostages; the myth casts the gods in the same legal forms, so that the divine order is literally a treaty, not a family tree alone."
    },
