    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "In his own voice Odin tells how he hung on a windswept tree nine full nights, wounded with a spear and given to himself, to himself; none brought him bread or drink; he looked down, took up the runes, screaming, and fell back from the tree. The myth is the origin of runic writing and magic: the alphabet is won by a god's self-sacrifice on the gallows-tree, and with the runes come eighteen songs of power - healing, binding, blunting weapons, quenching fire, warding sorcery - and a closing taboo: Odin will teach the eighteenth song to no man or woman, only to a dead horse the king was going to bring back to life.",
      zh: "\u5967\u4E01\u89AA\u8FF0\uFF1A\u4ED6\u61F8\u65BC\u8FCE\u98A8\u4E4B\u6A39\u6574\u6574\u4E5D\u591C\uFF0C\u88AB\u77DB\u6240\u523A\uFF0C\u5C07\u81EA\u5DF1\u737B\u796D\u7D66\u81EA\u5DF1\uFF1B\u7121\u4EBA\u8CDC\u4ED6\u9EB5\u5305\u6216\u9152\u6F3F\uFF1B\u4ED6\u4FEF\u8EAB\u5411\u4E0B\uFF0C\u62FE\u53D6\u5982\u5C3C\u6587\uFF0C\u9577\u562F\u800C\u843D\u56DE\u6A39\u4E0B\u3002\u6B64\u5373\u5982\u5C3C\u6587\u5B57\u8207\u9B54\u6CD5\u4E4B\u8D77\u6E90\uFF1A\u5B57\u6BCD\u7D93\u7531\u795E\u5728\u7D5E\u5211\u4E4B\u6A39\u4E0A\u7684\u81EA\u6211\u72A7\u7272\u800C\u8D0F\u5F97\u3002\u96A8\u5982\u5C3C\u6587\u800C\u4F86\u7684\u662F\u5341\u516B\u9996\u5492\u6B4C\u2014\u2014\u7642\u50B7\u3001\u675F\u7E1B\u3001\u920D\u5316\u5175\u5203\u3001\u7184\u706B\u3001\u62B5\u79A6\u5DEB\u8853\u2014\u2014\u6700\u5F8C\u662F\u7981\u5FCC\uFF1A\u7B2C\u5341\u516B\u9996\u5492\u6B4C\uFF0C\u5967\u4E01\u4E0D\u80AF\u50B3\u6388\u65BC\u4EBA\uFF0C\u800C\u662F\u50B3\u7D66\u4E00\u5339\u570B\u738B\u6B32\u5FA9\u6D3B\u4E4B\u6B7B\u99AC\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); consulted against Dronke 1997",
    narrative: {
      en: "The myth of the runes is told by Odin in the first person, in the stern voice of a man remembering his own initiation: I know that I hung on a windswept tree nine long nights, wounded with a spear, given to Odin, myself to myself; on that tree of which no man knows from what root it rises. He had no bread and no horn of drink; he peered downward, took up the runes - screaming I took them, and I fell back from there - and then, from the sons of men happy, glad of harvest, grew I, and wise; word from word gave me word, deed from deed gave me deed.\n\nThe passage is the origin myth of the runic alphabet and, with it, of magic: the letters are not invented but won, and the price is a god's voluntary death-shape. The theological shock is the reflexive formula - 'myself to myself' - Odin sacrifices Odin, offering and offering-place in one; scholars have long compared the Christian crucifixion and debated influence in either direction, but the Norse myth's own logic is that of an exchange: the god gives the highest thing he has (his own life-suspension) and receives in exchange the highest thing the cosmos has (the runes, the script that names everything).\n\nThen follow the eighteen songs. Their contents are a pharmacopoeia of magic: help in sorrow and pain; the charm that heals wounds; the spell that blunts a foe's weapons; the fetter-snare for the tongue; the counterspell that breaks fetters; the throwing that stops a spear in flight; the fire-song that quenches flame; the charm that turns hatred away; the stilling of storms at sea; the banishing of witches from their horses; the ward against sorcery; the raising of the dead to answer questions; the winning of a maiden's love; and the eighteenth that is never taught to anyone - that I shall tell to no man or woman but to that dead horse which the king was going to bring back to life. The joke closes the sequence: the eighteenth song is resurrection, and Odin has practiced it only on a horse.\n\nThe cultural function is to make writing sacred. Runic inscriptions were cut for memorials, ownership, curses and prayer, and - per this myth - are a gift that runs down from Odin's self-sacrifice: the letter-forms themselves are a wound on wood. The taboo of the last song is the myth's moral: the highest magic is hoarded by its inventor, and the anecdote of the dead horse turns the doctrine of resurrection into a barb at the expense of kings. In database terms, this is the Norse 'theft of fire' - the civilizing gift that Prometheus stole is here not stolen but self-sacrificed, and its vocabulary is not flame but letter.\n\nThe rune-won doctrine's ritual context completes the record: the self-hanging on the tree is the initiation-rite's mythic form (the ordeal-hanging documented in Germanic practice), and the reflexive formula ('myself to myself') makes Odin both offer and offering-place - the sacrifice's grammar stated in one clause. And the eighteen songs' catalog completes the record: the practical pharmacopoeia of galdr (healing, binding, blunting, quenching, warding, waking the dead) with the eighteenth (resurrection) withheld - the myth's moral: the highest magic hoarded by its inventor, and the dead-horse joke as the doctrine's barb. The record thus anchors the sacred-writing family: the alphabet won by a god's self-sacrifice, the letters as wounds on wood."
    },
    functions: [
      "Origin myth of the runic alphabet as a gift won by self-sacrifice",
      "Doctrine of magic: eighteen songs cataloging the practical pharmacopoeia of galdr",
      "Reflexive sacrifice: the god offers himself to himself, making writing a divine wound",
      "Closing taboo: the eighteenth (resurrection) song withheld from humans"
    ],
