    lat: 64.26,
    lon: -21.13,
    period: "Gylfaginning c. 1220-1230 (older material); Voluspa st. 4; principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2365 4to, GKS 2367 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923) / Project Gutenberg (Brodeur 1916)",
      work: "Snorri, Gylfaginning chs. 7-8, 34; Voluspa st. 4; critical ed. Faulkes (1987)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/pre/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "To defend the new world from the giants, the gods raised a wall around the middle of Ymir's eyebrows - the enclosure called Midgard - and beyond it the sea, whose edge the gods gave to the lands. Into that encircling ocean Odin cast Loki's monstrous middle child, the serpent Jormungand, which grew so vast that, lying in the ring of the sea, it gripped its own tail. The record is a double aition: of the coastlines of the inhabited world and of the apocalyptic serpent that will one day release its tail and poison the sky.",
      zh: "\u70BA\u9632\u7BC4\u5DE8\u4EBA\uFF0C\u8AF8\u795E\u5728\u5C24\u5F4C\u723E\u7709\u6BDB\u4E2D\u90E8\u7BC9\u8D77\u570D\u7246\u2014\u2014\u9019\u9053\u570D\u6B04\u540D\u70BA\u7C73\u5FB7\u52A0\u723E\u7279\u2014\u2014\u7246\u5916\u662F\u5927\u6D77\uFF0C\u8AF8\u795E\u628A\u6D77\u5CB8\u8CDC\u7D66\u9678\u5730\u3002\u5967\u4E01\u5C07\u6D1B\u57FA\u7684\u602A\u7269\u6B21\u5B50\u3001\u5DE8\u86C7\u8036\u5922\u52A0\u5F97\u62CB\u5165\u74B0\u7E5E\u7684\u5927\u6D0B\u4E4B\u4E2D\uFF0C\u86C7\u8EAB\u9577\u5F97\u5982\u6B64\u5DE8\u5927\uFF0C\u4EE5\u81F3\u76E4\u65BC\u6D77\u74B0\u4E4B\u4E0A\u6642\u7ADF\u80FD\u54AC\u4F4F\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u5C3E\u5DF4\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u96D9\u91CD\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\uFF1A\u65E2\u89E3\u91CB\u4EBA\u985E\u6240\u5C45\u4E16\u754C\u7684\u6D77\u5CB8\u7DDA\uFF0C\u4E5F\u89E3\u91CB\u7D42\u6709\u4E00\u65E5\u9B06\u958B\u5C3E\u5DF4\u3001\u6BD2\u5316\u5929\u7A7A\u7684\u672B\u65E5\u4E4B\u86C7\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The building of Midgard is described in Gylfaginning as urban planning of cosmic scale. After the gods slew Ymir and made the earth from his flesh, the sea from his blood and the sky from his skull, they took his eyebrows - the fringe of hair above the great corpse's eyes - and made of them the fortress-wall of the middle world, and called the enclosure Midgard, 'middle enclosure'. Beyond the wall they gave the lands to men, and the sea's edge to the shore-peoples; outside lies Utgard, the out-yard, where the giants live. The world of men is thus literally an eyebrow of the primordial corpse - a thin defensive fringe between two hostile zones.\n\nThen Odin conscripted the enemy's own offspring as the outer defense. Loki's three monstrous children - the wolf Fenrir, the serpent Jormungand and the girl Hel - had been brought before the gods in Asgard, and prophecy said they would cause great harm. Odin threw the serpent into the encircling ocean, where it grew so vast that, lying in the ring of all lands, it gripped its own tail. The World-Serpent thus became the mythic geometry of the ocean itself: the sea is a serpent, and every shore is the edge of its body.\n\nThe cultural functions are cartographic and eschatological at once. Midgard's wall explains why the inhabited world is an island-ring with hostile beyonds; the serpent explains the sea's unity (it has one body) and its menace (one bite poisons everything). The image is concentric: hearth, wall, shore, sea, serpent's scales, tail - the last linking back to the first, since the tail is in the serpent's own mouth. And the myth sets up the two signature encounters of Norse myth: Thor's fishing trip, when he hooks the serpent's head (the eye of the ocean glares into the fisher's), and the final battle at Ragnarok, when the tail releases and the serpent rises, and Thor kills it and dies of its venom after nine steps.\n\nVoluspa's opening cosmology carries the same architecture in compressed form: the sun and moon were set on their courses, the ground was green, and then the sibyl sees the Yggdrasil of fate and the genders of men - the middle world already fenced, already fated. The record anchors the motif 'world-serpent' (A-code world-encircling reptile) that the atlas tracks from Jormungand to Leviathan to the rainbow serpent of Australia: the ocean as an animal that has swallowed the world's edge."
    },
    functions: [
      "Cartographic aition: the inhabited world as an enclosure (eyebrow of Ymir) ringed by hostile beyonds",
      "Explains the sea as a single living body encircling all lands",
      "Sets up Thor's fishing exploit and the apocalyptic release of the serpent at Ragnarok",
      "Anchors the world-serpent motif family across world mythologies"
    ],
