    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "When Freyr cut his first tooth, the Aesir gave him as a tooth-gift the realm called Alfheim, home of the light-elves - fairer than the sun to look on. Below, in the dark, dwell the dark-elves, blacker than pitch. The passage is the Norse doctrine of the two elf-peoples, the gift-economy of childhood (a god's first tooth is worth a country), and the origin of the European fairy-land: the realm given away at a teething party.",
      zh: "\u5F17\u96F7\u521D\u6B21\u9577\u7259\u4E4B\u6642\uFF0C\u963F\u85A9\u795E\u65CF\u4EE5\u540D\u70BA\u963F\u723E\u592B\u6D77\u59C6\u7684\u570B\u5EA6\u76F8\u8D08\uFF0C\u90A3\u662F\u5149\u4E4B\u7CBE\u9748\u7684\u5C45\u6240\u2014\u2014\u5176\u8C8C\u7F8E\u65BC\u592A\u967D\u3002\u800C\u5728\u4E0B\u65B9\u5E7D\u6697\u4E4B\u8655\uFF0C\u4F4F\u8457\u6BD4\u67CF\u6CB9\u66F4\u9ED1\u7684\u6697\u7CBE\u9748\u3002\u6B64\u6BB5\u662F\u5317\u6B50\u300C\u5169\u652F\u7CBE\u9748\u65CF\u300D\u7684\u5B78\u8AAA\u3001\u7AE5\u5E74\u7684\u994B\u8D08\u7D93\u6FDF\uFF08\u795E\u7684\u4E73\u7259\u62B5\u5F97\u4E00\u570B\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EA6\u662F\u6B50\u6D32\u4ED9\u5883\uFF08fairy-land\uFF09\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\uFF1A\u4E00\u7247\u5728\u9577\u7259\u5BB4\u4E0A\u88AB\u9001\u51FA\u7684\u570B\u571F\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Gylfaginning installs the elves in two sentences attached to Freyr's biography: when the young Freyr cut his first tooth, the Aesir gave him as a tooth-gift the realm of Alfheim - and in it dwell the light-elves, fairer than the sun to look upon. But the dark-elves dwell below the earth, and they are unlike them in appearance, and unlike them too in their deeds: they are blacker than pitch. The two peoples, bright and black, share one name-family (alfar) and one geography: above, the shining; below, the pitch.\n\nGrimnismal confirms the upper geography in its litany of halls: Alfheim the gods gave to Freyr of old as a tooth-payment - confirming the tooth-gift doctrine in verse. The eddic elsewhere adds a third register: the elves appear as a people with their own halls (Lokasenna: why should the Aesir quarrel in the elves' home - the feast is held at Aegir's, but Loki's insults fly at elf-halls too), and as healers' kin (the ritual phrase 'helping-runes for birth ... on the beams of the elves' in Sigrdrifumal), and as a race whose women are dangerous to marry (the shield-maiden Brynhild warns of the elf-women). The sagas complete the picture: Iceland's social world is haunted by alf-folk, and the Kormaks saga hero is cured by an elf-sacrifice on a mound.\n\nThe cultural functions are threefold. First, an aition of the fairy-realm: medieval and modern 'elfland' - Tolkien's and Shakespeare's alike - descends from this doctrine of a bright country given away at a child's teething, populated by beings fairer than the sun. Second, a gift-economy of childhood: the Norse elite celebrated a boy's first tooth with a present of territory; the myth both mirrors and magnifies the real custom. Third, a moral geography: the light-elves and dark-elves encode the same above/below polarity as Asgard/Hel - illumination is a matter of residence, not species, since both peoples are alfar.\n\nIn the atlas's comparative frame, Alfheim answers the Greek Elysium and the Japanese Takamagahara: each tradition imagines a bright near-world adjacent to the human; the Norse one is distinctive for having been privately owned - a god's childhood fee.\n\nThe elves' wider economy completes the doctrine: the eddic poems' elf-halls (Lokasenna's feast at Aegir's attended by elf-kind), the healing-runes 'cut on the elves' beams' (Sigrdrifumal), and the sagas' elf-sacrifice cures (Kormaks saga's mound-offering) document the elf-folk as healers, neighbors and dangerous kin - a people in legal-social relation with humans, not mere spirits. And the tooth-gift's real custom completes the record: the Norse elite's teething-gifts (a boy's first tooth celebrated with a present) are the myth's mirror, the god's country-sized fee magnifying the farm's gift. The record thus anchors the elf-geography family: the bright above and the pitch-dark below, one name-family divided by residence."
    },
