    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "A magic mill named Grotti grinds whatever its operator commands: gold, peace, happiness - anything. Two giant-maidens, Fenja and Menja, are bought from Sweden and set to turn it; they grind gold and peace for the Danish king Frodi so well that he never needs to buy a man's life or justice - until the king overworks them, gives them no rest but the time a song takes, and they grind out an army and their own revenge. The mill is flung into the sea, where it still grinds - the aition of the maelstrom and, with it, of why the sea's salt is endless.",
      zh: "\u795E\u78E8\u683C\u7F85\u8482\u80FD\u78E8\u51FA\u64CD\u4F5C\u8005\u6240\u6C42\u4E4B\u7269\uFF1A\u9EC3\u91D1\u3001\u548C\u5E73\u3001\u5E78\u798F\u2014\u2014\u7121\u6240\u4E0D\u80FD\u3002\u5169\u540D\u5DE8\u4EBA\u65CF\u5C11\u5973\u82AC\u5A6D\u8207\u6885\u5C3C\u5A6D\u8CFC\u81EA\u745E\u5178\uFF0C\u88AB\u8FEB\u8F49\u52D5\u795E\u78E8\uFF1B\u5979\u5011\u70BA\u4E39\u9EA5\u738B\u5F17\u7F85\u8FEA\u78E8\u51FA\u9EC3\u91D1\u8207\u548C\u5E73\uFF0C\u4EE4\u5176\u7121\u9808\u8CB7\u547D\u6216\u8CB7\u516C\u6B63\u2014\u2014\u76F4\u5230\u570B\u738B\u82DB\u4F7F\u5979\u5011\uFF0C\u53EA\u8A31\u5979\u5011\u6B47\u606F\u4E00\u9996\u6B4C\u7684\u5DE5\u592B\uFF0C\u5979\u5011\u4FBF\u78E8\u51FA\u5927\u8ECD\u8207\u81EA\u8EAB\u7684\u5FA9\u4EC7\u3002\u795E\u78E8\u6700\u7D42\u88AB\u62CB\u5165\u6D77\u4E2D\uFF0C\u81F3\u4ECA\u4ECD\u5728\u78E8\u8F49\u2014\u2014\u9019\u662F\u6F29\u6E26\uFF08maelstrom\uFF09\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\uFF0C\u4E5F\u89E3\u91CB\u4E86\u6D77\u6C34\u4F55\u4EE5\u7121\u76E1\u5730\u9E79\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Skaldskaparmal via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "The mill is the Norse myth of prosperity's machinery. Grotti - 'the grinder' - is a quern-stone so heavy no man can turn it; its first wheel-turn was before any land existed, it ground the gravel that became the earth, and it has ground gold ever since. King Frodi of Denmark finds the two giant-maidens Fenja and Menja, daughters of the mountain-giant Hrungnir's kin, buys them as slaves from Sweden, and sets them at the mill; he orders them to grind gold, peace and happiness, and grants them no rest longer than the time a song takes.\n\nThe maidens comply - and compose. Their work-song (the poem Grottasongr itself) is a labor-arithmetic: they have turned the mill through wars and fortunes; they ground for Yngvi's hosts, ground the unsteady gold, ground peace for Frodi; and the king's wealth was such that no man needed to buy another's life or sue another for justice. But the song remembers too what they are: giant-kin, stronger than the strongest, born before the world. Their patience breaks: 'weary are we of the labor' - and they change the order. Now they grind out a host against the king: warships, warriors, a banner - and the sea-king Mysing comes, sacks the hall, burns Frodi's peace, and carries off the mill and the maidens.\n\nOn Mysing's ship he orders one last grinding - salt. The ship is heavy-laden; at midnight the maidens ask whether they are wearying the crew; the sea-king says they have ground long enough; but the mill has ground to the hull's waterline, and down goes the ship - mill, maidens, salt - and where it sank there has been a whirlpool ever since, where the sea runs through the eye of the stone: the maelstrom, whose waters are salt because the grinding never stopped.\n\nThe cultural functions are dense. The myth aitions three institutions at once: peace (it can be ground like gold - and it is perishable, resting on the labor of the unfree), the maelstrom (a whirlpool whose salt explains the sea), and the ethics of forced labor (the mill runs on giant-women bought as slaves, and their song is the exact price-list of the king's prosperity). Snorri embeds it in Skaldskaparmal as a kenning-source - 'Frodi's meal', 'the maidens' sea-mill' - and as a political fable for a thirteenth-century audience that understood grinding gold out of subject labor. In the atlas, Grotti answers the Chinese mulberry-field catastrophes and the Greek water-mill legends: machinery, labor and apocalypse in one mechanism."
    },
