    lat: 61.7,
    lon: 8.5,
    period: "Skirnismal c. 900-1000 CE; Gylfaginning c. 1220-1230; principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2365 4to, GKS 2367 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923) / Project Gutenberg (Brodeur 1916)",
      work: "Skirnismal; Snorri, Gylfaginning ch. 37; critical ed. Dronke (1997)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Seated on Odin's all-seeing throne, Freyr looks into Gymir's garden and falls mortally lovesick for the giant-maiden Gerd. His servant Skirnir rides as envoy, first offering her eleven golden apples and the ring Draupnir, then cursing her: her limbs shall wither, her face be seen only by terror, madness and misery follow her - unless she consents. She consents, nine nights hence, in the grove Barri. But the price is the god's own sword: Freyr has pledged it to Skirnir, and when Ragnarok comes he must fight Surtr bare-handed - and fall. The myth is the Norse aition of the harvest: the fertility god buys the earth's yielding with his own weapon, and pays at the world's end.",
      zh: "\u5F17\u96F7\u5750\u4E0A\u5967\u4E01\u7684\u5168\u77E5\u738B\u5EA7\uFF0C\u671B\u898B\u57FA\u7C73\u723E\u82B1\u5712\uFF0C\u70BA\u5DE8\u4EBA\u65CF\u5C11\u5973\u845B\u5FB7\u5BB3\u4E0A\u81F4\u547D\u7684\u76F8\u601D\u3002\u5176\u50D5\u65AF\u57FA\u5C3C\u723E\u5949\u547D\u70BA\u4F7F\uFF0C\u5148\u4EE5\u5341\u4E00\u679A\u91D1\u860B\u679C\u8207\u6307\u74B0\u5FB7\u7F85\u666E\u5C3C\u723E\u76F8\u8A98\uFF0C\u5F8C\u4EE5\u5492\u8A9E\u76F8\u8105\uFF1A\u82E5\u4E0D\u5BB9\u61C9\uFF0C\u5176\u80A2\u9AD4\u5C07\u67AF\u840E\uFF0C\u5176\u5BB9\u984F\u53EA\u88AB\u6050\u61FC\u6240\u898B\uFF0C\u760B\u72C2\u8207\u60B2\u82E6\u96A8\u884C\u2014\u2014\u845B\u5FB7\u9042\u5141\u65BC\u4E5D\u591C\u4E4B\u5F8C\u5728\u5DF4\u91CC\u6797\u4E2D\u76F8\u6703\u3002\u7136\u800C\u4EE3\u50F9\u662F\u795E\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u5BF6\u528D\uFF1A\u5F17\u96F7\u5DF2\u5C07\u5176\u62B5\u62BC\u7D66\u65AF\u57FA\u5C3C\u723E\uFF0C\u672B\u65E5\u4E4B\u6230\u4F86\u81E8\u6642\u4ED6\u53EA\u80FD\u7A7A\u624B\u8FCE\u6230\u8607\u723E\u7279\u2014\u2014\u4E26\u56E0\u6B64\u6B9E\u547D\u3002\u6B64\u795E\u8A71\u662F\u5317\u6B50\u8C50\u6536\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\uFF1A\u8C50\u9952\u4E4B\u795E\u4EE5\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u5175\u5668\u63DB\u53D6\u5927\u5730\u7684\u994B\u8D08\uFF0C\u4E26\u5728\u4E16\u754C\u7D42\u7D50\u4E4B\u6642\u511F\u4ED8\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Gylfaginning via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "The myth begins with surveillance. Freyr sits one day in Hlidskjalf, Odin's high seat from which all worlds are seen, though no one but its owner should sit there; he looks north to Jotunheim, sees a maiden walking from her father Gymir's hall to the storehouse, and the sight does its work: his hand grows slack, his mind sick, and no one can speak with him. Njord sends Skirnir, Freyr's servant, to ask the matter; Freyr confesses, and asks the impossible: to woo the girl for him, over her father's will and her own.\n\nSkirnir names his fee and takes the gear: Freyr's sword, the one that fights of itself. Then the embassy: past the hounds of Gymir's fence, to the garden where Gerd sits. First the gold: eleven apples of the goddess Iunn, the ring Draupnir that drops eight rings of its own kind every ninth night - she refuses: 'Gymir's gold I need not, nor the ring'. Then threats, in escalating verse: Skirnir draws (with what? the text withholds) a threatening shape and curses her: she shall sit lonely on the eagle's cliff, look toward the worlds, and find food more loathsome to her than to any man; she shall be seen of all as a terror; madness, misery and the restless urge shall follow her; her limbs shall grow; her face shall strike dread; with a three-leaved thurst-staff the giant Hrimgrimnir shall have her below the corpses' gate - and the wretched maid shall always serve, 'ever beneath the grips of men'. The curses land: Gerd bids him welcome - the ninth night, in the grove Barri, she will grant the god her love.\n\nBut the poem has one more stanza of freight: when Skirnir asks his wages, the exchange is stark - 'the sword I gave you is your fee; the maiden you won for me; and I know it well, that my fall draws near'. Gylfaginning confirms: because Freyr gave the sword away, he had none when he met Surtr at Ragnarok, and he slew Beli with a horn's point - and the Prose account adds the economics: had he kept the sword, the gods would have been served by it at Ragnarok. The sword bought the union of god and earth-giantess; the price is deferred to doomsday.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the harvest-doctrine and the marriage-ethics of the north. Skirnismal is the aition of the fields' fruitfulness: the union of the god (rain, sunshine) with Gerd (the fenced garden, her name means 'enclosure') is agriculture itself - a marriage of cultivation and enclosure. And it is a courtship-law myth: consent extracted by escalating threats, negotiated by an envoy who is paid in weapons - the poem's own editors debate whether the curses are ritual magic (a warlock's wooing) or a legal parable of forced marriage. For the atlas, the pledged sword is the pattern of the culture-gift bought with doom - the myth answers Prometheus' fire-theft and Freyr's own corn-splendor with a mortgage: the fertility god's armament is pawned to buy the harvest, and the interest comes due at the end of the world."
    },
