    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Freyr sat in Hlidskjalf, Odin's high seat, and looked into all worlds; northward he saw a fair maiden walking from her father Gymir's hall, and at that sight his hand fell and his mind grew sick. No man might speak with him, until Skirnir his servant asked and was told the cause.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Skirnismal st. 1-7 (Bellows 1923); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Skirnir offered the maiden eleven golden apples and the ring Draupnir, which drops eight rings of itself every ninth night; she refused them. Then he cursed her: lonely on the eagle's cliff she should sit, her limbs wither, her face be seen only as terror, madness and misery follow her, and a giant have her below the corpses' gate - and at that she consented: the ninth night hence, in the grove Barri, she would grant the god her love.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Skirnismal st. 16-36 (Bellows 1923); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "When Skirnir asked his fee, Freyr answered that he had given him the sword: 'you won the maiden; and I know it well, that my fall draws near'. For the sword was pledged, and when Ragnarok came Freyr had none, and met Surtr, and fell.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Skirnismal st. 40 and Gylfaginning ch. 37 (Bellows; Brodeur); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
