    lat: 64.26,
    lon: -21.13,
    period: "Voluspa st. 40-41, 57 c. 900-1000 CE; Gylfaginning c. 1220-1230; Vafthrudnismal st. 44; principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2365 4to, GKS 2367 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923) / Project Gutenberg (Brodeur 1916)",
      work: "Voluspa st. 40-41; Vafthrudnismal st. 44; Snorri, Gylfaginning ch. 51; critical ed. Dronke (1997)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Ragnarok begins not with a battle but with a sky. In the east a giantess rears the wolf-kin of the old giant; one of them, Managarm, feeds on the flesh of the dead and the moon; the sun's wolf Snorride? - Skoll - swallows the sun, Hati takes the moon, and the stars vanish from heaven. Then comes the Fimbulwinter: three winters together, no summer between, snow from all quarters, no warmth in the sun - and after those, three more. Brother will fight brother; the wind carries wolf-howls; the world is a spear-age, a sword-age, an axe-age, a wind-age, a wolf-age, before the world goes down. The record is the Norse doctrine that civilizations end as climates: apocalypse arrives as weather, and the moral collapse precedes the physical one.",
      zh: "\u8AF8\u795E\u9EC3\u660F\u7684\u958B\u7AEF\u4E26\u975E\u6230\u9663\uFF0C\u800C\u662F\u5929\u7A79\u3002\u6771\u65B9\u6709\u5973\u5DE8\u4EBA\u5728\u8001\u5DE8\u4EBA\u7684\u72FC\u65CF\u4E2D\u990A\u5B50\uFF1B\u5176\u4E2D\u540D\u70BA\u746A\u7D0D\u52A0\u723E\u59C6\u8005\uFF0C\u4EE5\u6B7B\u8005\u4E4B\u8089\u70BA\u98DF\uFF0C\u4E26\u5C07\u541E\u6708\uFF1B\u541E\u65E5\u4E4B\u72FC\u65AF\u79D1\u723E\u5C07\u541E\u4E0B\u592A\u967D\uFF0C\u54C8\u8482\u596A\u6708\uFF0C\u661F\u8FB0\u81EA\u5929\u7A79\u96B1\u6C92\u3002\u96A8\u5F8C\u800C\u4F86\u7684\u662F\u82AC\u5E03\u723E\u4E4B\u51AC\uFF1A\u4E09\u51AC\u76F8\u9023\uFF0C\u590F\u4E0D\u81F3\uFF0C\u56DB\u65B9\u964D\u96EA\uFF0C\u65E5\u5149\u7121\u6EAB\u2014\u2014\u6B64\u5F8C\u9084\u6709\u4E09\u51AC\u3002\u5144\u5F1F\u5C07\u76F8\u4E92\u6B98\u6BBA\uFF1B\u98A8\u4E2D\u6EFF\u662F\u72FC\u568E\uFF1B\u4E16\u754C\u6210\u70BA\u77DB\u4E4B\u7D00\u3001\u528D\u4E4B\u7D00\u3001\u65A7\u4E4B\u7D00\u3001\u98A8\u4E4B\u7D00\u3001\u72FC\u4E4B\u7D00\u2014\u2014\u7136\u5F8C\u4E16\u754C\u50BE\u8986\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5317\u6B50\u7684\u6559\u7FA9\uFF1A\u6587\u660E\u4EE5\u6C23\u5019\u7D42\u7D50\uFF0C\u672B\u65E5\u4EE5\u5929\u6C23\u964D\u81E8\uFF0C\u800C\u9053\u5FB7\u7684\u5D29\u6F70\u5148\u65BC\u7269\u7406\u7684\u5D29\u6F70\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Gylfaginning via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "V\xF6lusp\xE1's doom-sequence opens in the east, in the iron-wood (J\xE1rnvi\xF0r), where a giantess rears Fenrir's kin; among them is 'Managarm, the moon's feeder', who fills himself with the life-blood of doomed men and reddens with the blood of the gods - 'sun will be darkened, winds will howl murderously over the sky'. The parallel prose in Gylfaginning names the two wolves: Skoll ('treachery') who shall swallow the sun, and Hati ('hate'), the wolf that goes before the moon - and when both have taken their quarry, the stars go out.\n\nThen the weather. Vafthrudnismal's prophecy gives the clearest statement: 'first comes the winter called Fimbulwinter: three winters together, no summer between; before that there have been three other winters, and then the world will be a war-age, brothers will fight brothers, and no one will spare another.' Gylfaginning adds the meteorology: snow drifts from all quarters, and the sun gives no heat. Human order collapses in the same stanzas - kinship-slaying, perjury, the axe-age and sword-age litany of V\xF6lusp\xE1 st. 45: 'brothers will fight brothers and slay each other; sisters' sons will break kinship-bonds; hard it is in the world, great whoredom, an axe-age, a sword-age, shields are cleft, a wind-age, a wolf-age, until the world falls.'\n\nThe cultural functions are the doctrine of climate-collapse and the moral sequencer. In a Norse environment where three bad winters could kill a colony (Iceland's and Greenland's actual famine cycles), the myth formalizes the observation: the end is not a monster first but a sky first - the food-system fails before the armies march. And the sequence is deliberate: the wolves take the lights (cosmological failure), the winter comes (climatological failure), kinship breaks (social failure), and only then do the monsters and the fire ride (military failure). The myth asserts that doom is legible in advance in exactly this order - a doctrine of prophecy by weather-station.\n\nFor the atlas the record is the eschatology family's hinge record: it documents a European tradition where the apocalypse is a climate event with a moral prologue, comparable to the Chinese catastrophes of the Five Ages and the Maya's collapsing world-ages - but here with named wolves, a nameable winter, and a schedule.\n\nThe wolves' genealogy completes the doctrine: Skoll and Hati are Fenrir's kin (reared in the iron-wood by the giantess), so the sun-eaters are the bound wolf's family - the gods' management of the monster-brood failing at the schedule's end. And the climate-doctrine's realism completes the record: three rainless, sunless winters is precisely the northern famine-experience (Iceland's and Greenland's colony-mortality cycles), so the myth formalizes observation as eschatology - the apocalypse arrived as weather-report before it arrived as battle. The record thus anchors the climate-collapse family: doom's order (sky, weather, kinship, army) legible in advance, the north's famine-memory written as prophecy."
    },
