    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Gylfaginning via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "Voluspa's doom-litany assigns the sea-approach: 'from the east steers Hrym with shields high; Jormungand writhes in giant-fury and beats the waves; the eagle screams, slays corpses with his beak; Naglfar is loosed. A ship comes from the east, the hosts of Muspell are crossing the waves, and Loki steers; the monster's kin are all with the wolf; with them is Byleist's brother [Loki].' Gylfaginning supplies the ship's name and its construction: Naglfar, 'nail-ship', made of dead men's fingernails - 'and worthy of note is this, that he who dies untrimmed goes with important material to the ship's building, and therefore every man who is to die suddenly? should have uncut nails?' - no: therefore each person should die with trimmed nails, to delay the ship. Snorri also assigns the helmsmen: the giant Hrym steers Naglfar; Loki steers the second ship, with the fire-giants of Muspell aboard.\n\nThe myth's first function is body-discipline as eschatology. Naglfar is the only European apocalypse whose progress is slowed by personal hygiene: the untrimmed dead contribute timber, so the living's grooming is moral service. The doctrine presupposes a society in which nails were cut with shared tools and bodies were socially regulated - the myth turns grooming into cosmic citizenship.\n\nThe second function is Loki's release. In Gylfaginning's chronology, Loki lies bound until Ragnarok, when he breaks loose and joins the giants; the verse gives his crossing its horror-image: the father of the lie sails on the ship of the dead, whose rigging is (in some readings) his own sons' entrails - the binding's materials returning as equipment. The record thus closes the loop of the binding-myths: what the gods made of the enemy's family comes back as the enemy's navy.\n\nFor the atlas, Naglfar anchors the doom-fleet motif: the image of the end arriving by sea - comparable to the Turkish-Mongol flood-myths and the Chinese ghosts' ghost-ships - here with a construction doctrine: the enemy's vessel is built, plank by plank, out of the carelessness of the living.\n\nThe nail-doctrine's social logic completes the record: in a society where nails were cut with shared tools and the dead's bodies were community property, the myth turns grooming into cosmic citizenship - the untrimmed dead contribute timber to the enemy's navy, so the living's care delays the doom. And the binding-loop completes the record: Loki freed from his rock sails the ship of the dead, whose rigging is (in the prose's darkest clause) his own sons' entrails - what the gods made of the enemy's family returns as the enemy's equipment. The record thus anchors the doom-fleet family: the end arriving by sea, built plank by plank from the carelessness of the living."
    },
