    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Hel is a country with a compass: her realm lies northward and downward, nine nights' ride from the living, past the echoing river Gjoll, over the bridge Gjallarbru that rings under a living rider, through the iron-fenced gate Helgrind, into the hall Eljudnir ('damp-with-sleet'), where platters are 'hunger', the knife is 'famine', and the fallen stand. The dead of sickness and age go there; the battle-slain go to Odin. The record is the Norse geography of ordinary death - unmajestic, domestic, and strictly zoned.",
      zh: "\u6D77\u723E\u4E4B\u5883\u81EA\u6709\u65B9\u4F4D\uFF1A\u5176\u570B\u5728\u5317\u800C\u5411\u4E0B\uFF0C\u8DDD\u751F\u8005\u4E5D\u591C\u4E4B\u7A0B\uFF0C\u8D8A\u904E\u8FF4\u8072\u4E4B\u6CB3\u5409\u5967\u723E\uFF0C\u8E0F\u904E\u5409\u4E9E\u62C9\u723E\u6A4B\u2014\u2014\u6D3B\u4EBA\u904E\u6A4B\u6642\u6A4B\u8EAB\u8F5F\u97FF\u2014\u2014\u7A7F\u904E\u9435\u67F5\u4E4B\u9580\u6D77\u723E\u683C\u6797\u5FB7\uFF0C\u9032\u5165\u5927\u5EF3\u57C3\u723E\u5C24\u5FB7\u5C3C\u723E\uFF08\u300C\u843D\u96F9\u6FD5\u51B7\u300D\uFF09\uFF0C\u5EF3\u4E2D\u7897\u76E4\u540D\u70BA\u300C\u98E2\u300D\uFF0C\u9910\u5200\u540D\u70BA\u300C\u5331\u300D\uFF0C\u5E8A\u4F4D\u540D\u70BA\u300C\u75C5\u300D\u3002\u75C5\u8001\u800C\u6B7B\u8005\u5165\u6B64\u570B\uFF1B\u6230\u6B7B\u8005\u5247\u6B78\u5967\u4E01\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5317\u6B50\u5C0D\u300C\u5C0B\u5E38\u4E4B\u6B7B\u300D\u7684\u5730\u7406\u63CF\u8FF0\u2014\u2014\u7121\u69AE\u8000\u3001\u5C45\u5BB6\u6C23\u606F\u3001\u4E14\u5206\u5340\u56B4\u660E\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Gylfaginning assigns the realm its place and its people. When Odin threw Loki's daughter Hel down into Niflheim, he gave her authority over nine worlds, 'that she should apportion all abodes to those that were sent to her': this is the underworld of the unchosen dead - those who die of sickness or old age, as against the battle-slain whom the valkyries take to Odin. Her hall is Eljudnir, 'sprinkled with rain'; her dishes 'Hunger', her knife 'Famine', her thralls 'Sloth' and 'Sourness', her thresholds 'Pitfall', her bed 'Sickness', her curtains 'Misfortune' - a hall named for the deficits of a starving household. She is half flesh-colored and half blue-black, 'easily recognized, and more downcast and fierce-looking than the others'.\n\nThe geography is mapped in the Hermothr episode. The rider comes to the river Gjoll, the bridge Gjallarbru across it, 'roofed with shining gold', and meets the maiden Modgudr, who keeps it: five companies of dead men rode over, but it rang less under them; nothing is darker than under the dead man's eyebrows; the rider shakes the bridge and resounds like gold, and has not the color of dead men - his 'breast heaves with more hot air than a dead man's breath'. The bridge-keeper's questions are a customs post between the living and the dead; only Sleipnir's eight legs and a god's heat could cross ringing. Beyond lies the iron gate Helgrind ('the gates of Hel'), through which even Hermothr must creep sidewise past the hound Garmr.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are a taxonomy of deaths and a domestication of the grave. Norse society sorted its dead by manner of dying: the field of battle to Valhalla or Freyja, the sea to Ran's net, the bed to Hel - and this record maps the last, ordinary, majority death, without glory and without punishment, in a hall that is simply poverty and sickness continued. That absence of judgment is the record's sharpest comparative datum: Hel is not a hell; she is a large, gray household. (V\xF6lusp\xE1's N\xE1str\xF6nd, with its serpent and corpse-shore, is the separate doctrine for oath-breakers and murderers - a different record here.)\n\nIn the atlas's cosmology family, Hel's realm answers the Greek Hades and the Mesopotamian House of Dust - each an unmajestic underworld for the ordinary dead - but the Norse one adds a bridge-keeper, a customs-post, and a ring underfoot: the afterlife with the acoustics of frontier law."
    },
