    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "V\xF6lusp\xE1's eschatology has one hall set apart: a hall stands far from the sun, its doors facing north, dripping venom, on Nastrand, the shore of corpses - through it flows a river of knives. Oath-breakers, murderers and those who seduce others' wives wade there; but worse is Nidhoggr: the dragon sucks the bodies of the dead, and at the end of the vision it flies from Nastrand, the corpses in its feathers. The record is the Norse doctrine of posthumous punishment - the only zone of moral judgment in a death-system otherwise without hell.",
      zh: "\u5728\u300A\u5973\u5148\u77E5\u7684\u9810\u8A00\u300B\u7684\u672B\u4E16\u5716\u666F\u4E2D\uFF0C\u6709\u4E00\u5EA7\u5927\u5EF3\u88AB\u55AE\u7368\u5283\u51FA\uFF1A\u90A3\u5EF3\u9060\u96E2\u592A\u967D\uFF0C\u9580\u671D\u5317\u65B9\uFF0C\u6EF4\u843D\u6BD2\u6DB2\uFF0C\u7ACB\u65BC\u7D0D\u65AF\u7279\u9686\u5FB7\u2014\u2014\u5C4D\u9AB8\u4E4B\u6FF1\u2014\u2014\u5EF3\u4E2D\u6D41\u904E\u4E00\u689D\u4EE5\u5200\u5203\u532F\u6210\u7684\u6CB3\u3002\u80CC\u8A93\u8005\u3001\u6BBA\u4EBA\u8005\u8207\u8A98\u60D1\u4ED6\u4EBA\u59BB\u5B50\u8005\u5728\u6B64\u6D89\u6C34\u800C\u884C\uFF1B\u800C\u66F4\u70BA\u53EF\u6015\u7684\u662F\u5C3C\u5FB7\u970D\u683C\uFF1A\u90A3\u689D\u9F8D\u5438\u98DF\u6B7B\u8005\u7684\u5C4D\u9AD4\uFF0C\u4E26\u5728\u5E7B\u8C61\u7D42\u7D50\u4E4B\u6642\u81EA\u7D0D\u65AF\u7279\u9686\u5FB7\u98DB\u51FA\uFF0C\u7FBD\u7FFC\u9593\u593E\u5E36\u5C4D\u9AB8\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5317\u6B50\u95DC\u65BC\u6B7B\u5F8C\u61F2\u7F70\u7684\u6559\u7FA9\u2014\u2014\u5728\u539F\u672C\u7121\u300C\u5730\u7344\u300D\u53EF\u8A00\u7684\u6B7B\u4EA1\u9AD4\u7CFB\u4E2D\uFF0C\u9019\u662F\u552F\u4E00\u5E36\u6709\u9053\u5FB7\u5BE9\u5224\u8272\u5F69\u7684\u5340\u57DF\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Voluspa's underworld geography distinguishes two zones. The first is Hel's ordinary realm - the hall eastward in the cold realms, the doors northward, venom dripping from the roof: 'that hall is woven of serpents' spines'. The second is the punishment-hall: on Nastrand, the corpse-strand, stands a great hall 'far from the sun', its doors facing north; venom-drops fall through its smoke-hole; and through its doorway runs a river, Slidr, made of knives. Oath-breakers, murderers and seducers of others' wives wade through it 'each and every day', and Nidhoggr - the corpse-gnawing dragon of Yggdrasil's root - 'sucks the corpses of the dead'.\n\nThe record's cultural function is to install a moral perimeter inside an otherwise judgment-free death system. Norse eschatology is famously unmoralistic: ordinary death goes to Hel's gray household, battle-death to Valhalla, sea-death to Ran - none of it graded by virtue. The only transgressions that earn posthumous fire are legal ones - oath-breaking, killing, and the destruction of marriage-bonds - the three crimes of a society built on contract and kinship. The myth thereby encodes the point that morality in the Norse system is contract-morality: heaven and hell are divided not by faith but by whether you kept your word.\n\nThe dragon itself is a doctrine of duration. Nidhoggr appears twice in the sibyl's vision: at the root of the world-tree (the cosmological gnawing, Grimnismal) and here on the corpse-shore (the moral gnawing); at the vision's end it flies 'from Nastrand, corpses in its feathers' - the final image of the poem before the sibyl sinks away, a dragon flying off with its harvest even after the world's burning. The persistence is the point: in the renewal stanza where Baldr returns and the fields grow, the dragon is allowed no place - it flies away rather than perishes. The cosmos can be made green again; the record of crimes cannot.\n\nIn the atlas's afterlife family, Nastrand answers the Greek Tartarus and the Chinese underworld courts: each a punishment-zone inside a larger death-real; the Norse one is distinctive for naming its crimes in legal vocabulary (perjury, homicide, adultery) rather than sins of belief, and for its river made of knives - the punishment mirroring the weapon-crime.\n\nThe dragon's double appearance completes the doctrine: at Yggdrasil's root (the cosmological gnawing, Grimnismal's limb) and on the corpse-shore (the moral gnawing, Voluspa's punishment-limb) - one beast serving both the world-tree's metabolism and the moral order's enforcement. And the final image completes the eschatology: at the vision's end the dragon flies from Nastrand with corpses in its feathers - the only being that leaves the renewed world without being renewed - so the record's closure is the doctrine: the cosmos can be made green again, but the record of crimes cannot be erased. The record thus anchors the punishment-zone family: the sole hell in a judgment-free death-system, its crimes named in legal vocabulary and its executioner a dragon."
    },
