    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The most important mythic document of the north is staged as an interrogation: a vala - a dead or trance-bound prophetess - is summoned from her grave to recite before 'the sons of Heimdall' the whole history of the world, from the void to Ragnarok to the green renewal. She asks for silence and spells; she names herself one whom men hallowed when Heimdall's kind yet lived; she demands to know why she is compelled, and agrees to recite 'till they bid me carry my keg of mead, till I die'. The poem is a complete cosmology and eschatology delivered by an unwilling professional woman - the genre-selfportrait of Norse myth.",
      zh: "\u5317\u65B9\u6700\u91CD\u8981\u7684\u795E\u8A71\u6587\u737B\u4EE5\u8CEA\u8A62\u7684\u5F62\u5F0F\u5448\u73FE\uFF1A\u4E00\u4F4D\u5973\u5148\u77E5\u2014\u2014\u4EA1\u6545\u6216\u5165\u8FF7\u7684\u9810\u8A00\u8005\u2014\u2014\u88AB\u81EA\u5893\u4E2D\u559A\u8D77\uFF0C\u5411\u300C\u6D77\u59C6\u9054\u723E\u7684\u5B50\u5B6B\u300D\u8FF0\u8AAA\u4E16\u754C\u81EA\u9D3B\u8499\u81F3\u8AF8\u795E\u9EC3\u660F\u3001\u518D\u5230\u5927\u5730\u56DE\u9752\u7684\u5168\u90E8\u6B77\u53F2\u3002\u5979\u8981\u6C42\u5B89\u975C\u8207\u5492\u8A9E\uFF1B\u81EA\u7A31\u66FE\u53D7\u4EBA\u795D\u8056\uFF0C\u5F7C\u6642\u6D77\u59C6\u9054\u723E\u7684\u65CF\u985E\u5C1A\u5728\uFF1B\u5979\u8CEA\u554F\u81EA\u5DF1\u70BA\u4F55\u88AB\u8FEB\u958B\u53E3\uFF0C\u53C8\u7B54\u61C9\u8FF0\u8AAA\uFF0C\u300C\u76F4\u81F3\u4ED6\u5011\u547D\u6211\u7AEF\u8D77\u871C\u9152\u6876\uFF0C\u76F4\u81F3\u6211\u6B7B\u53BB\u300D\u3002\u9019\u9996\u8A69\u7531\u4E00\u4F4D\u4E0D\u60C5\u9858\u7684\u8077\u696D\u5973\u6027\u50B3\u8FF0\uFF0C\u662F\u5317\u6B50\u795E\u8A71\u7684\u5B8C\u6574\u5B87\u5B99\u8AD6\u8207\u672B\u4E16\u8AD6\u2014\u2014\u4E5F\u662F\u9019\u4E00\u6587\u985E\u7684\u81EA\u756B\u50CF\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Voluspa - 'the sibyl's prophecy' - opens with the sibyl demanding the audience's attention and stating her credentials: attention I ask from the sacred races, great and small, the sons of Heimdall; father of the sword-slain? - 'you, Valfather, willed that I should tell the oldest tales of men, those I remember'. She recites the cosmogony (the void, Ymir, the sun and moon set on courses, the first humans of Ask and Embla), the golden age, the first war, the deaths of Baldr, the family of Loki, the doom-litany, the battles of Ragnarok, the renewal - sixty-six stanzas, the world's whole biography.\n\nBut the performance is contractual, and the sibyl's voice is its most important datum. She is not a spontaneous oracle: she asks for silence and spells ('be silent all, ye holy races... I tell of the giant-sibyl of old'), and there is a missing stanza - the Hauksbok preserves st. 28's ritual frame of 'sitting out' (utiseta), in which a practitioner performs necromantic spellwork; the prose of later manuscripts says Odin summoned her with magic from her grave. Her resistance is explicit: in the Hauksbok version she demands, 'would you have me tell further, of the doom of the gods?'; she delays ('I know how many nights have passed'), and Baldrs draumar shows the same ritual economy at the grave: the waking costs spells, and the oracle resents being compelled. The profession is real: staff-bearing traveling seeresses performed exactly such recitations at farmsteads (Eirik's saga's Greenland seance), and the poem's stage directions are the liturgy of that profession.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are threefold. First, it is the corpus's frame-story: the myth database's oldest complete document is itself a myth about myth-telling - prophecy as a paid, compelled, gendered performance. Second, it is the doctrinal master-text: the creation, the Norns, the first war, Baldr's death, the Fimbulwinter, the battles, the renewal - the skeleton on which Snorri built Gylfaginning. Third, it is the document of the myth's own death: the sibyl is dead, or lives by spells; she speaks 'the oldest tales'; the last lines have her sinking away - 'now she sinks' - and the poem ends with the dragon flying off and the formula 'she saw and she saw' fading into vision. In a Christian Iceland that kept the old verses, the sibyl's withdrawal is the tradition's own image of its transmission: performed once more, from a grave, for a listening elite.\n\nFor the atlas, the record anchors the prophetic-vision genre across the whole collection: the Maya priests' books, the Chinese oracle-bones' divisions, the Zoroastrian apocalypse of Bahman Yasht - each tradition's end-time text; the Norse one is delivered by an unwilling woman who invoices her audience in mead."
    },
