    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Gylfaginning via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "The renewal is staged as an inventory of what comes back. V\xF6lusp\xE1's sequence: 'the sun turns black, the earth sinks in the sea, the hot stars fall from the sky, and the fire leaps high about the citadel of the gods' - and then the next stanza turns the page: 'but there will yet arise a green earth from the sea, and waterfalls fall, and the eagle flies that hunts fish on the mountain'. The world's renewal is told in ecological shorthand: green ground, running water, a working food-chain. Vafthrudnismal confirms the detail through Odin's exam: 'unsown fields will bear fruit; all sickness will heal; Baldr will come; H\xF6d and Baldr both settle in Hropt's victory-halls, the gods' dwellings - do you know yet?' - the harvest and the healed body, the returned god and the killer reconciled, in the same breath.\n\nThe institutions refound themselves. The surviving gods meet on Idavoll, 'the plain where the gods met before', and there they find in the grass the golden game-pieces the older gods played with - the golden age's board-game recovered by literal rediscovery, the restoration thesis of the whole cycle: institutions can be lost and refound with the selfsame objects. Snorri completes the roster: Vidar and Vali live, neither sea nor Surtr's fire has harmed them; Modi and Magni, Thor's sons, have Mjollnir; Hoenir works prophecy and chooses lots; and 'the sons of Baldr lived and were busy? ' - Baldr and H\xF6d return from Hel to sit in Odin's old seat.\n\nHumanity too is renewed: Lif ('life') and Lifthrasir ('striving-for-life') hide in Hoddmimir's wood, feed on the morning dew, and from them come so many generations that the world is peopled - the remnant-pair doctrine, the same grammar as Bergelmir's raft at creation: every world-epoch begins with one couple saved by geography. And the architecture of the new age is specified: a hall more beautiful than the sun stands at Gimle, roofed with red gold, where righteous people shall live and enjoy pleasure forever; also Brimir's hall at Okolnir, full of good drink; and Nidafjoll, where the dragon Nidhoggr is - and the last lines of the poem fly the dragon from the corpse-shore with corpses in its feathers: the renewal admits no criminals, but neither does it erase the record of crime.\n\nThe cultural function is the Norse doctrine of survivable endings. Where most mythologies make the end absolute (the deluge, the flood, the apocalyptic furnace), the Norse eschatology is scheduled, fought, and survived: the gods can lose and the world can go on, because the end is a regime change, not a deletion. Snorri's Gylfaginning closes its catechism with exactly this - the young king's lesson is that the pagan world knew its own mortality and planned beyond it. And the genre's politics are visible: the stanzas are the victory-catalog of what a thirteenth-century Icelander thought irreplaceable - law-courts refound, games recovered, kinship reconciled, harvest, healing, and a hall better than the sun for the righteous.\n\nFor the atlas, the record anchors the world-renewal motif family (A-code world renewal, resurrection): the Chinese mulberry-catastrophes' survivors, the Maya's new world-ages, the Zoroastrian renovation at Frashegird - each an after-the-end inventory; the Norse one is unique in making the inventory institutional (game-pieces, courts, halls) and in ending with an unpunished dragon."
    },
