    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Gylfaginning - 'the tricking of Gylfi' - is the most consequential frame-story in Norse literature, because it is the container of the entire mythic textbook. The prose begins in Snorri's prologue: the Aesir were human chiefs out of Asia (Tyrkland) who migrated north, bringing crafts and cult; their wisdom was mistaken for divinity. Then the frame: King Gylfi, ruler of 'the land that is now called Sweden', once granted a wandering woman as much land as four oxen could plough in a day and a night - and she turned out to be the goddess Gefjon, whose four ox-sons ploughed Zealand out of his kingdom. Angry, or curious, Gylfi determined to seek the Aesir and learn whether their power was of their own nature or of craft. Under the assumed name Gangleri ('the weary traveler'), he sets out for Asgard.\n\nThe catechism is the Edda's body. In a great hall Gylfi meets three enthroned figures - the highest named H\xE1r (High), the middle Jafnh\xE1r (Equal-high), the third Thridi (Third) - and asks, in the manner of a catechumen: 'who is the highest and most ancient of all gods? what was the beginning? how was the earth made? what of the sun and moon? the Norns? the gods' dwellings? the deaths of Baldr, of Thor, of Odin? what comes after?' - and each question is answered from the poems: Gylfaginning is a prose exegesis that quotes Voluspa, Vafthrudnismal, Grimnismal as scripture. The three answers are marked by the refrain 'so it is said' - and the whole is delivered as something told, not believed.\n\nThe frame's punchline is the trick's exposure. When the teaching is done and Gylfi has heard of the renewal after Ragnarok, he looks around - and sees no hall and no lords: the place is open country. 'Gangleri saw around him a great plain, and no hall and no stronghold; he went his way and came to his kingdom, and told what he had seen and heard.' The three teachers were the three Aesir (Odin among them?) showing a king 'an illusion of the great power that they had'. Snorri's stated purpose, in the prologue to Skaldskaparmal, is that these stories are preserved 'for the needs of poetry' - to teach the kenning-system to young skalds - so the illusion-frame is also a quarantine: the myths may be used as language, not as doctrine.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the learned-Christian quarantine and the pedagogy of deception. Iceland converted in 1000 CE but kept its verse; Snorri, writing two centuries later under a church that burned heretics elsewhere in Europe, needed a container that let him explain Thor's fishing and Odin's runes without endorsing them - the answer was a frame that defines the myths as illusion, quotes them as sources, and licenses them as craft. The result is the single most important document of the myth corpus: without Gylfaginning's catechism, most of the Edda's mythology would exist only in fragmentary verse; with it, the myths survive in narrative form - preserved by a Christian's irony, which is itself a critical fact for the digital historian.\n\nFor the atlas, the record anchors the frame-trick family: the Chinese 'Yellow Emperor's dialogues', the Persian Bundahishn's schoolroom of Ohrmazd, the Christian catechism itself - each a container-story; Snorri's is distinctive in making the container dissolve at the end, leaving the reader with the myths and no teacher."
    },
    functions: [
      "Quarantine-frame: pagan myth presented as learned illusion, licensed only as poetic craft",
      "Pedagogy by catechism: creation to Ragnarok answered by three enthroned teachers",
      "Prose exegesis quoting eddic verse as scripture - the myths' survival strategy",
      "Dissolving container: the teacher vanishes, leaving the reader with the stories"
    ],
