    license: "Public domain (CPB 1883); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Composed for the death of Eirik Bloodaxe (c. 954), this skaldic poem is the earliest surviving vision of Valhalla from inside: the gods wake in Valhalla asking why the day is darkened and what the din of arms means; Bragi answers that Odin has bidden Eirik with his host to come up. Sigmund rises to bid the king welcome and asks the poem's great joke: why should Odin take Eirik, a king who might have lived, when the gods could have taken famous kings grown old? Odin answers: 'Grey-haired are the gods... the wolf howls at the gates' - and the einherjar brew ale for the king's coming. The record documents the hall as a working, political institution, and a court-poet's craft of consoling a dynasty by drafting its dead king into Odin's army.",
      zh: "\u6B64\u541F\u904A\u8A69\u70BA\u57C3\u91CC\u514B\xB7\u8840\u65A7\u4E4B\u6B7B\uFF08\u7D04954\u5E74\uFF09\u800C\u4F5C\uFF0C\u662F\u73FE\u5B58\u6700\u65E9\u7684\u300C\u81EA\u5167\u90E8\u300D\u6240\u898B\u74E6\u723E\u54C8\u62C9\u5E7B\u8C61\uFF1A\u74E6\u723E\u54C8\u62C9\u4E2D\u7684\u8AF8\u795E\u9192\u4F86\uFF0C\u554F\u767D\u65E5\u70BA\u4F55\u660F\u6697\u3001\u5175\u5668\u4E4B\u8072\u70BA\u4F55\u5608\u96DC\uFF1B\u5E03\u62C9\u57FA\u7B54\u9053\uFF0C\u5967\u4E01\u5DF2\u53EC\u57C3\u91CC\u514B\u53CA\u5176\u90E8\u5C6C\u524D\u4F86\u3002\u897F\u683C\u8499\u5FB7\u8D77\u8EAB\u8FCE\u63A5\u570B\u738B\uFF0C\u4E26\u554F\u51FA\u8A69\u4E2D\u8457\u540D\u7684\u8ABF\u4F83\uFF1A\u795E\u738B\u4F55\u4EE5\u8981\u5E36\u8D70\u672C\u53EF\u9577\u58FD\u7684\u57C3\u91CC\u514B\uFF0C\u800C\u4E0D\u5E36\u8D70\u90A3\u4E9B\u5DF2\u529F\u6210\u540D\u5C31\u7684\u5E74\u8001\u541B\u738B\uFF1F\u5967\u4E01\u7B54\uFF1A\u300C\u8AF8\u795E\u696D\u5DF2\u84BC\u8001\u2026\u2026\u72FC\u5728\u9580\u5916\u55E5\u53EB\u300D\u2014\u2014\u6069\u8D6B\u91CC\u4E9E\u70BA\u738B\u4E4B\u4F86\u81E8\u91C0\u9152\u5099\u5BB4\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u5C07\u74E6\u723E\u54C8\u62C9\u63CF\u7E6A\u70BA\u4E00\u500B\u904B\u4F5C\u4E2D\u3001\u5177\u653F\u6CBB\u6027\u7684\u6A5F\u69CB\uFF0C\u4EA6\u5C55\u793A\u5BAE\u5EF7\u8A69\u4EBA\u5982\u4F55\u900F\u904E\u5C07\u4EA1\u738B\u7DE8\u5165\u5967\u4E01\u8ECD\u5718\u4F86\u64AB\u6170\u738B\u65CF\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse (skaldic verse)",
    canonical_translation: "Corpus Poeticum Boreale, ed. Eirikr Magnusson and William Morris, Oxford 1883 (public domain); quoted in Fagrskinna",
    narrative: {
      en: "Eiriksmal ('the lay of Eirik') is the earliest skaldic poem that opens the door of Valhalla and looks in. Its dramatic frame: the gods wake from sleep and ask 'what nightmare is this? why is the day darkened and the earth trembling? what din is this, as if hosts were coming?' - Bragi answers that the din is the coming of the dead king: 'the All-father has bidden Eirik, the sea-king, to come up with his host to Odin's halls.' Sigmund, leader of the chosen, rises to greet him: 'why hast thou taken Eirik from the lands rather than other kings, that thy hand should make the battle-beaten king come into Odin's hall?' \n\nOdin's answer is the poem's doctrine compressed: 'Grey-haired are the gods, and grown old? - the einherjar are few? ' - no; the verse reads: 'Grey-haired are the gods and few the chosen; the wolf howls at the gates; grey-headed they gather for the wolf's attack; I have gotten me Eirik, whom I had rather? ' - the point is demographic: Valhalla needs recruits against Ragnarok, and the All-father drafts the best of the battle-slain by preference. Then the hall prepares the feast: 'Ale for Eirik is brewed in Valhalla; the vessel with the rim? is rinsed; the warriors chosen; the shield-wood (targets) laid; we await Eirik.' The poem ends with the welcome at the doors.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are threefold. First, it documents the myth-as-institution: the hall has a census, a recruitment policy, kitchen preparations and a door-protocol - the afterlife as a functioning barracks, not a metaphor. Second, it is court politics in mythic dress: the poem was composed for Eirik's dynasty (his widow Gunnhildr's court), and its joke - Sigmund asking why the gods take a useful king - is the consolation-argument of a court poet to a royal widow: your husband was too valuable to die of disease; the gods wanted him. Third, it is documentary evidence for the Norse doctrine that kings are drafted: the dead king's afterlife career is a conscription, and the poem's performance at court converted mourning into enlistment news.\n\nIts companion, Hakonarmal (Eyvindr skaldaspillir, c. 961, for Hakon the Good - a Christian-leaning king!), does the same work and even has Odin send the valkyries down to choose the king alive; taken together the two poems are the best documentation of Valhalla's ideological work in tenth-century court culture. In the atlas, the record anchors the chosen-death and royal-drafting motif family, answering the Chinese astral promotion of dead officials and the Islamic martyrdom doctrine - each a death-to-office pipeline; the Norse one runs through a barracks with a welcome-committee."
    },
