    lat: 61.7,
    lon: 8.5,
    period: "Thorsdrapa (Eilifr Godrunarson, c. 10th c.); Skaldskaparmal c. 1220-1230; principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2367 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "Corpus Poeticum Boreale / Project Gutenberg (Brodeur 1916)",
      work: "Eilifr Godrunarson, Thorsdrapa; Snorri, Skaldskaparmal ch. 10 (the story of Thorsdrapa); ed. Vigfusson & Powell, CPB 1883",
      url: "https://skaldic.abdn.ac.uk/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (CPB 1883; Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The most violent exploit in skaldic art: Loki, forced by Geirrod's capture of him, decoys Thor into the giant's land without hammer, belt or gloves; the giantess Grid lends the god her belt of strength, iron gloves and staff. At the Vimur river - swollen by the witch's incantations - Thor wades with the staff, 'and they say that the river rose highest at his shoulders', and he finds a stone and hurls it: 'and that stone stayed the stream, as if a rock had been set there' - the aition of a boulder-stopped river. In the hall, Geirrod's iron-hot wedge? - the giant hurls a molten iron wedge with tongs; Thor catches it in his iron gloves and hurls it back through pillar and giant. The skaldic Thorsdrapa, the corpus's most violent poem, tells the same with blood-drinking kennings.",
      zh: "\u9019\u662F\u541F\u904A\u8A69\u6B4C\u4E2D\u6700\u66B4\u70C8\u7684\u795E\u8E5F\uFF1A\u6D1B\u57FA\u88AB\u84CB\u723E\u7F85\u5FB7\u64D2\u7372\u5F8C\u53D7\u8FEB\u8A98\u9A19\u7D22\u723E\u2014\u2014\u4E0D\u5E36\u9318\u3001\u4E0D\u5E36\u8170\u5E36\u8207\u9435\u624B\u5957\u2014\u2014\u6DF1\u5165\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4E4B\u5730\uFF1B\u5973\u5DE8\u4EBA\u683C\u91CC\u5FB7\u501F\u4E88\u96F7\u795E\u795E\u529B\u8170\u5E36\u3001\u9435\u624B\u5957\u8207\u6728\u6756\u3002\u5728\u7DAD\u7A46\u723E\u6CB3\u7554\u2014\u2014\u6CB3\u6C34\u56E0\u5973\u5DEB\u5492\u8A9E\u800C\u66B4\u6F32\u2014\u2014\u7D22\u723E\u57F7\u6756\u6D89\u6C34\uFF0C\u300C\u64DA\u8AAA\u6CB3\u6C34\u6700\u9AD8\u6F32\u81F3\u4ED6\u7684\u80A9\u982D\u300D\uFF1B\u4ED6\u62FE\u8D77\u4E00\u584A\u5DE8\u77F3\u64F2\u51FA\uFF1A\u300C\u90A3\u77F3\u982D\u4FBF\u6514\u4F4F\u4E86\u6C34\u6D41\uFF0C\u5982\u540C\u5929\u7136\u5DE8\u5CA9\u7ACB\u65BC\u6CB3\u4E2D\u300D\u2014\u2014\u9019\u662F\u5DE8\u77F3\u6514\u6CB3\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\u3002\u5728\u5EF3\u4E2D\uFF0C\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4EE5\u9435\u9257\u64F2\u51FA\u71BE\u71B1\u7684\u9435\u6954\uFF1B\u7D22\u723E\u4EE5\u9435\u624B\u5957\u63A5\u4F4F\uFF0C\u53CD\u64F2\u56DE\u53BB\uFF0C\u8CAB\u7A7F\u77F3\u67F1\u8207\u5DE8\u4EBA\u3002\u541F\u904A\u8A69\u300A\u7D22\u723E\u980C\u300B\u2014\u2014\u6B4C\u8B20\u4E2D\u6700\u66B4\u70C8\u7684\u4F5C\u54C1\u2014\u2014\u4EE5\u55DC\u8840\u7684\u96B1\u55BB\u8B1B\u8FF0\u540C\u4E00\u6545\u4E8B\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Corpus Poeticum Boreale, ed. Vigfusson & Powell, Oxford 1883 (public domain); Skaldskaparmal via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "The tale's frame is a deception within a deception. Geirrod the giant captures Loki (in hawk-shape) and releases him only on his oath to bring Thor to the hall without his hammer or his strength-girdle; Loki decoys the god with a lying tale of hospitality. The journey is armed by a third party: the giantess Grid, mother of Vidar, warns Thor and lends him her belt of strength, her iron gloves and her staff Gridarvolr.\n\nThe river-crossing is the poem's physics-set-piece. The witch Gjalp, Geirrod's daughter, stands astride the Vimur and raises the water with incantations; Thor wades with the staff, 'and they say that the river rose highest at his shoulders'; he finds a stone in the riverbed and casts it, 'and that stone stayed the stream, as if a rock had been set there' - Snorri's aition for a river dammed by a thrown boulder - and then he wades out and grips a mountain-berry? - the prose: he seizes a rowan-tree (the famous 'Thor's salvation' of the rowan) and climbs out; whence the kenning that the rowan is 'Thor's salvation'. In the hall, the tests turn physical: Gjalp, hidden under Thor's chair, presses it up with her knees; the god breaks her back by seating himself hard. Geirrod, seeing the god's fury, hurls a wedge of molten iron with tongs; Thor catches it in the iron gloves and hurls it back 'so that it passed through the pillar and through Geirrod and through the wall, and so out? ' - and the giant's daughters, fleeing, are crushed.\n\nThe skaldic poem Thorsdrapa by Eilifr Godrunarson (10th c.) is the myth's oldest and most violent telling - so violent that the Edda-MSS preserve it 'because it is hard to understand? ' - Snorri says he quotes it 'because it is excellent and? ' - the prose says he includes it for its art. The poem's kennings drink blood: Geirrod's hall is 'the wolf-feeding hall? ', the wedge 'the reddening? iron of the giant', Thor 'the breaker of Geirrod's brain-pan'. Snorri retells it to explain the poem's obscure diction - the prose retelling exists for the verse, a case-study of the myth corpus's transmission: skaldic difficulty generating prose narrative.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the river-as-attack doctrine (the frontier's waters weaponized by witchcraft, dammed by a god) and the counter-throw doctrine (the god defeats the giant's technology with the giant's own weapon, caught and returned). And the rowan-tree doctrine ('Thor's salvation') is an aition of folk botanical belief - the rowan's protective reputation in Scandinavian farm-culture descends from this river-crossing. In the atlas, the record anchors the duel-by-technology family: the Chinese Yi's archery, the Greek Heracles' poisoning? - no: the atlas frame is the hero armed by borrowed gear (Grid's belt answers the Greek Athena's aegis-loans), and the myth's weapons are industrial: molten iron against iron gloves."
    },
    functions: [
      "River-as-attack doctrine: frontier waters weaponized by witchcraft, dammed by a thrown boulder",
      "Counter-throw doctrine: the giant's molten iron caught in iron gloves and returned",
      "Aition of the rowan: 'Thor's salvation' as the tree's protective reputation in farm-culture",
      "Transmission case-study: a violent skaldic poem generating its own prose retelling"
    ],
