    period: "Skaldskaparmal c. 1220-1230 (older material); Grimnismal st. 11, 15; principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2367 4to, GKS 2365 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "Project Gutenberg (Brodeur 1916) / sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923)",
      work: "Snorri, Skaldskaparmal ch. 3; Grimnismal st. 11, 15; critical ed. Faulkes (1987)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/pre/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "When the Aesir kill her father Thjazi, the giantess Skadi comes armed to Asgard for atonement. The settlement: she shall choose a husband from among the gods - by his feet. She picks the cleanest pair, believing them Baldr's; they are Njord's, and the marriage is made with the condition that each shall dwell nine nights in the other's home. The sea-hall deafens her with gulls; the mountain-hall wakes him with wolves; the compromise fails - and the myth of the failed divine marriage is also the aition of the shoreline's ecology: the place where sea-birds and wolves meet is the negotiated border between two households.",
      zh: "\u963F\u85A9\u795E\u65CF\u8A85\u6BBA\u5176\u7236\u590F\u8332\u5F8C\uFF0C\u5973\u5DE8\u4EBA\u65AF\u5361\u8482\u6301\u68B0\u524D\u4F86\u963F\u65AF\u52A0\u5FB7\u8981\u6C42\u511F\u4ED8\u3002\u548C\u89E3\u689D\u4EF6\u662F\uFF1A\u5979\u53EF\u5F9E\u795E\u65CF\u4E2D\u81EA\u9078\u4E00\u4F4D\u4E08\u592B\u2014\u2014\u4F46\u53EA\u80FD\u770B\u8173\u3002\u5979\u6311\u4E2D\u96D9\u8DB3\u6700\u6F54\u6DE8\u8005\uFF0C\u4EE5\u70BA\u662F\u5DF4\u723E\u5FB7\uFF0C\u5BE6\u5247\u662F\u5C3C\u5967\u5FB7\u3002\u5A5A\u7D04\u9644\u52A0\u689D\u6B3E\uFF1A\u592B\u59BB\u9808\u8F2A\u6D41\u5728\u5C0D\u65B9\u5BB6\u4E2D\u5404\u4F4F\u4E5D\u591C\u3002\u6D77\u908A\u5927\u5EF3\u7684\u6D77\u9CE5\u9CF4\u8072\u4EE4\u5979\u5FB9\u591C\u96E3\u7720\uFF1B\u5C71\u4E2D\u5927\u5EF3\u7684\u72FC\u568E\u53C8\u5435\u9192\u4E86\u6D77\u795E\u3002\u59A5\u5354\u5931\u6557\u2014\u2014\u800C\u9019\u5247\u300C\u5931\u6557\u7684\u795E\u8056\u806F\u59FB\u300D\u795E\u8A71\uFF0C\u540C\u6642\u4E5F\u662F\u6D77\u5CB8\u7DDA\u751F\u614B\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\uFF1A\u6D77\u9CE5\u8207\u72FC\u7FA4\u76F8\u9047\u4E4B\u5730\uFF0C\u6B63\u662F\u5169\u6236\u4EBA\u5BB6\u8AC7\u5224\u51FA\u7684\u908A\u754C\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The myth begins as a legal action. Skadi - 'the shining bride of the gods'? - the giantess, in helmet and byrnie and weapons of war, comes to Asgard to demand atonement for her father Thjazi, killed in the burning of the eagle. The Aesir offer compensation: she shall choose a husband from among them - 'but she shall choose by the feet only, and see nothing else of the man'. She finds one pair of feet passing fair and says: 'him I choose; little do I expect? ' - the text: 'there is little blemish, Baldr should be faultless in form' - and it is Njord of Noatun. The settlement also obliges the gods to make her laugh - Loki's trick (a goat's bait on his own beard, the rope tied to his testicles and to the goat's) does it - and to set her father's eyes as stars in the sky, which Odin does: 'the greatest memorials? ' - the two eyes thrown into the heaven as stars.\n\nThe marriage's terms are the myth's experimental clause: each shall dwell nine nights in the other's home. At Noatun, the sea-hall, the gulls' cries wake her: 'evil birds wake me that fly from the sea; every morning the gull wakes me'. At Thrymheim, the mountain-hall, the wolves' howls wake him: 'sick? ' - the prose: 'the wolves' howling wakes him, sick? ' - 'I was waked by the howling of wolves, he said? ' - the poem Grimnismal supplies the speeches in st. 11 and 15: each spouse testifies against the other's home. Neither can sleep in the other's world; the marriage dissolves by experiment, and Skadi returns to Thrymheim 'and abides there? ' - the text: 'she went up? ' - Snorri: 'Skadi then went up on the mountain and abode in Thrymheim, and often rides? ' - she keeps to the mountains, hunting on snowshoes with bow, and is called the snowshoe-goddess (ondurd\xEDs).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the doctrine of habitat-marriage and the aition of the shore's ecology. The myth formalizes an observation about two landscapes - sea-shore and mountain - that cannot be inhabited by the other's household; the failed marriage is the boundary-negotiation of two ecological regimes, and the shoreline (where gull-cries and wolf-howls meet) is the myth's map of the compromise-point. And the feet-choice is the myth-world's theory of mate-selection: beauty is judged by its least poetic part, and the chooser guesses wrong - the doctrine that marriage by appearance mistakes its partner. Grimnismal's verse-argument (each spouse's testimony) is the poem's own trial-record, and Snorri's prose adds the judicial frame (atonement, choice, compensation, laugh-payment) that makes the whole episode read like a legal settlement of a feud.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the divine-marriage family (Zeus-Hera's institution, the Izanagi-Izanami separation): each a marriage myth; the Norse one is distinctive in being failed by design - an experiment in cohabitation whose result is geography: the sea stays with the sea-folk, the mountain with the ski-goddess, and the boundary is the beach."
    },
