    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Tyr is the boldest and most courageous of the gods, and it is well for men of victory to call on him: 'it is proven that he rules over victory in battle'. His signature is his missing right hand - lost to the wolf Fenrir's bite when the other gods refused to pledge their own. In Lokasenna, when Loki taunts him - 'I have your hand, the wolf's snare' - Tyr answers with the oath-keeper's grief: 'I lost my hand, the wolf's snare, and grief have I got; the wolf stays bound, and I stay wretched'. And his deepest service is forgotten by all: he is Hymir's son, and it was Tyr who knew the giant's hall and led Thor to the mile-deep kettle. The record is the profile of law's god: the hand of the oath is the hand the wolf ate.",
      zh: "\u63D0\u723E\u662F\u8AF8\u795E\u4E2D\u6700\u52C7\u6562\u3001\u6700\u5805\u6BC5\u8005\uFF0C\u6B32\u6C42\u6230\u52DD\u8005\u5B9C\u547C\u5176\u540D\uFF1A\u300C\u78BA\u5207\u53EF\u77E5\uFF0C\u4ED6\u638C\u7BA1\u6230\u9B25\u4E2D\u7684\u52DD\u5229\u300D\u3002\u5176\u6A19\u8A8C\u662F\u7F3A\u5931\u7684\u53F3\u624B\u2014\u2014\u7576\u773E\u795E\u7686\u4E0D\u80AF\u4EE5\u624B\u4F5C\u4FDD\u6642\uFF0C\u4ED6\u7684\u624B\u88AB\u5DE8\u72FC\u82AC\u91CC\u723E\u54AC\u53BB\u3002\u5728\u300A\u6D1B\u57FA\u7684\u5435\u7F75\u300B\u4E2D\uFF0C\u6D1B\u57FA\u8B4F\u8AF7\u4ED6\u2014\u2014\u300C\u6211\u63E1\u6709\u4F60\u7684\u624B\uFF0C\u90A3\u72FC\u7684\u9396\u93C8\u300D\u2014\u2014\u63D0\u723E\u4EE5\u5B88\u8A93\u8005\u7684\u60B2\u50B7\u4F5C\u7B54\uFF1A\u300C\u6211\u5931\u4E86\u624B\uFF0C\u6210\u4E86\u72FC\u7684\u9396\u93C8\uFF0C\u6211\u6EFF\u61F7\u54C0\u50B7\uFF1B\u72FC\u88AB\u7E1B\u4F4F\uFF0C\u800C\u6211\u6DD2\u82E6\u5EA6\u65E5\u300D\u3002\u800C\u4ED6\u6700\u6DF1\u7684\u529F\u52DE\u537B\u88AB\u773E\u795E\u907A\u5FD8\uFF1A\u4ED6\u672C\u662F\u5DE8\u4EBA\u5E0C\u7C73\u723E\u4E4B\u5B50\uFF0C\u6B63\u662F\u4ED6\u77E5\u9053\u5DE8\u4EBA\u5927\u5EF3\u4E4B\u8DEF\uFF0C\u5F15\u7D22\u723E\u53D6\u56DE\u90A3\u53E3\u6DF1\u9054\u4E00\u91CC\u7684\u5DE8\u91DC\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u6CD5\u5F8B\u4E4B\u795E\u7684\u8096\u50CF\uFF1A\u8A93\u8A00\u4E4B\u624B\uFF0C\u6B63\u662F\u88AB\u72FC\u5403\u6389\u7684\u90A3\u96BB\u624B\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Gylfaginning's profile is brief and exact: Tyr is the boldest and most courageous of the gods, and he has great authority over victory in battle; 'it is well for men of valour to call on him'. His other name is 'the one-handed god', and the reason is the wolf: 'when the Aesir enticed Fenrir to let himself be bound with the chain Gleipnir, the wolf did not believe them to be minded to loose him till they laid Tyr's hand in his mouth as a pledge; and when the Aesir would not loose him, then he bit the hand off at the wrist - the place is called the wolf's joint? - and Tyr is one-handed and is not called a reconciler of men'. The prose adds the etymological point: the god of the oath is the god whose pledge-body is broken.\n\nLokasenna's flyting gives the grief its voice. Loki taunts: 'Be silent, Tyr! Thine hand? ' - the verses: 'I have thy hand, the snare of the wolf, and my son? ' - Loki claims credit for the binding and for the wolf's kinship? - the exchange: Loki says 'I was the father of the wolf? ' - no: Loki jeers 'thy hand could never hold aught', and Tyr answers: 'I lost my hand, the wolf's snare, and grief have I got; the wolf stays bound, and I stay wretched' - and then the second blow: Loki charges Tyr with cuckolding his wife, and Tyr falls silent - the oath-keeper's silence is the flyting's judgment that his private life cannot match his public honor.\n\nThe forgotten service is the kettle-quest's frame. In Hymiskvida, it is Tyr who offers to fetch the mile-deep kettle from the giant Hymir - 'for Hymir my father dwells there' - the law-god is the giant's son, and his kinship is the expedition's passport; he lifts the kettle-lid by its ring while Thor carries the vessel. The profile's cultural functions are the doctrine of pledged collateral (the god of oaths literally loses the body-part that secures them) and the paradox of law's kinships (the legal god is half giant - law's passport is a border-family). The etymology 'Tiwaz' ties him to the Indo-European sky-father (cf. Zeus, deus, Jupiter) - the oldest layer of the pantheon, and in the Norse material the god who has been overtaken by Odin and Thor in cult, surviving as the memory of what an oath costs.\n\nFor the atlas, the record anchors the sacrificial-office family: the god whose office is embodied (the hand as the pledge) - answering the Greek Zeus Horkios and the Roman Fides: each a deity of the sworn word; the Norse one pays for his office in flesh."
    },
    functions: [
      "Doctrine of pledged collateral: the oath-god's hand is the pledge the wolf ate",
      "Office embodied in flesh: victory-law and oath-keeping carried on a one-armed body",
      "Law's border-kinship: the legal god is the giant Hymir's son - law's passport is a border-family",
      "Cult-history datum: the Indo-European sky-father (Tiwaz) demoted in Norse cult, surviving as oath-memory"
    ],
