    summary: {
      en: "Loki is the son of the giant Farbauti ('dangerous striker') and Laufey ('leafy isle'), brother of Helblindi and Byleistr; Snorri calls him 'the calumniator and cheat of the gods', handsome and fair of face but evil in disposition, and 'the causer of? ' - the father of the lie. His children with Angrboda are the myth's doom-clock: Fenrir the wolf, Jormungand the serpent, Hel the half-dead girl; and by his wife Sigyn, the sons Narfi and Vali whose entrails bind him. And in Lokasenna's opening he is revealed as Odin's blood-brother - sworn in the morning of time, before the gods' order existed. The record is the genealogy of the trickster as the pantheon's internal border: kin to giants, brother to Odin, father to the world's end.",
      zh: "\u6D1B\u57FA\u662F\u5DE8\u4EBA\u6CD5\u723E\u9B91\u8482\uFF08\u300C\u5371\u96AA\u7684\u64CA\u6253\u8005\u300D\uFF09\u8207\u52DE\u83F2\uFF08\u300C\u7E41\u8302\u4E4B\u5CF6\u300D\uFF09\u4E4B\u5B50\uFF0C\u6D77\u723E\u5E03\u6797\u8FEA\u8207\u5E03\u840A\u65AF\u8036\u723E\u7684\u5144\u5F1F\uFF1B\u65AF\u8AFE\u91CC\u7A31\u4ED6\u70BA\u300C\u8AF8\u795E\u7684\u8AB9\u8B17\u8005\u8207\u6B3A\u8A50\u8005\u300D\uFF0C\u9762\u5BB9\u4FCA\u7F8E\u800C\u5FC3\u5730\u90AA\u60E1\uFF0C\u662F\u300C\u8B0A\u8A00\u4E4B\u7236\u300D\u3002\u4ED6\u8207\u5B89\u683C\u723E\u6CE2\u9054\u6240\u751F\u7684\u5B50\u5973\u662F\u795E\u8A71\u7684\u672B\u65E5\u6642\u9418\uFF1A\u5DE8\u72FC\u82AC\u91CC\u723E\u3001\u5927\u86C7\u8036\u5922\u52A0\u5F97\u3001\u534A\u6B7B\u7684\u5C11\u5973\u6D77\u723E\uFF1B\u4ED6\u8207\u59BB\u5B50\u897F\u683C\u6069\u6240\u751F\u7684\u7D0D\u723E\u83F2\u8207\u74E6\u5229\uFF0C\u5176\u8178\u7D22\u65E5\u5F8C\u6210\u70BA\u6346\u7E1B\u4ED6\u7684\u7E69\u7D22\u3002\u800C\u300A\u6D1B\u57FA\u7684\u5435\u7F75\u300B\u958B\u7BC7\u63ED\u793A\u4ED6\u7ADF\u8207\u5967\u4E01\u7D50\u70BA\u8840\u76DF\u5144\u5F1F\u2014\u2014\u5728\u9060\u53E4\u6642\u5149\u3001\u795E\u754C\u79E9\u5E8F\u5C1A\u672A\u5EFA\u7ACB\u4E4B\u524D\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u5448\u73FE\u8A6D\u8A08\u8005\u7684\u8B5C\u7CFB\uFF1A\u4ED6\u65E2\u662F\u795E\u754C\u7684\u5167\u90E8\u908A\u754C\u2014\u2014\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4E00\u65CF\u4E4B\u89AA\u3001\u5967\u4E01\u8840\u8A93\u4E4B\u5F1F\u3001\u672B\u65E5\u4E4B\u5B50\u7684\u7236\u89AA\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Gylfaginning's dossier opens with the parentage: Loki is the son of Farbauti the giant and Laufey, 'who is counted among the goddesses'? - the text: 'Laufey or Nal is his mother, and Byleistr and Helblindi are his brothers'; and the portrait: 'Loki is handsome and fair of face, but evil in disposition, and very changeable of mood? ' - the prose: 'he surpasses all men in the art of cunning, called the sly one? ' - the dossier reads: 'the calumniator and cheat of the gods, the father of the monster-fish? - the serpent? - Hel and the wolf and, further, the causer of all misfortune among gods and men'. The dossier's punch: Loki is the god-company's own member - 'he is counted among the Aesir' - because of his oath of blood-brotherhood: in Lokasenna's opening, Loki reminds Odin - 'remember, Odin, in the morning of time? we two mixed our blood together; then you said you would never taste ale unless it were brought to us both' - the blood-drinking pact that made the trickster a god's brother by the oldest ritual the north knows.\n\nThe brood is the myth's eschatology in genealogy. With the giantess Angrboda, Loki fathered the wolf Fenrir, the serpent Jormungand and Hel; Snorri's chapter reads the family as a prophecy: the three children are the materials of the doom (the wolf to swallow Odin, the serpent to kill Thor, Hel to keep Baldr). By his wife Sigyn he had the sons whose bodies become his binding - Narfi and Vali, one killed by the gods, the other's entrails made into the fetter that holds Loki to the rock. The genealogy thus runs from trickster to catastrophe in one generation: his love-life is the myth's weapon-manufacture.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the internal-border doctrine (the pantheon's trickster is kin to its enemies and brother to its king - the oath-economy's cost made visible) and the lineage-of-doom doctrine (the myth-world's evil is familial, not foreign: the monsters are Loki's children, raised in Asgard itself). And the status is legally precise: Loki is not a giant among the gods nor a god among giants, but a sworn kinsman - the member whose membership is a contract, which is why the flyting is possible (a blood-brother may insult) and why the binding is a family matter (his wife Sigyn holds the bowl). In the atlas, the record anchors the trickster-kinship family (the Greek Hermes, son of Zeus; the Chinese Monkey, made a god by appointment): each a trickster inside the pantheon; the Norse one is unique in being bound by blood-oath to the king and in manufacturing the apocalypse as paternity."
    },
    functions: [
      "Internal-border doctrine: the trickster is kin to the enemies and blood-brother to the king",
      "Lineage-of-doom: the monsters are Loki's children, raised in Asgard itself",
      "Blood-oath economy: membership as contract - insultable, bindable, family-cared-for",
      "Weapon-manufacture as paternity: his love-life produces the doom-clock"
    ],
