    lat: 55.83,
    lon: 10.59,
    period: "Hervarar saga ok Heidreks 13th century (older heroic material; verse Hervararkvida of older date); principal MS Hauksbok AM 544 4to; English: Nora Kershaw Chadwick, Stories and Ballads of the Far Past, 1921",
    source: {
      archive: "archive.org (Kershaw 1921) / handrit.is",
      work: "Hervarar saga ok Heidreks (Hervararkvida); ed. C. C. Rafn, Fornaldar sogur; English trans. Nora Kershaw 1921; trans. Christopher Tolkien 1960",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/storiesandballa00chadgoog"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Kershaw 1921); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Hervor is the daughter of Angantyr, the berserk who died with his eleven brothers on the island of Sams\xF8, buried in a mound with the cursed sword Tyrfing. Raised as a bondmaid in her mother's house, she learns her father's name, dresses as a man, and takes ship to the haunted island. Alone at night she walks the burning fire of the grave-mounds and calls the dead: 'Wake, Angantyr! Hervor wakes you, only daughter of Yngvi's kin!' The dead warn her; father Angantyr tells her the sword burns its holders; she persists, and the mound opens, and she takes Tyrfing from the dead man's hand - the only person to claim a cursed weapon from its own corpse. The record is the fornaldarsaga's signature descent: a living woman entering the world of the dead by command-voice, and winning its treasure against its own warning.",
      zh: "\u8D6B\u723E\u6C83\u662F\u72C2\u6230\u58EB\u5B89\u7518\u63D0\u723E\u4E4B\u5973\u2014\u2014\u4ED6\u8207\u5341\u4E00\u4F4D\u5144\u5F1F\u6B7B\u65BC\u85A9\u59C6\u7D22\u5CF6\uFF0C\u9023\u540C\u8A5B\u5492\u4E4B\u528D\u63D0\u723E\u82AC\u4E00\u540C\u846C\u65BC\u58B3\u4E18\u3002\u8D6B\u723E\u6C83\u5728\u6BCD\u89AA\u5BB6\u4E2D\u88AB\u7576\u4F5C\u5974\u5A62\u990A\u5927\uFF0C\u5F97\u77E5\u7236\u89AA\u4E4B\u540D\u5F8C\u5373\u6539\u8457\u7537\u88DD\uFF0C\u4E58\u8239\u524D\u5F80\u90A3\u5EA7\u9B27\u9B3C\u4E4B\u5CF6\u3002\u591C\u6DF1\u4EBA\u975C\uFF0C\u5979\u7368\u81EA\u7A7F\u8D8A\u58B3\u5893\u9593\u7684\u9B3C\u706B\uFF0C\u9AD8\u8072\u547C\u559A\u4EA1\u8005\uFF1A\u300C\u9192\u4F86\u5427\uFF0C\u5B89\u7518\u63D0\u723E\uFF01\u559A\u4F60\u7684\u662F\u8D6B\u723E\u6C83\uFF0C\u82F1\u683C\u7DAD\u4E00\u65CF\u7684\u7368\u5973\uFF01\u300D\u4EA1\u8005\u51FA\u8A00\u8B66\u544A\uFF1B\u7236\u89AA\u5B89\u7518\u63D0\u723E\u66F4\u544A\u8A34\u5979\uFF0C\u6B64\u528D\u5FC5\u5C07\u53CD\u566C\u5176\u4E3B\u3002\u5979\u6BEB\u4E0D\u9000\u7E2E\uFF1B\u58B3\u4E18\u7D42\u65BC\u958B\u555F\uFF0C\u5979\u81EA\u6B7B\u4EBA\u624B\u4E2D\u53D6\u8D70\u63D0\u723E\u82AC\u2014\u2014\u552F\u4E00\u6562\u5F9E\u8A5B\u5492\u6B66\u5668\u7684\u5C4D\u4E3B\u624B\u4E2D\u5C07\u5176\u596A\u8D70\u7684\u4EBA\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u300A\u4E0A\u53E4\u50B3\u8AAA\u85A9\u8FE6\u300B\u7684\u4EE3\u8868\u6027\u51A5\u5E9C\u4E4B\u884C\uFF1A\u4E00\u540D\u751F\u8005\u5973\u5B50\u4EE5\u865F\u4EE4\u4E4B\u8072\u8E0F\u5165\u4EA1\u8005\u4E16\u754C\uFF0C\u7121\u8996\u5176\u8B66\u544A\uFF0C\u8D0F\u5F97\u5176\u4E2D\u5BF6\u7269\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Nora Kershaw Chadwick, Stories and Ballads of the Far Past, Cambridge 1921 (public domain); Christopher Tolkien, The Saga of King Heidrek the Wise, 1960",
    narrative: {
      en: "The saga's first movement is Hervor's doubling. Her father Angantyr, berserk of the Arngrim-lineage? - of Arngrim's sons, died on Sams\xF8 in the berserk-duel with the Jomsviking? - with the brothers' enemy, all twelve brothers in one mound with the sword Tyrfing, forged by dwarfs and cursed to kill a man every time it is drawn. Her mother, fearing her lineage, has Hervor raised as a bondmaid with other women's children, unnamed; but the girl 'grew? ' - the saga: 'she was as strong as the men, and when she might? ' - she grew into a shieldmaiden's temperament, learned her father's name from an old woman, took weapons and men's clothes, joined a vikings-ship as Harv? - as Hervard, and commanded crews in raids - 'and when the ship's men knew she was a woman, they thought great things of her'? - the saga's note of collective respect.\n\nThe mound-raising is the saga's descent-scene, told in the verse of Hervararkvida. Hervor lands on Sams\xF8 alone at night while her crew stays aboard 'for fear of the ghosts' - the island's fires burn above the mounds, and none may land after sunset. She walks into the fire, 'and it did her no harm', and comes to the mound of her father and uncles, and calls the dead with the command-voice of the ritual waking: 'Wake up, Angantyr! Hervor wakes you, only daughter of Yngvi and Tota? - of Yngvi's kin; give me from the mound the dwarven sword!' The dead answer in fear - 'son, what has come to thee? ' - the dead Angantyr warns his daughter in the mound's voice: the sword will be her death, all its holders die; she answers with her lineage's logic: 'no one? ' - she will have the sword though it be her death. Angantyr then relents with the dead's only gift: 'the sword burns and is ruined? ' - he says the mound opens? - and the fire about the mounds is stilled, and she takes Tyrfing from the dead man's right hand - 'and she greeted her kin and went away with the sword', and the saga's verse closes: 'now fare I from the mounds? ' - she leaves the island as the dead quiet behind her.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the descent-by-voice doctrine and the cursed-gift's claiming. Hervor's waking of the dead is the sagas' formalized necromancy: the living may enter the dead's world by speaking the lineage-code (her name and descent), and the dead may answer with warnings - the mound as a court of ancestors with jurisdiction over their own gear. Her victory is the cycle's hinge: the cursed sword enters the living world through a woman's claim, and its curse (to kill whenever drawn) will run through her son Heidrek and her grandson Angantyr - the saga's whole plot is the sword's biography, and this record is its first act. And the gender-doctrine is pointed: the lineage's weapon passes through a bondmaid raised in disguise - the fornaldarsaga's signature heroine, whose claim to arms outranks the dead's own caution.\n\nFor the atlas, the record anchors the descent-for-retrieval family (the Mesopotamian descent of Inanna, the Greek Herakles' Cerberus-fetch, the Japanese Okuninushi's underworld visit): each a living person entering the dead's realm for an object; the Norse one is distinctive in being performed by a woman in command-voice, at night, into a fire that does not burn her."
    },
    functions: [
      "Descent-by-voice doctrine: the living enter the dead's realm by speaking lineage-code",
      "Mound as ancestor-court: the dead hold jurisdiction over their own gear and may warn",
      "Cursed-gift claiming: the sword's biography begins with a woman's claim against the dead's warning",
      "Gender-doctrine: the lineage's weapon passes through a bondmaid raised in disguise"
    ],
