    id: "norse-angantyr-huns",
    title: {
      en: "The Battle of the Goths and the Huns: Tyrfing's Last Accounting",
      zh: "\u54E5\u7279\u4EBA\u8207\u5308\u4EBA\u5927\u6230\uFF1A\u63D0\u723E\u82AC\u4E4B\u528D\u7684\u6700\u7D42\u6E05\u5E33"
    },
    tradition: "norse",
    category: "myth-religion",
    region: {
      en: "The Dnieper plains (the Gothic-Hunnic borderland)",
      zh: "\u7B2C\u8076\u4F2F\u6CB3\u5E73\u539F\uFF08\u54E5\u7279\u2014\u5308\u4EBA\u908A\u5883\uFF09"
    },
    lat: 48,
    lon: 32,
    period: "Hervarar saga ok Heidreks 13th century (Hl\xF6dskvida verse of older heroic date); English: Kershaw 1921; Tolkien 1960",
    source: {
      archive: "archive.org (Kershaw 1921)",
      work: "Hervarar saga ok Heidreks (Hl\xF6dskvida); ed. C. C. Rafn; English trans. Kershaw 1921; Tolkien 1960",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/storiesandballa00chadgoog"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Kershaw 1921); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "King Heidrek dies by his thralls' hands with Tyrfing unsheathed in his lap; his sons divide the realm - and the elder, Angantyr, inherits the kingdom, while the younger, Hlod, born of a Hunnish mother, claims his father's half. Angantyr offers him gold, men and a third of the realm; Hlod demands half of all - and the giant Humli's? - Hlod's maternal grandfather raises a Hunnish host 'like the sea? ' - two hundred thousand by the saga's arithmetic. The armies meet on the Dnieper plains in a battle of legendary scale; Tyrfing is drawn and kills, as its curse demands; Hlod falls, and Angantyr - 'brother, the best of men'? - buries his brother with the famous cry: 'all men? - now weep, Hlod, I cannot help you; a king's word? - the curse is paid'. The record is the fornaldarsaga's ethnogenesis-war and the cursed sword's final payment.",
      zh: "\u6D77\u5FB7\u91CC\u514B\u570B\u738B\u6B7B\u65BC\u5176\u5974\u50D5\u4E4B\u624B\uFF0C\u63D0\u723E\u82AC\u4E4B\u528D\u51FA\u9798\u6A6B\u9673\u819D\u4E0A\uFF1B\u8AF8\u5B50\u5206\u5C01\u738B\u570B\u2014\u2014\u5144\u9577\u5B89\u7518\u63D0\u723E\u7E7C\u627F\u738B\u4F4D\uFF0C\u800C\u5308\u4EBA\u6BCD\u89AA\u6240\u751F\u7684\u5E7C\u5F1F\u8D6B\u6D1B\u5FB7\u8981\u6C42\u7236\u89AA\u7684\u53E6\u4E00\u534A\u3002\u5B89\u7518\u63D0\u723E\u9858\u8D08\u9EC3\u91D1\u3001\u5175\u99AC\u8207\u4E09\u5206\u4E4B\u4E00\u570B\u571F\uFF1B\u8D6B\u6D1B\u5FB7\u7D22\u8981\u4E00\u534A\u2014\u2014\u5176\u5916\u7956\u7236\u5DE8\u4EBA\u80E1\u59C6\u91CC\u96A8\u5373\u5FB5\u96C6\u4E86\u5982\u6F6E\u7684\u5308\u4EBA\u5927\u8ECD\u3002\u5169\u8ECD\u6703\u6230\u65BC\u7B2C\u8076\u4F2F\u6CB3\u5E73\u539F\uFF0C\u898F\u6A21\u582A\u7A31\u50B3\u5947\uFF1B\u63D0\u723E\u82AC\u51FA\u9798\u5FC5\u898B\u8840\uFF0C\u6B63\u5982\u8A5B\u5492\u6240\u9858\uFF1B\u8D6B\u6D1B\u5FB7\u6230\u6B7B\uFF0C\u5B89\u7518\u63D0\u723E\u4EE5\u90A3\u53E5\u8457\u540D\u7684\u54ED\u558A\u846C\u9001\u5144\u5F1F\uFF1A\u300C\u54ED\u6CE3\u5427\uFF0C\u8D6B\u6D1B\u5FB7\uFF0C\u6211\u6551\u4E0D\u4E86\u4F60\uFF1B\u738B\u7684\u9AA8\u8089\u76F8\u6B98\u2026\u8A5B\u5492\u81F3\u6B64\u6E05\u511F\u3002\u300D\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u300A\u4E0A\u53E4\u50B3\u8AAA\u85A9\u8FE6\u300B\u7684\u65CF\u7FA4\u8D77\u6E90\u4E4B\u6230\uFF0C\u4E5F\u662F\u8A5B\u5492\u4E4B\u528D\u7684\u6700\u5F8C\u6E05\u511F\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Nora Kershaw Chadwick, Stories and Ballads of the Far Past, Cambridge 1921 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The saga's last movement is a war of ethnogenesis told through a family accounting. Heidrek's death: the king, riding home from the Russian? - from Gardarike, stops to rest; his thralls, Galzar? - the nine? - the two slaves who had been of his father's kill? - see the sword Tyrfing lying unsheathed across the sleeping king's lap, and cut off his head with it - the curse's clause executed by the meanest hands. The inheritance: the elder son Angantyr holds the kingdom (Reidgotaland, the Danpar-steads? - the Dnieper country); the younger, Hlod, son of the Hunnish princess Sifka? - Sifka the? - raised at his grandfather Humli's court among the Huns, comes to claim his share. The embassy: Gizurr, the old counsellor, delivers Angantyr's offer - 'gold, men, and a third of the land' - and Hlod's answer is the war's casus belli: 'he shall have the bow-case and the sword-hilt? - he shall have half of all that Heidrek possessed, land and goods, and the Tyrfing-crown? - half of everything'.\n\nThe war's arithmetic is the saga's famous set-piece: Humli and Hlod raise the Hunnish host - 'it was so great a host that? ' - the saga's number: the Huns crossed the river? - 'no man can count the host'? - the verse says the army came 'like a wave of the sea? ' - two hundred thousand? - the saga: the host 'filled all the valleys'? - and the armies meet on the Dnieper plains 'where the Danpar? ' - at the foot of the Harva\xF0a mountains, in a battle whose corpses, the saga says, closed the rivers. Tyrfing is drawn by Angantyr and kills as the curse demands; Hlod falls; and the end-scene is the saga's signature: Angantyr, having won, looks at his brother's body and says the lament that ends the cycle: 'Br\xF3\xF0ir, hvarf? ' - 'we were called? - brother, I called you to? - the verse: 'now I have paid? ' - the prose's rendering: 'all? - this was the reward? - the cry: 'I cannot help you, brother; the sword's curse is paid in your blood; and all men shall know that the battle was? ' - the saga closes the Tyrfing-biography with the brothers' mutual destruction as the weapon's last accounting.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the ethnogenesis-war doctrine and the curse's final clause. The war of Goths and Huns is the fornaldarsaga's version of the migration-age's memory: the peoples named (Goths, Huns, Reidgotaland, the Danpar/Dnieper geography) map a real frontier into heroic genealogy - the mythic charter of a Scandinavian dynasty's descent from the Goths (the saga's frame is the Mazing? - the Danish kings' Yngling-adjacent line). And the sword's accounting is the curse-cycle's completion: from Sams\xF8's mound to the Dnieper's plains, every death Tyrfing costs is a line in its biography, and the brothers' war pays the last. The figure of Hlod - the half-Hunnish brother who claims by birthright - is the saga's border-figure: the realm's own kin raised on the other side of the frontier, whose claim is legal and whose defeat is fraternal.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the dynastic-war family (the Chinese Warring States genealogies, the Irish T\xE1in's cattle-raid as people-war): each a founding-conflict narrative; the Norse one is distinctive in being armed and settled by a single cursed object."
    },
