    period: "Fafnismal c. 1000-1200; Volsunga saga 13th century; principal MS NKS 1824 b 4to (14th c.)",
    source: {
      archive: "archive.org (Morris & Magnusson 1870) / sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923)",
      work: "Fafnismal st. 32-44; Volsunga saga chs. 18-19; English: Morris & Magnusson 1870; Bellows, Poetic Edda 1923",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Morris & Magnusson 1870); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "After killing the dragon Fafnir, Regin the smith demands that Sigurd roast the heart and serve it to him. Roasting it, Sigurd touches the heart, burns his finger, and puts the finger in his mouth - and understands the speech of birds. The nuthatches in the tree counsel him: Regin plots his death; eat the heart yourself; drink the dragon's blood; take the treasure and ride away. Sigurd beheads Regin, takes the hoard, and rides the Rhine-road with the ring Andvaranaut - the curse now his. The record is the aition of prophetic speech and the trickster-smith's end: knowledge arrives by accident of appetite, and the smith who forged the plot is executed by the plot's product.",
      zh: "\u8A85\u6BBA\u5DE8\u9F8D\u6CD5\u592B\u7D0D\u4E4B\u5F8C\uFF0C\u9435\u5320\u96F7\u91D1\u8981\u6C42\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u70E4\u88FD\u9F8D\u5FC3\u5949\u4E0A\u3002\u70E4\u7099\u4E4B\u969B\uFF0C\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u4EE5\u624B\u89F8\u5FC3\uFF0C\u71D9\u50B7\u4E86\u624B\u6307\uFF0C\u4FBF\u628A\u624B\u6307\u653E\u5165\u53E3\u4E2D\u2014\u2014\u7ADF\u5F9E\u6B64\u807D\u5F97\u61C2\u9CE5\u8A9E\u3002\u6A39\u4E0A\u7684\u4E94\u96C0\u52F8\u544A\u4ED6\uFF1A\u96F7\u91D1\u6B63\u5728\u8B00\u5283\u4F60\u7684\u6B7B\u8DEF\uFF1B\u4F60\u81EA\u5DF1\u5403\u4E0B\u9019\u9846\u5FC3\u5427\uFF0C\u518D\u98F2\u9F8D\u8840\uFF0C\u53D6\u8D70\u5BF6\u85CF\uFF0C\u7B56\u99AC\u9060\u53BB\u3002\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u65AC\u4E0B\u96F7\u91D1\u9996\u7D1A\uFF0C\u596A\u5F97\u85CF\u91D1\uFF0C\u5E36\u8457\u8A5B\u5492\u6307\u74B0\u5B89\u5FB7\u74E6\u5229\u5967\u7279\u8E0F\u4E0A\u840A\u8335\u4E4B\u8DEF\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u300C\u9810\u8A00\u4E4B\u8A9E\u300D\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\uFF0C\u4E5F\u662F\u8A6D\u8A08\u9435\u5320\u7684\u7D42\u5C40\uFF1A\u77E5\u8B58\u56E0\u53E3\u8179\u4E4B\u6B32\u7684\u5076\u7136\u964D\u81E8\uFF0C\u800C\u8B00\u5283\u4E00\u5207\u9670\u8B00\u7684\u9435\u5320\uFF0C\u6700\u7D42\u6B7B\u65BC\u9670\u8B00\u4E4B\u679C\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Volsunga saga via Morris & Magnusson 1870",
    narrative: {
      en: "The scene is the Sigurd-cycle's hinge of knowledge. After the dragon-slaying, Regin - the brother who raised Sigurd for this exact purpose - drinks Fafnir's blood and demands his due: 'hew out the heart of the dragon, and roast it, and let me eat thereof'. Sigurd roasts the heart on a spit; testing it with his finger, he burns it, and sets the finger in his mouth - 'and at that? ' - the prose: 'and when the heart's blood? - the verse: 'then the heart's? - the touch of the heart's blood on his tongue gives him the speech of birds. The nuthatches (nut-breakers, the saga's 'nathratch-crew? - the poem: 'smjat? - the sparrows/nuthatches) in the tree above speak counsel: 'here sits Sigurd, dyed in the blood of Fafnir's sister-son? - the birds say: Regin plots betrayal; better that Sigurd should eat the heart himself, drink the blood, and take the treasure; 'then shall thou know the speech of all? - then wisdom shall wax in him' - the doctrine: eating the dragon is eating wisdom.\n\nSigurd's execution of the counsel is the smith's end. He beheads Regin as he sleeps - the smith who forged the whole plot (the reforged sword Gram, the pit, the plan) dies by the plot's product; the birds' last word: the hoard is the ring Andvaranaut, which shall be his death - and Sigurd loads Grani with the gold and rides the Rhine-road. The prose adds the doctrine of the fire: the dragon's glance? - no: the blood of Fafnir on his hands? - the saga: 'he washed? - he bathes in the dragon's blood, and becomes horn-proof all over but one spot (the leaf-shaped spot where a linden-leaf stuck between his shoulders - the Germanic Achilles-heel, documented here).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the knowledge-by-accident doctrine and the smith's-guilt resolution. In the Norse imagination, wisdom is a substance in bodies - dragon's heart-blood on the tongue is the same economy as Odin's mead (Kvasir's blood) and Mimir's well: knowledge is drunk, eaten, pawned - never simply had. And Sigurd's accidental acquisition (burned finger, reflex lick) marks the difference between earned wisdom (Odin's sacrifice) and received wisdom (the hero's snack) - the tradition's two knowledge-grants. The smith's death is the plot-structure's symmetry: Regin lives only for the dragon's killing, and the killing completes him - the helper-smith as the hero's disposable instrument, whose end the birds prescribe in advance.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the animal-counsel family (the Greek Halcyon? - the frame is birds advising heroes: the Chinese cranes, the Islamic Hudhud of Solomon): each a speech-to-heroes by birds; the Norse one is triggered by dragon's blood and prescribes regicide."
    },
