    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923) / archive.org (Morris & Magnusson 1870)",
      work: "Sigrdrifumal; Volsunga saga ch. 21; English: Bellows 1923; Morris & Magnusson 1870",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Morris & Magnusson 1870); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Sigurd rides to Hindarfjall and finds a hall ringed with fire-shields; inside, a armored sleeper lies as if dead. Cutting the byrnie from her back with the sword Gram, he wakes her - the valkyrie Brynhild, punished by Odin with the thorn of sleep for choosing the wrong victory. She gives him the mead of memory, and teaches the rune-lore: runes of birth, of sea, of speech, of healing, of beer, of the waves, of law-speech, of victory - cut on which objects, sung in which need. The record is the myth corpus's most complete magic-textbook, delivered by a woman whose own fate is the cycle's hinge.",
      zh: "\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u9A0E\u81F3\u6B23\u9054\u5C71\uFF0C\u898B\u5230\u5927\u5EF3\u56DB\u5468\u74B0\u7E5E\u8457\u706B\u76FE\uFF1B\u5EF3\u5167\uFF0C\u4E00\u4F4D\u62AB\u7532\u7684\u6C89\u7761\u8005\u975C\u81E5\u5982\u6B7B\u3002\u4ED6\u4EE5\u5BF6\u528D\u683C\u62C9\u59C6\u5272\u958B\u5979\u80CC\u4E0A\u7684\u7532\u80C4\uFF0C\u5C07\u5979\u559A\u9192\u2014\u2014\u90A3\u6B63\u662F\u56E0\u300C\u9078\u932F\u4E86\u52DD\u5229\u8005\u300D\u800C\u906D\u5967\u4E01\u65BD\u4EE5\u7761\u523A\u61F2\u7F70\u7684\u5973\u6B66\u795E\u5E03\u502B\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\u3002\u5979\u4EE5\u8A18\u61B6\u4E4B\u871C\u9152\u76F8\u8D08\uFF0C\u4E26\u50B3\u6388\u5982\u5C3C\u4E4B\u5B78\uFF1A\u751F\u7522\u4E4B\u7B26\u3001\u822A\u6D77\u4E4B\u7B26\u3001\u8A00\u8A9E\u4E4B\u7B26\u3001\u7642\u50B7\u4E4B\u7B26\u3001\u5564\u9152\u4E4B\u7B26\u3001\u6CE2\u6FE4\u4E4B\u7B26\u3001\u6CD5\u5EAD\u9673\u8A5E\u4E4B\u7B26\u8207\u52DD\u5229\u4E4B\u7B26\u2014\u2014\u5404\u81EA\u523B\u65BC\u4F55\u7269\uFF0C\u7528\u65BC\u4F55\u6642\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u795E\u8A71\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u6700\u5B8C\u6574\u7684\u9B54\u6CD5\u6559\u79D1\u66F8\uFF0C\u7531\u4E00\u4F4D\u81EA\u8EAB\u547D\u904B\u7E6B\u65BC\u6574\u500B\u50B3\u8AAA\u6A1E\u7D10\u7684\u5973\u5B50\u89AA\u6388\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Sigrdrifumal ('the lay of Sigrdrifa', the victory-bringer) is the Sigurd-cycle's textbook-poem, and its frame is the waking. Sigurd rides up Hindarfjall and sees a hall 'with a ring of fire about it' - shields on the fire? - 'one of which bore a? ' - the verse: 'a man lay sleeping in armor? ' - Sigurd cuts the byrnie from the sleeper with Gram, 'and the byrnie was as if grown to the flesh'? - the prose adds theOdin-thorn detail - and she wakes: 'what bit through the byrnie? who has awakened me?' She is Sigrdrifa (Brynhild), a valkyrie: 'two kings fought? - I chose victory for Agnar, the younger? - and Odin struck me with the sleep-thorn, and said I should never? - no man should win me who knew no fear' - the punished chooser, asleep on the fire-hill until the one who dares wakes her.\n\nThe teaching begins with the mead of memory: she gives Sigurd the memory-beer (minni-ol) 'mixed with might? - with song-galders and strength-runes', a cup blessed in names of gods and beings - the ritual of the bragarfull, the oath-cup, taught in detail. Then the rune-catalog, the poem's core: 'all the runes of birth (help-runes) that are needful at the childbed'; 'all the sea-runes for the rudder and the oar'; 'all the speech-runes for the tongue, that no man may? - harm you with word-wrongs'; 'all the ale-runes, that another's wife? - that witchcraft fail in the drink'; and 'the victory-runes, which must be cut on the sword-hilt and on the? - the sword's blade and named twice in Tyr's name'. The teaching names the cutting-surfaces: shield, oak? - the catalog: 'on shield, on oak-bark? - on the ear of grain? - on glass? - on gold, on amulets, on the wolf's claw, on the bear's paw? - on ice and? - on the serpent's eggs' - a full material inventory of runic practice, and the poem's instruction 'cut them, name them, think them' - the three operations of the rune-worker.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the magic-textbook doctrine and the punished-chooser frame. The rune-catalog is the corpus's most complete statement of runic praxis - birth-charms, sea-charms, speech-law, beer-purity, victory-naming - documented here as a valkyrie's curriculum, which makes the magic-system's teacher female and its content legalistic (speech-runes as courtroom protection, ale-runes as anti-witchcraft). And the waking-frame gives the teaching its irony: the woman who teaches victory-runes lost her office for choosing the wrong victory; the textbook is written by the punished. In the saga's prose, the teaching continues into love-oaths and the ring Andvaranaut - the cycle's fatal pledge - so the waking is both the magic-lesson and the engagement-scene.\n\nFor the atlas, the record anchors the magic-curriculum family (the Chinese calligraphy-magic of talismans, the Islamic hic? - the frame is taught spell-systems): each a codified magic taught by a master; the Norse one is distinctive in being taught by a punished immortal to a doomed hero, with a material inventory as its spine."
    },
    functions: [
      "Magic-textbook doctrine: the corpus's most complete runic praxis, with material inventory",
      "Legalistic magic: speech-runes for court, ale-runes against witchcraft - spellcraft as social protection",
      "Punished-chooser frame: the victory-teacher lost her office for choosing the wrong victory",
      "Oath-cup ritual: the memory-beer's blessing formula taught in full"
    ],
