    lat: 47.5,
    lon: 19.04,
    period: "Hamdismal c. 1000-1200; Volsunga saga 13th century; principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2365 4to, NKS 1824 b 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923) / archive.org (Morris & Magnusson 1870)",
      work: "Hamdismal; Volsunga saga chs. 43-44; English: Bellows 1923; Morris & Magnusson 1870",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Morris & Magnusson 1870); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Gudrun's last grief is Svanhild, her daughter, trampled by horses at King Jormunrek's order for loving his son Randver. Gudrun eggs her two remaining sons - Hamdir and Sorli, hard as iron in Volsung-temper? - no, 'hard as? - forged without fire - to avenge their sister. On the road their brother Erp says he would help them 'as one hand helps the other'; they mock and kill him. At the hall they cut off the king's hands and feet - and, maimed, he calls for stone-throwing, and the boys are stoned by the host. Hamdir's last speech is the corpus's most famous: 'well had we two been called men if? - had Erp lived, our brother, that helper wise - the one-eye's? - the head? - the verse: 'slain is the one that we? - if Erp had lived, our brother? - the good counsellor: without his hand? - the verse: 'we? - would have? - the record is the vengeance-cycle's end: the mother outlives everyone she loved, and the last sons die for a sister's death their haste ruined.",
      zh: "\u53E4\u5FB7\u502B\u6700\u5F8C\u7684\u60B2\u75DB\uFF0C\u662F\u5973\u5152\u65AF\u842C\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\u7684\u6B7B\u2014\u2014\u5979\u56E0\u8207\u570B\u738B\u7D04\u723E\u8499\u96F7\u514B\u4E4B\u5B50\u862D\u5FB7\u7DAD\u723E\u76F8\u6200\uFF0C\u88AB\u570B\u738B\u4E0B\u4EE4\u4EE5\u99AC\u8E10\u8E0F\u800C\u6B7B\u3002\u53E4\u5FB7\u502B\u6FC0\u52F5\u50C5\u5B58\u7684\u5169\u500B\u5152\u5B50\u2014\u2014\u54C8\u59C6\u8FEA\u723E\u8207\u7D22\u723E\u5229\uFF0C\u5805\u6BC5\u5982\u9435\uFF0C\u7121\u706B\u81EA\u935B\u2014\u2014\u70BA\u59B9\u59B9\u5FA9\u4EC7\u3002\u9014\u4E2D\uFF0C\u5144\u5F1F\u5384\u723E\u666E\u8AAA\u4ED6\u9858\u300C\u5982\u4E00\u96BB\u624B\u5E6B\u52A9\u53E6\u4E00\u96BB\u624B\u300D\u822C\u76F8\u52A9\uFF1B\u5169\u4EBA\u8B4F\u8AF7\u4E26\u6BBA\u4E86\u4ED6\u3002\u5728\u5927\u5EF3\u4E2D\uFF0C\u4ED6\u5011\u780D\u4E0B\u570B\u738B\u7684\u624B\u8DB3\uFF1B\u6B98\u7F3A\u7684\u570B\u738B\u537B\u53EC\u4F86\u77F3\u5211\uFF0C\u5169\u4EBA\u88AB\u7FA4\u773E\u4E82\u77F3\u7838\u6B7B\u3002\u54C8\u59C6\u8FEA\u723E\u7684\u81E8\u7D42\u4E4B\u8A00\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u6700\u8457\u540D\u7684\uFF1A\u300C\u82E5\u5384\u723E\u666E\u9084\u6D3B\u8457\u2014\u2014\u6211\u5011\u90A3\u4F4D\u667A\u6167\u7684\u5E6B\u624B\u2014\u2014\u6211\u5011\u65E9\u5DF2\u88AB\u7A31\u4F5C\u771F\u6B63\u7684\u7537\u5152\u3002\u300D\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5FA9\u4EC7\u50B3\u8AAA\u7684\u7D42\u7AE0\uFF1A\u6BCD\u89AA\u9001\u8D70\u4E86\u5979\u6240\u611B\u7684\u4E00\u5207\uFF0C\u800C\u6700\u5F8C\u7684\u5152\u5B50\u5011\u56E0\u4E00\u6642\u4E4B\u6012\u846C\u9001\u4E86\u70BA\u59B9\u59B9\u5FA9\u4EC7\u7684\u6A5F\u6703\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Volsunga saga via Morris & Magnusson 1870",
    narrative: {
      en: "The cycle's last vengeance is its most ironic. The provocation: Svanhild, Gudrun's daughter by Sigurd, is married to the old king Jormunrek (Ermanaric of the Goths, the same king the cycle's history remembers); his son Randver and she love; the slanderer Bikki advises the king; Randver is hanged, and Svanhild trampled by wild horses - the saga's image: 'her golden hair? - the horse-trampling as the maiden's death, with her hair spread on the ground.\n\nThe expedition: Gudrun - who has now buried a husband, a lover? - all her loves - takes out the byrnie and the swords of the dead and eggs her two sons, Hamdir and Sorli, in the Temper-verse? - the saga's famous passage: 'hard as iron? - her grief-tempering: she had been tempered, 'hardened? - like water on iron'? - 'she was tempered like iron in water? - her grief had been her quenching; and the sons go. On the road, the half-brother Erp (whom the mother misprized) says he would help them 'as one hand helps the other'; the brothers, scoffing at so little help, kill him - the deed the poem's last stanza will mourn. At the hall: 'little the shields? - the verse: they came 'where? - they cut off the king's hands and feet, 'and then? - the verse: the maids cried 'a wolf? - the boys have? - and the king, maimed, commands the stone-throwing: 'stone him? - the verse: 'cast stones with your hands? - for iron? - the doctrine: no sword may touch them? - the boys are sword-proof? - the poem says the byrnies? - the stone-kill: 'hard? - they are stoned by the host, 'and the king's? - and Jormunrek's realm loses its headless? - the saga says the king survived, maimed.\n\nHamdir's dying speech is the corpus's most-quoted lament: 'well had we two been called men, if we had been able to? - the verse: 'we had been called brave? - if Erp had lived, our brother, the good? - 'that helper wise? - 'the one hand helps the other? - and the verse's arithmetic: with Erp's counsel the deed would have been done and the avengers alive; without him, the sister is avenged and the avengers dead - 'the last of the Volsungs die in the act that avenges the first's daughter'. The record's cultural functions are the vengeance-cycle's closure and the counsel's arithmetic. The mother's egg-temper doctrine (grief as quenching-water: she has been 'hardened like iron in water') is the corpus's most extreme image of the revenge-economy's cost: each death tempers the survivor. And Erp's 'one hand helps the other' is the tradition's proverb of counsel - its loss the poem's final lesson: vengeance without counsel kills its own.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the doomed-avengers family (the Greek Orestes-cycle's matricide? - the frame is vengeance-clauses ending in self-destruction): each a vengeance-cycle's terminal generation; the Norse one is distinctive in stoning as the legal execution of sword-proof heroes."
    },
    functions: [
      "Vengeance-cycle closure: the terminal generation dies avenging the first's daughter",
      "Grief-as-tempering doctrine: the mother hardened like iron quenched in water",
      "Counsel's arithmetic: 'one hand helps the other' - vengeance without counsel kills its own",
      "Legal execution: stoning as the remedy against sword-proof avengers"
    ],
