      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Ragnar was cast into the snake-pit by King Aella; the shirt sent by his wife Aslaug warded the serpents, but when it was stripped the adders swarmed. In the pit he composed his death-song, stanza on stanza cataloguing the battles of his life. Each stanza ended: 'we swung our swords' - and the last: 'the little pigs would grunt now, if they knew the boar's lot.' The song reached his sons, and the invasion of England followed - the elegy was operational.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Ragnars saga lo\xF0br\xF3kar (Rafn 1829); not verbatim. / Paraphrase of Krakumal (per Hollander 1945); not verbatim."
      }
