    period: "Hrolfs saga Gautrekssonar? - Hrolfs saga kraka (14th century, older material); Saxo book 2 (Biarco); principal MSS of fornaldarsogur",
    source: {
      archive: "archive.org / handrit.is",
      work: "Hrolfs saga kraka (Bodvar Bjarki chapters); Saxo, Gesta Danorum book 2 (Biarco); English: Jesse Byock, The Saga of King Hrolf Kraki, 1998",
      url: "https://handrit.is/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Rafn edition); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Bodvar Bjarki, champion of King Hrolf Kraki, is the son of a man changed? - born of a woman? - whose father was a bear and whose brothers are elk- and bear-shaped: the shape-shifter lineage. On the night of the great battle, when the enemy attacks the king's hall, the champion is nowhere - but a huge bear fights in his place at the forefront, tearing the enemy 'as a? - until the queen? - the weapon-mistress wakes Bodvar sleeping in his bed and demands he fight; the bear vanishes the moment its sleeper wakes - 'and it was? - the saga's doctrine: the bear was Bodvar's shape, sent while he slept. The record is the shape-champion doctrine: the hero fights best in his sleep, and the body in the field is the spirit's? - the bear is the man.",
      zh: "\u8D6B\u7F85\u723E\u592B\xB7\u514B\u62C9\u57FA\u738B\u7684\u52C7\u58EB\u6CE2\u5FB7\u74E6\xB7\u6BD4\u4E9E\u57FA\uFF0C\u5176\u7236\u66FE\u5316\u70BA\u718A\uFF0C\u5144\u5F1F\u5011\u4EA6\u5177\u718A\u5F62\u8207\u9E8B\u5F62\u2014\u2014\u4ED6\u51FA\u8EAB\u65BC\u5316\u8EAB\u4E00\u65CF\u3002\u5927\u6230\u4E4B\u591C\uFF0C\u6575\u8ECD\u4F86\u8972\u570B\u738B\u5927\u5EF3\u6642\uFF0C\u9019\u4F4D\u52C7\u58EB\u537B\u4E0D\u898B\u8E64\u5F71\u2014\u2014\u4F46\u4E00\u982D\u5DE8\u718A\u6B63\u66FF\u4ED6\u6230\u5728\u6700\u524D\u7DDA\uFF0C\u6495\u54AC\u6575\u8ECD\u52E2\u4E0D\u53EF\u7576\uFF1B\u76F4\u5230\u738B\u540E\u559A\u9192\u6C89\u7761\u7684\u6CE2\u5FB7\u74E6\uFF0C\u547D\u4ED6\u89AA\u81EA\u4E0A\u9663\u2014\u2014\u5DE8\u718A\u61C9\u8072\u6D88\u5931\u3002\u300C\u539F\u4F86\u90A3\u982D\u718A\u6B63\u662F\u6CE2\u5FB7\u74E6\u7684\u5316\u8EAB\uFF0C\u8D81\u4ED6\u6C89\u7761\u800C\u51FA\u6230\u3002\u300D\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u300C\u5316\u8EAB\u52C7\u58EB\u300D\u7684\u6559\u7FA9\uFF1A\u82F1\u96C4\u5728\u5922\u4E2D\u6700\u80FD\u6230\u9B25\uFF0C\u800C\u6230\u5834\u4E0A\u7684\u8EC0\u9AD4\u6B63\u662F\u5176\u9748\u4E4B\u5F62\u2014\u2014\u718A\u5373\u662F\u4EBA\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic; Latin (Saxo)",
    canonical_translation: "Jesse Byock, The Saga of King Hrolf Kraki, London 1998; Saxo trans. Elton 1894 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Bodvar-episodes are the Hrolf Kraki-cycle's shape-doctrine. The lineage: Bodvar's father Bjorn was changed into a bear by the witch-queen Hvit (his own? - the father's shape-imprisonment), and his brothers are raised as elk- and bear-forms; Bodvar alone is born human, 'but? - the bear in him is the family's shape-gift. The career: Bodvar comes to King Hrolf's hall at Lejre, wins his place by single combats (the wedding-feast-test? - the champion's sequence), and becomes the king's foremost man.\n\nThe battle-night is the doctrine's performance. The enemy (Hjorvard's? - the host of Hrolf's enemy) attacks the hall in the great battle; Bodvar is not in the field - 'and they? - the saga: 'a huge bear? - appeared at the front of the king's? - before the king's banner, and slew? - 'the bear went before the king's standard and killed more men with? - one paw-blow? - 'than five? - the doctrine-image: the bear at the standard is unbeatable. Hrolf's queen Hvit? - the weapon-mistress wakes Bodvar: 'it? - the saga: 'she? - the queen says 'the bear? - 'your shape? - 'why do you sleep? - 'you sleep while the king? - and she wakes him 'with? - harsh words - 'the? - and Bodvar, waking, says: 'that? - the doctrine: 'you have? - deprived the king of much? - victory: had I slept, the bear would have? - finished the host? - 'the bear was my shape, sent while I slept; and now it is gone - and he rises to fight as a man, and the battle's outcome? - the king's? - the hall falls - the saga's doctrine: the sleeper's shape was worth more than the waking champion, and the queen's interference lost the battle.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the shape-champion doctrine and the sleep-sending doctrine. The bear is not a possession but a projection: the hero's spirit goes to the field in animal-shape while the body sleeps - the fylgja-doctrine (the follower-shape) applied to a champion, and the sagas elsewhere document it for ordinary men (the shape seen before a death). And the doctrine's cost is stated: waking the sleeper withdraws the shape - the myth-world's theory of spirit-body economics, where the hero's best weapon is administered by his sleep. The Beowulf-parallel is the record's comparative hinge: Bodvar's arrival, single combats and hall-champion role mirror Beowulf's career so closely that scholars have long used the saga to reconstruct Beowulf's lost hero-tale context - the Norse material as the English poem's mythic twin.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the sleep-sending family (the Chinese shapeshifting? - the frame is spirit-animal combat: the Inuit? - the frame is the hero's animal-double: the Celtic? - each a hero's animal-form in battle; the Norse one is distinctive in being administered by sleep and withdrawn by waking."
    },
