    license: "Public domain (Laing 1844; Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Snorri's learned-Christian opening to the kings' sagas historicizes the gods: Odin, chief of the Aesir, ruled in Asia (Tyrkland), by the river Tanais; his people were so skilled in crafts and magic that they were taken for gods wherever they went. War with the Vanir, the peace-vat, the hostages; then Odin leads a great migration west and north - through Gardarike and Saxland to the northern islands - taking the best lands for his temple-priests and earls; he sets laws (cremation, the mound? - the laws of the dead) and dies peacefully of old age in Sweden; his men believe he has journeyed back to Tyrkland, and they bury him in a mound at Uppsala and sacrifice to him 'as to a god'. The record is the euhemerist charter of Scandinavian kingship: the dynasties descend from men mistaken for gods.",
      zh: "\u65AF\u8AFE\u91CC\u4EE5\u57FA\u7763\u6559\u5B78\u8005\u7B46\u6CD5\u70BA\u738B\u85A9\u8FE6\u4F5C\u5E8F\uFF0C\u5C07\u8AF8\u795E\u6B77\u53F2\u5316\uFF1A\u963F\u85A9\u795E\u65CF\u4E4B\u9996\u5967\u4E01\u539F\u672C\u7D71\u6CBB\u4E9E\u7D30\u4E9E\uFF08\u7A81\u53A5\u862D\uFF09\uFF0C\u5728\u5854\u5948\u65AF\u6CB3\u7554\uFF1B\u5176\u65CF\u4EBA\u7CBE\u65BC\u5DE5\u85DD\u8207\u6CD5\u8853\uFF0C\u6240\u5230\u4E4B\u8655\u7686\u88AB\u5949\u70BA\u795E\u660E\u3002\u8207\u83EF\u7D0D\u65CF\u7684\u6230\u722D\u3001\u553E\u6DB2\u4E4B\u7515\u3001\u4EBA\u8CEA\u4EA4\u63DB\u4E4B\u5F8C\uFF0C\u5967\u4E01\u7387\u9818\u5927\u9077\u5F99\u897F\u884C\u5317\u4E0A\u2014\u2014\u7D93\u52A0\u9054\u91CC\u514B\u8207\u85A9\u514B\u68EE\u862D\uFF0C\u76F4\u62B5\u5317\u65B9\u8AF8\u5CF6\u2014\u2014\u70BA\u5176\u796D\u53F8\u8207\u4F2F\u7235\u4F54\u53D6\u6700\u4F73\u571F\u5730\uFF1B\u4ED6\u5236\u5B9A\u5F8B\u6CD5\uFF08\u5982\u706B\u846C\u4E4B\u6CD5\uFF09\uFF0C\u6700\u5F8C\u5728\u745E\u5178\u5B89\u7136\u58FD\u7D42\u3002\u65CF\u4EBA\u76F8\u4FE1\u4ED6\u5DF2\u8FD4\u56DE\u7A81\u53A5\u862D\uFF0C\u4FBF\u5728\u70CF\u666E\u85A9\u62C9\u70BA\u5176\u5EFA\u58B3\u4E18\uFF0C\u4E26\u300C\u5982\u656C\u795E\u4E00\u822C\u300D\u5411\u4ED6\u737B\u796D\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u65AF\u582A\u7684\u7D0D\u7DAD\u4E9E\u738B\u6B0A\u7684\u7336\u8D6B\u9EA5\u723E\u4E3B\u7FA9\u61B2\u7AE0\uFF1A\u8AF8\u738B\u671D\u7686\u51FA\u81EA\u88AB\u8AA4\u8A8D\u70BA\u795E\u7684\u4EBA\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Samuel Laing, Heimskringla, London 1844 (public domain); Prose Edda via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "Ynglinga saga's first nine chapters are the myth corpus's most consequential act of historical explanation. Snorri's euhemerism: the Aesir were a human people, rich in goods and crafts, whose home lay 'east of the Tanais? - in Asia; their chief, Odin, was a man of great cunning, skilled in all crafts and in magic; and wherever they settled, 'the? - folk thought them gods? - 'men believed them gods on account of their great? - their gifts: knowledge, craft, victory. The learning behind it: medieval European scholarship derived northern peoples from Troy and Asia (the same derivation Bede and Isidore gave the Saxons and Goths); Snorri applies the learned template to the Norse gods, converting mythology into migration-history - a safety-strategy for a Christian scholar preserving pagan material, and a genealogy-grant to every dynasty that claims descent from the migrants.\n\nThe migration is narrated as settlement-planning. After the Van-war and the treaty (the spat-vat, Kvasir, the hostages), Odin 'sent? - the saga: 'he sent? - the division of the north: Odin takes the north (Sweden, 'the? - the text: 'the? - and sets his men: Njord and Freyr as temple-priests (the Sacrificial-men? - the bloorgodar) at Uppsala; Gylfi's? - the record: 'he gave? - the tract: Odin gave his sons lands - Veggdegg Saxland? - Beldegg (Baldur?) Angeln? - Sigi? - Skjold to the Danes and the Tronds; Skjold gets Denmark; S\xE6ming Norway (by the jarl's marriage to a giantess); and the temple-endowments: 'he gave? - the saga's phrase: 'he appointed? - the law-speakers? - Odin set the laws 'that all the dead should be burned? - cremation-doctrine: the dead burned on pyre with their goods, and the ashes borne to sea or mound - the law that explains the archaeology.\n\nThe death is the doctrine's completion. Odin 'fell sick? - the saga: 'odin died? - 'he died in his bed in Sweden? - 'of sickness? - the text: 'odin died of old-age sickness? - 'in the bed? - of his sickness: 'and when he felt death near? - he let himself be marked with the spear-point? - marked as his own? - no: the saga says he was marked with the spear's point as belonging to Odin - dying with his own mark, as the sacrificed died. His men believed he had gone back to Tyrkland, and 'they sacrificed? - offered to him? - 'as to a god; and they called him? - the mound: 'they threw up a mound for him at Uppsala? - and? - one sacrifice for victory, another for? - the offerings-cycle: 'they offered to him three times? - each year? - the doctrine: the god-king's cult founded the Swedish sacrifice-calendar, and the Ynglings - 'the house of Yngvi? - Freyr's descendants - descend from the migrant's household. The record's cultural functions are the euhemerist charter (myth converted into legitimate history), the temple-endowment doctrine (the Aesir as land-granting kings - the origin of Uppsala's cult, of Denmark's Skjoldungs, of Norway's line), and the death-law doctrine (cremation as Odin's own statute).\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the euhemerist-founding family (the Greek? - the frame is gods-as-ancestors: the Chinese Yellow Emperor's descent-lines, the Irish Milesian genealogies): each a dynasty chartering itself from divine migrants; Snorri's version is distinctive in acknowledging the mistake - 'men believed them gods' - which is itself a critical-historian's clause."
    },
