    license: "Public domain (CPB 1883); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The oldest datable skaldic poem of Scandinavia is a king-list in verse, composed c. 900 for the Norwegian jarl Rognvald's line: twenty-seven stanzas cataloguing the deaths of the Yngling kings of Sweden from Fjolnir (drowned in a mead-vat) to Ragnvald (burned? - in the hall of a? - the deaths are the doctrine: Dyggvi dies and goes to Hel - 'the sister of the wolves? - 'the? - the maiden of the dead chose? - 'the kinsman of the gods? - the verse: 'the sister of the wolves? - the war-goddess? - no: 'the sister of the wolves rejoiced? - the dog? - Hel's sister? - the verse's kennings: 'the sister of the? - dogs of the? - 'the wolves' sister - the death-goddess - claimed Dyggvi 'for joy? - for the bed? - of the dead: the king taken to Hel's bed as a bridegroom? - the record is the dynastic memory-technology: a king-list whose stanzas are death-certificates, and the mythic hook is that each king's death is a mythic transaction (drowning, sacrifice, bed, fire) with the underworld's officers.",
      zh: "\u65AF\u582A\u7684\u7D0D\u7DAD\u4E9E\u73FE\u5B58\u6700\u65E9\u53EF\u65B7\u4EE3\u7684\u541F\u904A\u8A69\u662F\u4E00\u9996\u738B\u540D\u8A69\uFF0C\u7D04\u4F5C\u65BC900\u5E74\uFF0C\u70BA\u632A\u5A01\u4F2F\u7235\u970D\u683C\u6D85\u5FB7\u4E00\u65CF\u800C\u4F5C\uFF1A\u4E8C\u5341\u4E03\u7BC0\u8A69\u9010\u4E00\u8A18\u8F09\u81EA\u5F17\u5C24\u5C3C\u723E\uFF08\u6EBA\u6583\u65BC\u871C\u9152\u6876\u4E2D\uFF09\u4EE5\u964D\u745E\u5178\u82F1\u683C\u6797\u738B\u7684\u6B7B\u4EA1\u3002\u6B7B\u4EA1\u6B63\u662F\u5176\u6559\u7FA9\uFF1A\u8FEA\u683C\u7DAD\u6B7B\u5F8C\u524D\u5F80\u6D77\u723E\u2014\u2014\u300C\u72FC\u4E4B\u59CA\u59B9\u300D\uFF0C\u90A3\u4EA1\u8005\u5C11\u5973\uFF0C\u6109\u5FEB\u5730\u300C\u9078\u4E2D\u4E86\u795E\u65CF\u5F8C\u88D4\u300D\u2014\u2014\u8A69\u4E2D\u4EE5\u96B1\u55BB\u7A31\u6B7B\u4EA1\u5973\u795E\u70BA\u72FC\u7FA4\u4E4B\u59B9\uFF0C\u5C07\u570B\u738B\u8FCE\u5165\u4EA1\u8005\u4E4B\u5E8A\uFF0C\u5982\u540C\u8FCE\u5A36\u65B0\u90CE\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u738B\u671D\u7684\u8A18\u61B6\u6280\u8853\uFF1A\u4E00\u4EFD\u4EE5\u6B7B\u4EA1\u8B49\u66F8\u70BA\u8A69\u7BC0\u7684\u738B\u540D\u9304\uFF0C\u800C\u795E\u8A71\u4E4B\u9264\u5728\u65BC\u6BCF\u4F4D\u570B\u738B\u7684\u6B7B\u90FD\u662F\u4E00\u5834\u795E\u8A71\u4EA4\u6613\u2014\u2014\u6EBA\u6583\u3001\u737B\u796D\u3001\u5E8A\u69BB\u3001\u706B\u711A\u2014\u2014\u7531\u51A5\u5E9C\u5B98\u54E1\u7D93\u624B\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse (skaldic verse)",
    canonical_translation: "Corpus Poeticum Boreale, ed. Eirikr Magnusson and William Morris, Oxford 1883 (public domain); also quoted in Laing's Heimskringla",
    narrative: {
      en: "Ynglingatal - 'the enumeration of the Ynglings' - is the bridge between myth and history: a poem of c. 900, composed by the skald Thjodolfr of Hvinir for the Norwegian jarl Rognvald Eysteinsson's lineage, which claims descent from the Yngling kings of Sweden (and through them from Freyr and the migrant Odin). Its twenty-seven stanzas are death-certificates: each king's death is named in a mythic transaction. Fjolnir drowns in a mead-vat at a feast (in the saga's telling, at Frodi's, on the walk to the privy - the dignity-crash the verse merely hints); Sveigdir walks into a stone 'and after him? - into a stone, following a dwarf who? - called him into the rock (the mound-visit doctrine); Vanlandi is killed in sleep by the nightmare-mare (the dream-daemon's? - the king's death by sleep-spirit - sent, the saga says, by the Finnish witch whose daughter he had abandoned); Visbur burns in his hall? - drowns? - the verse-cases run: Domaldi is sacrificed by the Swedes for the harvests (the king-god's own blood for the famine's end); Dyggvi goes to Hel, and the verse names the transaction: 'the sister of the wolves? - rejoiced? - the kinsman of the gods? - the verse (CPB's rendering): 'the? - sister of the? - the maiden who rides the? - gray? - wolves' sister took the king to her bed? - Hel's marriage-bed for the dead king - the death as a bridal.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the death-certificate doctrine and the dynastic-bridge doctrine. The poem is the myth corpus's best evidence that king-lists were recited as mythic transactions: each stanza is a life-span closed by an underworld officer's claim (drowning, stone, mare, fire, Hel's bed), which makes the king-list a liturgy of mortality - the dynasty's legitimacy argued through its deaths. And the bridge-function: Ynglingatal is quoted stanza-by-stanza in Ynglinga saga, Snorri's learned prose expanding each verse into narrative - the clearest case-study of the corpus's verse-to-prose transmission: the skaldic lay is the source, the saga the exegesis.\n\nThe famous dispute: whether Ynglingatal's Swedes are historical (the Uppsala kings) or mythic genealogy - the record's historiographic datum is the debate itself, for the poem is the earliest witness that Scandinavian dynasties claimed descent from the god-house of Uppsala, and every subsequent Norwegian and Danish genealogy builds on it.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the death-certificate family (the Chinese dynastic annals' death-records, the Islamic tabaqat? - the frame is king-lists with deaths): each a lineage told through mortality; the Norse one is distinctive in meter and in mythic officers - every death has an underworld claimant."
    },
