    summary: {
      en: "The Danish dynasty's founder is Skjold (Shield), son of Odin - or, in the famous Beowulf parallel, Scyld Scefing, the foundling who arrives alone in a boat laden with weapons and treasure, grows to rule, and at death is laid back in the boat with treasure and sails out to no one knows where. Saxo's version: the brothers Dan and Angul name the people; the progenitor Lame? - the record is the Danish ethnogenesis charter: the shield-founder, the boat-baby, the name-brothers, and the genealogical chain from Odin to the historical kings.",
      zh: "\u4E39\u9EA5\u738B\u671D\u7684\u958B\u5275\u8005\u540D\u70BA\u65AF\u514B\u7D04\u723E\u5FB7\uFF08\u76FE\uFF09\uFF0C\u4E43\u5967\u4E01\u4E4B\u5B50\u2014\u2014\u6216\u5982\u300A\u8C9D\u5967\u6B66\u752B\u300B\u8457\u540D\u5E73\u884C\u6587\u672C\u6240\u8A00\uFF0C\u662F\u540D\u70BA\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\xB7\u5E0C\u5F17\u5BE7\uFF08Scyld Scefing\uFF09\u7684\u68C4\u5B30\uFF1A\u7368\u81EA\u4E58\u4E00\u8449\u8F09\u6EFF\u5175\u5203\u8207\u8CA1\u5BF6\u7684\u5C0F\u821F\u800C\u4F86\uFF0C\u9577\u6210\u70BA\u738B\uFF0C\u6B7B\u6642\u53C8\u88AB\u653E\u56DE\u821F\u4E2D\uFF0C\u96A8\u5BF6\u7269\u6F02\u5411\u7121\u4EBA\u77E5\u66C9\u4E4B\u5730\u3002\u85A9\u514B\u7D22\u7248\u672C\uFF1A\u5144\u5F1F\u4E39\u8207\u5B89\u53E4\u723E\u70BA\u6C11\u65CF\u547D\u540D\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u4E39\u9EA5\u65CF\u6E90\u61B2\u7AE0\uFF1A\u76FE\u4E4B\u958B\u5275\u8005\u3001\u821F\u4E2D\u4E4B\u5B30\u3001\u547D\u540D\u5144\u5F1F\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u81EA\u5967\u4E01\u81F3\u53F2\u5BE6\u541B\u738B\u7684\u5BB6\u8B5C\u93C8\u3002"
    },
    language: "Latin (Saxo); Old Norse (Skjoldunga fragments); Old English (Beowulf parallel)",
    canonical_translation: "Oliver Elton, The First Nine Books of the Danish History, London 1894 (public domain); Arngrimur Jonsson, Rerum Danicarum Fragmenta, 1596",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Danish founding-myth survives in fragments across four traditions. The genealogical charter: Snorri's Skaldskaparmal lists Skjold as Odin's son, given Denmark in the migration-division (Ynglinga saga's grant), and the dynasty named Skjoldungs for him; Arngrimur Jonsson's 1596 Latin epitome preserves the lost Skjoldunga saga's king-list (the saga burned? - lost, the only Old Norse chronicle of the early Danes); Saxo gives the brothers Dan and Angul, 'by whom? - the text: 'the Danes and the Angles? - took their names - the ethnonym-aition: the people named for the brothers; and the record's most famous parallel: Beowulf's Scyld Scefing - the foundling in the boat, 'a little? - the verse: 'of old? - a little boat? - the baby alone with a sheaf? - the vessel 'laden with weapons? - and treasure, who grows to rule the neighboring tribes 'till? - 'every? - and at his death: 'they laid him in the boat? - with rings and? - 'the? - treasure? - and 'they let the water? - bear him away, 'and no man? - knows who received that load - the death-sailing.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the ethnonym-aition doctrine and the boat-founder doctrine. Dan and Angul are the charter of two peoples (Danes, Angles - the English people's own claimed ancestry), so the Danish founding-myth doubles as the origin-story of England's colonizers - a genealogical bridge the Anglo-Norman? - the medieval genealogists exploited. And the boat-founder (Scyld/Skjold's name means 'shield' - the defense of the people) is the myth-image of leadership arriving from the sea and leaving by it: the founder with no parents (scefing - 'with a sheaf' or 'of the? - the sheaf-born? - the corn-child? - the grain-fertility reading) whose death returns him to the water that brought him - the circle-of-the-sea doctrine: the founder is the sea's loan, repaid at death.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the founder-in-a-boat family (the Chinese? - the frame is waterborne founders: the Moses-basket of the Hebrew record, the Japanese? - each a founder arriving or departing by water; the Danish one is distinctive in being both - arriving and departing by the same tide.\n\nThe four-tradition survival is the record's critical datum: Skjoldunga saga (lost, fragments), Saxo's Latin history, Snorri's land-grants and Beowulf's boat-baby preserve one founding-doctrine in four genres - the founding-myth's cross-corpus circulation documented from Old English to Old Norse to Latin. And the boat-founder's doctrine completes the profile: the founder with no parents (the sheaf-born corn-child on the strongest reading) whose death returns him to the water that brought him - the sea's loan repaid, the founder as the people's shield whose name is the dynasty's. The record thus anchors the Danish founding family: the shield-king, the name-brothers and the boat-baby as one nation's charter."
    },
