    summary: {
      en: "Saxo's Danish history begins with an ethnonym-aition: Humble? - the record is the charter of the Danish nation: the brothers Dan and Angul, 'whose names? - 'from whom the Danes and the Angles? - took their names; Dan rescues? - the record is the founding-charter in Saxo's Latin: the brothers as progenitors, Dan as the people's name-father and king-making? - the ethnogenesis doctrine: a nation named for a man, a neighbor-nation named for his brother.",
      zh: "\u85A9\u514B\u7D22\u7684\u4E39\u9EA5\u53F2\u4EE5\u4E00\u5247\u65CF\u540D\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\u958B\u7BC7\uFF1A\u9019\u662F\u4E39\u9EA5\u6C11\u65CF\u7684\u61B2\u7AE0\u2014\u2014\u5144\u5F1F\u4E39\u8207\u5B89\u53E4\u723E\uFF0C\u300C\u4E39\u9EA5\u4EBA\u8207\u76CE\u683C\u9B6F\u4EBA\u7686\u5F97\u540D\u65BC\u4ED6\u5011\u300D\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u85A9\u514B\u7D22\u62C9\u4E01\u6587\u7B46\u4E0B\u7684\u958B\u570B\u61B2\u7AE0\uFF1A\u5144\u5F1F\u70BA\u5148\u7956\uFF0C\u4E39\u4F5C\u70BA\u6C11\u65CF\u7684\u547D\u540D\u4E4B\u7236\u8207\u738B\u6B0A\u7DE0\u9020\u8005\u2014\u2014\u65CF\u6E90\u8AD6\u4E4B\u6559\u7FA9\uFF1A\u4E00\u500B\u6C11\u65CF\u4EE5\u4E00\u4EBA\u547D\u540D\uFF0C\u53E6\u4E00\u9130\u65CF\u5247\u4EE5\u5176\u5144\u5F1F\u547D\u540D\u3002"
    },
    language: "Latin",
    canonical_translation: "Oliver Elton, The First Nine Books of the Danish History, London 1894 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Saxo's prologue to the Danish history is an ethnogenesis-charter in learned Latin dress. The progenitors: Humble (Humli?) and Lother? - the brothers of the record are Dan and Angul, 'the sons? - of Humble? - Saxo's text: 'Dan and Angul, whose father was? - Humble? - 'two sons of Humble? - and the aition: 'from them the Danes and the Angles? - 'the Danes and Angles took their names - the doctrine: a nation is the name of its founder's people, and the founder's brother names the neighbor-nation. Dan's career in Saxo: the name-father who wins the realm by? - 'he? - the record's image: Dan 'won by valor? - the crown and held it; Angul's posterity are the Angles, who (in the learned medieval derivation) passed to Britain - the Danish charter doubling as England's origin-claim.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the name-charter doctrine and the two-peoples doctrine. The ethnonym-aition is the myth corpus's version of the universal naming-charter (the Israel? - the frame: peoples named for eponymous ancestors - the Greek Hellen's sons, the Israelite tribes); Saxo's contribution is the sibling-split: one progenitor, two nations, the frontier drawn inside the family - the myth-image of the Danish-Anglian kinship that the migration-age made historical. And the record's critical-historical datum: Saxo writes in Christian learned Latin, converting the Danish national myth into the genre of Roman origo-gentis - the gens' founding-book - so the Norse material here survives in the European historiographic genre of the people-book.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the eponym-founders family (Hellen's sons for the Greek peoples, the Chinese Yellow Emperor's lines, the Islamic Isma'il? - the frame is eponymous ancestry): each a people named for an ancestor; the Danish one is distinctive in the sibling-pair and its English corollary.\n\nThe charter's genealogical afterlife is the record's own: the Danish kings' descent-claims (through Dan) run into Saxo's royal list and into the medieval genealogies that connected the Danes to the Angles of England - one founding-brother pair serving two nations' legitimacy. And Saxo's learned frame completes the doctrine: the origo-gentis genre (the Roman people-book) gives the Danish myth its European historiographic form, the nation's charter written in the empire's own genre. The record thus anchors the eponym-founder family: the people as the founder's name, the neighbor-nation as the brother's - the frontier drawn inside the founding family, documented in Latin for a European audience.\n\nThe two-brothers doctrine's ethnic logic completes the charter: the frontier between the Danes and the Angles is drawn inside the founding family, so kinship and division are one genealogy - the myth-world's theory of neighboring peoples as siblings whose boundary is a name. And the record's reception completes the document: the Angles' British migration (the historical settlement of England) made the charter's corollary geopolitical, and the medieval genealogists exploited it - the Danish founding-book serving as England's origin-claim as well. The record thus anchors the eponym-founders family: Hellen's sons for the Greek peoples, the Yellow Emperor's lines for the Chinese, Dan and Angul for the north - each a people named for an ancestor, distinctive in the sibling-pair."
    },
