    lat: 59.5,
    lon: 16.5,
    period: "Gylfaginning ch. 1 c. 1220-1230 (older material); Ynglinga saga ch. 5; Skaldic: Bragi's Ragnarsdrapa? - Gefjon verses; principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2367 4to, AM 41 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "Project Gutenberg (Brodeur 1916) / sacred-texts.com (Laing 1844)",
      work: "Snorri, Gylfaginning ch. 1 (Gefjon); Ynglinga saga ch. 5; skaldic Gefjon-verses; critical ed. Faulkes (1987)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/pre/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Laing 1844); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Gefjon, a goddess in Odin's retinue, comes as a wandering woman to King Gylfi of Sweden and is granted as much land as four oxen can plough in a day and a night. Her oxen are her four giant-sons in ox-shape; they pull so hard that the plough cuts the land out whole, and she hauls it into the sea and sets it - and it is called Zealand, and the pit where the land was torn out is Lake Malaren, 'with headlands? - the bays? - shaped as the island's. The record is the aition of Denmark's biggest island and Sweden's biggest lake, and the charter of the Sweden-Denmark border: the sea between them is a ploughed-out absence.",
      zh: "\u5967\u4E01\u96A8\u5F9E\u4E2D\u7684\u5973\u795E\u683C\u8299\u55AC\u88DD\u6D41\u6D6A\u5973\u5B50\u4F86\u5230\u745E\u5178\u738B\u5C45\u723E\u83F2\u8655\uFF0C\u7372\u8CDC\u300C\u56DB\u982D\u725B\u4E00\u665D\u591C\u6240\u80FD\u7281\u51FA\u300D\u7684\u571F\u5730\u3002\u5979\u7684\u725B\u6B63\u662F\u56DB\u500B\u5316\u8EAB\u70BA\u725B\u7684\u5DE8\u4EBA\u5152\u5B50\uFF1B\u7281\u529B\u4E4B\u5927\uFF0C\u7ADF\u5C07\u6574\u584A\u571F\u5730\u5B8C\u6574\u7281\u8D77\uFF0C\u62D6\u5165\u6D77\u4E2D\u5B89\u7F6E\u2014\u2014\u662F\u70BA\u897F\u862D\u5CF6\uFF1B\u800C\u571F\u5730\u88AB\u7281\u8D70\u7684\u5751\u4FBF\u662F\u6885\u62C9\u502B\u6E56\uFF0C\u6E56\u7063\u7684\u5F62\u72C0\u6070\u5982\u5CF6\u4E0A\u5CFD\u7063\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u65E2\u662F\u4E39\u9EA5\u6700\u5927\u5CF6\u5DBC\u8207\u745E\u5178\u6700\u5927\u6E56\u6CCA\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\u6545\u4E8B\uFF0C\u4E5F\u662F\u745E\u5178\u2014\u4E39\u9EA5\u908A\u754C\u7684\u61B2\u7AE0\uFF1A\u5169\u8005\u4E4B\u9593\u7684\u6D77\uFF0C\u6B63\u662F\u4E00\u7247\u88AB\u7281\u8D70\u7684\u7A7A\u7F3A\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain); Ynglinga saga via Laing 1844",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Gefjon-myth is the myth corpus's most literal founding-geography. The frame: Gylfi, the Swedish king of the Gylfaginning frame-story, had (the prose says) promised a wandering woman as much land as four oxen could plough in a day and a night; she is Gefjon, of the Aesir's retinue; her oxen are her four sons by a giant, in ox-shape; the plough 'cut? - so hard that the land was 'loosened? - torn up? - 'and drawn out into the sea; and it 'lay? - westward? - in the sea - 'and it is called Zealand? - 'selund? - the text: 'where she set the land, it is called Zealand'; and 'where the land had been taken from, there is now a lake? - the sea? - the lake: 'the lake is called Loginn? - Malaren (Logrinn, 'the lake'), 'and the bays of the lake? - 'the? - its inlets correspond to the headlands of Zealand - the cartographic correspondence the myth asserts: lake-arms and island-coasts fit each other, as a torn piece fits its hole.\n\nThe skaldic evidence: Bragi's Ragnarsdrapa? - the shield-poem tradition has Gefjon-verses (Bragi's lausavisu on Gefjon? - the skaldic fragments name her as plougher of the island - the myth's court-currency. Snorri's prose adds the etymology: 'Gefjun? - 'she is called? - the goddess of ploughing? - her name connects to 'gift' (gefa, to give) - the generous goddess, whose land-gift is also a land-theft (the ploughed-out piece is Sweden's loss) - the myth-world's border-treaty: Denmark's gain is Sweden's hole.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the cartographic-aition doctrine and the border-charter doctrine. The myth reads the map as narrative: the lake's shape matching the island's is the observation the myth encodes, and the plough is the explanation - a founding-geography as exact as any Greek aition. And the border-charter: the Sweden-Denmark strait is the myth's plough-line - the sea between the kingdoms is not natural but engineered, made by a goddess's labor-contract with a king who guessed wrong about the price. In the atlas, the record anchors the plough-founders family (the Chinese? - the frame is ploughed or carved land: the Greek? - each a founder shaping territory; the Norse one is distinctive in measuring the grant by a day-and-night of oxen and in the cartographic fit.\n\nThe myth's cartographic claim is its critical datum: the lake's inlets matching the island's coasts is an observable geography (Malaren's arms and Zealand's fjords), and the myth encodes the observation as narrative - the founding-geography as map-reading. And the skaldic currency completes the record: Bragi's Gefjon-verses and the kenning-system's citations (Gefjon as the plougher of the island) evidence the myth's court-currency centuries before Snorri, so the doctrine is attested in verse, prose and place-name. The record thus anchors the border-charter family: the Sweden-Denmark strait as a ploughed-out absence, the two kingdoms' frontier written as a labor-contract that went wrong."
    },
