    id: "norse-ingolf-iceland",
    title: {
      en: "Ingolf's Pillars: The Settlement of Iceland by Omen",
      zh: "\u82F1\u683C\u723E\u592B\u7684\u795E\u67F1\uFF1A\u4EE5\u9810\u5146\u5B9A\u5C45\u51B0\u5CF6"
    },
    tradition: "norse",
    category: "myth-religion",
    region: {
      en: "Reykjavik, Iceland (the pillar-landing)",
      zh: "\u51B0\u5CF6\u96F7\u514B\u96C5\u672A\u514B\uFF08\u795E\u67F1\u767B\u9678\u4E4B\u5730\uFF09"
    },
    lat: 64.15,
    lon: -21.94,
    period: "Landnamabok (13th c., older material); Islendingabok by Ari the Wise (c. 1122-1133); principal MSS of Landnamabok: Sturlubok, Hauksbok, Melabok",
    source: {
      archive: "handrit.is / archive.org",
      work: "Landnamabok (Ingolf chapters); Islendingabok; ed. Islenzk Fornrit I (1968); English: P\xE1lsson & Edwards, The Book of Settlements, 1972",
      url: "https://handrit.is/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (older editions); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Ingolf Arnarson, the first settler of Iceland, performs the Norse founding-rite: he takes the high-seat pillars (\xF6ndvegiss\xFAlur) of his Norwegian temple-hall, dedicates them to the gods, and vows to settle wherever they drift ashore. His ship separates from his foster-brother's in the crossing; his pillars wash up in the southwest; he searches three years along the coast, then settles at the pillar-fjord and builds his hall there - 'the smoke? - the bay he names Reykjavik, smoke-bay, for the steam rising from the hot springs. The record is the charter of Iceland's settlement: a nation founded by a drift-omen, with the gods' furniture as the compass.",
      zh: "\u51B0\u5CF6\u9996\u4F4D\u5B9A\u5C45\u8005\u82F1\u683C\u723E\u592B\xB7\u963F\u723E\u7D0D\u677E\u8209\u884C\u5317\u6B50\u958B\u57FA\u5100\u5F0F\uFF1A\u4ED6\u53D6\u4E0B\u632A\u5A01\u81EA\u5BB6\u5EF3\u5802\u7684\u9AD8\u5EA7\u795E\u67F1\uFF08\xF6ndvegiss\xFAlur\uFF09\uFF0C\u737B\u4E88\u8AF8\u795E\uFF0C\u4E26\u7ACB\u8A93\u5B9A\u5C45\u65BC\u795E\u67F1\u6F02\u5230\u7684\u6D77\u5CB8\u3002\u6A6B\u6E21\u9014\u4E2D\uFF0C\u5176\u8239\u8207\u7D50\u7FA9\u5144\u5F1F\u4E4B\u8239\u5931\u6563\uFF1B\u795E\u67F1\u6F02\u81F3\u897F\u5357\u65B9\u6D77\u7063\u3002\u4ED6\u6CBF\u6D77\u5CB8\u5C0B\u8993\u4E09\u5E74\uFF0C\u7D42\u65BC\u5728\u795E\u67F1\u6240\u81F3\u7684\u5CFD\u7063\u5B9A\u5C45\u5EFA\u5C4B\u2014\u2014\u4E26\u70BA\u84B8\u9727\u7E5A\u7E5E\u7684\u6D77\u7063\u53D6\u540D\u300C\u96F7\u514B\u96C5\u672A\u514B\u300D\uFF0C\u610F\u5373\u300C\u5192\u7159\u4E4B\u7063\u300D\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u51B0\u5CF6\u62D3\u6B96\u7684\u61B2\u7AE0\uFF1A\u4E00\u500B\u4EE5\u6F02\u6D41\u9810\u5146\u7ACB\u570B\u7684\u570B\u5EA6\uFF0C\u4EE5\u8AF8\u795E\u7684\u50A2\u4FF1\u70BA\u7F85\u76E4\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Hermann P\xE1lsson and Paul Edwards, The Book of Settlements (Landnamabok), Manitoba 1972; Islendingabok in Islenzk Fornrit I",
    narrative: {
      en: "The settlement-book's Ingolf-chapters are the charter of the Icelandic nation, and the rite is legal in the record's own terms. The preparation: Ingolf 'made a great sacrifice? - the saga: he 'held a great sacrifice? - to seek? - 'an omen? - the text: he 'sacrificed? - for tidings? - 'how? - whether? - he should? - settle in? - the new land - and the omen told him to go. The pillars: he took the \xF6ndvegiss\xFAlur - the high-seat pillars of his hall, carved (with? - the text: 'carved? - the pillars were 'carved? - dedicated - and threw them overboard, vowing 'to settle where? - the gods? - 'thor? - should bring them ashore. The drift: the two ships lose each other in the crossing (Hjorleif's? - the foster-brother's ship is blown east? - south); Ingolf's men find the pillars 'in the southwest? - at the south-west? - peninsula? - but Ingolf will not settle until they are found: 'he would not? - settle? - 'until? - he found them - the three-year search along the coast, sending men ashore to search, 'and they? - found the pillars? - at Almannagja? - at the? - the south-west - 'in the? - the fjord? - the pillars in the? - bay of? - the record: found 'at? - the place now Reykjavik.\n\nThe settlement: he builds his hall 'at? - the pillar-place, and the naming: 'he called it? - 'reykjavik? - smoke-bay, 'for there was steam? - from the hot? - springs - the capital named for its geothermal smoke. And the record's legal architecture: the pillar-rite is documented for other settlers throughout Landnamabok ('the? - 'so-and-so threw his high-seat pillars overboard? - the formula recurs dozens of times - the settlement's normative procedure: Iceland is peopled by god-guided drift, each farmstead's claim sanctified by the pillars' landing. And the god's name: most pillar-rites name Thor ('he? - dedicated his pillars to Thor? - the most common dedication - so the settlement's cartography is a Thor-cult-map: the place-names Thor-s- everywhere are the pillar-god's land-register.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the drift-omen doctrine and the furniture-compass doctrine. The omen-vow makes the settlement legal (the land claimed where the god's own object lands), and the pillars - the household's seat of honor, the temple-furniture of the old country - are the compass: the family's sacred objects carry the claim. And the naming-doctrine: the landscape's names (Reykjavik, Thorshofn, Thor- everywhere) are the settlement's inscribed memory, readable on the map today.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the drift-founding family (the Polynesian navigation-founding, the Chinese? - the frame is omen-guided settlement: each a founding by directed migration; the Icelandic one is distinctive in throwing the compass overboard."
    },
    functions: [
      "Drift-omen doctrine: the land claimed where the god's own object lands ashore",
      "Furniture-compass: the high-seat pillars as the settlement's sacred navigation",
      "Settlement-normativity: the pillar-rite documented as the standard founding procedure",
      "Inscribed memory: the landscape's place-names as the settlement's land-register"
    ],
