    language: "Latin",
    canonical_translation: "Francis J. Tschan, Adam of Bremen: History of the Archbishops of Hamburg-Bremen, New York 1959 (paraphrased); critical ed. Bernhard Schmeidler, 1917",
    narrative: {
      en: "Adam's Uppsala-chapter is the single most-quoted paragraph in the study of Norse religion, and its critical handling is itself the record's lesson. The description: 'the Swedes have? - a temple 'very famous? - called Ubsola? - Uppsala, 'not far from? - Sigtuna? - Birka? - 'the city of the? - Sigtuna; the temple 'entirely adorned? - 'built of gold? - the text's phrasing: 'totum auro? - 'all golden? - 'in it stand three images? - the triad: 'Thor, the mightiest, sits in the middle, in a? - throne; Wodan and Fricco on either side; 'and their offices? - 'Thor, they say, rules the air? - thunder, lightning, winds, rains, fair weather, crops; Wodan, that is 'fury? - war, and gives? - courage? - in war; Fricco 'bestows? - peace and? - pleasure? - 'fruits? - 'the priesthood: 'each image has its? - priests? - and offerings; 'in famine? - plague? - they sacrifice? - to the? - images.\n\nThe sacrifice: 'nine males? - of every? - male? - living species? - the famous clause: 'it is customary to offer nine heads? - males of every male species? - 'with the bodies? - hung? - in the grove? - that borders? - the temple; 'the grove? - so sacred that? - each tree? - is divine? - 'every tree in it is held? - sacred, by? - the deaths? - of the sacrificed; 'there are? - dogs? - and horses? - and men? - hanging? - side by? - side. And the golden chain: 'a chain? - of gold? - rings the? - temple's? - gable? - 'which? - shines? - 'visible? - to those? - approaching? - far? - off, because the? - temple stands on a plain? - surrounded by mountains? - 'like a? - theater.\n\nThe nine-year feast: 'a general? - sacrifice of nine? - years? - 'at which all? - come? - 'the kings? - and the? - people? - 'all? - 'and no one? - spares? - 'the Christians? - are? - 'the only exception? - 'they? - buy themselves out? - ransom themselves from the sacrifices. Adam's informants are Danish (he had never seen Uppsala), and his chapter's mixture of report and polemic - 'the wretched? - priest? - Olof? - 'the? - is the record's critical lesson: the best-documented Norse cult-site is documented by its enemy's church, and the record's value is in the correspondence (Thor-cult, Freyr-phallus, grove-sacrifice, nine-year cycle confirmed independently by Snorri and the sagas) rather than in the gold.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the outsider's-temple family (the Chinese Jesuit reports on Chinese temples? - the frame is Christian/Muslim descriptions of other cults): each a cult-site documented by its opponents; the Uppsala one is distinctive in its independent corroboration."
    },
