    lat: 59.9,
    lon: 17.64,
    period: "Ynglinga saga chs. 15-18 c. 1225 (Ynglingatal verse c. 900); Adam of Bremen 4.27 (c. 1070s); H\xE1konar saga parallel",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Laing 1844) / archive.org (Adam of Bremen)",
      work: "Snorri, Ynglinga saga chs. 15-18 (Domaldi); Adam of Bremen 4.27; English: Laing 1844",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/heim/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Laing 1844); Adam passages are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The Swedish kings' greatest office was the harvest, and the price was their blood. King Domaldi reigns in famine: first they sacrifice cattle, then men, and the famine worsens; the assembly resolves 'that they should? - sacrifice their king? - 'for? - plenty? - the king's own blood for the seasons; they take Domaldi - the saga's image: 'they reddened? - the altars? - 'red? - with his blood - and the harvests return. The nine-year cycle: Adam of Bremen's parallel clause, the great feast at Uppsala every nine years, all come; the record is the corpus's most explicit kingship-for-harvest transaction: the god-king's blood as the price of the crops.",
      zh: "\u745E\u5178\u8AF8\u738B\u6700\u5927\u7684\u8077\u8CAC\u662F\u8C50\u6536\uFF0C\u800C\u4EE3\u50F9\u662F\u4ED6\u5011\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u8840\u3002\u591A\u99AC\u723E\u8FEA\u738B\u5728\u4F4D\u6642\u9B27\u9951\u8352\uFF1A\u570B\u6C11\u5148\u737B\u796D\u725B\u7FA4\uFF0C\u5F8C\u737B\u796D\u4EBA\uFF0C\u707D\u60C5\u53CD\u800C\u52A0\u91CD\uFF1B\u8B70\u6703\u9042\u6C7A\u5B9A\u300C\u4EE5\u570B\u738B\u70BA\u796D\uFF0C\u63DB\u53D6\u8C50\u5E74\u300D\u2014\u2014\u570B\u738B\u4E4B\u8840\u5373\u5B63\u7BC0\u4E4B\u50F9\u3002\u4ED6\u5011\u6BBA\u6B7B\u591A\u99AC\u723E\u8FEA\u2014\u2014\u85A9\u8FE6\u5BEB\u9053\u300C\u796D\u58C7\u88AB\u4ED6\u7684\u8840\u67D3\u7D05\u300D\u2014\u2014\u8C50\u5E74\u96A8\u5373\u56DE\u6B78\u3002\u4E0D\u4F86\u6885\u7684\u4E9E\u7576\u4EA6\u6709\u5E73\u884C\u8A18\u8F09\uFF1A\u70CF\u666E\u85A9\u62C9\u6BCF\u4E5D\u5E74\u8209\u884C\u5927\u796D\uFF0C\u5168\u6C11\u7686\u81F3\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u6700\u76F4\u767D\u7684\u300C\u738B\u6B0A\u63DB\u8C50\u6536\u300D\u4EA4\u6613\uFF1A\u795E\u738B\u4E4B\u8840\uFF0C\u6B63\u662F\u838A\u7A3C\u7684\u4EE3\u50F9\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic; Latin (Adam)",
    canonical_translation: "Samuel Laing, Heimskringla, London 1844 (public domain); Adam per Tschan 1959",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Domaldi-story is the kingship-harvest transaction's clearest statement. The famine: Domaldi's reign 'there was? - famine? - the saga's sequence: 'the first year? - they? - sacrificed? - cattle? - 'the second year? - men? - 'the third? - the crops? - worse; and the assembly's resolution: 'they took? - the resolution 'to sacrifice? - their king? - for? - plenty? - 'and they? - reddened? - the altars? - with his blood? - 'and they? - colored? - the? - altars? - red - the verse (Ynglingatal): 'the? - folk of the? - Swedes? - reddened? - the? - earth? - the altars? - with the? - king's blood? - 'and the? - harvests? - 'the seasons? - returned: the famine ends with the king's blood on the altar.\n\nThe nine-year cycle: Adam's parallel clause documents the institutional form - the great feast at Uppsala every ninth year (and the nine-males doctrine, recorded in the temple-record); Snorri's H\xE1konar saga adds the Christian king's dilemma at the same feast (the horse-blood record). The Ynglingatal verse-fragment for Domaldi (quoted by Snorri) is the corpus's most quoted sacrifice-verse: 'and the? - folk? - the? - 'the Swedish? - folk? - reddened? - the? - 'gor? - 'the grove? - the altars' - the grove-red, the altar-red: the record's image of the transaction.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the kingship-harvest economy and the sacrificial-office doctrine. The myth-world's theory of kingship is explicit: the king's body is the realm's collateral - his blood buys the seasons, as the god-king's burial-mound keeps them (the Freyr-mound record); and the assembly's resolution documents the transaction's institutional form: the people decides, the king is the price. In the atlas, the record anchors the king-as-harvest family (the Maya? - the frame is royal sacrifice for fertility: the Aztec? - each a kingship-harvest transaction; the Swedish one is distinctive in its assembly-clause and its verse's image of the reddened altars.\n\nThe transaction's institutional frame completes the doctrine: the assembly's resolution (the people decides, the king pays) is documented as legal procedure - the Swedish kingship's contract with the harvest stated in the saga's own legal vocabulary. And the parallel evidence completes the picture: Adam of Bremen's nine-year cycle and nine-males doctrine at the same site, and the Ynglingatal verse's image ('the Swedes reddened the altars with the king's blood') as the transaction's memorial in meter. The record thus anchors the kingship-harvest family: the realm's seasons as the king's body-debt, documented in saga, verse and chronicle - the north's most explicit statement that the king exists for the harvest."
    },
