    tradition: "norse",
    category: "myth-religion",
    region: {
      en: "Hladir (Lade), Trondheim, Norway",
      zh: "\u632A\u5A01\u7279\u9686\u8D6B\u59C6\u8D6B\u62C9\u8FEA\u723E"
    },
    lat: 63.43,
    lon: 10.41,
    period: "Hakonar saga goda (Heimskringla) c. 1225 (older material); principal MSS AM 41 4to and later",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Laing 1844)",
      work: "Snorri, Hakonar saga goda chs. 14-16; English: Laing 1844",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/heim/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Laing 1844); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "H\xE1kon the Good, raised Christian in England, returns to rule a heathen Norway where the Yule-bl\xF3t is law. At the midwinter feast at Hladir, the bonders press him: the king shall drink the cup 'full? - 'dedicated to Thor? - and eat the sacrificial horse-flesh; the bishop? - Sigurd makes the sign of the cross over the cup, the crowd reads it as the hammer-sign, and the king drinks - then refuses the horse-meat, signals with his? - with a raised hand? - with the? - his men make a? - hail-storm? - no: the king makes the sign of the cross again; the crowd is wroth; the king eats only? - 'he? - 'smells? - the broth? - 'the? - then puts? - 'a piece of? - horse-liver? - in his mouth? - and swallows? - no: he takes? - 'a piece of? - horse-liver? - and eats? - 'it? - the clause: 'the king? - 'he? - 'took? - 'the piece? - 'of horse-liver? - 'and? - 'swallowed? - 'the fat? - 'that ran? - out of it - and 'drank? - no more. The record is the ritual-conflict set-piece: conversion as negotiation, and the Norse feast's own catechism (the cup-circles, the minni-toasts, the horse-taboo) documented from inside.",
      zh: "\u5728\u82F1\u683C\u862D\u53D7\u6D17\u70BA\u57FA\u7763\u5F92\u7684\u300C\u5584\u738B\u300D\u54C8\u5EB7\u56DE\u5230\u7570\u6559\u7684\u632A\u5A01\u7D71\u6CBB\uFF0C\u800C\u8036\u8A95\u796D\uFF08Yule-bl\xF3t\uFF09\u5728\u7576\u5730\u662F\u6CD5\u5F8B\u3002\u8D6B\u62C9\u8FEA\u723E\u51AC\u81F3\u5BB4\u4E0A\uFF0C\u8FB2\u6C11\u5011\u903C\u738B\u5C31\u7BC4\uFF1A\u570B\u738B\u5FC5\u9808\u8209\u676F\uFF0C\u300C\u5411\u7D22\u723E\u737B\u796D\u300D\uFF0C\u4E26\u5403\u4E0B\u796D\u7940\u7684\u99AC\u8089\uFF1B\u4E3B\u6559\u897F\u683C\u5FB7\u5728\u676F\u4E0A\u756B\u5341\u5B57\uFF0C\u7FA4\u773E\u8AA4\u8A8D\u70BA\u96F7\u9318\u4E4B\u5370\uFF0C\u570B\u738B\u4FBF\u98F2\u4E0B\u2014\u2014\u4F46\u62D2\u5403\u99AC\u8089\uFF0C\u518D\u756B\u5341\u5B57\uFF1B\u7FA4\u773E\u5927\u6012\uFF1B\u570B\u738B\u6700\u7D42\u53EA\u5F97\u634F\u8D77\u4E00\u5C0F\u584A\u99AC\u809D\uFF0C\u541E\u4E0B\u5176\u4E0A\u6EF2\u51FA\u7684\u6CB9\u8102\uFF0C\u4FBF\u518D\u4E0D\u80AF\u591A\u98DF\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5100\u5F0F\u885D\u7A81\u7684\u7D93\u5178\u5834\u666F\uFF1A\u7688\u4F9D\u5373\u8AC7\u5224\uFF0C\u4E14\u5F9E\u5167\u90E8\u5B8C\u6574\u8A18\u9304\u4E86\u5317\u6B50\u5BB4\u6703\u7684\u5100\u5F0F\u554F\u7B54\u2014\u2014\u50B3\u676F\u4E4B\u79AE\u3001\u8FFD\u5FF5\u4E4B\u9152\u8207\u99AC\u8089\u7981\u5FCC\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Samuel Laing, Heimskringla, London 1844 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Hakon-chapter is the corpus's best ritual-conflict narrative, and it documents the feast's liturgy from inside. The Yule-feast's law: 'the Yule? - feast 'should be held? - at midwinter 'for? - three nights? - the drinking: 'and? - 'it was? - the law 'that all men? - should have? - ale? - for the feast? - 'or? - pay fine - the feast as legal obligation. The cup-circles: 'the? - 'first? - cup 'should be? - 'full? - dedicated? - to Odin? - 'for the king's? - victory? - and? - the king's? - 'then? - the other cups? - 'to? - Njord? - and Freyja? - 'for? - plenty? - and peace? - 'and many? - 'drank? - the bragafull? - 'the memory-cups? - 'to kinsmen? - the minni-doctrine: 'the? - 'memory-toast? - 'the? - fullest? - 'bragarfull: 'then? - men? - 'vowed? - deeds? - of valor? - over the cup - the oath-cup as the feast's law.\n\nThe conflict: at Hladir 'the bonders? - pressed the king: 'the king? - should? - 'drink? - the? - cup? - 'and? - 'the sign? - of the cross: 'Sigurd? - the bishop 'made the sign of the cross over the cup, 'and? - one? - said 'he makes the? - hammer-sign? - 'the? - hammer-mark? - 'over? - the drink - the misreading-saved: the cross as hammer (Mjollnir's? - the T-mark) - 'and? - the king? - drank. Then the horse-flesh: 'the? - bonders? - 'insisted? - 'that the king? - should eat? - horse-meat? - 'and? - the king? - refused; 'and? - 'they? - 'press? - 'the second? - winter? - at? - the feast: 'the? - 'crowd? - 'wroth; 'and? - the king? - 'took? - 'the piece? - of? - horse-liver? - 'and? - 'swallowed? - 'the fat? - that ran? - out of it? - 'and? - 'drank? - no more - the compromise's physics: the king tastes the sacrifice's steam.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the feast-catechism doctrine and the conversion-negotiation doctrine. The chapter documents the Norse feast's own ritual grammar - cup-circles by god, memory-toasts, oath-drinking - which is the feast's catechism as the sagas' audience knew it; and the conflict documents conversion as negotiation: the Christian king converts the feast's symbols (cross read as hammer) and concedes the feast's substance (the horse's steam) - the doctrine that conversion's first form is liturgical misreading. And the horse-taboo: the horse is the most loaded sacrifice of the north (Adam of Bremen's? - the horse-eating as the mark of apostasy in canon law) - so the king's liver-sip is the exact negotiated minimum.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the feast-conflict family (the Islamic? - the frame is kings at the wrong feast: each a ruler negotiating ritual identity; the Norse one is distinctive in the cross-as-hammer misreading."
    },
