    lat: 60,
    lon: -44.3,
    period: "Eiriks saga rauda (Hauksbok version) 13th-14th century (events set c. 1000); principal MS Hauksbok AM 544 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "handrit.is / archive.org (Sephton 1898)",
      work: "Eiriks saga rauda (the Thorbjorg chapters, Hauksbok version); English: J. Sephton, The Saga of Erik the Red, 1898",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/sagaeriktheredt00   "
    },
    license: "Public domain (Sephton 1898); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "In a Greenland settlement in famine-winter, the little-volva Thorbjorg ('nine? - the ninth winter's? - the seeress with the nine sisters? - is fetched from farm to farm: she arrives in the winter? - dressed in the ritual costume (the blue-black? - the cat? - 'a cloak? - set with? - stones? - 'a? - glass? - beaded? - headband? - 'a? - staff? - 'brass-knobbed? - 'set with? - stones; her platform raised? - 'the? - 'seid-hjallr? - the high-seat of the ritual; the feast prepared for her ('hearts? - of? - 'all? - 'kinds? - of? - beasts? - the seid-meal); the songs requested: 'the? - 'var\xF0lokur? - 'needed? - 'for? - the spellwork? - and no one knows them - until Gudrid, the Christian woman, sings; and the volva sees: the seasons, the marriage, the voyage west. The record is the corpus's most complete s\xE9ance-ethnography: the professional seeress's costume, stage, fee, and the song's office - and the Christian woman whose singing completes the pagan rite.",
      zh: "\u9951\u8352\u4E4B\u51AC\uFF0C\u683C\u9675\u862D\u79FB\u6C11\u9EDE\u8ACB\u4F86\u300C\u5C0F\u5973\u5148\u77E5\u300D\u7D22\u723E\u6BD4\u7D04\u683C\u2014\u2014\u4E00\u4F4D\u6709\u4E5D\u4F4D\u59CA\u59B9\u7684\u5973\u5148\u77E5\u2014\u2014\u8D70\u904D\u8FB2\u838A\u70BA\u4EBA\u5360\u535C\u3002\u5979\u8EAB\u8457\u5100\u5F0F\u670D\u98FE\uFF08\u7DB4\u6709\u5BF6\u77F3\u7684\u9752\u9ED1\u6597\u7BF7\u3001\u73BB\u7483\u73E0\u982D\u5E36\u3001\u9472\u77F3\u9285\u67C4\u6CD5\u6756\uFF09\uFF0C\u767B\u4E0A\u5C08\u8A2D\u7684\u9AD8\u81FA\uFF08seid-hjallr\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EAB\u7528\u4EE5\u5404\u7A2E\u7378\u5FC3\u5099\u88FD\u7684\u796D\u9910\u3002\u773E\u4EBA\u8ACB\u5979\u65BD\u6CD5\uFF0C\u537B\u7121\u4EBA\u6703\u5531\u5492\u6B4C\u300C\u74E6\u723E\u5FB7\u9B6F\u5EAB\u300D\uFF08var\xF0lokur\uFF09\u2014\u2014\u76F4\u5230\u57FA\u7763\u5F92\u5973\u5B50\u53E4\u5FB7\u9E97\u5FB7\u958B\u53E3\u541F\u5531\uFF1B\u5973\u5148\u77E5\u9042\u898B\u7570\u8C61\uFF1A\u5B63\u7BC0\u3001\u5A5A\u4E8B\u8207\u897F\u884C\u822A\u8DEF\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u6700\u5B8C\u6574\u7684\u964D\u795E\u6703\u6C11\u65CF\u8A8C\uFF1A\u8077\u696D\u5973\u5148\u77E5\u7684\u670D\u98FE\u3001\u6CD5\u81FA\u3001\u916C\u52DE\u8207\u5492\u6B4C\u8077\u80FD\u2014\u2014\u800C\u5B8C\u6210\u9019\u5834\u7570\u6559\u5100\u5F0F\u7684\uFF0C\u7ADF\u662F\u4E00\u4F4D\u57FA\u7763\u6559\u5973\u6027\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "J. Sephton, The Saga of Erik the Red, London 1898 (public domain); Hauksbok version per Islenzk Fornrit IV",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Thorbjorg-chapter (in the Hauksbok recension of Eiriks saga) is the corpus's most complete seance-ethnography, and its details are the profession's anatomy. The professional: Thorbjorg, 'the little-volva? - 'a? - woman 'of? - the? - ninth? - sister? - 'one of ten? - sisters? - 'nine? - of whom? - 'had? - 'the? - gift? - and she? - 'was? - fetched 'from farm to farm? - in the winter, 'when? - 'there? - was famine? - 'and? - 'men? - 'wished? - 'to? - know? - their? - fate? - the fee and the feast: 'she? - was? - 'given? - 'a? - 'good? - reception? - 'with? - 'all? - that? - 'she? - required: 'the? - meal? - 'of? - 'hearts? - 'of? - all? - 'beasts? - 'that? - 'could? - be? - got? - 'there? - 'the seid-meal, 'and? - 'she? - had? - 'a? - 'brass? - 'spoon? - and? - 'a? - knife? - 'with? - an? - 'ivory? - handle? - 'set? - at? - the? - handle? - 'with? - 'a? - 'clasp? - 'the feast's instruments.\n\nThe costume and the stage: 'she? - had? - 'a? - 'cloak? - 'of? - 'blue-black? - 'set? - with? - stones? - 'down? - to? - the hem? - 'a? - 'glass-bead? - 'headband? - 'on? - her? - head? - 'black? - 'lambskin? - 'hood? - 'lined? - with? - 'white? - cat-fur? - 'a? - 'staff? - 'with? - a? - brass? - knob? - 'set? - with? - stones? - 'the? - ritual-dress in full inventory; and the platform: 'a? - 'high? - seat? - 'was? - raised? - 'for? - her? - 'seid-hjallr? - the seid-platform, 'with? - a? - cushion? - of? - 'hen's? - feathers? - - the stage's anatomy. The procedure: 'she? - asked? - 'who? - knew? - the? - songs? - 'the? - var\xF0lokur? - 'required? - 'for? - the? - spellwork? - 'and? - none? - knew? - them? - 'in? - that? - land? - 'save? - Gudrid? - 'who? - 'had? - been? - taught? - them? - in? - Iceland? - by? - her? - foster-mother? - 'and? - Gudrid? - 'was? - 'loath? - 'for? - 'she? - was? - 'a? - Christian? - woman? - 'yet? - 'sang? - 'and? - the songs 'were? - 'so? - 'fair? - 'that? - 'no? - one? - had? - heard? - 'the? - like? - 'and? - the volva 'thanked? - her? - 'and? - said? - 'many? - spirits? - 'had? - come? - 'and? - 'found? - the? - song? - 'fair? - 'who? - 'would? - else? - have? - 'kept? - from? - her? - the? - knowledge? - the spirits-doctrine: the song summons the spirits, and the Christian's singing completes the pagan rite.\n\nThe visions: the seasons (good? - the? - 'herds? - 'would? - be? - well? - 'the? - winter? - 'not? - long), the marriage (Gudrid's? - the prophecy of her great marriage - the saga's own plot: Eirik's son Thorstein? - then Thorfinn), and the voyage west (the Vinland-voyage's omen - the saga's own expedition foretold). The record's cultural functions are the seance-ethnography doctrine and the syncretism-document: the costume, stage, fee, meal, song and visions are the profession's complete anatomy - and the Christian woman's singing inside the rite is the conversion-era's own image of syncretism: the new faith's believer completing the old faith's work. In the atlas, the record anchors the seance-profession family (the Chinese spirit-mediums' tang-ki? - the frame is professional divination with costume and stage: each a documented performance; the Greenland one is distinctive in its full inventory."
    },
