    summary: {
      en: "After the flyting, the gods hunt Loki - he hides as a salmon in Franang's falls, inventing the net (and thus making his own trap's blueprint); the gods take the invention from his own burnt fireplace, catch him, and bind him in a cave with the entrails of his own son. Over his face Skadi fastens a serpent whose venom drips; his wife Sigyn holds a bowl over him - but when she turns to empty it, the venom falls on his face and his writhing shakes the earth: earthquakes are Loki's thrashing. The record is the corpus's moral-geology: the trickster's punishment is the world's background process, and the release is scheduled - at Ragnarok, 'then? - shall? - he? - break? - loose.",
      zh: "\u5435\u7F75\u4E4B\u5F8C\uFF0C\u8AF8\u795E\u8FFD\u6355\u6D1B\u57FA\u2014\u2014\u4ED6\u5316\u8EAB\u9BAD\u9B5A\u85CF\u65BC\u5F17\u62C9\u5357\u7011\u5E03\uFF0C\u9084\u767C\u660E\u4E86\u6F01\u7DB2\uFF08\u89AA\u624B\u7E6A\u51FA\u81EA\u5DF1\u843D\u7DB2\u7684\u85CD\u5716\uFF09\uFF1B\u8AF8\u795E\u5F9E\u4ED6\u71D2\u71EC\u7684\u7210\u908A\u53D6\u5F97\u9019\u9805\u767C\u660E\uFF0C\u6355\u7372\u4E86\u4ED6\uFF0C\u4E26\u4EE5\u5176\u89AA\u751F\u5152\u5B50\u7684\u8178\u7D22\u5C07\u4ED6\u7E1B\u65BC\u6D1E\u7A74\u4E4B\u4E2D\u3002\u65AF\u5361\u8482\u5728\u4ED6\u982D\u4E0A\u61F8\u639B\u6BD2\u86C7\uFF0C\u6BD2\u6DB2\u6EF4\u843D\uFF1B\u5176\u59BB\u897F\u683C\u6069\u4EE5\u7897\u76F8\u63A5\u2014\u2014\u4F46\u5979\u8F49\u8EAB\u5012\u7A7A\u6BD2\u6DB2\u4E4B\u6642\uFF0C\u6BD2\u6DB2\u4FBF\u843D\u5728\u6D1B\u57FA\u81C9\u4E0A\uFF0C\u4ED6\u7684\u62BD\u6410\u9707\u64BC\u5927\u5730\uFF1A\u5730\u9707\u5373\u6D1B\u57FA\u7684\u6399\u624E\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u300C\u9053\u5FB7\u5730\u8CEA\u5B78\u300D\uFF1A\u8A6D\u8A08\u8005\u7684\u61F2\u7F70\u6210\u70BA\u4E16\u754C\u7684\u80CC\u666F\u904E\u7A0B\uFF0C\u800C\u91CB\u653E\u65E9\u6709\u5B89\u6392\u2014\u2014\u8AF8\u795E\u9EC3\u660F\u4E4B\u6642\uFF0C\u300C\u4ED6\u5FC5\u6399\u812B\u300D\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The binding-tale is the trickster's punishment-engineering, told as a hunt. The hiding: Loki 'ran? - to? - Franang's? - falls? - 'and? - 'sat? - 'there? - 'in? - 'salmon? - shape? - 'and? - 'the? - device: 'he? - 'had? - 'devised? - a net? - 'from? - 'the? - bladder? - of? - fish? - 'and? - 'burned? - it? - when? - 'he? - saw? - the? - Aesir? - coming? - 'the? - ashes? - 'in? - his? - own? - hearth? - told? - the? - hunters? - the? - invention's? - shape? - the trap's blueprint is the fugitive's own hearth-ash: the gods read the net's pattern from his burnt prototype and make it - 'and? - 'they? - 'took? - 'the? - net? - 'and? - 'caught? - 'him? - at? - the? - falls? - the doctrine: the trickster invents the instrument of his own capture.\n\nThe binding: in a cave, they set three? - stones? - the slab-seats: 'a? - flat? - stone? - 'and? - 'bored? - holes? - 'through? - it? - 'for? - 'the? - fetters? - and the fetters are family: 'the? - entrails? - 'of? - 'his? - son? - N\xE1r? - or? - Vali? - the prose: 'they? - 'changed? - Vali? - 'into? - a? - wolf? - 'and? - 'he? - 'tore? - his? - brother? - to? - pieces? - 'and? - 'the? - gods? - 'made? - fetters? - of? - his? - entrails? - the binding-material is the trickster's own line; and the poison-protocol: 'Skadi? - 'took? - a? - venomous? - serpent? - 'and? - 'fastened? - it? - 'up? - over? - him? - 'so? - 'that? - the? - venom? - 'should? - drip? - on? - his? - face? - 'and? - Sigyn? - 'his? - wife? - 'sat? - 'over? - him? - 'and? - held? - a? - bowl? - 'over? - him? - to? - catch? - the? - venom? - 'and? - 'when? - 'the? - bowl? - was? - full? - 'she? - went? - out? - 'to? - empty? - it? - 'and? - 'the? - venom? - 'fell? - on? - his? - face? - 'and? - 'he? - 'writhed? - 'so? - 'that? - the? - earth? - 'shook? - 'and? - 'that? - 'is? - 'now? - called? - earthquake? - the moral-geology: earthquakes are the punisher's thrashing.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the self-instrumentation doctrine and the punishment-as-process doctrine. The binding is made of the bound: the net invented by the fugitive, the fetters of his own son's body - the myth corpus's theory of justice as self-manufacture. And the punishment is a background process, not a scene: the bowl-cycles and the writhings are the world's permanent weather - the trickster's guilt as the earth's periodic tremor. The release is scheduled (the record's eschatological clause): 'then? - shall? - he? - break? - loose? - 'at? - Ragnarok? - 'with? - 'all? - 'the? - 'father? - 'of? - the? - lie's? - ruin - the punishment has a diary date.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the self-made-instrument family (the Chinese? - the frame is punishers bound by their own devices): each a justice made of the criminal's own manufacture; the Norse one is distinctive in the earthquake-clause."
    },
