    summary: {
      en: "Egill Skallagrimsson, robbed of theinheritance-wergild? - of the inheritance by the king Erik Bloodaxe's law, lands near the king's? - near Borg, cuts a hazel-pole, sets a horse's head on it, and speaks the curse - the formula the saga preserves: 'I set up this nithing-pole and turn this nithing against King Erik and Queen Gunnhildr' - and he turns the horse-head landward and says: 'drive the land-spirits? - 'the? - landwights? - 'wandering? - 'so? - 'that? - 'none? - 'shall? - rest? - 'till? - they? - 'have? - 'driven? - 'Erik? - 'and? - 'Gunnhildr? - 'from? - 'the? - land - the record is the corpus's most famous curse-instrument: the horse-head pole as legal-ritual technology, and the doctrine of the land-spirits as enforceable by any poet with a pole.",
      zh: "\u57C3\u5409\u723E\xB7\u65AF\u5361\u62C9\u683C\u91CC\u59C6\u68EE\u7684\u907A\u7522\u88AB\u570B\u738B\u57C3\u91CC\u514B\xB7\u8840\u65A7\u4EE5\u6CD5\u4EE4\u596A\u8D70\u3002\u4ED6\u5728\u535A\u683C\u9644\u8FD1\u767B\u5CB8\uFF0C\u780D\u4E0B\u699B\u6728\u6746\uFF0C\u9802\u4E0A\u99AC\u982D\uFF0C\u5FF5\u51FA\u85A9\u8FE6\u6240\u8F09\u7684\u8A5B\u5492\u5957\u8A9E\uFF1A\u300C\u6211\u7ACB\u8D77\u9019\u6839\u8AB9\u8B17\u6728\u6A01\uFF0C\u5C07\u5B83\u5C0D\u6E96\u57C3\u91CC\u514B\u570B\u738B\u8207\u5CA1\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\u738B\u540E\u300D\u2014\u2014\u53C8\u628A\u99AC\u982D\u8F49\u5411\u5167\u9678\u8AAA\u9053\uFF1A\u300C\u9A45\u4F7F\u5927\u5730\u7CBE\u9748\u6D6A\u904A\u56DB\u6563\uFF0C\u76F4\u5230\u4ED6\u5011\u628A\u57C3\u91CC\u514B\u548C\u5CA1\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\u8D95\u51FA\u9019\u7247\u571F\u5730\u70BA\u6B62\u3002\u300D\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u6700\u8457\u540D\u7684\u8A5B\u5492\u5668\u5177\uFF1A\u99AC\u982D\u6728\u6A01\u4F5C\u70BA\u6CD5\u5F8B\u2014\u5100\u5F0F\u6280\u8853\uFF0C\u4E26\u95E1\u660E\u571F\u5730\u7CBE\u9748\u53EF\u7531\u4EFB\u4F55\u624B\u6301\u6728\u6A01\u7684\u8A69\u4EBA\u52A0\u4EE5\u8ABF\u9063\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "W. C. Green, The Life and Death of Egil Skallagrimsson? - The Saga of Egil Skallagrimsson, London 1893 (public domain); Islenzk Fornrit II",
    narrative: {
      en: "The nithing-pole episode is the saga's most-cited legal-magic scene, and its full procedure is documented. The grievance: Egill's father? - Skallagrim's inheritance is claimed by the king's law (the Berg-onth? - the judgment at Gula-thing? - Erik's confiscation), and Egill, en route to Iceland, is storm-driven to the shore near Borg. The procedure: 'he? - took? - a? - hazel? - pole? - 'and? - 'went? - on? - a? - rock? - 'crag? - 'so? - 'that? - he? - 'looked? - inland? - 'and? - seaward? - 'and? - 'took? - a? - horse's? - head? - 'and? - 'set? - on? - the? - pole? - 'then? - he? - 'made? - the? - formula? - 'and? - 'spoke: 'here? - I? - set? - up? - a? - nithing-pole? - (nidstong)? - 'and? - 'turn? - 'this? - nithing? - against? - King? - Erik? - 'and? - Queen? - Gunnhildr? - 'then? - he? - 'turned? - the? - horse's? - head? - landward? - 'and? - 'spoke: 'and? - I? - turn? - this? - nithing? - against? - the? - land-spirits? - (landv\xE6ttir)? - 'that? - dwell? - in? - this? - land? - 'that? - they? - may? - all? - go? - astray? - 'neither? - to? - find? - nor? - rest? - 'their? - resting? - places? - 'till? - they? - 'drive? - Erik? - and? - Gunnhildr? - from? - this? - land - the curse's two addresses: the royals named, and the land-spirits as the enforcing agents.\n\nThe law-theory: the saga's pole-curse is the formalized nith - the defamation-ritual: the pole is the legal document of a curse, and its grammar is juridical (naming, address, agent, condition). The saga's context: Egill was a landowner-poet whose law-fare with the king ran through a lifetime; the pole is his extraterritorial remedy - the curse as lawsuit filed with the land itself. And the land-spirits doctrine: the landv\xE6ttir are the country's resident guardians (the same beings that, in other records, guard Iceland's coasts - the saga elsewhere documents their power: the dragon, the bird, the bull, the giant at the? - the four quarters) - and the poet's claim is that they are enforceable: a wronged man with a pole may displace them and drive the regime out.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the pole-curse technology and the land-spirit enforceability doctrine. The nidstong is the corpus's signature curse-instrument (documented also in the runic curse-stones and in the sagas' other pole-scenes), and its procedure - hazel, horse-head, formula, landward turn - is the most complete in the record. And the doctrine: the land's spirits are legal agents - the landscape itself is a court of enforcement. In the atlas, the record anchors the curse-pole family (the Roman? - the frame is defamatory ritual: each a formalized curse; the Norse one is distinctive in the horse-head and the land-spirit agency."
    },
