    lat: 49.6,
    lon: 8.4,
    period: "Reginsmal c. 1000-1200; Volsunga saga chs. 1-14 (13th c.); principal MSS Codex Regius GKS 2365 4to, NKS 1824 b 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923) / archive.org (Morris & Magnusson 1870)",
      work: "Reginsmal; Volsunga saga chs. 1-14; English: Bellows 1923; Morris & Magnusson 1870",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Morris & Magnusson 1870); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Loki, hunting with Odin and Hoenir, kills the otter - who is the dwarf Andvari's son Otr in otter-shape; the weregild demanded by Hreidmar is the otter-skin filled and covered with gold. Loki takes the dwarf Andvari (fishing in the falls as a pike), seizes his whole hoard, and Andvari - robbed - lays the curse on the ring Andvaranaut: 'the ring? - 'shall? - 'be? - 'the? - 'death? - 'of? - 'all? - 'who? - 'own? - 'it? - and the gold's curse works through the cycle: Hreidmar killed by Fafnir and Regin; Fafnir becomes a dragon on the hoard; Sigurd wins and dies of it; the Rhine sinks the gold. The record is the corpus's central curse-charter: gold as a circulating death-sentence, and the weregild's irony - the compensation-payment becomes the family's destroyer.",
      zh: "\u6D1B\u57FA\u8207\u5967\u4E01\u3001\u6D77\u5C3C\u723E\u540C\u884C\u72E9\u7375\u6642\uFF0C\u6BBA\u6B7B\u4E86\u4E00\u982D\u6C34\u737A\u2014\u2014\u90A3\u6B63\u662F\u77EE\u4EBA\u5B89\u5FB7\u74E6\u5229\u4E4B\u5B50\u5967\u7279\u6240\u5316\u3002\u8D6B\u840A\u5FB7\u746A\u7D22\u8981\u7684\u8D16\u7F6A\u91D1\uFF0C\u9808\u5C07\u6C34\u737A\u76AE\u88DD\u6EFF\u3001\u8986\u6EFF\u9EC3\u91D1\u3002\u6D1B\u57FA\u6349\u4F4F\u5728\u7011\u5E03\u4E2D\u5316\u70BA\u68AD\u5B50\u9B5A\u5782\u91E3\u7684\u77EE\u4EBA\u5B89\u5FB7\u74E6\u5229\uFF0C\u596A\u5176\u5168\u90E8\u85CF\u91D1\uFF1B\u5B89\u5FB7\u74E6\u5229\u88AB\u596A\u5F8C\uFF0C\u5C0D\u6307\u74B0\u5B89\u5FB7\u74E6\u5229\u5967\u7279\u65BD\u4E0B\u8A5B\u5492\uFF1A\u300C\u51E1\u6301\u6709\u6B64\u74B0\u8005\uFF0C\u7686\u5C07\u56E0\u5B83\u800C\u6B7B\u3002\u300D\u8A5B\u5492\u5FAA\u50B3\u8AAA\u9031\u671F\u904B\u8F49\uFF1A\u8D6B\u840A\u5FB7\u746A\u6B7B\u65BC\u6CD5\u592B\u7D0D\u8207\u96F7\u91D1\u4E4B\u624B\uFF1B\u6CD5\u592B\u7D0D\u5316\u70BA\u5DE8\u9F8D\u76E4\u8E1E\u85CF\u91D1\uFF1B\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u5F97\u91D1\u800C\u4EA1\uFF1B\u9EC3\u91D1\u7D42\u6C89\u840A\u8335\u6CB3\u5E95\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u6838\u5FC3\u8A5B\u5492\u61B2\u7AE0\uFF1A\u9EC3\u91D1\u5373\u6D41\u8F49\u7684\u6B7B\u5211\u5224\u6C7A\uFF0C\u800C\u8D16\u7F6A\u91D1\u7684\u53CD\u8AF7\u5728\u65BC\u2014\u2014\u8CE0\u511F\u4E4B\u7269\u53CD\u800C\u6EC5\u7D55\u4E86\u6574\u500B\u5BB6\u65CF\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Volsunga saga via Morris & Magnusson 1870",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Andvari-curse is the Sigurd-cycle's economic engine, and its charter is laid in the cycle's first generation. The kill: the three gods walking see an otter with a salmon? - Otr, Hreidmar's son, in otter-shape; Loki kills it with a stone - and Hreidmar's weregild: the otter-skin filled with gold and covered over 'nose? - 'and? - 'every? - hair? - the payment's impossible precision. The extraction: Loki takes Andvari (the dwarf fishing as a pike? - in the falls, the dwarf who 'lived? - 'in? - 'a? - stone? - 'and? - 'was? - 'rich? - 'as? - 'a? - 'pike? - 'in? - the? - falls), seizes his hoard, and leaves him the ring Andvaranaut - and the dwarf's curse is the record's charter: 'the? - ring? - 'shall? - 'be? - 'the? - 'death? - 'of? - 'every? - one? - 'that? - owns? - it? - 'and? - 'the? - gold? - 'likewise? - the weregild is paid - and the curse enters circulation.\n\nThe curse's working is the cycle's plot: Hreidmar, paid, is killed by Fafnir and Regin ('a? - 'father's? - death? - 'for? - the? - hoard); Fafnir takes the hoard to Gnita-heath and becomes a dragon - the gold's holder literally shape-shifts into its keeper-beast; Sigurd slays him and takes the ring; and the cycle's deaths run (Sigurd, Gunnar, Hogni, Atli, the sons); the gold ends in the Rhine - 'and? - 'the? - Rhine? - 'shall? - 'rule? - 'the? - rings - Gunnar's line - and the curse's circuit closes where the gold sank. The record's cultural functions are the gold-as-death-sentence doctrine and the weregild-irony doctrine. The curse-charter makes gold a circulating sentence: each holder's death is the ring's clause executed, and the economy of the whole heroic corpus (the Niflung-cycle) is legal-economically structured by it - the weregild, the north's legal payment, becomes the family's destroyer: compensation transmuted into contagion. And the otter-weregild's symbolism: the payment is demanded in the shape of the victim - the legal debt measured in the victim's own skin.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the cursed-treasure family (the Greek Polydoros-gold, the Mesopotamian? - each a hoard with a death-clause; the Norse one is distinctive in the weregild-frame and the dragon-transformation.\n\nThe curse's mechanics run through the whole cycle as a legal ledger: each holder's death is the ring's clause executed (Hreidmar killed for the hoard, Fafnir transformed by it, Sigurd murdered for it, the Gjukungs destroyed with it, Atli killed by it), and the saga's arithmetic - 'the ring shall be the death of everyone that owns it' - is the Niflung-cycle's plot-generator: the treasure's biography is a death-register. And the weregild-frame is the doctrine's legal genius: the curse is installed at the moment of legal payment, so the north's own compensation-institution (the weregild that settles feuds) becomes the mechanism of its greatest feud - the myth's critique of the gold-economy stated in law's own terms. The record thus anchors the cursed-treasure family: gold as circulating sentence, installed by the robbed and executed by the paid."
    },
