    license: "Public domain (Kershaw 1921); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "King Svafrlami forces the dwarfs Dvalin and Durin to forge him a sword with a gold hilt, never failing, never rusting, cutting stone and iron as cloth - and they forge it under duress. At the gate the king says 'the sword? - 'the? - work? - shall? - 'be? - 'as? - 'I? - 'bade? - the dwarfs answer: the blade shall be the death of a man each time it is drawn, three shameful deeds each time? - and it shall be Svafrlami's own bane - and they vanish. The curse works exactly: Svafrlami dies by it (Angantyr's hand); it kills whenever drawn through three generations (Arngrim's sons' berserk-duel, Heidrek's crimes, the final battle); and Hervor's mound-claim gives it its last biography. The record is the corpus's weapon-curse charter: a forged object with a contractual kill-clause, and the doctrine that a weapon taken by force pays in its owners' blood.",
      zh: "\u65AF\u74E6\u5F17\u723E\u62C9\u7C73\u738B\u5F37\u4EE4\u77EE\u4EBA\u5FB7\u74E6\u6797\u8207\u90FD\u6797\u70BA\u4ED6\u935B\u9020\u4E00\u67C4\u5BF6\u528D\uFF1A\u91D1\u67C4\u3001\u6C38\u4E0D\u5931\u624B\u3001\u6C38\u4E0D\u751F\u93FD\u3001\u65AC\u77F3\u9435\u5982\u65AC\u5E03\u2014\u2014\u77EE\u4EBA\u88AB\u8FEB\u5728\u8105\u8FEB\u4E0B\u935B\u6210\u3002\u57CE\u9580\u524D\uFF0C\u570B\u738B\u7A31\u8B9A\u5BF6\u528D\u300C\u6B63\u5982\u6211\u6240\u547D\u300D\uFF0C\u77EE\u4EBA\u7B54\u9053\uFF1A\u6B64\u528D\u6BCF\u51FA\u9798\u4E00\u6B21\u5FC5\u596A\u4E00\u547D\uFF0C\u6BCF\u6B21\u9084\u8981\u72AF\u4E0B\u4E09\u4EF6\u53EF\u6065\u4E4B\u4E8B\uFF0C\u800C\u4E14\u5B83\u7D42\u5C07\u6210\u70BA\u65AF\u74E6\u5F17\u723E\u62C9\u7C73\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u6B7B\u52AB\u2014\u2014\u8A00\u7562\u6D88\u5931\u3002\u8A5B\u5492\u5206\u6BEB\u4E0D\u5DEE\u5730\u61C9\u9A57\uFF1A\u65AF\u74E6\u5F17\u723E\u62C9\u7C73\u6B7B\u65BC\u6B64\u528D\uFF1B\u4E09\u4EE3\u4E4B\u9593\uFF0C\u51FA\u9798\u5FC5\u898B\u8840\uFF08\u963F\u6069\u683C\u91CC\u59C6\u8AF8\u5B50\u7684\u72C2\u6230\u58EB\u6C7A\u9B25\u3001\u6D77\u5FB7\u91CC\u514B\u7684\u7F6A\u884C\u3001\u6700\u5F8C\u7684\u6C7A\u6230\uFF09\uFF1B\u8D6B\u723E\u6C83\u7684\u63A2\u5893\u4E4B\u8209\u66F4\u70BA\u5176\u6DFB\u4E0A\u6700\u5F8C\u7684\u50B3\u8A18\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u5175\u5668\u8A5B\u5492\u61B2\u7AE0\uFF1A\u4E00\u628A\u5E36\u6709\u5951\u7D04\u6BBA\u622E\u689D\u6B3E\u7684\u935B\u9020\u4E4B\u7269\u2014\u2014\u4EE5\u5F37\u529B\u596A\u5F97\u7684\u5175\u5668\uFF0C\u5FC5\u4EE5\u6301\u6709\u8005\u7684\u8840\u511F\u9084\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Nora Kershaw Chadwick, Stories and Ballads of the Far Past, Cambridge 1921 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Tyrfing-charter is the fornaldarsaga's weapon-curse doctrine in its purest form. The forging: Svafrlami 'caught? - the dwarfs 'as? - 'they? - 'came? - 'out? - of? - the? - stone? - 'at? - dusk? - 'and? - 'forced? - them? - 'to? - forge? - 'a? - sword: 'a? - hilt? - of? - gold, 'never? - failing? - 'of? - blow? - 'nor? - rusting, 'that? - would? - 'cut? - stone? - and? - iron? - 'as? - 'though? - 'it? - were? - cloth? - the specification's perfection is the curse's mechanism: a perfect sword can only be a death-instrument. The curse's terms are contractual: 'when? - 'the? - king? - 'said? - 'the? - work? - 'was? - 'as? - 'he? - bade? - 'the? - dwarfs? - said: 'the? - sword? - 'shall? - 'be? - 'the? - 'death? - 'of? - 'a? - man? - 'each? - 'time? - 'it? - 'is? - drawn? - 'and? - 'three? - 'shameful? - 'deeds? - 'with? - 'it? - each? - time? - 'and? - 'it? - shall? - 'be? - 'thy? - 'own? - 'bane? - 'and? - they? - 'went? - 'into? - the? - rock? - the kill-clause, the shame-clause, and the suicide-clause, addressed to the forced buyer.\n\nThe curse's working through three generations is the saga's plot-architecture: Svafrlami dies by it (Angantyr's? - Arngrim's? - the first killing); the sword passes to Arngrim and his berserk-sons (the Sams\xF8-duel, the mound-burial); Hervor's claim wakes it; Heidrek's crimes (the shame-clause in each drawing) and the riddle-contest's collapse (Odin's death-sentence); and the end: Angantyr kills Hlod - the brothers' war as the sword's last accounting. The record's cultural functions are the forced-purchase doctrine and the perfect-weapon paradox. The curse's terms are addressed to the buyer who forced the sale: the weapon taken by duress carries its seller's revenge as a contract - the myth-world's theory of duress in exchange. And the perfect-weapon paradox: the sword's specifications (never failing, cutting as cloth) are the very terms of the curse - perfection in a weapon is its kill-clause: the better the sword, the more deaths it must deliver.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the weapon-curse family (the Chinese? - the frame is cursed blades: the Japanese Muramasa's blood-hunger, the Greek? - each a weapon with a kill-clause; the Norse one is distinctive in its contractual terms and the shame-counter."
    },
