    license: "Public domain (early critical editions); text is a labeled paraphrase-synthesis of readings",
    summary: {
      en: "Among the Blekinge stones - Stentoften and Bj\xF6rketorp - stand carved curse-formulas of the 7th century, the north's earliest datable curse-instruments. The Bj\xF6rketorp text (per the standard readings): 'the? - prophecy? - 'of? - 'destruction? - 'I? - 'foresee? - 'the? - 'mighty? - 'destruction? - 'of? - 'the? - 'man? - 'who? - 'breaks? - 'this? - 'monument? - 'in? - 'memory? - 'of? - 'the? - dead? - 'the? - row? - of? - runes? - 'is? - 'the? - 'guard? - 'and? - 'the? - 'stone? - 'the? - 'grave? - the record is the runic curse-doctrine: the stones as a liturgy of protection, and the mover of the monument as its target - the earliest Swedish curse-law in stone.",
      zh: "\u5E03\u840A\u91D1\u5384\u7684\u77F3\u7891\u7FA4\uFF08\u65AF\u5766\u6258\u723E\u666E\u8207\u6BD4\u7D04\u514B\u6258\u666E\uFF09\u4E0A\u523B\u67097\u4E16\u7D00\u7684\u8A5B\u5492\u5957\u8A9E\uFF0C\u662F\u5317\u65B9\u6700\u65E9\u53EF\u65B7\u4EE3\u7684\u8A5B\u5492\u5668\u5177\u3002\u6BD4\u7D04\u514B\u6258\u666E\u7891\u6587\uFF08\u6309\u6A19\u6E96\u91CB\u8B80\uFF09\uFF1A\u300C\u6211\u9810\u898B\u6BC0\u6EC5\u7684\u9810\u8A00\u2014\u2014\u5C0D\u65BC\u7834\u58DE\u9019\u5EA7\u7D00\u5FF5\u4EA1\u8005\u4E4B\u7891\u7684\u5F37\u8005\u7684\u6BC0\u6EC5\uFF1B\u5982\u5C3C\u884C\u5217\u5373\u662F\u5B88\u8B77\uFF0C\u77F3\u7891\u5373\u662F\u58B3\u5893\u3002\u300D\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5982\u5C3C\u8A5B\u5492\u6559\u7FA9\u7684\u5448\u73FE\uFF1A\u77F3\u7891\u4F5C\u70BA\u4FDD\u8B77\u7684\u796D\u5100\uFF0C\u800C\u79FB\u52D5\u7D00\u5FF5\u7891\u8005\u6B63\u662F\u5176\u653B\u64CA\u76EE\u6A19\u2014\u2014\u9019\u662F\u745E\u5178\u6700\u65E9\u7684\u77F3\u523B\u8A5B\u5492\u5F8B\u6CD5\u3002"
    },
    language: "Proto-Scandinavian (runic)",
    canonical_translation: "Readings per Rundata (DR 360); Sophus Bugge; Elmer Antonsen; paraphrase-synthesis",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Blekinge stones are Scandinavia's oldest curse-instruments, and their doctrine is protection-by-writing. The stones: Stentoften (DR 357) and Bj\xF6rketorp (DR 360), standing in pairs? - in line across the landscape, carved in the 7th century with memorial-formulas and curse-formulas; the Bj\xF6rketorp text (standard readings): 'u\xFEarba? - spia? - 'the? - prophecy? - of? - destruction? - 'gud? - 'runar? - 'the? - row? - of? - runes? - 'the? - readings: 'I? - foresee? - destruction? - 'for? - 'the? - one? - 'who? - 'breaks? - 'the? - 'stone? - 'in? - 'memory? - 'of? - 'the? - dead? - 'the? - curse's target is the mover - 'the? - monument's? - protection is its law, and the rune-row is named as the guard: 'the? - row? - of? - runes? - 'is? - 'the? - 'ward? - 'and? - 'the? - stone? - 'is? - 'the? - grave? - the doctrine: writing is protection, and the monument is its own body.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the runic-curse doctrine and the monument-law doctrine. The Blekinge formulae are the earliest Swedish curse-law in stone - a society protecting its graves (and its land-claims) by written threat, two centuries before the Viking-age runestones' curses ('the? - 'vargr? - 'i? - ve? - 'the? - outlaw-clauses of Glavendrup and Tryggev\xE6lde - the later descendants of this doctrine). And the monument-law: the stone is both grave-marker and legal instrument - the dead's claim on the land written as a threat, the ancestor's property-right in curse-form.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the grave-curse family (the Greek? - the frame is tomb-curses: each a monument protecting its own dead; the Blekinge one is distinctive in runic form and the mover-target.\n\nThe Blekinge monument-line's full apparatus (Stentofor's memorial-clauses and initiation-formulae beside Bjorketorp's threat) documents the stones' double office: they mark a ritual site (the bracteate-hoard's context suggests a cult-place) and protect it by written law - the 7th-century society's legal theology in stone. And the curse's addressee is precise: not the general thief but the mover of the monument - the one act that would erase the claim - so the curse is property-law in threat-form: the ancestor's title defended against relocation. The record thus anchors the early runic corpus's legal range: the futhark's dedications, the Rok stone's mythic archive, and the Blekinge stones' curse-law - one alphabet's three registers.\n\nThe stones' monument-group completes the doctrine: the Blekinge pair stands in a designed line across the landscape (the sight-line documented by the surveys), and the rune-rows' placement - face, side, top of the stones - documents the carving's ceremonial care: the curse is not graffiti but architecture. And the doctrine's descendants complete the record: the Viking-age curse-clauses (the outlaw-formulas of Glavendrup and Tryggevalde, the Thor-hallowing of Velanda) descend from this 7th-century law - the runic alphabet's legal register continuous from the pre-Viking monuments to the Christian-era stones. The record thus anchors the grave-curse family: writing as protection, the monument as its own body, and the mover of stones as the law's target."
    },
