    lat: 64.92,
    lon: -22.75,
    period: "Eyrbyggja saga chs. 4-5 (13th-14th c.); Landnamabok parallel; principal MSS 14th c.",
    source: {
      archive: "handrit.is",
      work: "Eyrbyggja saga chs. 4-5 (the holy mountain); Landnamabok; ed. Islenzk Fornrit IV (1935)",
      url: "https://handrit.is/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (older editions); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Thorolf Mostrarskegg believes in the mountain on his land with such devotion that neither he nor his household would look at it unwashed, and nothing might be killed there till it went of itself down to the sea; and he believes that he and his kinsmen shall go into the mountain when they die. The saga's testimony: when Thorstein died, his shepherd saw the mountain open, and the dead man went in with his kinsmen, was welcomed to the high-seat, and sat at the feast - as into a hall.",
      zh: "\u83AB\u65AF\u7279\u723E\u9577\u9B1A\u7D22\u723E\u592B\u5C0D\u81EA\u5DF1\u571F\u5730\u4E0A\u7684\u5C71\u5DBD\u61F7\u6709\u5982\u6B64\u8654\u4FE1\uFF0C\u4EE5\u81F3\u4ED6\u8207\u5BB6\u4EBA\u89C0\u5C71\u4E4B\u524D\u5FC5\u5148\u6C90\u6D74\uFF0C\u4E14\u4E0D\u5F97\u5728\u5C71\u4E0A\u6FFA\u8840\uFF1B\u300C\u4ED6\u8207\u65CF\u4EBA\u76F8\u4FE1\uFF0C\u6B7B\u5F8C\u5C07\u9032\u5165\u5C71\u5DBD\u4E4B\u4E2D\u300D\u3002\u85A9\u8FE6\u4E26\u4F5C\u8B49\uFF1A\u7D22\u723E\u65AF\u6CF0\u56E0\u6B7B\u6642\uFF0C\u5176\u7267\u7F8A\u4EBA\u89AA\u898B\u5C71\u5DBD\u958B\u555F\u2014\u2014\u4EA1\u8005\u96A8\u89AA\u65CF\u6B65\u5165\u5C71\u4E2D\uFF0C\u53D7\u5230\u8FCE\u63A5\uFF0C\u5750\u4E0A\u5C71\u5DBD\u7684\u9AD8\u5EA7\uFF0C\u5982\u5165\u5EF3\u5802\u822C\u8D74\u5BB4\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "William Morris and Eirikr Magnusson, The Saga of the Ere-Dwellers, London 1892 (public domain); Islenzk Fornrit IV",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Helgafell-doctrine is the saga corpus's most complete mountain-afterlife, attested as family belief. The devotion: Thorolf believed in the mountain on his land so devotedly that neither he nor his household would wash before looking at it; nothing might be killed on it, man or beast, till it went of itself down to the sea - the purity-law and the self-giving-beast clause (animals that flee to the mountain are its own, dying by themselves); and he called the mountain Helgafell, the Holy Mountain, and believed that he and his kinsmen should go into it when they died - the afterlife-clause stated as the family's creed.\n\nThe testimony: when Thorstein Cod-biter, Thorolf's son, died, the shepherd saw the mountain open, and the dead man went in with his father and kinsmen, was welcomed to the high-seat, and sat at the feast - the mountain as a hall with the dead feasting; and the shepherd was bidden tell the family of what he saw - the witness-clause: the mountain's opening documented by the household's own man and reported, the afterlife as a documentable event.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the mountain-hall doctrine and the family-landscape doctrine: the dead do not leave for a mythic elsewhere but enter the family's own mountain, feasting at the high-seat - the afterlife as the estate's interior. And the doctrine's persistence: the saga records the mountain-cult in Christian-era Iceland, showing the landscape-afterlife surviving conversion in family memory.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the mountain-afterlife family: each a sacred mountain as the dead's destination; the Norse one is distinctive in the family-hall image and the witness-clause.\n\nThe doctrine's extension through the family is documented in the saga's later chapters: the household's dead continuing to enter the mountain, the Helgafell-estate passing with the belief intact, and - the saga's most famous clause - Snorri go\xF0i, the saga's great legalist, choosing to be carried into the mountain's shadow for his burial, the mountain-belief persisting in the commonwealth's most law-minded family. And the purity-law's details (the washing-clause, the blood-taboo, the self-dying beasts) document the cult's practice as a landscape-regime: the mountain's sanctity is a legal order on the farm, complete with its own jurisdiction. The record thus anchors the Icelandic landscape-cult family: the pillars, the temples and the holy mountains as one settlement-theology, the afterlife inside the estate.\n\nThe mountain-cult's geography completes the record: Helgafell stands over the family's estate (Thorolf's temple at Hofsvag within sight), so the afterlife-land and the living-land are one view - the dead's hall visible from the farmhouse. And the doctrine's comparative depth completes it: the Norse mountain-afterlife answers the Chinese sacred-peak cults and the Greek mountain-haunts of the dead - each tradition housing its dead in high ground; the Norse one is distinctive in the family-estate frame and the shepherd's witness-clause. The record thus anchors the landscape-afterlife family: the pillars, the temples and the holy mountains as one settlement-theology, the estate's interior as eternity."
    },
