    summary: {
      en: "In a Cumbrian churchyard stands a sixteen-foot Viking-age cross whose panels carve the Ragnarok sequence alongside Christian imagery: Thor's fishing for the serpent, Loki bound, the bird over the doomed warrior? - the scene-set, and the crucifixion on the same stone. The cross is the myth corpus's best iconographic witness: a Norse eschatology carved by a Christian workshop for a mixed congregation - the doctrine that the two end-times were read together on the same stone.",
      zh: "\u574E\u5E03\u91CC\u4E9E\u6559\u5802\u5893\u5730\u4E2D\u77D7\u7ACB\u8457\u4E00\u5EA7\u5341\u516D\u82F1\u5C3A\u9AD8\u7684\u7DAD\u4EAC\u6642\u4EE3\u5341\u5B57\u67B6\uFF0C\u5176\u5D4C\u677F\u4E0A\u4E26\u5217\u96D5\u6709\u8AF8\u795E\u9EC3\u660F\u5834\u666F\u8207\u57FA\u7763\u6559\u5716\u50CF\uFF1A\u7D22\u723E\u91E3\u86C7\u3001\u88AB\u7E1B\u7684\u6D1B\u57FA\u2026\u2026\u4EE5\u53CA\u540C\u4E00\u77F3\u4E0A\u7684\u5341\u5B57\u67B6\u53D7\u96E3\u50CF\u3002\u6B64\u5341\u5B57\u67B6\u662F\u795E\u8A71\u8A9E\u6599\u6700\u4F73\u7684\u5716\u50CF\u898B\u8B49\uFF1A\u7531\u57FA\u7763\u6559\u4F5C\u574A\u70BA\u6DF7\u5408\u6703\u773E\u6240\u96D5\u7684\u5317\u6B50\u672B\u4E16\u89C0\u2014\u2014\u986F\u793A\u5169\u7A2E\u7D42\u672B\u8AD6\u5728\u540C\u4E00\u584A\u77F3\u982D\u4E0A\u88AB\u4E26\u7F6E\u95B1\u8B80\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse iconography (sculpted stone)",
    canonical_translation: "Identifications per Richard Bailey, England's Earliest Sculptors, 1980; Collingwood 1927",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Gosforth cross is the myth corpus's densest iconographic document: a single stone whose panels juxtapose the Norse end-time with the Christian one. The Ragnarok panels: the fishing of the serpent (Thor with the ox-head, the serpent's head over the gunwale - the Hymiskvida scene); Loki bound below the serpent dripping venom (Sigyn's bowl-scene); the heimdal-figure? - the horn-blower? - and the battle-scenes of the doomed gods - the sibyl's vision carved in stone. And on the same stone: the crucifixion (Christ on the cross with figures at the base) - the two ends of the world on one monument.\n\nThe workshop's context: Gosforth is a Viking-settled region of Cumbria (the Norse colony of the north-west), and the cross's carving has been read as a Christian mission's accommodation: the converts' old mythology rendered in the new faith's stone, the end-times parallel (Ragnarok's doom, Christ's victory) preached visually. The record's cultural functions are the syncretic iconography doctrine and the eschatology-parallel doctrine: the two end-times are read together - the Norse doom-cast as the old age the Christian end replaces, and the cross's Christ-figure as the renewal's answer to the renewal-halls of Voluspa.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the carved-eschatology family: each a tradition's end-time carved for a mixed audience; the Gosforth one is distinctive in the deliberate pairing of dooms.\n\nThe cross's companion stones in the same churchyard (the hogback graves and the lesser cross-shafts) document a sculpture-school's whole repertoire: the mixed congregation's dead buried under mixed imagery - the Norse beast-ornament and the Christian vine-scroll on one stone. And the cross's dating (c. 930-950, the generation of the Norse settlement's conversion in the north-west) fixes the record's context: the mission's accommodation is datable to the second generation of settlers, when the old mythology was carved into the new faith's stone rather than suppressed. The record thus anchors the carved-myth corpus's English evidence: the same Ragnarok-scenes documented in Sweden's picture-stones and in Cumbria's crosses - one myth-cycle, two media, one conversion-generation.\n\nThe cross's workshops-context completes the record: the Cumbrian sculpture-school's style (the Viking-age vine-scroll and the Norse beast-ornament interlaced) documents a mixed workshop of Norse and English carvers - the iconography's syncretism produced by the community's own mixed hands. And the eschatology-pairing completes the doctrine: the panels read downward from the crucifixion? - the ordering debated - but the juxtaposition's intent is fixed: the two end-times taught together, Ragnarok as the old age's doom and Christ's victory as the new age's answer - the mission's visual catechism. The record thus anchors the carved-eschatology family: the same myth-cycle documented in Sweden's picture-stones and in England's crosses, one conversion-generation apart."
    },
