    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic; Latin (Passio Olavi)",
    canonical_translation: "Samuel Laing, Heimskringla, London 1844 (public domain); Passio Olavi per paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The St-Olaf cycle is the north's royal martyrdom, and its doctrine is death-as-sanctity. The battle: Olaf, exiled king, returns with a small host to reclaim Norway and falls at Stiklestad against the bonders (the peasant-levy raised against the king's law); the saga's death-scene: the king wounded, the blindness of the fight, the body hidden in the sand by the river. The incorruption: the body is found a year later 'with? - the? - hair? - and? - nails? - grown, 'sweet? - smelling? - 'and? - 'the? - sand? - 'about? - it? - 'pure? - the incorruption-clause documented as the cult's proof; the translation to Nidaros (the Christ-church), the shrine's founding, and the cult's explosion: pilgrims, cures, the miracles recorded in the Passio Olavi (the blind, the lame, the mad healed at the grave).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the martyr-king doctrine and the shrine-state doctrine: the king's violent death is read as martyrdom (the law-king killed by the lawless), and the shrine becomes the kingdom's own sanctity - the patron of Norway, whose cult the medieval state and church both served. And the saga's politics: Snorri's St-Olaf is the kings' saga's longest book - the martyr-cult as the dynasty's charter. In the atlas, the record anchors the martyr-king family: each a ruler sanctified by a violent death; the Nidaros one is distinctive in the incorruption-clause and the state-cult.\n\nThe battle's politics: Olaf returned from exile with a Swedish-Norwegian host and fell against the peasant-levy raised by the lords he had lawed against - the martyrdom's ambiguity documented in the saga itself, which records the bonders' case as well as the king's. And the cult's explosion is dated precisely: the translation to Nidaros within a year of the death, the shrine's founding in the Christ-church, and the miracles' spread through the north - the martyr-king's cult as the state's own legitimacy, served by crown and church alike. The record thus completes the kings'-saga corpus's arc: the Ynglings' god-kings of the heathen age and the martyr-king of the Christian one are the same institution - the ruler whose body is the realm's collateral - now paid in sanctity rather than in blood.\n\nThe battle's politics completes the doctrine: the bonders' host was raised by the lords the king had lawed against, and the saga records their case as well as the king's - the martyrdom's ambiguity is the saga's own honesty, the law-king killed by men who claimed law's cause. And the cult's spread completes the record: the shrine at Nidaros became the north's greatest pilgrimage-site, the Passio Olavi's miracle-books served the cult across Europe, and the king's cult became the Norwegian state's charter - crown and church both serving the martyr's altar. The record thus anchors the martyr-king family: the realm's collateral paid in sanctity, distinctive in the incorruption-clause and the state-cult."
    },
