    source: {
      archive: "handrit.is / archive.org",
      work: "Oddr Snorrason, Olafs saga Tryggvasonar; Hallfredar saga; ed. Islenzk Fornrit VIII",
      url: "https://handrit.is/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (older editions); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Olaf Tryggvason, the baptised viking-king, converts Norway and the Norse west by force and mission: the image-breaking, the forced baptisms, the missions to Iceland. And the sagas' finest episode is the poet's choice: Hallfred the troubadour? - the court-poet 'would? - not? - 'leave? - 'his? - 'faith? - 'with? - the? - 'old? - gods? - 'and? - the? - king? - 'grants? - 'him? - 'baptism? - 'and? - 'keeps? - 'his? - 'love? - the record is the conversion-king's portrait and the conversion-economy's cost as the poets documented it.",
      zh: "\u53D7\u6D17\u7684\u7DAD\u4EAC\u570B\u738B\u5967\u62C9\u592B\xB7\u7279\u91CC\u683C\u74E6\u677E\u4EE5\u6B66\u529B\u548C\u50B3\u6559\u4F7F\u632A\u5A01\u53CA\u5317\u6B50\u897F\u90E8\u7688\u4F9D\uFF1A\u7834\u9664\u795E\u50CF\u3001\u5F37\u884C\u65BD\u6D17\u3001\u6D3E\u9063\u50B3\u6559\u5718\u9060\u8D74\u51B0\u5CF6\u3002\u800C\u85A9\u8FE6\u6700\u7CBE\u5F69\u7684\u6BB5\u843D\u662F\u300C\u8A69\u4EBA\u7684\u6289\u64C7\u300D\uFF1A\u5BAE\u5EF7\u8A69\u4EBA\u54C8\u723E\u5F17\u96F7\u5FB7\u4E0D\u9858\u6368\u68C4\u5C0D\u820A\u795E\u7684\u4FE1\u4EF0\uFF0C\u570B\u738B\u537B\u70BA\u4ED6\u65BD\u6D17\u3001\u4E26\u4FDD\u5168\u5176\u611B\u60C5\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u7688\u4F9D\u4E4B\u738B\u7684\u8096\u50CF\uFF0C\u4E5F\u662F\u8A69\u4EBA\u7B46\u4E0B\u7688\u4F9D\u7D93\u6FDF\u7684\u4EE3\u50F9\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Oddr Snorrason's saga per paraphrase; Hallfredar saga in Islenzk Fornrit VIII",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Tryggvason-missions are the conversion-economy's political narrative, and the sagas' finest episode is its human cost. The missions: the king converts Norway 'by? - 'fire? - and? - 'baptism? - (the image-breaking at the temples, the forced baptisms at the feasts) and sends missionaries to Iceland and the west (the Thangbrand mission of the Icelandic record); the saga-portrait: the king as the conversion's enforcer and its martyr-figure (he falls at Svolder, 1000, the year of Iceland's conversion - the sagas' paired dates).\n\nThe poet's choice: Hallfredr, the court-skald, 'would? - 'not? - 'renounce? - 'his? - 'faith? - 'with? - the? - old? - gods? - 'and? - the? - king? - 'says: 'then? - 'be? - 'baptized? - 'and? - 'keep? - 'thy? - 'love? - 'the? - conversion-contract: baptism accepted, love kept - the poet converts on the terms of his own heart (the saga's clause: he keeps his beloved in Iceland), and the conversion-economy's cost is the poet's biography - the skald caught between the old gods' verse and the new king's faith.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the conversion-enforcer doctrine and the poet's-choice doctrine: the mission-king's violence documented as the north's conversion-mechanism, and the poets' conversion-costs as the record's human ledger. In the atlas, the record anchors the mission-king family: each a ruler converting by force; the Tryggvason one is distinctive in the poets' testimonies.\n\nThe missions' enforcement is documented in its costs: the image-breaking at the Norse temples (the saga's burning of the hof at Trondheim), the forced baptisms at the assemblies, and the missionaries' violence abroad (Thangbrand's killings in Iceland). And the record's finest human document is the poets': Hallfredr's conversion-contract (baptism for love kept), and the sagas' testimony that the skaldic art survived conversion intact - the mead-myth's craft outlasting its gods. The conversion-economy's ledger thus runs both ways: the king's force converts the north, and the poets' craft converts the force into literature - the Tryggvason-missions documented as both politics and poetry.\n\nThe king's saga-frame completes the doctrine: the Tryggvason-cycle is the conversion-economy's founding narrative, and its finest literary product is the poets' testimony - Hallfredr's contract (baptism for love kept) and the skalds' survival of the conversion with their craft intact, the mead-myth's art outlasting its gods. And the missions' geography completes the record: Norway, Iceland, the Faroes, Shetland and Orkney converted in one reign's program - the north's faith-change as a single political operation, documented king by king and farm by farm. The record thus anchors the mission-king family: the ruler as the conversion's enforcer, distinctive in the poets' ledger of its costs."
    },
