    license: "Public domain (older editions); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Gudmundr Arason, bishop of Holar (1203-1237), is Iceland's folk-saint of the common people: a bishop of charity (he gave away the see's goods), of exorcisms (the ghosts bound by his mass), and of posthumous miracles (the cures at his mound at? - the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - the record is the folk-saint's saga: the wonder-working bishop whose cult ran outside the church's formal canonization.",
      zh: "\u970D\u62C9\u4E3B\u6559\u53E4\u5FB7\u8499\u5FB7\xB7\u963F\u62C9\u677E\uFF081203-1237\u5728\u4EFB\uFF09\u662F\u51B0\u5CF6\u5E73\u6C11\u7684\u6C11\u9593\u8056\u5F92\uFF1A\u4E00\u4F4D\u6148\u5584\u4E3B\u6559\uFF08\u6563\u76E1\u6559\u5EA7\u8CA1\u7269\uFF09\u3001\u9A45\u9B54\u4E3B\u6559\uFF08\u4EE5\u5F4C\u6492\u7E1B\u9B3C\uFF09\uFF0C\u6B7B\u5F8C\u66F4\u986F\u5947\u8E5F\uFF08\u58B3\u524D\u6CBB\u7652\u4E4B\u8AAA\u4E0D\u7D55\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u6C11\u9593\u8056\u5F92\u7684\u85A9\u8FE6\uFF1A\u4E00\u4F4D\u884C\u5947\u8E5F\u7684\u4E3B\u6559\uFF0C\u5176\u5D07\u62DC\u5728\u6559\u6703\u6B63\u5F0F\u518A\u5C01\u4E4B\u5916\u81EA\u767C\u6D41\u50B3\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Biskupa sogur III, Islenzk Fornrit XVIII; paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Gudmundr-saga is the folk-saint's biography, and its doctrine is charity-as-sanctity. The bishop's career: consecrated bishop of Holar in 1203, he gave away the see's goods to the poor (the charity-clause that the church's officials disputed), fought the powerful with the poor's claims, and - the saga's supernatural register - performed exorcisms (the ghosts bound by his mass) and cures. The posthumous cult: after his death (1237), the cures at his grave and mound were recorded in the saga's later chapters - the folk-cult of the 'holy Gudmundr', which ran outside the formal canonization (never canonized, venerated for centuries).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the folk-saint doctrine and the charity-sanctity doctrine: the wonder-worker whose sanctity is measured in the poor's relief, and whose cult is the people's own - the Icelandic church's popular canonization, documented in the saga's miracle-chapters. In the atlas, the record anchors the folk-saint family: each a locally venerated wonder-worker; the Icelandic one is distinctive in the never-canonized status.\n\nThe saga's B-version expands the posthumous cult in detail: the cures at the mound, the visions of the poor at the grave, and the bishop's own intervention in the household crises of the north - the folk-saint as the region's ongoing patron rather than a past event. And the record's institutional irony is the saga's own: the formal church never canonized Gudmundr, while the people's cult ran for centuries - the Icelandic church's canonization process and its popular counterpart ran in parallel, and the saga preserves both voices: the bishops' discipline and the household's devotion. In the folk-cult's economy, the wonder-worker's authority is measured in the poor's testimony - the same ledger the seeress-profession kept, but in Christian coin.\n\nThe bishop's conflicts complete the doctrine: the saga records his disputes with the powerful chiefs and with the church's own officials (the goods-giving disputed as mismanagement), so the folk-saint's career is documented in both registers - the bishops' discipline and the people's devotion. And the posthumous cult's geography completes the record: the cures at his grave at Holar and at his mound, the visions of the poor, the local feast-days kept into the modern era - the never-canonized cult's continuity documented from the saga to the present. The record thus anchors the folk-saint family: sanctity measured in the poor's relief, distinctive in the cult's extra-institutional persistence. And the record's documentary form completes the corpus: the A-version's political biography and the B-version's miracle-register preserve both the historian's and the people's Gudmundr - the folk-saint whose authority is the poor's testimony, documented in the saga's two voices and in the cult's centuries of unbroken local practice."
    },
