    language: "Old Swedish (runic)",
    canonical_translation: "Readings per Rundata (S\xF6 Fv1954;20) and Magnus K\xE4llstr\xF6m; paraphrase-synthesis",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Kvinneby amulet is the north's most personal runic prayer-object. The object: a small copper plate, worn (the suspension-hole), inscribed on both sides with a runic formula; per the standard readings, the text invokes Thor's protection for the wearer ('may? - Thor? - protect? - the? - bearer? - the? - 'against? - 'fever? - 'and? - 'peril? - 'at? - sea? - the formula's two offices: health and sea-voyage. The doctrine: the god's protection as a wearable inscription - the amulet as a portable prayer, and the hammer-god as the mariner's and the sick man's guardian.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the wearable-prayer doctrine and the Thor-protection doctrine: the myth corpus's most direct runic address to Thor, evidence that the god's cult ran in personal protective practice, not only in temple-rites. In the atlas, the record anchors the protective-amulet family: each a wearable divine inscription; the Kvinneby one is distinctive in its copper plate and Thor-address.\n\nThe amulet's find-context (a grave-field at Kvinneby, recovered in 1954) and its material - a copper plate cut and folded for wearing - document the practice's portability: the prayer is not inscribed on a church-wall or a runestone but on the body itself, suspended at the neck. Its doctrine completes the runic corpus's range: the Rok stone writes myth for a churchyard audience, the Velanda stone hallows a boundary, and the Kvinneby plate carries the god into the wearer's shirt - protection as a technology of the person. And its Thor-address is the record's sharpest datum: the hammer-got invoked not for battle but for fever and sea-voyage, the two perils the Swedish farmer's household actually faced.\n\nThe amulet's reading-history completes the record: the corrosion and the small letters made the text's reconstruction a scholarly project (the readings refined from the first publication to Kallstrom's analysis), so the record's provenance is reconstructed - the myth survives through the philologists' successive restorations, as the Rok stone's does. And the object's biography completes the doctrine: the copper cut, folded and worn, deposited in the grave-field at the owner's death - the prayer's life-cycle documented from wearing to burial. The record thus anchors the protective-amulet family: the god's protection as a wearable inscription, distinctive in the Thor-address and the plate's intimacy. The doctrine's comparative placement completes the runic corpus: the same alphabet that writes the Rok stone's mythic archive and the Velanda stone's boundary-law here writes a private prayer - the runic register running from state-charter to body-worn petition, with Thor as the one god addressed across all three."
    },
