    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Odin, disguised as Grimnir ('the masked one'), is tortured by King Geirrod between two fires for eight nights until his son Agnar gives him a drink; freed by the draught, the god recites the whole cosmological litany: the named halls of the gods (Valhalla, Thrudheim, Breidablik, Himinbjorg, Glitnir, Valaskjalf, Sokkvabekk, Gimle), the rivers, the world-tree Yggdrasil's anatomy, the einherjar's feasting, and the doom-knowledge of his torturer's own fate. The poem is the corpus's single most complete cosmological document: a geography of heaven delivered as a torture-victim's boast.",
      zh: "\u5967\u4E01\u55AC\u88DD\u70BA\u300C\u8499\u9762\u8005\u300D\u683C\u91CC\u59C6\u5C3C\u723E\uFF0C\u88AB\u84CB\u723E\u7F85\u5FB7\u738B\u56DA\u65BC\u5169\u706B\u4E4B\u9593\u6298\u78E8\u516B\u591C\uFF0C\u76F4\u5230\u5176\u5B50\u963F\u683C\u7D0D\u5949\u4E0A\u4E00\u676F\u9152\uFF1B\u98F2\u7F77\uFF0C\u795E\u660E\u6717\u8AA6\u51FA\u6574\u90E8\u5B87\u5B99\u980C\u6B4C\uFF1A\u8AF8\u795E\u7684\u5177\u540D\u5927\u5EF3\uFF08\u74E6\u723E\u54C8\u62C9\u3001\u65AF\u9B6F\u5FB7\u6D77\u59C6\u3001\u5E03\u840A\u9054\u5E03\u5229\u514B\u3001\u6D77\u59C6\u6BD4\u6B50\u3001\u683C\u5229\u7279\u5C3C\u723E\u3001\u74E6\u62C9\u65AF\u5361\u723E\u592B\u3001\u7D22\u514B\u74E6\u8C9D\u514B\u3001\u5409\u59C6\u840A\uFF09\u3001\u8AF8\u6CB3\u3001\u4E16\u754C\u6A39\u7684\u7D50\u69CB\u3001\u82F1\u9748\u6230\u58EB\u7684\u5BB4\u5E2D\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u5176\u6298\u78E8\u8005\u81EA\u8EAB\u547D\u904B\u7684\u672B\u65E5\u77E5\u8B58\u3002\u6B64\u8A69\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u6700\u5B8C\u6574\u7684\u5B87\u5B99\u8AD6\u6587\u737B\uFF1A\u4EE5\u53D7\u5211\u8005\u7684\u81EA\u8A87\u5F62\u5F0F\u50B3\u6388\u7684\u5929\u754C\u5730\u7406\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); consulted against Dronke 1997",
    narrative: {
      en: "Grimnismal is the myth corpus's cosmological litany in narrative dress, and its frame gives the teaching its price. The prose frame (in the Codex Regius): Odin and Frigg watch their foster-sons Geirrod and Agnar; Frigg's deception sends Geirrod to the throne and Odin to a wanderer's torture: bound between two fires for eight nights, 'no one? - none came to him? - 'save? - Agnar, the son, 'gave? - him? - 'a? - horn? - of? - drink. The freed god's recitation is then the poem's body: stanza by stanza, the halls are named with their functions (Valhalla's five hundred and forty doors; Thrudheim, Thor's; Breidablik, Baldr's, 'where? - no? - unclean? - thing? - is; Himinbjorg, Heimdall's at the bridge's edge; Gimle, 'more? - beautiful? - than? - the? - sun), the rivers of the world run their catalog (the eddic hydrology, from Sid and Vid to the venom-streams), and the tree's anatomy is given: three roots, three wells, the eagle, the squirrel, the harts, the wounds.\n\nThe teaching's mechanism is the riddle of disguise: the torturer does not know he is torturing the cosmologist, and the draught of mead (given by the displaced son) converts the victim into the lecturer - wisdom in this corpus is always priced (nine nights on the tree; an eye in Mimir's well; eight nights between the fires). And the poem's ending is its doctrine: Odin names Geirrod's fate ('the? - sword? - of? - 'thy? - own? - 'son? - shall? - kill? - thee? - Fjolnir's? - descendant? - and Geirrod, rising to free him, falls on his own sword - the cosmology taught at the cost of the torturer's life, the lecture-fee collected at once.\n\nThe record's cultural functions: the hall-catalog is the corpus's liturgy of sacred places (the template for temple-dedications and the kenning-system's geography); the Yggdrasil-stanzas are the world-tree doctrine's fullest verse; and the frame documents the wanderer-disguise motif (Grimnir, like Gangleri and Gestumblindi) - the god inspecting kings under a false name, teaching by ordeal. In the atlas, the record anchors the cosmological-litany family: each a tradition's geography of heaven recited in verse; the Norse one is distinctive in being delivered under torture.\n\nFor the database, this whole-poem node complements the thematic records it feeds: the Yggdrasil record, the Valhalla record, the Bifrost record and the Gimle record each cite Gr\xEDmnism\xE1l's stanzas; this node holds the poem complete, as one arc from torture to cosmology to doom-prophecy."
    },
