    id: "norse-poetic-edda-vafthrudnismal",
    title: {
      en: "Vafthrudnismal: Odin Wagers His Head on the World's Biography",
      zh: "\u300A\u74E6\u592B\u7279\u9B6F\u5FB7\u5C3C\u723E\u4E4B\u6B4C\u300B\uFF1A\u5967\u4E01\u4EE5\u982D\u9871\u62BC\u6CE8\u4E16\u754C\u4E4B\u50B3\u8A18"
    },
    tradition: "norse",
    category: "myth-religion",
    category_note: "wisdom-contest catechism",
    region: {
      en: "Vafthrudnir's hall in Jotunheim (symbolic pin at Jotunheimen, Norway)",
      zh: "\u7D04\u9813\u6D77\u59C6\u74E6\u592B\u7279\u9B6F\u5FB7\u5C3C\u723E\u4E4B\u5EF3\uFF08\u8C61\u5FB5\u6027\u5B9A\u4F4D\u65BC\u632A\u5A01\u5C24\u901A\u9ED1\u9580\u5C71\uFF09"
    },
    lat: 61.7,
    lon: 8.5,
    period: "c. 900-1000 CE; principal MS Codex Regius GKS 2365 4to",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923)",
      work: "Vafthrudnismal (complete poem); critical ed. Ursula Dronke (Poetic Edda II, 1997)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Odin travels to the all-wise giant Vafthrudnir under the name Gagnrad and stakes his head on a question-contest: the questions run through the whole mythic chronology - the oldest days (Ymir, the rime-giants, Bergelmir's flood), the cosmology (Njord's homes, the sun and moon, day and night, the winds, Yggdrasil), the creation of humans (Ask and Embla), and the eschatology (the Fimbulwinter, the survivors Lif and Lifthrasir, Baldr's return). The giant answers all - until Odin asks the one question only Odin can know: 'what did Odin whisper in Baldr's ear before the pyre?' - and the giant concedes: 'my head I forfeit; I met the wisest.' The poem is the corpus's chronology-catechism: mythic knowledge as a gambling-game with the head as stake.",
      zh: "\u5967\u4E01\u5316\u540D\u5CA1\u683C\u62C9\u5FB7\u4F86\u5230\u5168\u667A\u5DE8\u4EBA\u74E6\u592B\u7279\u9B6F\u5FB7\u5C3C\u723E\u8655\uFF0C\u4EE5\u982D\u9871\u70BA\u6CE8\u9032\u884C\u554F\u7B54\u7AF6\u8CFD\uFF1A\u554F\u984C\u8CAB\u7A7F\u6574\u500B\u795E\u8A71\u5E74\u8868\u2014\u2014\u592A\u53E4\uFF08\u5C24\u5F4C\u723E\u3001\u971C\u5DE8\u4EBA\u3001\u8C9D\u683C\u723E\u7C73\u723E\u7684\u6D2A\u6C34\uFF09\u3001\u5B87\u5B99\u8AD6\uFF08\u5C3C\u5967\u5FB7\u7684\u5C45\u6240\u3001\u65E5\u6708\u3001\u665D\u591C\u3001\u8AF8\u98A8\u3001\u4E16\u754C\u6A39\uFF09\u3001\u4EBA\u985E\u4E4B\u9020\uFF08\u963F\u65AF\u514B\u8207\u6069\u5E03\u62C9\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u672B\u4E16\uFF08\u82AC\u5E03\u723E\u4E4B\u51AC\u3001\u5016\u5B58\u8005\u5229\u592B\u8207\u5229\u592B\u7279\u8D6B\u62C9\u897F\u723E\u3001\u5DF4\u723E\u5FB7\u6B78\u8FD4\uFF09\u3002\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4E00\u4E00\u4F5C\u7B54\u2014\u2014\u76F4\u5230\u5967\u4E01\u554F\u51FA\u53EA\u6709\u5967\u4E01\u80FD\u7B54\u7684\u4E00\u554F\uFF1A\u300C\u5DF4\u723E\u5FB7\u767B\u706B\u846C\u5806\u524D\uFF0C\u5967\u4E01\u5728\u4ED6\u8033\u908A\u4F4E\u8A9E\u4E86\u4EC0\u9EBC\uFF1F\u300D\u5DE8\u4EBA\u8A8D\u8F38\uFF1A\u300C\u6211\u7684\u982D\u8F38\u4E86\uFF1B\u6211\u9047\u898B\u4E86\u6700\u6709\u667A\u6167\u8005\u3002\u300D\u6B64\u8A69\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u5E74\u8868\u554F\u7B54\u66F8\uFF1A\u795E\u8A71\u77E5\u8B58\u4F5C\u70BA\u4EE5\u982D\u9871\u70BA\u6CE8\u7684\u8CED\u5C40\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); consulted against Dronke 1997",
    narrative: {
      en: "Vafthrudnismal is the myth corpus's chronology-exam, and its stakes give the doctrine its edge. The frame: Odin, under the name Gagnrad, visits the giant Vafthrudnir, 'wise? - 'for? - 'seeking? - knowledge; the terms are set in the hall's law: the guest's head is forfeit if he cannot answer. The contest's questions are the myth-cycle's chronology in exam-form: the oldest days (whence came Ymir? whence the rime-giants? how came Bergelmir's flood?), the cosmology (whence the moon and sun? the day and night? the winds? what under Yggdrasil's roots?), the creation (whence the first humans? 'ask? - 'how? - 'ask? - 'and? - 'embla? - came? - to? - the? - shore? - of? - no? - strength? - 'the? - - the Ask-Embla catechism), and the eschatology (what of the Fimbulwinter? who shall survive? what after the wolf's work? - the renewal-doctrine: Lif and Lifthrasir in the holt, Baldr's return, the sun's daughter).\n\nThe giant's answers are the poem's value: each is a doctrine stated in two lines, and Snorri mined the poem wholesale for Gylfaginning (the Bergelmir stanza, the Ask-Embla stanza, the renewal-stanzas are the catechism's sources verbatim). The contest's end is the myth's signature move: Odin asks the unanswerable - 'what? - 'spake? - 'Odin? - 'himself? - 'in? - 'his? - son's? - ear? - 'before? - the? - pyre? - and Vafthrudnir concedes at once: 'no? - one? - 'knows? - 'that? - 'save? - 'thou? - 'who? - 'spakest? - it? - 'and? - Baldr? - 'who? - heard? - 'it? - 'my? - head? - I? - forfeit? - 'for? - 'I? - have? - met? - 'the? - 'wisest? - the exam's last question is the examiner's identity: the visitor is the god himself, and the knowledge he asks for is his own secret.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the wisdom-contest doctrine (knowledge as a gambling-ritual with the head as stake - the same economy as Heidrek's riddle-contest and Vegtam's waking) and the chronology-catechism doctrine (the myth-cycle's timeline taught as question-answer, the corpus's own textbook-form). And the Odin-disguise motif completes the corpus: Gagnrad, like Grimnir and Gestumblindi, is the god testing keepers of knowledge under a false name - the wanderer's audit. In the atlas, the record anchors the wisdom-contest family: each a tradition's catechism as a wagered game; the Norse one is distinctive in the head-stake and the withheld answer.\n\nFor the database, this whole-poem node holds the poem complete, complementing the thematic records it feeds: the Bergelmir record, the Ask-Embla record, the renewal record and the sun-moon record each cite Vafthrudnismal's stanzas; here the exam runs whole, from the void to the last question."
    },
