    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); consulted against Dronke 1997 and Olive Bray 1908",
    narrative: {
      en: "Havamal - 'the lay of the High One' - is the myth corpus's composite wisdom-book, and its four strata are one document's anatomy. The gnomic stratum (st. 1-79): the guest-craft maxims ('all? - 'doorways? - 'before? - 'you? - 'go? - in? - 'should? - 'be? - 'spied? - out? - the north's host-ethics), wealth's vanity ('wealth? - 'dies? - 'as? - 'friends? - die? - 'and? - 'the? - 'spear? - 'spares? - none), and the fame-doctrine ('cattle? - die? - 'and? - 'kinsmen? - die? - 'but? - the? - 'fame? - of? - 'a? - dead? - man's? - deeds? - 'never? - dies? - the corpus's central ethical formula, repeated in three stanzas with variations on the second life (the judgment-day of each man's? - 'the? - 'one? - 'thing? - 'that? - 'never? - dies: the fame of deeds).\n\nThe Odin-episodes (st. 96-110, 138-165): the god's autobiography in the first person - the Billing's-daughter deception ('the? - 'maid? - 'who? - 'promised? - 'by? - night? - 'and? - found? - 'me? - mocked? - at? - dawn), the Gunnlod-mead theft ('Gunnlod? - 'gave? - me? - 'on? - the? - 'golden? - seat? - 'drink? - of? - the? - precious? - mead? - 'and? - 'I? - 'paid? - her? - 'rath? - 'well? - 'for? - 'her? - 'true? - faith? - the trickster's self-accounting), the rune-winning (the nine nights on the tree, myself to myself), and the eighteen songs with the eighteenth withheld (the dead-horse clause). The Loddfafnir-stratum (st. 111-137): the injunctions - commandments to a learner ('hear? - 'thou? - Loddfafnir? - 'and? - 'take? - 'the? - counsel: rise? - not? - at? - night? - save? - to? - serve? - or? - guard? - - the behavioral code: fire-watch, ale-craft, wife-craft, weapon-craft, the north's etiquette as commandment.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the ethical-charter doctrine and the autobiography-doctrine: the poem is the corpus's moral textbook (quoted by the sagas for conduct) and the god's self-portrait (the thefts, the seductions, the sacrifice told by the thief himself). And its composite nature is the critical datum: four strata (gnomic, mythic, instructional, love-abuse) compiled in the Codex Regius as one lay - the tradition's own anthology, preserved as a single document. In the atlas, the record anchors the wisdom-book family: each a tradition's proverb-collection with a divine speaker; the Norse one is distinctive in the god's first-person crimes.\n\nFor the database, this whole-poem node complements the rune-record (norse-havamal-runes holds st. 138-145): here the poem runs whole, from guest-craft to the eighteenth song."
    },
