    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "A. G. Brodeur, Snorri Sturluson: The Prose Edda, New York 1916 (public domain); consulted against Faulkes 1987",
    narrative: {
      en: "Gylfaginning complete is the myth corpus's master-narrative, and this record holds it as one arc rather than as the episode-records it feeds. The prologue: Snorri's learned-Christian opening - the Aesir as Asia-men, Odin as migrating chief, the temples' founding - the safety-frame in which the catechism runs. The journey: King Gylfi of Sweden, after losing land to Gefjon's plough, travels to Asgard as Gangleri ('the weary wanderer') and finds a hall where three enthroned men - High, Equal-high, Third - answer questions in the catechumen's order.\n\nThe catechism's arc runs the whole mythic chronology: the void and Ymir (the ice-fire cosmogony, Audhumla, Buri), the world's building (flesh, blood, skull, the Dvergatal, Midgard from the eyebrows), the sun-moon-day-night machinery (Mundilfari's children, Svalinn, the wolves), the norns and the wells, the halls and gods in profile (Thor, Baldr, Njord, Freyr, Freyja, Tyr, Bragi, Heimdall, Hodr, Vidar, Vali, Ullr, Forseti), Loki's brood and its bindings (Fenrir, the serpent, Hel; T\xFDr's hand; Gleipnir's six ingredients), the death of Baldr (Loki's arrow, the funerals, Hermothr's ride, Thokk's refusal), Loki's binding (the net, the entrails, the bowl, the earthquakes), the Ragnarok-forecast (the Fimbulwinter, the wolves, Naglfar, the battles: Odin-Fenrir, Thor-serpent, Tyr-Garm, Freyr-Surt, Heimdall-Loki, Thor's nine paces), and the renewal (the green earth, Baldr's return, Gimle, Lif and Lifthrasir). Then the exposure: the hall vanishes, the field is empty, and Gylfi walks home with the stories - the catechism's container dissolved.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the compilation-doctrine and the survival-doctrine: Snorri's synthesis reconciles the eddic poems into a single narrative (quoting Voluspa, Vafthrudnismal, Grimnismal as scripture), and it is the document through which the myths survived - without Gylfaginning, most of the corpus would exist only in fragmentary verse. And the frame's irony is the record's critical datum: a Christian scholar preserving a doomed religion's stories by presenting them as an illusion that vanishes. In the atlas, the record anchors the master-summary family: each a tradition's whole theology in one narrative; Snorri's is distinctive in dissolving at the end.\n\nFor the database, this whole-text node complements the frame-record (norse-gylfaginning-frame holds the frame-story and its pedagogy): here the catechism runs complete, void to renewal.\n\nThe catechism's internal economy completes the document: each answer is sourced ('so it is said' - the refrain quoting the eddic poems as scripture), so the whole text is an exegesis machine - the myths' first systematic prose treatment, in which Voluspa, Vafthrudnismal and Grimnismal are reconciled into one chronology. And the record's survival-claim is the critical datum: most of the world's knowledge of Norse myth runs through this one document - the catechism's apparatus (the named horses, the six Gleipnir-ingredients, the renewal-roster) exists nowhere else in prose, so the whole-text node documents both the myths and the document that carries them."
    },
