    license: "Public domain (Morris & Magnusson 1870); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The great heroic saga as a single arc: Odin's genealogy (from Sigi to Volsung), the apple-sign of ancestry, Signy and Sigmund's vengeance (the wolf-cloak, Sinfjotli, the burning hall), Sigmund's sword Gram from Odin, the dragon-slaying of Sigurd (Fafnir, Regin, the birds, Brynhild), the oath with the Gjukungs, the shape-change that wins Brynhild, the betrayal and bed-death, Gudrun's vengeance on Atli, and the last sons' stone-stoning at Jormunrek's hall. The saga is the corpus's great genealogy-of-doom: one family from god-given sword to the Rhine's swallowed gold.",
      zh: "\u5049\u5927\u7684\u82F1\u96C4\u85A9\u8FE6\u4F5C\u70BA\u55AE\u4E00\u5F27\u7DDA\uFF1A\u5967\u4E01\u7684\u4E16\u7CFB\uFF08\u81EA\u897F\u5409\u81F3\u6C83\u723E\u677E\u683C\uFF09\u3001\u7956\u5148\u4E4B\u860B\u679C\u7684\u5FB5\u5146\u3001\u897F\u683C\u59AE\u8207\u897F\u683C\u8499\u5FB7\u7684\u5FA9\u4EC7\uFF08\u72FC\u76AE\u3001\u8F9B\u83F2\u7D04\u7279\u5229\u3001\u711A\u71D2\u5927\u5EF3\uFF09\u3001\u5967\u4E01\u6240\u8CDC\u5BF6\u528D\u683C\u62C9\u59C6\u3001\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u5C60\u9F8D\uFF08\u6CD5\u592B\u7D0D\u3001\u96F7\u91D1\u3001\u773E\u9CE5\u3001\u5E03\u502B\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\uFF09\u3001\u8207\u5409\u70CF\u57FA\u5144\u5F1F\u7ACB\u8A93\u3001\u5316\u8EAB\u8D0F\u5F97\u5E03\u502B\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\u3001\u80CC\u53DB\u8207\u5E8A\u4E0A\u4E4B\u6B7B\u3001\u53E4\u5FB7\u502B\u5C0D\u963F\u7279\u5229\u7684\u5FA9\u4EC7\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u672B\u4EE3\u4E4B\u5B50\u5728\u7D04\u723E\u8499\u96F7\u514B\u5EF3\u4E2D\u906D\u64F2\u77F3\u4E4B\u5211\u3002\u6B64\u85A9\u8FE6\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u5B8F\u5927\u547D\u904B\u4E16\u7CFB\uFF1A\u81EA\u795E\u8CDC\u5BF6\u528D\u81F3\u840A\u8335\u541E\u91D1\u7684\u4E00\u65CF\u4E4B\u53F2\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "William Morris and Eirikr Magnusson, Volsunga Saga, London 1870 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Volsunga saga is the corpus's heroic master-saga, and this record holds the whole arc. The genealogy: Odin's line from Sigi (the god's son, slayer of a thrall, a sea-king's line) to Volsung, whose hall is built around the apple-sign ('a? - 'apple? - 'from? - 'the? - 'god? - 'cast? - 'in? - her? - lap? - the ancestry-token: Hljod the valkyrie's apple, the apple of dises? - 'the? - sign? - of? - the? - d\xEDsir? - promising the great child; and Signy and Sigmund's vengeance-plot (the shape-shifting into wolves, Sinfjotli's years in the wolf-coat, the hall burned with the Sigar? - with the Sigurd? - with the Siggeir's kin inside, Signy entering the fire after her revenge).\n\nThe Sigurd-arc: Sigmund's death (Odin's spear breaking Gram on the battlefield - the god withdrawing his gift), the smith Regin raising the orphan, the reforged sword, the dragon Fafnir and the hoard's curse (Andvari's ring), the birds' counsel, the waking of Brynhild and the oath, the Gjukungs' hall and the shape-change that wins Brynhild for Gunnar, the double marriage, the queens' quarrel, the murder in bed, Brynhild's death on the pyre. The Gudrun-arc: the marriage to Atli, the warning-ring with wolf's hair, the brothers' deaths, the sons served at the feast, the bed-kill and the burning; and the last arc: Svanhild's trampling and the sons stoned at Jormunrek's - the saga closing on the corpus's most complete fate-architecture, all of it prophecied in Gripir's opening.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the saga-as-synthesis doctrine (the prose saga knitting the eddic heroic poems into one narrative - the same learned-compiler work as Gylfaginning, in the heroic register) and the doom-genealogy doctrine (a family's whole biography from god-gift to gold's deposit, each generation paying the last's debts). And its reception is the record's critical datum: the saga is the source of the whole European Sigurd-Niflung tradition (the German Nibelungenlied's parallel, Wagner's Ring at one remove) - the most transmitted heroic text of the north. In the atlas, the record anchors the saga-cycle family: each a tradition's complete heroic biography; the Norse one is distinctive in Odin's active role as the family's engineer and destroyer.\n\nFor the database, this whole-text node complements the episode-records it feeds (Gripir, the birds, Brynhild, Gudrun-Atli, Hamdir-Sorli): here the saga runs whole.\n\nThe saga's craft completes the doctrine: the verse-insets (quoted from the Edda-tradition of Sigurd-poems) run inside the prose like the saga's own score, and the genealogy's arithmetic is the plot's engine - every generation inherits the last's debts (the sword's shards, the ring's curse, the oath's forfeit), so the family's biography is a ledger. And its modern afterlife is the record's reception-datum: Wagner's Ring, the operatic tradition and the fantasy-literature's dragon-slayers all descend from this one saga - the most transmitted heroic text of the north, documented here whole."
    },
