    lat: 49.6,
    lon: 8.4,
    period: "c. 1000-1200 CE; principal MS Codex Regius GKS 2365 4to; Volsunga prose parallel",
    source: {
      archive: "sacred-texts.com (Bellows 1923)",
      work: "Fafnismal (complete); critical ed. Ursula Dronke (Poetic Edda II, 1997)",
      url: "https://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/poe/"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The dragon-slaying poem in full: Sigurd hides in the pit, Gram meets the dragon's belly; the dying Fafnir - the greedy brother transformed - curses the gold and teaches the victor doom-lore (the Norns, the world-tree, the Fimbulwinter? - the knowledge-dying exchange), and warns that Regin will betray him. Regin drinks the dragon's blood, demands the heart, and threatens; Sigurd beheads him as he sleeps, and the birds complete the counsel. The poem is the dragon-dialogue's classic form: a dying monster teaching its slayer wisdom.",
      zh: "\u5C60\u9F8D\u4E4B\u6B4C\u5168\u7BC7\uFF1A\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u4F0F\u65BC\u5751\u4E2D\uFF0C\u683C\u62C9\u59C6\u76F4\u523A\u9F8D\u8179\uFF1B\u5782\u6B7B\u7684\u6CD5\u592B\u7D0D\u2014\u2014\u88AB\u8CAA\u5A6A\u8B8A\u5F62\u7684\u5144\u5F1F\u2014\u2014\u8A5B\u5492\u9EC3\u91D1\uFF0C\u5411\u52DD\u5229\u8005\u50B3\u6388\u5384\u904B\u4E4B\u5B78\uFF08\u547D\u904B\u4E09\u5973\u795E\u3001\u4E16\u754C\u6A39\u2026\u2026\uFF09\uFF0C\u4E26\u8B66\u544A\u96F7\u91D1\u5FC5\u5C07\u80CC\u53DB\u3002\u96F7\u91D1\u98F2\u9F8D\u8840\u3001\u7D22\u8981\u9F8D\u5FC3\u3001\u63DA\u8A00\u5831\u5FA9\uFF1B\u897F\u683C\u9B6F\u5FB7\u8D81\u5176\u719F\u7761\u65AC\u5176\u9996\uFF0C\u773E\u9CE5\u5B8C\u6210\u737B\u7B56\u3002\u6B64\u8A69\u662F\u9F8D\u4E4B\u5C0D\u8AC7\u7684\u7D93\u5178\u5F62\u5F0F\uFF1A\u5782\u6B7B\u7684\u602A\u7269\u5411\u8A85\u6BBA\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u82F1\u96C4\u50B3\u6388\u667A\u6167\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Fafnismal is the dragon-dialogue's classic form, and its doctrine is wisdom from the doomed. The slaying: Sigurd digs the pit on the dragon's water-road and stabs upward with Gram - 'and? - 'the? - 'dragon's? - 'blood? - 'ran? - 'down? - 'on? - him? - the wound-scene; the dying Fafnir's exchange is the poem's core: the dragon curses the gold ('my? - 'brother? - 'Regin? - 'shall? - 'be? - 'thy? - 'death? - 'and? - 'the? - 'hoard? - 'thy? - ruin), asks Sigurd's name and lineage (the doomed refusing to die ignorant), and - the poem's signature - the monster teaches the victor: 'tell? - 'me? - 'of? - the? - norns? - 'who? - 'themselves? - begat? - the? - father? - of? - the? - gods? - 'the? - doom-lore catechism: the Norns, the world-tree's root and the corpse-hound? - the dragon's exam, answered by the hero, knowledge passing from the dying to the killing.\n\nRegin's end: the smith drinks the blood, carves the heart, demands it roasted; Sigurd touches it, burns his finger, licks - the birds' speech arrives (the record norse-sigurd-birds-tongue holds the scene); Regin threatens ('the? - 'gold? - 'alone? - 'has? - 'killed? - 'my? - brother? - 'the? - 'gold? - 'shall? - 'kill? - 'thee? - too) and Sigurd beheads him - the smith's last speech the poem's moral: the curse's arithmetic stated by its next victim. And the poems' closing counsel: the birds bid Sigurd ride to the fire-hill, wake the valkyrie, and take the ring - the doom-lore becomes the itinerary.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the dying-teacher doctrine (wisdom from the monster to its slayer - the corpus's signature exchange, from Baldr's dreams to the dragon) and the transformation-curse doctrine (the greedy brother become dragon: the hoard's keeper as the gold's own shape). And its relation to the saga: the prose (Volsunga chs. 18-19) follows the poem closely - verse-to-prose transmission again, the same method as Ynglingatal into Ynglinga saga. In the atlas, the record anchors the monster-dialogue family: each a dying monster teaching wisdom; the Norse one distinctive in the exam-structure.\n\nFor the database, this whole-poem node complements the episode-records (the birds' counsel; the Andvari-curse; Gripir's prophecy): here the dragon-dialogue runs whole.\n\nThe poem's exam-structure completes the doctrine: the dying dragon's questions ('who are thy fathers? what of the norns? what of the world's end?') run the myth-cycle's catechism as a deathbed-exam, answered by the slayer - wisdom's final transfer documented in dialogue-form, the monster as the tradition's last teacher. And the bird-coda completes the arc: the counsel that sends Sigurd to the fire-hill makes the doom-lore operational - the dragon's teaching becomes the itinerary, the wisdom and the plot one mechanism."
    },
    functions: [
      "Whole-poem node: the dragon-dialogue complete, from the pit to the birds",
      "Dying-teacher doctrine: wisdom passing from the doomed monster to its slayer",
      "Transformation-curse: the greedy brother become the hoard's own shape",
      "Verse-to-prose datum: the saga follows the poem closely"
    ],
