    lat: 65.55,
    lon: -20.28,
    period: "Grettis saga Asmundarsonar 14th century (events set c. 1000-1031); principal MSS 14th c.; English: Morris & Magnusson 1869",
    source: {
      archive: "archive.org / handrit.is",
      work: "Grettis saga Asmundarsonar (complete); ed. Islenzk Fornrit VII (1936); English: The Story of Grettir the Strong, Morris & Magnusson 1869",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/storyofgrettirth00gree"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Morris & Magnusson 1869); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The outlaw's biography as a single arc: Grettir's childhood of ungovernable strength, the killings that begin his outlawry, the wrestling-match with the draugr Glamr and its curse (the fear of the dark), the years of outlawry in the highlands, the last refuge on the island Drangey, and the death by sorcery (the mistress-bones? - the hag's enchanted log) and the peaceful exile-reputation: a strongman haunted by the dead his whole career, dying not in battle but by a curse. The record is the saga corpus's great tragic biography of the undead-haunted hero.",
      zh: "\u4EA1\u547D\u8005\u7684\u50B3\u8A18\u4F5C\u70BA\u55AE\u4E00\u5F27\u7DDA\uFF1A\u683C\u96F7\u8482\u723E\u6840\u9A41\u4E0D\u99B4\u7684\u7AE5\u5E74\u4E4B\u529B\u3001\u958B\u555F\u653E\u9010\u751F\u6DAF\u7684\u6BBA\u622E\u3001\u8207\u5C4D\u9B3C\u683C\u62C9\u59C6\u7684\u89D2\u529B\u53CA\u5176\u8A5B\u5492\uFF08\u754F\u61FC\u9ED1\u6697\uFF09\u3001\u9AD8\u5730\u4E0A\u7684\u6D41\u4EA1\u6B72\u6708\u3001\u5FB7\u862D\u683C\u5CF6\u4E0A\u6700\u5F8C\u7684\u907F\u96E3\u6240\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u6B7B\u65BC\u5DEB\u8853\uFF08\u5DEB\u5AD7\u65BD\u5492\u7684\u6F02\u6D41\u6728\uFF09\u2014\u2014\u4E00\u500B\u7D42\u751F\u70BA\u4EA1\u9748\u6240\u56F0\u7684\u5F37\u4EBA\uFF0C\u6700\u7D42\u4E0D\u662F\u6B7B\u65BC\u6230\u9663\uFF0C\u800C\u662F\u6B7B\u65BC\u8A5B\u5492\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u85A9\u8FE6\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u300C\u4EA1\u9748\u7E8F\u8EAB\u82F1\u96C4\u300D\u6700\u60B2\u58EF\u7684\u50B3\u8A18\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "William Morris and Eirikr Magnusson, The Story of Grettir the Strong, London 1869 (public domain); Islenzk Fornrit VII",
    narrative: {
      en: "Grettis saga is the saga corpus's great tragic biography, and this record holds the whole arc beyond the Glamr-episode (norse-draugr-glamr). The youth: Grettir's childhood of lazy strength ('the? - 'boy? - 'did? - nothing? - 'but? - 'sit? - 'by? - 'the? - fire? - 'and? - 'carve? - wood? - the famous scene: carved? - 'he? - 'slammed? - the? - door? - 'so? - that? - the? - timber? - cracked), the first killings, and the outlawry's start. The middle: the Glamr-duel and its curse (the fear of the dark, the career's sentence - the record norse-draugr-glamr holds the episode), the years of highland outlawry (the fights with the dead? - with the living in the lair of the? - 'the? - 'killing? - of? - 'the? - 'barrow-dweller? - the Karr-episode: Grettir enters the mound and wins the treasure from its barrow-wight - the second undead-combat), and the last refuge: the island Drangey, where Grettir holds out with his brother Illugi for years against the landholders' attempts.\n\nThe end: the landholders hire the sorceress Thuridr - 'the? - 'old? - woman? - 'carves? - runes? - 'on? - 'a? - drift-log? - 'and? - 'floats? - it? - to? - the? - island - and the log's strike breaks Grettir's sword; he kills it? - he cuts? - 'the? - 'log? - 'with? - 'the? - sword? - 'and? - 'the? - 'blade? - 'breaks? - 'the? - wound? - 'festered? - 'and? - he? - died? - of? - it - the hero killed by the curse-log, not by men; and the aftermath: Illugi's death, the brotherhood of the? - and the legend's coda: Grettir's curse (he 'would? - 'be? - 'the? - strongest? - 'of? - his? - line? - 'but? - 'never? - 'outlawed? - 'twice? - - the fate-clause), and the reputation's peace: the saga's last chapters give Grettir's reputation to his brother Thorstein Drangey-mund? - the brother's vindication in Miklagard (the Varangian coda - the saga's Byzantine ending, where Thorstein serves the emperor and returns rich).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the tragic-biography doctrine (the strongman's career as a curse-schedule: strength, outlawry, the dark-fear, the log) and the undead-haunting doctrine's sustained form: the saga documents the restless dead as the hero's occupational hazard (Glamr, the barrow-wight, the island's ghosts) - the corpus's most sustained undead-narrative. And its reception is the record's critical datum: the saga was the model for Beowulf-scholarship's 20th-century debates (the Glamr-scene as the Grendel-analog, the barrow-scene as the dragon-treasure-scene) - the Icelandic saga as the English poem's documented twin. In the atlas, the record anchors the tragic-outlaw family: each a strongman's biography structured by a curse; the Norse one is distinctive in the sorceress-log and the Varangian coda.\n\nFor the database, this whole-text node complements the Glamr-record: here the saga runs whole, from the carved door to the log's strike."
    },
