    canonical_translation: "Readings per Rundata; Bugge, Wess\xE9n, Antonsen, Page; paraphrase-synthesis",
    narrative: {
      en: "The runic corpus is the myth tradition's oldest writing, and this record holds the selected inscriptions as one register. The chronological arc: the futhark's dedications and the Blekinge stones' curse-law (7th c.) - the earliest monuments' legal-theological register; the R\xF6k stone's mythic-literary archive (c. 800) - the number-riddle poem with Theodoric and (per the strongest modern reading) an eschatology: sun and moon swallowed, sea over earth, the ice-cold wolf; the Viking-age runestones' memorial-industry (thousands raised in the 10th-11th centuries, the Jelling charter at their head); and the personal register: the Velanda hallowing, the Glavendrup outlaw-curse, the Kvinneby amulet's prayer.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the epigraphic-register doctrine (the myth tradition's oldest documentary layer, independent of the manuscripts) and the writing-as-law doctrine (memorial, curse, hallowing and claim as one alphabet's offices). And its comparative value for the database is the cross-check: the runic monuments corroborate the manuscript corpus (the R\xF6k stone's pre-Viking mythic narrative; the Jelling stones' conversion dated independently of Adam of Bremen; the Thor-hallowing formulas confirming the god's cult in land-law) - the archaeology of myth, readable in stone.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the epigraphic-corpus family: each a tradition's oldest writing carrying its myths; the runic one is distinctive in spanning personal prayer to state-charter. The individual inscriptions each carry their own record (R\xF6k, Jelling, Velanda, Kvinneby, Bj\xF6rketorp, Glavendrup-Tryggev\xE6lde); this node holds the register complete.\n\nThe corpus's disciplinary method completes the record: the readings themselves are scholarship in action (the Rok cipher-sections recovered by successive generations of runologists; the Kvinneby corrosion resolved by comparative formulae), so the runic register's myths exist only through the philologists' successive restorations - the database's own method (provenance, reconstruction, review) modeled on the stones. And the register's geographic spread completes the corpus: Norway, Sweden, Denmark, Gotland and the Norse west (the Manx and Cumbrian crosses) - one alphabet serving the whole Norse world, from the 7th-century curse-law to the 11th-century prayer-plate.\n\n The register's documentary method completes the corpus: each inscription's readings are the philologists' successive restorations (the Rok cipher-sections recovered over a century; the Kvinneby corrosion resolved by comparative formulae), so the runic myths exist only through scholarship in action - the database's own provenance-method modeled on the stones, with reconstruction and review flagged record by record. And the register's geographic spread completes the whole: Norway, Sweden, Denmark, Gotland and the Norse west - one alphabet serving the entire Norse world, from the 7th-century curse-law to the 11th-century prayer-plate, the oldest documentary layer of the myth tradition."
    },
