    summary: {
      en: "The corpus's master-poem in full: the compelled seeress recites the world's whole biography in sixty-six stanzas - the void, Ymir, the sun and moon, the first humans, the golden age, the first war, Gullveig, the norns, Baldr's death, the genealogy of doom (Loki's brood), the Fimbulwinter, the doom-litany, the battles, the renewal (Baldr's return, Gimle, the last pair), and the dragon flying from the corpse-shore. The poem is the north's prophetic history of the world and the skeleton of all Norse mythography.",
      zh: "\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u6838\u5FC3\u9577\u8A69\u5168\u7BC7\uFF1A\u53D7\u8FEB\u7684\u5973\u5148\u77E5\u4EE5\u516D\u5341\u516D\u7BC0\u8A69\u53E5\u8FF0\u8AAA\u4E16\u754C\u7684\u5B8C\u6574\u50B3\u8A18\u2014\u2014\u9D3B\u8499\u3001\u5C24\u5F4C\u723E\u3001\u65E5\u6708\u3001\u6700\u521D\u4EBA\u985E\u3001\u9EC3\u91D1\u6642\u4EE3\u3001\u7B2C\u4E00\u5834\u6230\u722D\u3001\u53E4\u723E\u7DAD\u683C\u3001\u547D\u904B\u4E09\u5973\u795E\u3001\u5DF4\u723E\u5FB7\u4E4B\u6B7B\u3001\u672B\u65E5\u65CF\u8B5C\uFF08\u6D1B\u57FA\u4E4B\u7FA4\uFF09\u3001\u82AC\u5E03\u723E\u4E4B\u51AC\u3001\u672B\u65E5\u980C\u3001\u8AF8\u6230\u3001\u66F4\u65B0\uFF08\u5DF4\u723E\u5FB7\u6B78\u8FD4\u3001\u5409\u59C6\u840A\u3001\u672B\u4E16\u96D9\u89AA\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u81EA\u5C4D\u9AB8\u4E4B\u6FF1\u98DB\u51FA\u7684\u5DE8\u9F8D\u3002\u6B64\u8A69\u662F\u5317\u65B9\u7684\u4E16\u754C\u9810\u8A00\u53F2\uFF0C\u4E5F\u662F\u6240\u6709\u5317\u6B50\u795E\u8A71\u66F8\u5BEB\u7684\u9AA8\u67B6\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); consulted against Dronke 1997",
    narrative: {
      en: "Voluspa complete is the myth corpus's master-poem, and this record holds it as one vision. The frame: Odin compels the vala - the dead or trance-bound seeress - to recite before 'the sons of Heimdall' (the frame-record norse-voluspa-sibyl holds the performance); the recitation is the poem's body, and its architecture is the world's biography in six movements. The creation-movements: the void ('there? - 'was? - 'no? - sand? - 'no? - sea? - 'no? - cool? - waves), Ymir and the rime-giants, the sun and moon set on courses, the first humans of Ask and Embla, the golden age and its end (the three giant-maidens with gold), the first war (Gullveig thrice burned, the Vanir's broken oaths).\n\nThe fate-movements: the norns at the well ('lawless? - 'they? - 'were? - born? - 'till? - 'three? - came), the deaths of Baldr (the mistletoe, the blind Hodr, Loki's forcing), Loki's brood (the wolf, the serpent, the hag of the iron-wood), the Fimbulwinter and the doom-litany ('an? - axe-age? - 'a? - sword-age? - 'a? - wind-age? - 'a? - wolf-age? - 'till? - 'the? - world? - falls), the battles (Naglfar, the Muspell-riders, Odin-Fenrir, Thor-serpent, the nine paces, Surt's fire). And the renewal-movement: the green earth from the sea, Baldr and Hodr's return, Gimle, the game-pieces found in the grass, Lif and Lifthrasir - and the dragon flying from the corpse-shore, corpses in its feathers, 'and? - 'now? - 'she? - will? - sink.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the master-poem doctrine (the corpus's complete cosmology and eschatology in one vision, the skeleton on which Gylfaginning was built) and the prophecy-genre doctrine (the world's history recited by an unwilling professional woman - myth as a compelled, paid, gendered performance). And the poem's two versions are the record's critical datum: the Codex Regius text (canonical) and the Hauksbok variant (adding the utiseta-frame, the giant-sibyl's title, and other stanzas) - the master-text's own manuscript-history documented. In the atlas, the record anchors the prophetic-history family: each a tradition's whole world-story recited by a seer; the Norse one is distinctive in sixty-six stanzas and an unwilling voice.\n\nFor the database, this whole-poem node complements the frame-record and the thematic records it feeds (the creation, war, Baldr, renewal records): here the vision runs whole, void to dragon.\n\nThe poem's architecture completes the doctrine: the six movements are symmetric (creation and renewal share the same plain, the same game-pieces, the same sun), so the vision is built as a palindrome of the world's biography - the end written to mirror the beginning. And the seeress's voice completes the genre: the refrain 'do you understand yet, or what?' punctuates the stanzas like an exam-instructor's challenge - prophecy as pedagogy, the audience required to answer, the corpus's own reader-address built into the master-poem."
    },
