    narrative: {
      en: "Atlakvida is the Atli-story's compact verse-telling, and its doctrine is the gift-economy's death-logic. The lure: Atli's messenger promises the brothers the Rhine-gold ('rings? - 'and? - 'the? - tribute? - of? - Hun-land? - 'the? - - and the brothers' reply is the poem's most famous clause: they knew the trap and rode - 'gold? - 'they? - 'could? - not? - refuse? - the gift-economy's death-wish stated as ethics: to refuse a lord's summons is cowardice, so the invitation is a death-sentence accepted in gift-currency. Gudrun's warning is the ring with wolf's hair wound in it - the coded signal - and the messenger lies.\n\nThe test-scenes: the heart-demand (the thrall's trembling heart exposed as false, Hogni laughing while they cut - 'the? - 'brave? - 'heart? - 'beat? - 'little? - 'when? - 'laid? - 'on? - 'the? - 'hand), the snake-pen and Gunnar's harp (the toes striking the strings, the serpents lulled, one adder striking - the harp's last note), and Gudrun's vengeance (the sons' hearts served at the feast, the bed-kill, the burning). The lay's closing is the corpus's grief-clause: Gudrun's answer to the sea ('the? - 'waves? - 'bore? - me? - 'to? - 'another? - shore? - - the vengeance-cycle's continuation, and the poem's final doctrine: 'so? - 'shall? - 'a? - 'sister? - 'mourn? - 'her? - brothers? - 'the? - record's? - ethic of kin-grief as the fate's own register.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the gift-economy's death-logic doctrine (the invitation as sentence, acceptance as honor) and the compact-verse doctrine: the lay is the corpus's shortest complete heroic narrative (thirty-nine stanzas), the saga's story told in a tenth of the prose - the eddic economy of narrative. And its relation to the Atli-prose (Volsunga chs. 39-44) and to the Greenland lay Atlamal (the dialogue-version) is the record's comparative datum: one story, three tellings, the corpus's transmission documented. In the atlas, the record anchors the vengeance-lay family: each a tradition's kin-feud in verse; the Norse one is distinctive in the harp's toes and the served hearts.\n\nFor the database, this whole-poem node complements the Gudrun-Atli record (which synthesizes the prose): here the lay runs whole.\n\nThe lay's Attila-kernel completes the record: Atli is the tradition's memory of the Hunnish king (Attila, d. 453, died in his bed - the saga's bed-kill as the historical king's death re-read as vengeance), so the lay documents the migration-age's memory in heroic verse - the north's oldest historical trauma converted to myth. And the grief-clause completes the doctrine: the closing counsel ('so shall a sister mourn her brothers') makes the vengeance-cycle's cost its own ethic - the corpus's most compact statement that victory and mourning are one payment."
    },
