    license: "Public domain (Laing 1844); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Snorri's kings' history as a single document: sixteen sagas running from the mythic opening (Ynglinga saga: Odin's migration, the euhemerist charter, the Yngling death-catalog) through the viking kings (Harald Fairhair's unification, the conversion-kings Olaf Tryggvason and Olaf Haraldsson) to the medieval dynasties - the myth-to-history arc of a whole kingdom, in which the gods are the kings' ancestors and the myths are the dynasty's charter. The record is the corpus's great reception-document: mythology as royal history.",
      zh: "\u65AF\u8AFE\u91CC\u7684\u738B\u8005\u53F2\u4F5C\u70BA\u55AE\u4E00\u6587\u737B\uFF1A\u5341\u516D\u90E8\u85A9\u8FE6\u81EA\u795E\u8A71\u958B\u7BC7\uFF08\u5C24\u6069\u683C\u6797\u52A0\u85A9\u8FE6\uFF1A\u5967\u4E01\u9077\u5F99\u3001\u7336\u8D6B\u9EA5\u723E\u4E3B\u7FA9\u61B2\u7AE0\u3001\u82F1\u683C\u6797\u6B7B\u4EA1\u6E05\u55AE\uFF09\uFF0C\u7D93\u7DAD\u4EAC\u8AF8\u738B\uFF08\u7D71\u4E00\u632A\u5A01\u7684\u91D1\u9AEE\u54C8\u62C9\u723E\u3001\u7688\u4F9D\u4E4B\u738B\u5967\u62C9\u592B\xB7\u7279\u91CC\u683C\u74E6\u677E\u8207\u5967\u62C9\u592B\xB7\u54C8\u62C9\u723E\u677E\uFF09\uFF0C\u76F4\u81F3\u4E2D\u4E16\u7D00\u738B\u671D\u2014\u2014\u4E00\u500B\u738B\u570B\u81EA\u795E\u8A71\u5230\u6B77\u53F2\u7684\u5B8C\u6574\u5F27\u7DDA\uFF0C\u5176\u4E2D\u8AF8\u795E\u5373\u570B\u738B\u7684\u7956\u5148\uFF0C\u795E\u8A71\u5373\u738B\u671D\u7684\u61B2\u7AE0\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u6700\u5B8F\u5927\u7684\u63A5\u53D7\u53F2\u6587\u737B\uFF1A\u795E\u8A71\u4F5C\u70BA\u738B\u5BB6\u6B77\u53F2\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Samuel Laing, Heimskringla, London 1844 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Heimskringla - 'the circle of the world' (its opening words) - is the myth corpus's great reception-document, and this record holds the arc whole. The mythic opening: Ynglinga saga's euhemerist charter (the Aesir from Asia, Odin's migration, the temple-endowments, the death-laws, the Yngling death-catalog quoted from Thjodolfr's Ynglingatal) - the gods historicized as migrating chiefs, the dynasty's ancestors documented from the god-house of Uppsala. The viking middle: Halfdan the Black's dreams (the prose-history's own prophecy-motifs), Harald Fairhair's unification and his hair-vow, the conversion-kings (Olaf Tryggvason's fall at Svolder in the year of Iceland's conversion; Olaf Haraldsson's martyrdom at Stiklestad and the incorruption at Nidaros) - the mythic motifs (dreams, omens, the martyr-cult) running inside the political history.\n\nThe medieval close: Magnus the Good and Harald Hardrada (the varangian king, dying at Stamford Bridge in 1066 - the saga's last great battle-date), down to Magnus Erlingsson - the dynasty's history continuous from Odin to the 12th century. The record's cultural functions are the reception-doctrine (myth converted into royal history - the euhemerist method deployed for legitimacy: the kings descend from gods whose divinity was a mistake) and the charter-doctrine: Heimskringla is the Norwegian monarchy's founding-book, its mythology the dynasty's title-deeds. And the source-method is the record's critical datum: Snorri quotes the skaldic poems as his evidence (Ynglingatal, the lays of the battle-poets) - the historian citing verse as documents, the myth-corpus's own evidentiary practice.\n\nIn the atlas, the record anchors the myth-to-history family: each a tradition's charter-document converting the gods into ancestors; Snorri's is distinctive in quoting its own verse-sources. For the database, this whole-text node complements the Ynglinga-record and the king-records it feeds: here the kingdom's arc runs whole, god to king.\n\nThe compilation's architecture completes the record: sixteen sagas in one frame (the opening words 'Heimskringla' - the circle of the world - naming the whole as a ring), with the mythic opening and the Christian close bracketing the viking middle - the kingdom's arc designed as a document, not a chronicle. And the conversion-clause completes the myth-history junction: the two Olafs' martyrdoms and miracles are told in the same evidentiary style as Odin's migration, so the myth-register and the hagiographic register are one continuous method - the king's body as the realm's collateral from heathen sacrifice to Christian shrine.\n\n The compilation's evidentiary method completes the record: Snorri names and quotes his skaldic sources in the text (Ynglingatal for the Ynglings, the battle-lays for the viking kings), so the myth-to-history arc is documented with its own apparatus - the historian citing verse as documents, which is the myth corpus's own evidentiary practice institutionalized. And the compilation's afterlife completes the corpus: Heimskringla became the Norwegian kingdom's founding-book (its copy-history running through the medieval state)s hands), the mythology as the dynasty)s title-deeds - the reception-document that made the gods the kings) ancestors in law as well as in story."
    },
