    license: "Public domain (Press 1899); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The great family saga of the Laxardal: the settlement-generation's gold (Melkorka the Irish princess, sold as a thrall and ransomed), the rise of the \xD6ssur-line, and the saga's famous core - Gu\xF0r\xFAn \xD3sv\xEDfsd\xF3ttir's four dreams (the water-laden hat, the silver ring from the lake, the gold ring, and the battle-woman's helm) interpreted as her four marriages, and her marriages' tragedies: Kjartan and Bolli's friendship broken, the killing, Bolli's slaying of Kjartan, the vengeance, and the saga's last word - Gu\xF0r\xFAn's answer to the question 'whom do you love most?' ('I? - 'was? - 'worst? - 'to? - him? - whom? - 'I? - loved? - most). The record is the mythic motifs' social register: dreams as prophecy, omen-reading as daily practice, and fate as marriage.",
      zh: "\u62C9\u514B\u65AF\u5CFD\u8C37\u7684\u5049\u5927\u5BB6\u65CF\u85A9\u8FE6\uFF1A\u62D3\u6B96\u4E16\u4EE3\u4E4B\u9EC3\u91D1\uFF08\u611B\u723E\u862D\u516C\u4E3B\u6885\u723E\u79D1\u723E\u5361\u6DEA\u70BA\u5974\u96B8\u5F8C\u88AB\u8D16\u56DE\uFF09\u3001\u5967\u8607\u723E\u4E00\u65CF\u7684\u8208\u8D77\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u85A9\u8FE6\u8457\u540D\u7684\u6838\u5FC3\u2014\u2014\u53E4\u5FB7\u502B\xB7\u5967\u65AF\u7DAD\u592B\u65AF\u591A\u8482\u723E\u7684\u56DB\u500B\u5922\uFF08\u76DB\u6C34\u4E4B\u5E3D\u3001\u6E56\u4E2D\u9280\u74B0\u3001\u91D1\u74B0\u3001\u6301\u68B0\u5973\u6B66\u8005\u4E4B\u76D4\uFF09\uFF0C\u88AB\u89E3\u70BA\u5979\u7684\u56DB\u6BB5\u5A5A\u59FB\uFF1B\u800C\u5A5A\u59FB\u7684\u60B2\u5287\u96A8\u4E4B\u800C\u4F86\uFF1A\u57FA\u4E9E\u723E\u5766\u8207\u535A\u5229\u7684\u53CB\u8ABC\u7834\u88C2\u3001\u6BBA\u622E\u3001\u535A\u5229\u5F12\u57FA\u4E9E\u723E\u5766\u3001\u5FA9\u4EC7\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u85A9\u8FE6\u7684\u7D50\u8A9E\u2014\u2014\u7576\u88AB\u554F\u300C\u4F60\u6700\u611B\u8AB0\u300D\u6642\uFF0C\u53E4\u5FB7\u502B\u7B54\u9053\uFF1A\u300C\u6211\u6700\u611B\u7684\u4EBA\uFF0C\u5F85\u4ED6\u6700\u58DE\u3002\u300D\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u795E\u8A71\u6BCD\u984C\u7684\u793E\u6703\u5C64\u9762\uFF1A\u5922\u5373\u9810\u8A00\u3001\u89E3\u5146\u70BA\u65E5\u5E38\u5BE6\u8E10\u3001\u547D\u904B\u5373\u5A5A\u59FB\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Muriel Press, The Laxdale Saga, London 1899 (public domain); Islenzk Fornrit V",
    narrative: {
      en: "Laxdaela saga is the family-sagas' masterpiece of fate-as-marriage, and its mythic register is the dreams. The dream-interpretation: Gu\xF0r\xFAn's four dreams told to Gestr the wise ('the? - 'dream-interpreter? - 'of? - the? - saga) and read as her four marriages - the hat heavy with water (the first marriage, weeping), the silver ring from the lake (the second, lost), the gold ring (the third, the king's? - 'the? - 'gold? - 'ring? - 'a? - king's? - son), and the helm of the battle-woman ('the? - 'fourth? - 'dream: 'I? - 'fancied? - I? - wore? - a? - helm? - 'of? - 'the? - 'wolf? - - the valkyrie-helm dream read as Kjartan: 'a? - 'warrior? - 'woman? - 'shall? - be? - 'thy? - fate? - - the four dreams as the four fates, prophecy domesticated into marriage-counsel.\n\nThe marriages' tragedies: Gu\xF0r\xFAn wed to Thorvald (divorced by her own wit - the trouser-legs clause), then to Thord, then to Kjartan's rival Bolli (the love-triangle's politics: Kjartan abroad in Norway, engaged to the king's sister Hildigunnr, returning to find Gu\xF0r\xFAn married), the feud's escalation (Kjartan's cattle-raids on Bolli's farm, the ambush at Svinadale? - Kjartan killed by Bolli 'of? - all? - men? - 'the? - dearest? - to? - him), and the vengeance (Gu\xF0r\xFAn's sons killing Bolli - the family consuming itself).\n\nThe saga's last scene is the corpus's most quoted marriage-fate: the old Gu\xF0r\xFAn answering her son's question ('whom? - 'didst? - thou? - love? - best): 'I? - 'was? - 'worst? - 'to? - 'him? - 'whom? - 'I? - loved? - most? - - the fate-doctrine's social form: love as the engine of ruin. The record's cultural functions are the dream-prophecy doctrine (the dreams read as marriages - prophecy as household counseling, the interpreter as a professional) and the myth-in-the-social doctrine: the saga's thrall-princess (Melkorka, the Irish king's daughter sold into slavery, her identity proven by the Irish speech), the valkyrie-helm imagery and the fate-formula show the mythic motifs functioning inside ordinary family law - the myth corpus's social register. In the atlas, the record anchors the dream-fate family: each a saga reading dreams as biography; the Laxdaela one is distinctive in the four-dream sequence.\n\nFor the database, this record documents how the mythic motifs (prophecy, fate, valkyrie-imagery, gold) run in the family-sagas - the myth-tradition's everyday operation.\n\nThe saga's gold-register completes the myth-in-the-social doctrine: Melkorka's ransoming (the Irish speech proving the princess-thrall's identity) and Hrosteolf's? - Hroskarr's? - the gold-hoarding episodes document wealth as the family's fate-engine, the same gold-economy the heroic corpus theorizes operating in household law. And the women's register completes the record: Gu\xF0r\xFAn, Melkorka, Hildigunnr and thethers? - the saga's women as its plot-engines - the family saga as the myth-motifs' social laboratory, where prophecy, fate and gold run through marriage-law."
    },
