    license: "Public domain (Dasent 1861); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The saga corpus's epic of law and fate: Gunnar of Hl\xED\xF0arendi, the champion who never wished to leave ('the? - 'famous? - 'slope-clause: 'how? - 'fair? - the? - slope? - 'is? - 'so? - fair? - 'to? - behold? - 'the? - 'bright? - 'wheat-field? - 'than? - 'to? - 'go? - 'under? - the? - woe - refusing exile for the land's beauty), his death at home by the law's doom (he would not bow his head), the lawyer Nj\xE1l's fate-foreknowledge ('the? - 'seer-lawyer: 'this? - 'shall? - 'be? - 'thy? - death? - 'the? - 'christening? - 'of? - thy? - sons? - and? - the? - burning), and the burning at Berg\xFE\xF3rshv\xE1ll that ends the family - with K\xE1ri's survival and the vendetta's closing. The record is the mythic motifs' legal register: oath, curse, fate and prophecy running inside the courts.",
      zh: "\u85A9\u8FE6\u8A9E\u6599\u4E2D\u6CD5\u5F8B\u8207\u547D\u904B\u7684\u53F2\u8A69\uFF1A\u8D6B\u5229\u9054\u502B\u8FEA\u7684\u69CD\u5C3C\u96C5\u723E\u2014\u2014\u90A3\u4F4D\u4E0D\u9858\u96E2\u9109\u7684\u52C7\u58EB\uFF08\u540D\u53E5\uFF1A\u300C\u9019\u5C71\u5761\u5982\u6B64\u79C0\u7F8E\uFF0C\u91D1\u8272\u9EA5\u7530\u5982\u6B64\u6085\u76EE\uFF0C\u5BE7\u9858\u6B7B\u5728\u6B64\u5730\uFF0C\u4E5F\u4E0D\u9858\u5728\u7570\u9109\u5C48\u819D\u300D\u2014\u2014\u4ED6\u62D2\u7D55\u6D41\u4EA1\uFF0C\u7518\u9858\u5C31\u6B7B\u65BC\u5BB6\u9580\uFF09\u3001\u4E0D\u80AF\u4F4E\u982D\u7684\u6B7B\u4EA1\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u5F8B\u5E2B\u5C3C\u96C5\u723E\u7684\u547D\u904B\u9810\u77E5\uFF08\u300C\u9019\u5C07\u662F\u4F60\u7684\u6B7B\uFF1A\u70BA\u5152\u5B50\u884C\u6D17\u79AE\uFF0C\u7136\u5F8C\u8FCE\u4F86\u706B\u711A\u300D\uFF09\u2014\u2014\u6700\u7D42\u8C9D\u683C\u7D22\u723E\u65AF\u74E6\u723E\u7684\u5927\u706B\u7D42\u7D50\u4E86\u6574\u500B\u5BB6\u65CF\uFF0C\u800C\u5361\u91CC\u5016\u5B58\u4E26\u4E86\u7D50\u5BBF\u6028\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u795E\u8A71\u6BCD\u984C\u7684\u6CD5\u5F8B\u5C64\u9762\uFF1A\u8A93\u8A00\u3001\u8A5B\u5492\u3001\u547D\u904B\u8207\u9810\u8A00\u5728\u6CD5\u5EAD\u4E4B\u5167\u904B\u884C\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Sir George Dasent, The Story of Burnt Njal, Edinburgh 1861 (public domain); Islenzk Fornrit XII",
    narrative: {
      en: "Nj\xE1ls saga is the saga corpus's epic of law, and its mythic register is the fate-motifs running inside the courts. The oath-and-doom register: Gunnar's oath-broken feud (the oath to leave Iceland broken at the shore - the famous clause: 'how? - 'fair? - the? - slope? - 'is? - 'so? - fair? - 'to? - behold? - 'the? - 'wheat-field? - 'than? - 'to? - go? - under? - 'woe? - 'and? - 'death? - 'he? - turned? - back? - 'and? - 'the? - 'outlaw's? - doom? - executed at home: he died 'shooting? - with? - the? - bow? - 'till? - the? - string? - broke? - 'and? - 'he? - died? - 'where? - he? - stood - the land-beauty as the fate's own argument), and Nj\xE1l's foreknowledge ('the? - 'lawyer's? - prophecy: 'the? - 'boy? - 'H\xF6skuld's? - death? - 'foretold? - 'at? - his? - christening? - 'the? - christening-prophecy clause: 'this? - 'shall? - 'be? - 'his? - death: 'that? - 'he? - 'shall? - 'be? - 'slain? - 'in? - 'the? - Althing's? - law-court? - - fate foretold in the church-rite, prophecy as christening-gift).\n\nThe burning: the burning at Berg\xFE\xF3rshv\xE1ll (the saga's great atrocity: the family burned in its hall, Nj\xE1l refusing to leave - 'he? - 'said? - 'he? - 'would? - 'die? - 'with? - his? - wife? - and? - sons? - the fate accepted as the lawyer's own counsel), the survival of K\xE1ri, and the vendetta's closing (the Althing's settlement and K\xE1ri's last rides). The record's cultural functions are the law-as-fate doctrine (the courts themselves as the fate's machinery: the legal outcomes - outlawry, compensation, settlement - execute the prophecies) and the fate-motif register: the saga's omens (the blood-rain, the dreams, the dead-speaking? - the ghost's warning at Hlidarendi) document the mythic motifs functioning inside legal procedure - the myth-tradition's law-court operation.\n\nAnd the saga's Christianity-frame is the record's critical datum: the conversion-scene is in the saga (the Godafoss-chapters, Nj\xE1l's own baptism discussed at the Thing), so the fate-motifs run inside the converted world - the mythic fate-doctrine persisting under Christian law. In the atlas, the record anchors the law-and-fate family: each a tradition's legal order run by fate-motifs; the Norse one is distinctive in the christening-prophecy and the land-beauty clause.\n\nFor the database, this record documents the mythic motifs' legal operation: oath, curse, prophecy and doom as the law-court's own machinery."
    },
