    lat: 38.14,
    lon: 22.55,
    period: "Compiled c. 1st-2nd century CE (attrib. Apollodorus; Frazer translation 1921)",
    source: {
      archive: "ToposText (J. G. Frazer, Loeb 1921)",
      work: "Apollodorus, Library, trans. J. G. Frazer (1921), \xA7\xA7 3.5.5-3.5.6",
      url: "https://topostext.org/work/150"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Frazer translation 1921)",
    summary: {
      en: "Antiope, daughter of Nycteus, was loved by Zeus and, threatened by her father, fled to Sicyon; pursued and captured, she gave birth to twins at Eleutherae, and the babies were exposed \u2014 a neatherd reared them, calling one Zethus and the other Amphion, who practised minstrelsy, for Hermes had given him a lyre. Grown, the brothers rescued their mother from the torments of Dirce, tied the woman to a bull, and \u2014 the two of them \u2014 built the walls of Thebes, the stones following Amphion's singing. And Amphion married Niobe, the story of her fourteen children being the price the family paid for one boast too many.",
      zh: "\u5B89\u63D0\u4FC4\u73C0\u70BA\u5B99\u65AF\u6240\u611B\u3001\u70BA\u7236\u6240\u9010\uFF0C\u65BC\u5384\u7409\u585E\u745E\u7522\u4E0B\u96D9\u751F\u5B50\uFF0C\u68C4\u65BC\u539F\u91CE\uFF1B\u7267\u4EBA\u6536\u990A\u53D6\u540D\u4EC4\u6258\u65AF\u8207\u5B89\u83F2\u7FC1\u2014\u2014\u5F8C\u8005\u5F97\u8D6B\u723E\u58A8\u65AF\u6240\u8CDC\u4E03\u5F26\u7434\u800C\u7FD2\u6A02\u3002\u5144\u5F1F\u9577\u6210\u5F8C\u6551\u51FA\u53D7\u8650\u7684\u6BCD\u89AA\uFF0C\u628A\u8FEB\u5BB3\u5979\u7684\u72C4\u723E\u523B\u7E1B\u65BC\u725B\u89D2\uFF1B\u7E7C\u800C\u7BC9\u5E95\u6BD4\u65AF\u57CE\u7246\u2014\u2014\u77F3\u584A\u96A8\u5B89\u83F2\u7FC1\u4E4B\u7434\u8072\u81EA\u52D5\u79FB\u4F86\u3002\u5B89\u83F2\u7FC1\u5A36\u5C3C\u4FC4\u67CF\uFF0C\u5176\u5341\u56DB\u5B50\u5973\u4E4B\u6B7B\u6210\u70BA\u6B64\u5BB6\u6700\u6158\u7684\u5831\u61C9\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sacred-place",
      "culture-hero"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; violence presented as historical material without endorsement."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Antiope was a daughter of Nycteus, and Zeus had intercourse with her. When she was with child, and her father threatened her, she ran away to Epopeus at Sicyon and was married to him. In a fit of despondency Nycteus killed himself, after charging his brother Lycus to punish Epopeus and Antiope; and Lycus marched against Sicyon, subdued it, slew Epopeus, and led Antiope away captive.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.5.5 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "On the way she gave birth to two sons at Eleutherae in Boeotia. The infants were exposed; but a neatherd found and reared them, calling one Zethus and the other Amphion. Zethus paid attention to cattle-breeding, but Amphion practised minstrelsy, for Hermes had given him a lyre.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.5.5 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Lycus and his wife Dirce imprisoned Antiope and treated her despitefully. But one day her bonds were loosed of themselves; unknown to her keepers she came to Mount Cithaeron, where her sons were pasturing, and was discovered by them; and the brothers fell upon Lycus and killed him. And they bound Dirce to a bull and let her die a wretched death \u2014 the pay-back, so the story goes, for the torment of their mother.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.5.5-3.5.6 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "Then, they say, the two brothers built the walls of Thebes, and the stones, when Amphion touched his lyre, moved of their own accord into their places in the wall \u2014 so that the city of Cadmus was finally rebuilt by a song; and Zethus married Thebe, after whom the city itself is named.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.5.6 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "5",
        text: "Amphion married Niobe, daughter of Tantalus, who bore him seven sons and seven daughters \u2014 or ten and ten, or two and three, or six and six, as the tellings differ. Being blessed with children, Niobe said that she was more blessed with children than Latona; and the goddess, stung by the taunt, incited Artemis and Apollo against them: Artemis shot down the females in the house, and Apollo killed all the males together as they hunted on Cithaeron. Niobe went to her father Tantalus at Sipylus, and there, on praying to Zeus, was transformed into a stone; and tears flow night and day from the stone.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.5.6 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
