    id: "greek-apollodorus-athens-founding",
    title: {
      en: "Erichthonius in the Chest: The Founding of Athens",
      zh: "\u7BB1\u4E2D\u5384\u745E\u514B\u6258\u5C3C\u4FC4\u65AF\uFF1A\u96C5\u5178\u4E4B\u8D77\u6E90"
    },
    tradition: "greek",
    category: "myth",
    region: {
      en: "Acropolis, Athens (symbolic pin)",
      zh: "\u96C5\u5178\u885B\u57CE\uFF08\u8C61\u5FB5\u6027\u5B9A\u4F4D\uFF09"
    },
    lat: 37.97,
    lon: 23.72,
    period: "Compiled c. 1st-2nd century CE (attrib. Apollodorus; Frazer translation 1921)",
    source: {
      archive: "ToposText (J. G. Frazer, Loeb 1921)",
      work: "Apollodorus, Library, trans. J. G. Frazer (1921), \xA7\xA7 3.14.5-3.14.7",
      url: "https://topostext.org/work/150"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Frazer translation 1921)",
    summary: {
      en: "When Cecrops died, Cranaus came to the throne \u2014 a son of the soil in whose reign the flood of Deucalion is said to have fallen \u2014 and named the country Atthis for a daughter who died a maid. Amphictyon expelled him and was in turn expelled by Erichthonius, whose birth the Athenians told at length: Athena came to Hephaestus for arms; the lame smith pursued her, and she, being a chaste virgin, wiped his seed from her leg with wool and threw it to the ground; and the earth, receiving it, bore Erichthonius, whom Athena reared in secret in a chest on the Acropolis. The sisters of Pandrosos opened the chest out of curiosity, saw the serpent coiled about the babe, and were driven mad and threw themselves from the rock; and Erichthonius, grown, expelled Amphictyon, became king of Athens, set up the wooden image of Athena in the Acropolis \u2014 and instituted the festival of the Panathenaea.",
      zh: "\u523B\u514B\u7F85\u666E\u65AF\u6B7B\u5F8C\u514B\u523A\u7459\u65AF\u7E7C\u4F4D\uFF08\u5176\u6642\u6070\u9022\u4E1F\u5361\u5229\u7FC1\u6D2A\u6C34\uFF09\uFF1B\u963F\u59C6\u83F2\u514B\u63D0\u7FC1\u9010\u4E4B\uFF0C\u53C8\u88AB\u5384\u745E\u514B\u6258\u5C3C\u4FC4\u65AF\u53D6\u4EE3\u3002\u50B3\u8AAA\uFF1A\u96C5\u5178\u5A1C\u8D74\u8D6B\u83F2\u65AF\u6258\u65AF\u8655\u6C42\u88FD\u6B66\u5668\uFF0C\u5320\u795E\u6C42\u611B\u88AB\u62D2\uFF0C\u7CBE\u6DB2\u843D\u65BC\u5973\u795E\u4E4B\u817F\uFF1B\u96C5\u5178\u5A1C\u4EE5\u7F8A\u6BDB\u62ED\u6DE8\u3001\u68C4\u65BC\u5730\u9762\uFF0C\u5927\u5730\u53D7\u4E4B\u800C\u751F\u5384\u745E\u514B\u6258\u5C3C\u4FC4\u65AF\u3002\u96C5\u5178\u5A1C\u5C07\u5B30\u5152\u85CF\u65BC\u7BB1\u5167\u7F6E\u885B\u57CE\uFF0C\u56D1\u6F58\u5FB7\u7F85\u7D22\u65AF\u52FF\u555F\uFF1B\u5176\u59CA\u59B9\u56E0\u597D\u5947\u5FC3\u555F\u7BB1\uFF0C\u898B\u86C7\u7E5E\u5B30\uFF0C\u53D7\u9A5A\u767C\u760B\u800C\u81EA\u885B\u57CE\u8E8D\u4E0B\u3002\u5384\u745E\u514B\u6258\u5C3C\u4FC4\u65AF\u9577\u6210\u70BA\u96C5\u5178\u738B\uFF0C\u7ACB\u96C5\u5178\u5A1C\u6728\u50CF\u65BC\u885B\u57CE\uFF0C\u4E26\u5275\u7ACB\u6CDB\u96C5\u5178\u5A1C\u7BC0\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "miraculous-birth",
      "sacred-kingship",
      "virgin-birth",
      "divine-smith"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "When Cecrops died, Cranaus came to the throne; he was a son of the soil, and it was in his time that the flood in the age of Deucalion is said to have taken place. He married a Lacedaemonian wife, Pedias, daughter of Mynes, and begat Cranae, Menaechme and Atthis; and when Atthis died a maid, Cranaus called the country Atthis.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.14.5 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Cranaus was expelled by Amphictyon, who reigned in his stead \u2014 some say a son of Deucalion, others a son of the soil; and when he had reigned twelve years he was expelled by Erichthonius. Some say that Erichthonius was a son of Hephaestus and Atthis, daughter of Cranaus; and some that he was a son of Hephaestus and Athena, as follows: Athena came to Hephaestus, desirous of fashioning arms. But he, being forsaken by Aphrodite, fell in love with Athena, and began to pursue her; but she fled. When he got near her with much ado (for he was lame), he attempted to embrace her; but she, being a chaste virgin, would not submit to him, and he dropped his seed on the leg of the goddess. In disgust she wiped off the seed with wool and threw it on the ground; and as she fled and the seed fell on the ground, Erichthonius was produced.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.14.6 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Him Athena brought up unknown to the other gods, wishing to make him immortal; and having put him in a chest, she committed it to Pandrosus, daughter of Cecrops, forbidding her to open the chest. But the sisters of Pandrosus opened it out of curiosity, and beheld a serpent coiled about the babe; and, as some say, they were destroyed by the serpent, but according to others they were driven mad by the anger of Athena and threw themselves down from the Acropolis.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.14.6 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "Having been brought up by Athena herself in the precinct, Erichthonius expelled Amphictyon and became king of Athens; and he set up the xoanon of Athena in the Acropolis, and instituted the festival of the Panathenaea, and married Praxithea, a Naiad nymph, by whom he had a son Pandion. So the boy in the chest, guarded by a snake, was the Athenians' founding secret: the earth-born king, the wool-wiped seed, and the festival in honour of the goddess who kept him.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 3.14.6-3.14.7 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
