    license: "Public domain (Frazer translation 1921)",
    summary: {
      en: "Athamas, king of Boeotia, takes a second wife, Ino, who plots against the children of his first marriage: the seed-corn is parched in secret, a famine comes, and an oracle is got to say the dearth will cease if Phrixus is sacrificed to Zeus. Nephele, the cloud-mother, catches her two children at the altar and gives them a ram with a golden fleece, borne through the sky by Hermes' gift. Over the strait between Sigeum and the Chersonese, Helle slips into the deep and is drowned \u2014 the sea is called Hellespont after her \u2014 and Phrixus is carried to Colchis, where Aeetes receives him and the ram is sacrificed; its fleece, hung on an oak in the grove of Ares, is the prize Jason will one day be sent to fetch.",
      zh: "\u6CE2\u5384\u63D0\u4E9E\u738B\u963F\u5854\u746A\u65AF\u7E8C\u5A36\u4F0A\u8AFE\uFF1B\u5F8C\u8005\u6697\u5BB3\u524D\u59BB\u4E4B\u5B50\uFF1A\u9EA5\u7A2E\u88AB\u70D8\u7119\u81F4\u9951\u8352\uFF0C\u4E26\u7AC4\u6539\u795E\u8AED\u7A31\u9808\u737B\u796D\u4F5B\u91CC\u514B\u7D22\u65AF\u3002\u96F2\u6BCD\u6D85\u6590\u52D2\u6551\u4E8C\u5B50\uFF0C\u8CDC\u91D1\u7F8A\u6BDB\u4E4B\u7F8A\u4E58\u4E4B\u98DB\u5929\uFF1B\u904E\u6D77\u5CFD\u6642\u8D6B\u52D2\u843D\u6C34\u800C\u4EA1\uFF0C\u6D77\u5CFD\u56E0\u5F97\u540D\u300C\u8D6B\u52D2\u65AF\u6EC2\u300D\uFF1B\u4F5B\u91CC\u514B\u7D22\u65AF\u81F3\u79D1\u723E\u57FA\u65AF\uFF0C\u5BB0\u7F8A\u737B\u795E\uFF0C\u7F8A\u6BDB\u61F8\u65BC\u963F\u745E\u65AF\u8056\u6797\u4E4B\u6A61\u6A39\u4E0A\u2014\u2014\u5F8C\u70BA\u4F0A\u963F\u5B8B\u9060\u5F81\u6240\u53D6\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "taboo-transgression",
      "culture-hero"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary source; no living community restrictions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Athamas, king of Boeotia, had by his first wife Nephele a son Phrixus and a daughter Helle. And he married a second wife, Ino, by whom he had Learchus and Melicertes. But Ino plotted against the children of Nephele and persuaded the women to parch the wheat; and having got the wheat they did so without the knowledge of the men. But the earth, being sown with parched wheat, did not yield its annual crops; so Athamas sent to Delphi to inquire how he might be delivered from the dearth. And Ino persuaded the messengers to say it was foretold that the infertility would cease if Phrixus were sacrificed to Zeus.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 1.9.1 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "When Athamas heard that, he was forced by the inhabitants of the land to bring Phrixus to the altar. But Nephele caught him and her daughter up, and gave them a ram with a golden fleece, which she had received from Hermes; and borne through the sky by the ram they crossed land and sea. But when they were over the sea which lies betwixt Sigeum and the Chersonese, Helle slipped into the deep and was drowned, and the sea was called Hellespont after her.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 1.9.1 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "But Phrixus came to the Colchians, whose king was Aeetes, son of the Sun and of Perseis, and brother of Circe and Pasiphae. He received Phrixus and gave him one of his daughters, Chalciope, in marriage; and Phrixus sacrificed the ram with the golden fleece to Zeus the Deliverer, and hung the fleece in the grove of Ares, where an unsleeping dragon guarded it \u2014 the fleece for which, one day, an oracle would send Jason and the Argonauts.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Apollodorus, Library 1.9.1 (Frazer 1921); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "helios",
      "hermes"
    ]
