    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The giant-lineages beyond Ymir collected: Aurgelmir (Ymir's name in the wisdom-contest), his odd physiogony (giants sweated from his left armpit; his feet engendered a son together), his descendants (Bergelmir, Thrudgelmir), the named giant-lines (Bolthorn, Bestla, Odin's giant-mother; Gymir and Aurboda, Gerd's parents; Hymir, Hlodi? - and the jotun-name book: Hrungnir, Thjazi, Geirrod, Hymir, Vafthrudnir), and the doctrine the genealogies serve: the giants are the gods' own kin, every god married or descended from a giant. The record is the corpus's giant-genealogy file.",
      zh: "\u5C24\u5F4C\u723E\u4E4B\u5916\u7684\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4E16\u7CFB\u5168\u96C6\uFF1A\u5967\u723E\u84CB\u723E\u7C73\u723E\uFF08\u5C24\u5F4C\u723E\u5728\u667A\u6167\u7AF6\u8CFD\u4E2D\u7684\u7570\u540D\uFF09\u53CA\u5176\u602A\u7570\u7684\u751F\u7406\u8B5C\u7CFB\uFF08\u5DE8\u4EBA\u81EA\u5176\u5DE6\u814B\u6C57\u6DB2\u800C\u751F\uFF0C\u96D9\u8173\u4EA4\u5408\u751F\u51FA\u4E00\u5B50\uFF09\u3001\u5176\u5F8C\u4EE3\uFF08\u8C9D\u683C\u723E\u7C73\u723E\u3001\u65AF\u9B6F\u5FB7\u84CB\u723E\u7C73\u723E\uFF09\u3001\u5177\u540D\u7684\u5DE8\u4EBA\u65CF\u7CFB\uFF08\u535A\u723E\u7D22\u6069\u8207\u8C9D\u65AF\u7279\u62C9\u2014\u2014\u5967\u4E01\u7684\u5DE8\u4EBA\u6BCD\u7CFB\uFF1B\u57FA\u7C73\u723E\u8207\u5967\u723E\u6CE2\u9054\u2014\u2014\u845B\u5FB7\u4E4B\u7236\u6BCD\uFF1B\u5E0C\u7C73\u723E\u7B49\u2014\u2014\u4EE5\u53CA\u5DE8\u4EBA\u540D\u518A\uFF1A\u8D6B\u6717\u683C\u5C3C\u723E\u3001\u590F\u8332\u3001\u84CB\u723E\u7F85\u5FB7\u3001\u5E0C\u7C73\u723E\u3001\u74E6\u592B\u7279\u9B6F\u5FB7\u5C3C\u723E\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u4E16\u7CFB\u6240\u670D\u52D9\u7684\u6559\u7FA9\uFF1A\u5DE8\u4EBA\u5373\u8AF8\u795E\u81EA\u8EAB\u7684\u89AA\u65CF\u2014\u2014\u6BCF\u4E00\u4F4D\u795E\u660E\u7686\u5A36\u5DE8\u4EBA\u70BA\u59BB\u6216\u70BA\u5DE8\u4EBA\u6240\u751F\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4E16\u7CFB\u6A94\u6848\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Henry A. Bellows, The Poetic Edda, New York 1923 (public domain); Gylfaginning via Brodeur 1916",
    narrative: {
      en: "The giant-genealogy file holds the corpus's kinship-map of Jotunheim. Aurgelmir's name is the doctrine's hinge: in Vafthrudnismal the exam-giant answers Odin's oldest question with the name Aurgelmir ('the? - 'mud? - 'el? - 'boiler? - 'the? - wet-el? - one? - - the primordial giant under his exam-name, whom the sibyl calls Ymir) - the two names one being, the genealogies' own evidence that the giants' memory ran older than the poems. The physiogony is the record's strangest clause: Aurgelmir's left armpit sweated a son and a daughter? - a man and a maid - and his two feet engendered together a six-headed son (Thrudgelmir) - the giants reproducing without mate, the body itself the lineage's engine; and Thrudgelmir's son is Bergelmir, the flood-remnant (norse-bergelmir-flood holds the ark-doctrine), from whom 'the? - rime-giants? - 'are? - 'come.\n\nThe god-giant intermarriage is the genealogy's other half: Odin's mother Bestla is Bolthorn's daughter (the chief-god half giant); Thor's mother is Jord, a giantess (and his foster-mother? - the? - - the strength-god wholly giant-reared); Njord marries Skadi (Thjazi's daughter); Freyr wins Gerd (Gymir's daughter); Bragi marries Idunn? - the? - 'goddess? - list? - and even Loki is a giant's son (Farbauti's) married to a goddess-sworn wife - the record norse-loki-genealogy holds the brood. The name-book completes the file: the named giants of the corpus (Hrungnir, Thjazi, Geirrod, Hymir, Vafthrudnir, Utgard-Loki, Baugi, Suttungr, Gymir, Hrym) - each with a family-place, each encounter a kin-encounter.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the kinship-enmity doctrine (the god-giant war as an ethnic myth inside one family: cousins at war, the corpus's own theory of enmity as kinship) and the genealogy-as-cartography doctrine (the jotun-names mapping the encounters: each hall named, each family placed). And the comparative frame completes it: the Greek Titan-genealogies and the Indo-European *Yemo? - the twin-sacrifice traditions - each a giant-lineage behind a divine order. In the atlas, the record anchors the giant-genealogy family: each a tradition's elder-race file; the Norse one is distinctive in the odd physiogony and the intermarried houses.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the creation-record (norse-creation-ymir) and the flood-record (norse-bergelmir-flood): here the lineage runs whole.\n\nThe record's name-lore completes the file: the giant-names' meanings (Aurgelmir 'mud-boiler', Farbauti 'dangerous-striker', Bolthorn 'evil-thorn', Hymir 'the? - dark? - one) - the genealogy as a name-dictionary, the elder race's identity carried in its titles. And the intermarriage-chart completes it: the gods' giant-kin tabulated (mothers, wives, fosterers) - the war of gods and giants as a family-feud documented genealogically."
    },
