    lat: 64.26,
    lon: -21.13,
    period: "Gylfaginning c. 1220-1230 (older material); comparative Indo-European and Near-Eastern parallels",
    source: {
      archive: "Project Gutenberg (Brodeur 1916) / archive.org",
      work: "Snorri, Gylfaginning chs. 3-4; comparative: Genesis 1.1-2; Enuma Elis; Rigveda 10.129 (Nasadiya); Apollodorus' Chaos; critical ed. Faulkes (1987)",
      url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/search/?query=prose+edda"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The Norse void collected against its parallels: Ginnungagap ('the? - 'yawning? - 'gap? - 'the? - gap that had no bottom on any side) read beside the Hebrew tohu-bohu (the formless waste before light), the Babylonian Apsu-Tiamat (the mingled sweet and salt waters), the Rigveda's Nasadiya hymn ('neither? - being? - 'nor? - non-being? - 'then? - 'there? - was? - neither? - death? - 'nor? - the? - immortal), the Greek Chaos of Hesiod and Apollodorus, and the Chinese Hundun (the uncarved block). The record is the void-motif's comparative file: what the Norse version adds is the elemental polarity (ice and fire meeting in the gap) and the gap's survivor - it persists as the myth-world's border-sea.",
      zh: "\u5C07\u5317\u6B50\u865B\u7A7A\u8207\u5176\u5E73\u884C\u6587\u672C\u4E26\u7F6E\u5C0D\u8B80\uFF1A\u91D1\u502B\u52A0\u9D3B\u6E9D\uFF08\u300C\u7121\u8AD6\u54EA\u4E00\u5074\u90FD\u6C92\u6709\u5E95\u7684\u5927\u5F35\u4E4B\u53E3\u300D\uFF09\u5C0D\u7167\u5E0C\u4F2F\u4F86\u7684\u300C\u7A7A\u865B\u6DF7\u6C8C\u300D\uFF08\u5149\u524D\u7684\u7121\u5F62\u8352\u91CE\uFF09\u3001\u5DF4\u6BD4\u502B\u7684\u963F\u666E\u8607\u8207\u63D0\u4E9E\u746A\u7279\uFF08\u751C\u6C34\u8207\u9E79\u6C34\u7684\u6DF7\u5408\uFF09\u3001\u300A\u68A8\u4FF1\u5420\u9640\u300B\u7684\u300C\u7121\u6709\u980C\u300D\uFF08\u300C\u65E2\u975E\u5B58\u5728\u4EA6\u975E\u5B58\u5728\uFF0C\u7121\u6B7B\u4EA6\u7121\u4E0D\u6B7B\u300D\uFF09\u3001\u8D6B\u897F\u4FC4\u5FB7\u8207\u963F\u6CE2\u7F85\u591A\u9B6F\u65AF\u7684\u5361\u4FC4\u65AF\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u4E2D\u570B\u7684\u300C\u6DF7\u6C8C\u300D\uFF08\u672A\u947F\u4E4B\u6DF7\u6C8C\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u865B\u7A7A\u6BCD\u984C\u7684\u6BD4\u8F03\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u5317\u6B50\u7248\u672C\u7684\u7368\u7279\u4E4B\u8655\u5728\u65BC\u5143\u7D20\u5169\u6975\uFF08\u51B0\u8207\u706B\u65BC\u9D3B\u6E9D\u4E2D\u76F8\u9047\uFF09\uFF0C\u4E14\u9D3B\u6E9D\u672C\u8EAB\u7559\u5B58\u70BA\u795E\u8A71\u4E16\u754C\u7684\u908A\u6D77\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic; comparative material in Hebrew, Akkadian, Sanskrit, Greek, Chinese",
    canonical_translation: "Brodeur 1916 (public domain); comparative passages paraphrased from standard translations",
    narrative: {
      en: "This comparative node holds the void-motif's witnesses. The Norse doctrine (norse-ginnungagap-void holds the full cosmogony): Ginnungagap is not nothing but a space - 'the? - 'gap? - 'that? - 'had? - no? - floor? - 'and? - no? - sky? - - and its two rims are the cosmos's elements (Niflheim's freezing rivers to the north, Muspell's fire to the south); creation happens in the gap where the elements meet, and the gap itself survives creation as the world's out-border (the record norse-world-serpent-midgard's enclosing sea reads as the gap's residue).\n\nThe parallels: Genesis's formless waste ('the? - 'earth? - 'was? - without? - form? - 'and? - void? - and? - darkness? - on? - the? - face? - of? - the? - deep? - - the deep as space, the spirit moving on the waters); the Enuma Elis's mingled waters (Apsu the sweet abyss, Tiamat the salt sea, their mingling begetting the gods - the polarity-doctrine in water-form); the Rigveda's Nasadiya hymn ('then? - 'there? - was? - neither? - being? - nor? - non-being? - 'no? - air? - 'no? - sky? - 'what? - stirred? - and? - where? - 'the? - one? - who? - looks? - down? - on? - it? - does? - not? - know? - - the void as an acknowledged question); Hesiod's Chaos ('first? - of? - all? - was? - the? - yawning? - chasm? - - the Greek void's own gaping-word, cognate-adjacent to the Norse gap); and the Chinese Hundun (the uncarved wholeness whose drilling kills it - the void as a living faceless thing).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the comparative-motif doctrine (the void as a near-universal creation-motif with local inflections: space, water, question, chasm, block) and the polarity-doctrine (the Norse version's signature: the void is not neutral but polarized - the elements' meeting is the world's conception, creation as chemical event rather than command). And the gap's survival completes the motif-file: unlike most parallels' voids (which are replaced by the created order), Ginnungagap persists as the margin - the myth-world's border-sea where the serpent lies. In the atlas, the record anchors the void-motif family: each a tradition's pre-creation space; the Norse one is distinctive in polarity and persistence.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the creation-records (the void-doctrine; the giant-genealogies): here the motif's wider kin laid out.\n\nThe record's comparative-method completes the file: the void-motif's witnesses read as a typology (space, water, question, chasm, block) rather than as borrowings - each tradition's pre-creation space analyzed by its own inflection, the comparative file's method documented as database-practice. And the gap's cartographic persistence completes it: the border-sea where the serpent lies, the gap in the map's margin - the void surviving as geography."
    },
