    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Kershaw 1921); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The dark-peoples file collected: Snorri's two orders (the dark-elves below the earth, blacker than pitch; the dwarfs in Svartalfaheimr, the smith-race born of Ymir's maggots), the confusion-of-terms (svartalfar and dvergar overlapping in the sources), the dwarf-portraits (Andvari the pike-fisher, Dvalin and Durin the forced smiths, the dwarf-dialogues of Hervarar saga where Tyrfing is forged under duress), and the crafts-doctrine (the dwarfs make the gods' signature objects: Mjollnir, Draupnir, Skidbladnir, Gleipnir, the ship's? - the ring's? - the treasure-economy of the under-earth). The record is the subterranean peoples' file.",
      zh: "\u5E7D\u6697\u4E4B\u65CF\u7684\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u65AF\u8AFE\u91CC\u7B46\u4E0B\u7684\u5169\u500B\u968E\u5E8F\uFF08\u5C45\u65BC\u5730\u4E0B\u7684\u6697\u7CBE\u9748\uFF0C\u6BD4\u67CF\u6CB9\u66F4\u9ED1\uFF1B\u5C45\u65BC\u65AF\u74E6\u7279\u963F\u723E\u592B\u6D77\u59C6\u7684\u77EE\u4EBA\u2014\u2014\u81EA\u5C24\u5F4C\u723E\u86C6\u87F2\u6240\u751F\u7684\u935B\u9020\u4E4B\u65CF\uFF09\u3001\u540D\u7A31\u7684\u6DF7\u6DC6\uFF08\u6587\u737B\u4E2D svartalfar \u8207 dvergar \u76F8\u4E92\u91CD\u758A\uFF09\u3001\u77EE\u4EBA\u8096\u50CF\uFF08\u68AD\u5F62\u5782\u91E3\u7684\u5B89\u5FB7\u74E6\u5229\u3001\u88AB\u8FEB\u935B\u528D\u7684\u5FB7\u74E6\u6797\u8207\u90FD\u6797\u3001\u300A\u8D6B\u723E\u6C83\u85A9\u8FE6\u300B\u4E2D\u8105\u8FEB\u4E4B\u4E0B\u7684\u935B\u9020\u5C0D\u8A71\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u5DE5\u85DD\u6559\u7FA9\uFF08\u77EE\u4EBA\u935B\u9020\u8AF8\u795E\u7684\u6A19\u8A8C\u4E4B\u7269\uFF1A\u5999\u723E\u5C3C\u723E\u3001\u5FB7\u7F85\u666E\u5C3C\u723E\u3001\u65AF\u57FA\u5FB7\u5E03\u62C9\u5FB7\u5C3C\u723E\u3001\u683C\u96F7\u666E\u5C3C\u723E\u2026\u2026\u5730\u4E0B\u4E16\u754C\u7684\u5BF6\u7269\u7D93\u6FDF\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5730\u4E0B\u6C11\u65CF\u4E4B\u6A94\u6848\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Brodeur 1916 (public domain); Hervarar saga via Kershaw 1921 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The dark-peoples file holds the under-earth's two orders and their confusion. The two-people doctrine (norse-alfheim-elves holds the light-elves' side): Snorri's dark-elves dwell below the earth, 'blacker? - 'than? - 'pitch? - 'and? - 'unlike? - the? - light-elves? - 'in? - deeds? - and? - looks; the dwarfs dwell in Svartalfaheimr ('the? - 'world? - 'of? - the? - 'black? - elves? - - Snorri's own name collapses the distinction: the dwarf-world is the dark-elf-world, and the sources use svartalfar and dvergar interchangeably) - the taxonomy's blur is the record's first datum: the two orders are one economy seen from above and below.\n\nThe dwarf-portraits: Andvari (the dwarf fishing in pike-shape, robbed of his hoard and laying the ring-curse - the record norse-andvari-ring-curse), Dvalin and Durin (the forced smiths of Tyrfing - the record norse-tyrfing-curse), the Dvergatal's catalog (norse-dvergatal-dwarfs holds the name-book), and the Hervarar-dialogues (the dwarfs speaking from the stone at dusk, forging under duress - the corpus's smith-dialogues where the underworld bargains by force). The crafts-doctrine: the dwarfs make the gods' signature objects - Mjollnir, Draupnir, Skidbladnir, Gungnir, Gleipnir, Brisingamen, the ship's? - the treasures-file (the record norse-mjolnir-forging holds the forge-competition) - so the under-earth is the gods' supply-chain: the dwarfs make, the gods circulate (Clunies Ross's exchange-economy).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the under-earth-economy doctrine (smithcraft as the subterranean order's office, the treasures' provenance as a dwarf-claim) and the taxonomy-blur doctrine (the sources' own confusion documented as a datum: dark-elves and dwarfs as one seen from above). And the folk-afterlife completes it: the sagas' and ballads' dwarf-encounters (the mountain-king's daughter, the troll-women? - the? - later? - - the under-earth folk's persistence in post-conversion folklore). In the atlas, the record anchors the under-earth-people family: each a tradition's subterranean smith-folk; the Norse one is distinctive in the maggot-birth and the duress-dialogues.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the elf-record, the dwarf-catalog and the forge-records: here the dark-side runs whole.\n\nThe record's folk-afterlife completes the file: the under-earth folk's persistence in post-conversion folklore (the mountain-king's halls, the troll-women's brides, the fairy-mounds of the ballads) - the dwarf-economy's survival in the converted landscape's below. And the craft-lineage completes it: the smith-hero tradition (V\xF6lundr, the dwarfs' apprentices) as the under-earth's export - the subterranean order's heroes documented above ground. The record's taxonomic-note completes the file: the scholars' debate on svartalfar versus dvergar (one race under two names, or two races confused by Snorri's systematizing) - the dark-peoples' taxonomy as the corpus's own open question, documented here with both readings and the sources' usage-patterns."
    },
