    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The feast-institution's origin-file collected: the gods' first communal feast (the mythic memory of the golden age's tables), the Aegir-feasts (the sea-giant's hall with the shining gold and self-pouring cups - the first hospitality the gods accept from a convert-giant), the kettle-quest that equips the feast (Hymir's mile-deep cauldron, stolen from the borderlands), and the feast-law (the cup-circles, the memory-toasts, the seat-rights and gift-cycles documented in the H\xE1kon-chapter and the flyting's rules). The record is the divine-hospitality file: how the gods learned to eat together.",
      zh: "\u5BB4\u5E2D\u5236\u5EA6\u7684\u8D77\u6E90\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u8AF8\u795E\u7684\u7B2C\u4E00\u6B21\u5171\u98DF\uFF08\u9EC3\u91D1\u6642\u4EE3\u684C\u5E2D\u7684\u795E\u8A71\u8A18\u61B6\uFF09\u3001\u57C3\u5409\u723E\u5BB4\uFF08\u6D77\u5DE8\u4EBA\u4E4B\u5EF3\u91D1\u5149\u9583\u720D\u3001\u9152\u676F\u81EA\u659F\u2014\u2014\u8AF8\u795E\u9996\u6B21\u63A5\u53D7\u4E00\u4F4D\u7688\u4F9D\u5DE8\u4EBA\u7684\u6B3E\u5F85\uFF09\u3001\u70BA\u5BB4\u5E2D\u53D6\u4F86\u7684\u5DE8\u91DC\uFF08\u5E0C\u7C73\u723E\u6DF1\u9054\u4E00\u91CC\u7684\u934B\uFF0C\u81EA\u908A\u5883\u596A\u5F97\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u5BB4\u5E2D\u4E4B\u6CD5\uFF08\u50B3\u676F\u4E4B\u79AE\u3001\u8FFD\u5FF5\u4E4B\u9152\u3001\u5EA7\u6B21\u4E4B\u6B0A\u8207\u8D08\u79AE\u4E4B\u74B0\u2014\u2014\u898B\u65BC\u54C8\u5EB7\u7BC7\u7AE0\u8207\u5435\u7F75\u7684\u898F\u5247\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u795E\u8056\u597D\u5BA2\u7684\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u8AF8\u795E\u5982\u4F55\u5B78\u6703\u540C\u684C\u800C\u98DF\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Brodeur 1916 (public domain); Bellows 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The divine-hospitality file holds the feast-institution's origins. The first communal feast: the golden age's tables (Voluspa's gleeful board-games and lack of gold - the record norse-golden-age-gods) are the feast's memory-image; but the institution's equipment is a theft: the gods cannot brew ale for all of them at once until Thor and Tyr fetch Hymir's mile-deep kettle from the borderlands (the record norse-hymir-kettle) - the feast's first hardware is war-booty, the communal feast founded on a raid.\n\nThe Aegir-feasts: the sea-giant converted to hospitality - his hall 'of? - 'shining? - 'gold? - 'the? - ale? - self-poured? - in? - the? - cups? - 'the? - - the gods accepting a giant's table (the record norse-lokasenna-flyting holds the feast's flyting); and the feast-law's grammar documented in the sources: the cup-circles by god (Odin for victory, Njord and Freyja for plenty), the memory-toasts (the bragafull), the seat-rights (Loki's blood-oath seat-claim), and the gift-cycles (the feast as the exchange-economy's ritual engine).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the feast-as-institution doctrine (the communal feast as the gods' society's core ritual - what the kettle's absence prevents, the feast's presence produces: alliance, counsel, flyting and gift) and the hospitality-economics doctrine (the feast hosted by converts and enemies: Aegir the giant, the bonders' halls of the Hakon-chapter - the borderlands' hospitality absorbed into Asgard's ritual). And the Edda's own feast-frame completes it: Skaldskaparmal's teaching is staged at Aegir's feast - the textbook itself a banquet-scene, Bragi lecturing the convert-giant over the ale. In the atlas, the record anchors the divine-feast family: each a tradition's gods' banquet as social charter; the Norse one is distinctive in the stolen kettle.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the kettle-record, the Aegir-flyting record and the Yule-feast record: here the institution's file runs whole.\n\nThe record's banquet-grammar completes the file: the feast's outputs documented (alliance, counsel, flyting, gift, recruitment - the entry-oaths sworn over the cups) - the communal meal as the gods' society's parliament and recruiting-office. And the corpus's own feast-frames complete it: Skaldskaparmal, Lokasenna and the Eiriksmal each staged at a feast - the myth-textbook, the audit and the death-welcome all banquet-scenes, the institution's centrality documented by its frame-ubiquity. The record's Aegir-biography completes the file: the host-giant's own career (the sea-god of the drowned, the gold-hall's shining wealth) - the convert-host's profile documented beside his feast, the hospitality's donor as a bordered being of the deep. And the corpus\u2019s own banquet-census completes it: the feasts named across the sources (the golden-age tables, Hymir\u2019s ale, Aegir\u2019s gold-hall, the Yule-feast at Hladir, Eirik\u2019s welcome in Valhalla) - the institution\u2019s scene-list documented, the gods\u2019 social life measured in meals."
    },
