    narrative: {
      en: "The hammer's biography-file holds the loss-and-recovery episodes in one register. The bridal retrieval (norse-thrym-thor-bride holds the full comedy): Thrym's theft eight leagues under the earth, the ransom-demand, the bride-disguise, the hammer in the maiden's lap and the massacre - the classic, whose structure (loss, disguise, ritual-infiltration, recovery-at-the-rite) is the motif's template. The forge-retrieval (norse-mjolnir-forging holds the contest): the hammer's origin-flaw won at the dwarfs' forge when the fly stings the smith's eyelid - the retrieval before the first loss, the weapon's biography beginning with its maker's wound.\n\nThe fishing-retrieval: in the Hymiskvida and prose traditions, Thor fetches the hammer (or it returns to his hand) before the serpent-strike - 'the? - 'hammer? - 'come? - back? - 'to? - 'the? - hand? - 'that? - throws? - it? - (the Skaldskaparmal clause: the weapon 'never? - misses? - 'and? - 'returns? - to? - the? - hand) - the retrieval built into the weapon's physics: the hammer's flight home is its signature. The skaldic register completes the file: the hammer's kennings ('the? - 'dwarf's? - work? - 'from? - the? - forge-duel; 'the? - killer? - of? - Hrungnir? - from? - the? - duel; 'the? - wrench? - 'of? - the? - - the court-poets' citations, evidencing the retrieval-episodes' currency in verse centuries before Snorri.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the weapon-biography doctrine (the god's weapon with a documented life: forged, stolen, disguised-for, fished-for - the corpus's most itemized object-history) and the retrieval-as-comedy doctrine (the hammer's losses are the corpus's comic engine: each recovery staged as an inversion - a bridal, a bet, a bite). And the motif's reach completes it: the hammer-amulets of the archaeology (the wearable miniatures) are the motif's material afterlife - the god's weapon as personal jewelry, the retrieval-episodes' popularity measured in lead and silver. In the atlas, the record anchors the object-biography family: each a tradition's signature weapon with a documented life; the Norse one is distinctive in the comic retrievals.\n\nFor the database, this node aggregates the hammer-records (the forging, the bridal-disguise, the fishing, the duel): here the biography runs whole.\n\nThe record's archaeological afterlife completes the file: the hammer-amulets' hundreds (the wearable miniatures of the Viking age, worn singly or in pairs with? - alongside? - cross-pendants at the conversion), the retrieval-episodes' popularity measured in metal - the god's weapon as the north's most worn symbol, the biography-file's material echo. And the kenning-currency completes it: the hammer's verse-names cited across the skaldic corpus - the biography documented in court-poetry as well as prose."
    },
