    license: "Public domain (Bellows 1923; Laing 1844); Adam passages are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Freyr's economy of sacrifice collected: the god's own sacrifice (the sword pledged for Gerd - the armament pawned for the harvest, its price paid at Ragnarok), the ship (Skidbladnir, folded like cloth in a purse, carrying the gods' whole host - the fertility-god's navy), the sacrificial cult (the Uppsala effigy with the enormous phallus, the thralls? - the dedicated servants: 'Frey's? - men? - and? - 'the? - god's? - guard? - at? - the? - temple), and the king's sacrifice (the Swedish kings' harvest-office as Freyr's line). The record is the Vanic-sacrifice file: the god who gives away power to give plenty.",
      zh: "\u5F17\u96F7\u737B\u796D\u7D93\u6FDF\u7684\u5168\u96C6\uFF1A\u795E\u81EA\u8EAB\u7684\u72A7\u7272\uFF08\u70BA\u5A36\u845B\u5FB7\u800C\u62B5\u62BC\u5BF6\u528D\u2014\u2014\u4EE5\u6B66\u5099\u63DB\u8C50\u6536\uFF0C\u4EE3\u50F9\u65BC\u8AF8\u795E\u9EC3\u660F\u511F\u4ED8\uFF09\u3001\u795E\u8239\uFF08\u65AF\u57FA\u5FB7\u5E03\u62C9\u5FB7\u5C3C\u723E\uFF0C\u53EF\u5982\u5E03\u5339\u647A\u5165\u888B\u4E2D\uFF0C\u8F09\u904B\u8AF8\u795E\u5168\u8ECD\u2014\u2014\u8C50\u9952\u4E4B\u795E\u7684\u8266\u968A\uFF09\u3001\u737B\u796D\u5D07\u62DC\uFF08\u70CF\u666E\u85A9\u62C9\u5DE8\u578B\u967D\u5177\u795E\u50CF\u3001\u795E\u4E4B\u50D5\u5F79\uFF1A\u300C\u5F17\u96F7\u7684\u4EBA\u300D\u8207\u795E\u5EDF\u5B88\u885B\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u570B\u738B\u7684\u72A7\u7272\uFF08\u745E\u5178\u8AF8\u738B\u7684\u8C50\u6536\u8077\u8CAC\u5373\u5F17\u96F7\u4E00\u8108\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u83EF\u7D0D\u737B\u796D\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u4E00\u4F4D\u6368\u68C4\u6B0A\u529B\u4EE5\u8CDC\u8C50\u9952\u7684\u795E\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic; Latin (Adam of Bremen)",
    canonical_translation: "Bellows 1923 (public domain); Laing 1844 (public domain); Adam per Tschan 1959",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Vanic-sacrifice file holds Freyr's economy whole. The god's own sacrifice (norse-freyr-gerd-skirnir holds the wooing; norse-freyr-beli-cult the bare-handed kill): the sword pledged to Skirnir as wooing-fee - the fertility-god's armament pawned for the harvest, the price collected at Ragnarok when he meets Surtr unarmed; and the ship: Skidbladnir, the dwarf-made vessel 'that? - 'has? - fair? - wind? - always? - 'and? - 'can? - be? - folded? - 'like? - a? - cloth? - 'and? - 'put? - in? - a? - purse? - - the god's navy in a pocket: plenty's transport, carrying the gods' whole host yet portable as a handkerchief.\n\nThe cult's sacrifices: the Uppsala effigy (Adam's Fricco with the enormous phallus - the record norse-uppsala-temple holds the outsider's description; the temple's phallic iconography confirming the fertility-cult's explicitness), the dedicated thralls ('Frey's? - men? - 'the? - temple-servants? - documented in the sagas: the god's household-slaves, dedicated men who served the shrine), and the sacrifice-calendar (the Swedish kings' harvest-office: Domaldi's blood for the seasons - the record norse-nine-year-blot - the king as the god's own collateral).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the sacrifice-economics doctrine (the Vanic god's whole economy is sacrifice: he pawns his weapon for love, lends his ship for transport, takes the kings' blood for harvests - the fertility-principle as an exchange-ledger) and the sacred-kinghip-through-the-god doctrine: the Yngling kings are Freyr's descendants, so the god's economy is the kingdom's - the temple's phallus, the king's mound, the harvest-office one institution. And the ship's reach completes it: Skidbladnir's portability is the motif's signature (the corpus's only foldable vehicle - plenty carried in a purse), the kenning-citations evidencing the god's navel? - the vessel's court-currency. In the atlas, the record anchors the fertility-sacrifice family: each a tradition's plenty-god with an exchange-economy; the Norse one is distinctive in the pawned sword.\n\nFor the database, this node aggregates Freyr's sacrifice-records: here the economy runs whole.\n\nThe record's cult-geography completes the file: the Vanic offices mapped onto Sweden's sacred landscape (the sea-hall at Noatun, the temple at Uppsala, the battle-field's share to Folkvangr) - the god's economy inscribed in place, the treaty-division of the record norse-vanir-society visible in geography. And the ship's liturgical use completes it: the skaldic evidence for the vessel carried in procession (the ship-cult's echoes in the harvest-rites) - the foldable navy as ritual property. The record's Ing-epithet completes the file: the god's name-economy (Yngvi-Freyr, the Ingunar-Freyr of the lore; the Yngling-name's derivation) - the god's identity and the dynasty's name fused, the sacrifice-economy's charter written in the royal title itself."
    },
