    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "T\xFDr's wound read in its legal frame: the hand as the pledge-body (the wolf's contract secured by the god's flesh), the oath-law's parallel institution (the temple-ring reddened in sacrifice-blood, the oath-hand dipped - the record norse-oath-ring-ullr), the wound's social meaning ('not? - called? - a? - reconciler? - of? - men? - - the prose's pointed clause: the pledge-keeper marked unfit for peacemaking), and the flyting's grief-clause ('the? - wolf? - 'stays? - bound? - 'and? - I? - stay? - wretched? - - the oath-keeper's ledger). The record is the oath-body file: law's price paid in a god's flesh.",
      zh: "\u5728\u6CD5\u5F8B\u6846\u67B6\u4E2D\u89E3\u8B80\u63D0\u723E\u4E4B\u50B7\uFF1A\u624B\u70BA\u64D4\u4FDD\u4E4B\u9AD4\uFF08\u5DE8\u72FC\u4E4B\u5951\u4EE5\u795E\u7684\u8840\u8089\u4F5C\u4FDD\uFF09\u3001\u8A93\u8A00\u6CD5\u7684\u5E73\u884C\u5236\u5EA6\uFF08\u4EE5\u796D\u8840\u67D3\u7D05\u7684\u795E\u5EDF\u6307\u74B0\u3001\u57F7\u74B0\u4E4B\u624B\u6D78\u8840\u2014\u2014\u898B\u6307\u74B0\u8A18\u9304\uFF09\u3001\u50B7\u6B98\u7684\u793E\u6703\u610F\u6DB5\uFF08\u300C\u4E0D\u88AB\u4EBA\u7A31\u70BA\u548C\u89E3\u8005\u300D\u2014\u2014\u6563\u6587\u4E2D\u7684\u8F9B\u8FA3\u4E00\u8A9E\uFF1A\u5B88\u4FE1\u8005\u88AB\u6A19\u8A18\u70BA\u4E0D\u5B9C\u8ABF\u505C\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u5435\u7F75\u4E2D\u7684\u60B2\u50B7\u689D\u6B3E\uFF08\u300C\u72FC\u88AB\u7E1B\u4F4F\uFF0C\u800C\u6211\u6DD2\u82E6\u5EA6\u65E5\u300D\u2014\u2014\u5B88\u8A93\u8005\u7684\u5E33\u7C3F\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A93\u8A00\u8EAB\u9AD4\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u6CD5\u5F8B\u4EE5\u795E\u7684\u8840\u8089\u511F\u4ED8\u4EE3\u50F9\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Brodeur 1916 (public domain); Bellows 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The oath-body file holds T\xFDr's wound in its legal frame. The hand as contract (norse-fenrir-gleipnir holds the binding): the wolf's bargain secured by the god's flesh - 'the? - 'pledge? - 'put? - 'in? - 'the? - mouth? - 'as? - 'trust? - 'that? - 'the? - band? - 'was? - no? - trick - the collateral-doctrine in body-form; and the oath-law's parallel institution: the temple-ring reddened in sacrifice-blood, the oath-hand dipped (the record norse-oath-ring-ullr) - the same grammar in the courts: the sworn word bound in blood and held in hand.\n\nThe wound's social meaning is the prose's pointed clause: T\xFDr 'is? - one-handed? - 'and? - is? - not? - called? - a? - reconciler? - of? - men? - - the pledge-keeper marked unfit for peacemaking: the hand that binds cannot broker. And the flyting's grief-clause completes the ledger: Loki's taunt ('I? - have? - thy? - hand? - the? - wolf's? - snare) and T\xFDr's reply ('the? - wolf? - 'stays? - bound? - 'and? - I? - 'stay? - wretched? - - the oath-keeper's account: the bargain kept, the cost permanent, the honor intact and the life diminished - the record norse-tyr-profile holds the profile.\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the oath-body doctrine (the sworn word secured in flesh - the law's collateral imagined anatomically) and the wound-ledger doctrine (the cost of keeping an oath itemized: the hand, the office, the wife's honor charged at the flyting - the oath-keeper's full account). And the comparative frame completes it: the Roman Fides and the Greek Zeus Horkios - each a deity of the sworn word; T\xFDr is distinctive in paying with body-parts. In the atlas, the record anchors the oath-cost family: each a tradition's sworn-word deity with a price; the Norse one is distinctive in the wound.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the profile-record, the binding-record and the oath-ring-record: here the legal frame runs whole.\n\nThe record's legal-iconography completes the file: the one-handed god as the oath-figure's body (the missing hand as the contract's receipt, visible at every assembly), and the law-codes' oath-gestures (the hand on the ring, the hand in the blood) as the myth's daily practice - the wound-doctrine documented in court-procedure. And the comparative frame completes it: the Mithraic? - the? - oath-hand traditions - each a sworn-word culture with a hand-sign; the Norse one pays in flesh.\n\n The record's flyting-silence completes the file: T\xFDr\u2019s second taunt answered with silence (the charge he cannot rebut) - the oath-keeper\u2019s ledger closed in silence, the profile\u2019s cost documented by what the god will not say."
    },
