    summary: {
      en: "The Gj\xFAki household's tragedy-file: the hall's society (Gunnar the ring-giver, Hogni the wise brother, Guthorm the instrument, Gudrun the sister), the queens' quarrel (Brynhild and Gudrun's river-bathing rank-debate: the proud wife bathed downstream? - 'the? - 'rank? - quarrel? - 'that? - 'unmans? - 'the? - king), Gunnar's broken oath (the oath on the ring by the water - the sworn word evaded by proxy), Brynhild's death-lay, Atli's marriage-plot and the hall's destruction - the record is the court-tragedy file: a royal household destroyed from within.",
      zh: "\u5409\u70CF\u57FA\u5BB6\u65CF\u7684\u60B2\u5287\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u5927\u5EF3\u7684\u793E\u6703\uFF08\u6563\u91D1\u8005\u7518\u7D0D\u3001\u667A\u8005\u970D\u683C\u5C3C\u3001\u5DE5\u5177\u4EBA\u53E4\u6258\u59C6\u3001\u59B9\u59B9\u53E4\u5FB7\u502B\uFF09\u3001\u738B\u540E\u7684\u722D\u57F7\uFF08\u5E03\u502B\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\u8207\u53E4\u5FB7\u502B\u65BC\u6CB3\u4E2D\u6C90\u6D74\u7684\u4F4D\u6B21\u4E4B\u722D\uFF1A\u9AD8\u50B2\u7684\u738B\u540E\u7ADF\u6D74\u65BC\u4E0B\u6D41\u2014\u2014\u300C\u90A3\u5834\u4EE4\u541B\u738B\u5931\u683C\u7684\u4F4D\u6B21\u4E4B\u722D\u300D\uFF09\u3001\u7518\u7D0D\u7684\u80CC\u8A93\uFF08\u5728\u6307\u74B0\u4E0A\u3001\u65BC\u6C34\u7554\u7ACB\u8A93\u2014\u2014\u537B\u4EE5\u66FF\u8EAB\u4EE3\u7406\u9003\u8A93\uFF09\u3001\u5E03\u502B\u5E0C\u723E\u5FB7\u7684\u6B7B\u4EA1\u4E4B\u6B4C\u3001\u963F\u7279\u5229\u7684\u806F\u59FB\u9670\u8B00\u8207\u5927\u5EF3\u7684\u6BC0\u6EC5\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5BAE\u5EF7\u60B2\u5287\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u4E00\u500B\u738B\u5BB6\u5B85\u90B8\u81EA\u5167\u90E8\u5D29\u584C\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Bellows 1923 (public domain); Morris & Magnusson 1870 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The court-tragedy file holds the Gj\xFAki household's inner destruction. The hall's society: Gunnar the ring-giver (the king whose charisma is gift), Hogni the wise brother (the counselor who speaks truth and is not heeded), Guthorm the instrument (the brother who does the killing because the others' hands are oath-bound), Gudrun the sister (the marriage-piece traded for the hall's security). The queens' quarrel is the file's signature scene: the river-bathing rank-debate (Brynhild insisting she is the greater queen, wading upstream; Gudrun revealing that Sigurd took Gunnar's shape through the fire - so the proud wife's marriage was a proxy's fraud) - 'the? - 'rank? - quarrel? - 'that? - 'unmans? - 'the? - king? - (the saga's clause: Gunnar's kingship destroyed by his wife's humiliation).\n\nGunnar's broken oath: the oath sworn on the arm-ring by the waterfall ('the? - 'oath? - 'by? - 'the? - water? - 'that? - 'he? - 'would? - not? - wed? - 'her? - - and the evasion by proxy: the oath's letter kept, its meaning destroyed by Sigurd-in-disguise - the record norse-oath-ring-ullr's grammar inverted: the sworn word evaded through ceremony). Brynhild's death-lay (the prophecy-spoken dying: the hall's future foretold as her pyre is lit), and Atli's plot (the marriage-alliance that trades Gudrun for peace, then the lure of the Rhine-gold that kills the brothers - the court's end).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the household-tragedy doctrine (the royal family destroyed not by monsters but by its own oath-evasion and women's war - the corpus's domestic register of doom) and the rank-quarrel doctrine (the queens' humiliation-battle as the state-crisis: status as politics). In the atlas, the record anchors the court-tragedy family: each a royal household's inner destruction; the Gj\xFAki one is distinctive in the oath-evasion by proxy.\n\nFor the database, this node holds the court-side records the cycle's battle-records lack: the hall's own destruction-file.\n\nThe record's comparative reach completes the file: the Gj\xFAki-court material's German twin (the Nibelungenlied's Kriemhild and Etzel-court: the same household-tragedy in a continental dress), so the tragedy-file documents one story's two literatures - the Rhine-court and the Norse hall as one narrative field. And the queens' quarrel's afterlife completes it: the river-bathing scene as the tradition's most-cited domestic scene - the court-tragedy's engine remembered long after the halls burned.\n\n The record's counsel-tragedy completes the file: Hogni's wisdom ignored (the brother who warns against the oath\u2019s evasion and the Atli-ride, and is taken anyway) - the court\u2019s counselor as the tragedy\u2019s Cassandra, the household\u2019s end documented through its one clear voice going unheeded."
    },
