    canonical_translation: "Elton 1894 (public domain); Waggoner 2009 (paraphrased)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The local-serpent file holds the dragon-motif's minor-key narratives. Saxo's Danish slayers: Frotho's dragon (the young prince's first exploit: the dragon of Rolg? - the? - wax-armed? - kill? - 'the? - 'wax? - 'shield? - 'that? - 'blinded? - the? - beast? - 'the? - youth's? - cunning? - kill), and the serpent-tales of the Danish kings (the hero-kings' serpent-fights as the royal-r\xE9sum\xE9's standard clause - the slaying as proof of kingship). Ragnar's lindworm: the sp\xE1-woman's? - the? - snake-gift (the girl's present that grew into a lindworm besieging the hall - the provenance-tale), and the slaying that earned the epithet (Ragnar killing the serpent in the shaggy-breeches? - 'the? - 'hairy? - breeches? - 'boiled? - 'in? - 'the? - viper? - - the record norse-ragnar-serpent-pit's cycle: the epithet's origin-story).\n\nThe saga-serpents complete the file: the lake-worms and ridge-serpents of the fornaldarsogur (the local dragons guarding hoards and fords, the smaller cousins of Fafnir), and the folk-register: the post-conversion dragon-lore of the ridges (the 17th-19th-century collected legends: the lindworms of the fields, the dragon-shaped ridge-formations explained as battles) - the motif's persistence in the converted landscape. The record's cultural functions are the dragon-as-local-doctrine (the serpent-motif's minor register: local hoards, local fords, local battles - the dragon mapped onto real geography) and the epithet-origin doctrine (the slaying as the name's proof: Lodbrok's breeches, Frotho's wax, the hero named by the monster's death). In the atlas, the record anchors the local-dragon family: each a tradition's minor-key serpent-narratives; the Norse one is distinctive in the epithet-proof.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the Fafnir-records and the Ragnar-records: here the motif's minor key runs whole.\n\nThe record's mapping completes the file: the local serpents' geography (the ridge-names and lake-names of Scandinavia carrying dragon-lore into the modern map), and the motif's iconographic reach (the Sigurd-scene carved on the runestones and stave-church portals - the record norse-iconographic-episodes) - the minor-key dragon documented in landscape and image as well as text.\n\n The record's iconographic bridge completes the file: the Sigurd-scene's carvings (the Ramsund rock-cutting, the Dr\xE4vle stone, the G\xF6k stone, the Hylestad stave-church portals) as the dragon-motif)s picture-record - the minor serpents) famous cousin documented in stone, linking this local file to the record norse-iconographic-episodes. And the motif)s name-geography completes it: the Fafnir-names and serpent-ridges of Scandinavia (the place-names) dragon-lexicon) - the minor-key dragon mapped onto the modern toponymy.\n\n The record's saga-register completes the file: the smaller serpent-episodes) narrative grammar (the hoard-guard, the ford-blocker, the vengeance-beast) - the dragon-motif)s three local offices, each with its own slaying-plot. And the folk-collectors' register completes it: the 19th-century legends) mapping (the collected serpent-tales of the Swedish and Danish countryside) - the motif)s local file continuing past the sagas into the ethnographic record."
    },
