    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Islenzk Fornrit editions; Gr\xE1g\xE1s ed. Finsen 1852; paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The outlaw-boundary file holds the saga-law's hero-tales. V\xEDga-Gl\xFAmr: the fate-cleared killer (the naming-day omen: 'thrice? - 'should? - 'he? - be? - outlawed? - 'and? - 'thrice? - cleared? - 'the? - grandfather's? - prophecy? - - the killer whose outlawries are cleared by settlement, the fate-meter running through the courts), and the land-wight's? - the? - 'the? - eye-motes? - episode (the old man's eye-rubbing that clears Gl\xFAmr's sight? - 'the? - 'old? - man? - 'rubbing? - his? - eye? - 'as? - Gl\xFAmr? - 'strikes? - - the sympathetic-magic detail the saga preserves), and the saga's verdict: the fate runs through the law.\n\nGrettir and G\xEDsli complete the register: Grettir's long outlawry (the dark-fear as the sentence's instrument - the record norse-grettir-saga), and G\xEDsli's dream-vigil (the outlaw haunted by two dreams - the bright dream-wife and the blood-handed dream-wife - 'the? - 'two? - 'women? - 'who? - 'come? - 'in? - sleep? - 'the? - - the fate's two counselors, and the sister's oath-betrayal (Th\xF3rd\xEDs revealing the brother-killer at the farm-council - the oath's last breach), the outlaw's end in the single combat against overwhelming odds ('the? - 'last? - 'stand? - 'with? - the? - sword? - 'stuck? - in? - the? - hillside? - - the hero's death-position preserved in verse).\n\nThe law's clauses complete the file: the Gr\xE1g\xE1s outlawry-terms (the lesser outlawry: three years and property; the full outlawry: the sk\xF3garma\xF0r - the forest-man, who may be killed by anyone; the fj\xF6rbaugsgar\xF0r? - the? - - the foreign-service option: three years overseas as the sentence's alternative), the law's grammar of exile. The record's cultural functions are the law-as-fate doctrine (the courts as the fate's machinery - the record norse-njalssaga's thesis continued) and the heroism-measured-by-law doctrine: the saga-hero's greatness calibrated in outlawry-terms. In the atlas, the record anchors the outlaw-boundary family: each a tradition's hero-versus-law register; the Norse one is distinctive in the fate-cleared killer.\n\nFor the database, this node holds the saga-law register: the boundary where heroism and outlawry meet.\n\nThe record's genre-boundary completes the file: the family-sagas' outlaw-tales as the myth-motifs' legal laboratory (the fate-prophecies running through the courts, the curses and oaths enforceable in law, the ghosts testifying in disputes) - the myth-tradition's motifs documented inside ordinary litigation, the corpus's social register at its most precise. And the outlaw's afterlife completes it: the sk\xF3garma\xF0r figure's folk-afterlife (the forest-man of later ballads) - the boundary-tales' persistence beyond the law-books."
    },
