    license: "Public domain (Laing 1844); Palsson & Edwards 1978 passages are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The chieftain-lines' founding-file: the Lade-jarls (Hakon Grjotgardsson raised by Harald Fairhair to rule Trondelag - the king-made jarl), the Orkney-jarls (Rognvald receiving the islands from Harald; the vengeance-line: Torf-Einar's peat-cutting nickname - the jarl named by fuel), and the jarl-doctrine: the jarls as the kings' delegated sovereignty, with title-deeds in verse (the H\xE1leygjatal and Ynglingatal composed for jarls). The record is the jarl-file.",
      zh: "\u914B\u9577\u4E16\u7CFB\u7684\u958B\u57FA\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u8D6B\u62C9\u8FEA\u723E\u4F2F\u7235\uFF08\u91D1\u9AEE\u54C8\u62C9\u723E\u62D4\u64E2\u54C8\u5EB7\u7D71\u6CBB\u7279\u502B\u5FB7\u62C9\u683C\u2014\u2014\u300C\u738B\u6240\u518A\u5C01\u4E4B\u4F2F\u7235\u300D\uFF09\u3001\u5967\u514B\u5C3C\u4F2F\u7235\uFF08\u7F85\u683C\u6069\u74E6\u723E\u5FB7\u53D7\u54C8\u62C9\u723E\u8CDC\u5CF6\uFF1B\u5FA9\u4EC7\u4E16\u7CFB\uFF1A\u6258\u723E\u592B-\u57C3\u7D0D\u723E\u4EE5\u6316\u6CE5\u70AD\u5F97\u7DBD\u865F\u2014\u2014\u300C\u4EE5\u71C3\u6599\u547D\u540D\u4E4B\u4F2F\u7235\u300D\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u4F2F\u7235\u6559\u7FA9\uFF1A\u4F2F\u7235\u4E43\u570B\u738B\u6388\u6B0A\u4E4B\u4E3B\u6B0A\u8005\uFF0C\u5176\u6191\u8B49\u5373\u980C\u8A69\uFF08\u300A\u8D6B\u840A\u5409\u4E9E\u5854\u723E\u300B\u8207\u300A\u82F1\u683C\u6797\u5854\u723E\u300B\u7686\u70BA\u4F2F\u7235\u800C\u4F5C\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u4F2F\u7235\u6A94\u6848\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Laing 1844 (public domain); Palsson & Edwards, Orkneyinga Saga, London 1978 (paraphrased)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The jarl-file holds the chieftain-lines' foundings. The Lade-jarls: Hakon Grjotgardsson's grant (Harald Fairhair raising the Hladir-house to rule Trondelag - the king-made jarl as the north's second power), and the jarl-house's poetry-commission (the H\xE1leygjatal composed for Hakon jarl - the earls claiming the same god-descent as the kings: the jarls' charter in the kings' genre).\n\nThe Orkney-jarls: Rognvald's grant (the islands received from Harald after the raid - the earldom as the king's compensation), the vengeance-line (Rognvald killed by Harald's son, avenged by Torf-Einar with the blood-eagle - the record norse-ivar-blood-eagle), and the nickname's provenance (Torf-Einar named for the peat-cutting his brothers scorned - the fuel-named jarl: the court's scorn converted into the dynasty's name). The jarl-doctrine completes the file: the jarls as delegated sovereignty (the earldoms as land-grants with title-deeds in verse - the corpus's theory of sub-royal power: power's second story, told in the same mythic grammar).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the delegated-sovereignty doctrine (the jarl as the king's tenant-in-chief: power descending by grant, legitimated by the same genealogies) and the nickname-provenance doctrine (the jarls named by their trade: the secondary aristocracy's self-made names). In the atlas, the record anchors the chieftain-line family: each a tradition's sub-royal power-file; the Norse one is distinctive in the verse title-deeds.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the king-records and the blood-eagle-record: here power's second story.\n\nThe record's charter-method completes the file: the jarls' genealogies composed by the same skalds who sang for kings (the H\xE1leygjatal's god-descent claimed for an earl's line), so the delegated power's legitimacy is documented in the same mythic grammar as the kings' - power's second story told in the first story's language. And the earldoms' archaeology completes it: the Orkney earls' burial-mounds and the Lade-hall's site - the chieftain-lines' material register alongside the verse title-deeds.\n\n The record's Hladir-palace register completes the file: the Lade-jarl)s hall as the north)s second court (the feasts at which the Yule-bl\xF3t's crisis played out - the record norse-yule-blot-hakon's stage), so the jarl-file)s power is documented at the feast-table as well as in the grant - the second court)s rituals attested beside its title-deeds.\n\n The record's dynastic-continuity completes the file: the Orkney-line)s centuries (the earls running from Torf-Einar to the Norse twilight) documented as the grant)s durability - the charter)s title-deeds outlasting the kings who issued them. And the Hla\xF0ir-palace)s archaeology completes it: the Lade-site)s hall-remains beneath Trondheim - the second court)s material seat documented beside its verse."
    },
