    license: "Public domain (Laing 1844); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The bl\xF3t's practice-variants collected: the scale-register (the household bl\xF3t at the farm's hall - the farmer's rite with ale and horse-flesh; the district-bl\xF3t at the hof; the national high-feast at Uppsala), the seasonal-register (the three annual feasts: midwinter, spring sigrbl\xF3t, autumn), the liturgical register (the hammer-sign over horn and cauldron - the hallowing-gesture documented), and the legal register (the law-codes' clauses: feast obligations and fines for absence - the rite as statute). The record is the sacrifice-practice file.",
      zh: "\u5E03\u6D1B\u7279\u796D\u7684\u5BE6\u8E10\u8B8A\u9AD4\u5168\u96C6\uFF1A\u898F\u6A21\u5C64\u7D1A\uFF08\u8FB2\u5BB6\u5927\u5EF3\u7684\u5BB6\u796D\u2014\u2014\u4EE5\u9EA5\u9152\u8207\u99AC\u8089\u884C\u796D\uFF1B\u795E\u5EDF\u5730\u5340\u796D\uFF1B\u70CF\u666E\u85A9\u62C9\u5168\u570B\u9AD8\u5BB4\uFF09\u3001\u5B63\u7BC0\u5C64\u7D1A\uFF08\u4E09\u5927\u5E74\u5EA6\u796D\uFF1A\u4EF2\u51AC\u796D\u3001\u6625\u5B63\u52DD\u5229\u796D\u3001\u79CB\u5B63\u796D\uFF09\u3001\u5100\u8ECC\u5C64\u7D1A\uFF08\u89D2\u676F\u8207\u934B\u4E0A\u7684\u96F7\u9318\u4E4B\u5370\u2014\u2014\u6709\u64DA\u53EF\u67E5\u7684\u795D\u8056\u624B\u52E2\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u6CD5\u5F8B\u5C64\u7D1A\uFF08\u6CD5\u5178\u689D\u6B3E\uFF1A\u796D\u5BB4\u7FA9\u52D9\u8207\u7F3A\u5E2D\u7F70\u6B3E\u2014\u2014\u796D\u5100\u5373\u6CD5\u4EE4\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u737B\u796D\u5BE6\u8E10\u6A94\u6848\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Laing 1844 (public domain); Gr\xE1g\xE1s ed. Finsen 1852 (public domain); paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The sacrifice-practice file holds the bl\xF3t's variants. The scale-register: the household-bl\xF3t (the farmer's own rite - the sagas' farm-sacrifices: ale and horse-flesh at the private hall; the record norse-nine-year-blot's national feast at the scale's top), the district-bl\xF3t at the hof (the go\xF0i's feast for the free farmers - the record norse-thorolf-mostarskegg's district-institution), and the legal register: the law-codes' feast-obligations (the fines for absence from the district-bl\xF3t - the rite as statute: attendance legally required, the corpus's sacrifice-law).\n\nThe seasonal-register: the three annual feasts (Snorri's triad: the midwinter bl\xF3t for crops and peace, the spring sigrbl\xF3t for victory, and the autumn-bl\xF3t for the winter's stores - the calendar-of-sacrifice: the year's rhythm as the rite's structure). The liturgical register: the hallowing-gesture (the hammer-sign over horn and cauldron - the Hakon-chapter's documented gesture: the Christian king's cross mistaken for it, the record norse-yule-blot-hakon), the cup-circles by god, and the meat's distribution (the sacrificial flesh boiled in the cauldrons and eaten by the feasters - the sacrifice as a meal: the communion-structure of the bl\xF3t).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the practice-variant doctrine (the bl\xF3t's scales and seasons as one institution's registers - the corpus's fullest ritual-practice documentation) and the sacrifice-as-meal doctrine (the god fed and the people fed in one act: the communion-grammar). In the atlas, the record anchors the sacrifice-practice family: each a tradition's rite documented at multiple scales; the Norse one is distinctive in the legal obligation.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the temple-records and the feast-records: here the practice-file runs whole.\n\nThe record's legal register completes the file: the law-codes' conversion-era clauses (the Gulathing-law's amended bl\xF3t-clauses outlawing the private sacrifice, the fines transformed into penalties) document the rite's end as statute - the practice-file's arc running from obligation to prohibition, the bl\xF3t's life documented from household hearth to banned rite. And the liturgical inventory completes the corpus: the hammer-sign, the cup-circles, the meat-boilers and the sprinkling-twigs - the full equipment-list of the north's sacrifice, documented across saga and law.\n\n The record's scale-economics completes the file: the three registers' costs documented (the farmer)s own beast at the hearth; the go\xF0i)s feast shared by the district; the king)s ninefold offering at Uppsala) - the sacrifice)s burden graded by scale, the rite)s social distribution visible in who paid. And the meal)s theology completes it: the gods) share and the people)s share from the same carcass - the communion-structure that made the bl\xF3t both worship and dinner, documented in the feast-liturgy)s own clauses."
    },
