    language: "Latin; Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Frederick Metcalfe, Passio et Miracula Beati Olaui, Oxford 1881 (public domain); paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The shrine's operation-log holds the martyr-king's cult beyond the core-record (norse-olaf-haraldsson-stiklestad). The cure-register: the shrine's vigil-cures (the sick keeping the saint's mass-nights at Nidaros - the record's healed blind, the freed cripples, the mad restored - the Passio's case-book in operation), and the pilgrimage-economy (the roads to the shrine, the offerings' flow - the cult as institution). The punishment-register: the shrine's vengeance (the thief struck blind at the relics; the perjurer's swelling? - 'the? - 'saint's? - 'judgment? - 'on? - 'the? - 'oath-breaker? - - the cult's legal face: the saint enforcing oaths sworn at his altar, the record norse-oath-ring-ullr's grammar in Christian dress).\n\nThe rescue-register: the king-at-sea (the drowning clients calling the saint's name - the shrine's patronage extended to the roads and waters), and the battle-standard (the saint's banner carried in the kings' wars - the cult as the state's flag). The political miracles complete the log: the realm's protection (the shrine as the kingdom's patron - the kings' legitimacy argued at the altar; the record norse-king-saint-synthesis holds the synthesis).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the shrine-operation doctrine (the cult documented as a working institution: cures, punishments, rescues, standards - the hagiography as administration) and the oath-enforcement doctrine (the saint as the converted oath-law's guarantor - the pagan oath-ring's successor). In the atlas, the record anchors the shrine-log family: each a cult's operation-file; the Nidaros one is distinctive in the standard.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the core-record: here the shrine's daily business.\n\nThe log's documentary method completes the file: each cure dated, witnessed and deposed (the shrine's witnesses named, the healers' depositions recorded - the Passio's evidentiary apparatus applied to the shrine's daily business), so the miracle-log is an institution's registry rather than a legend-collection: the cures entered as evidence, the shrine's power audited in writing. And the geography completes the register: the cures' clients documented across the Norse world (the pilgrims from Iceland, the Orkneys and the Faroes at Nidaros's feasts) - the shrine's catchment as the north's own pilgrimage-map, the cult's reach measured in travel-distance.\n\nThe log's liturgical frame completes the file: the cures documented at the shrine's feasts (the mass-days and the vigil-nights as the miracles' stage - the cult's calendar driving the registry), so the operation-log's rhythm is the church-year's own: the saint's power celebrated on schedule, the cures clustering where the crowds came. And the record's historiographic note completes it: the miracle-books' medieval editorial care (the older registers abridged into the Passio's composite - the cult's paper-trail layered), the shrine's file preserved through successive redactions - the north's best-documented cult documented through its own paperwork."
    },
