    license: "Public domain (older editions); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The conversion's royal register collected: the sanctified kings (St \xD3l\xE1fr's cult as the model; the cults proposed for \xD3l\xE1fr Tryggvason and for Knut the Saint of Denmark), the demonized kings (the sagas' villain-register: the oath-breaking and kin-slaying rulers cast as the anti-saints), and the mechanism (the cult as legitimacy: the king's violent death sanctified by the church, the dynasty's charter written in relics). The record is the royal-cult file: sanctity as statecraft.",
      zh: "\u7688\u4F9D\u6642\u4EE3\u7684\u738B\u5BB6\u767B\u8A18\u518A\u5168\u96C6\uFF1A\u53D7\u5C01\u8056\u5F92\u7684\u570B\u738B\uFF08\u8056\u5967\u62C9\u592B\u5D07\u62DC\u70BA\u7BC4\u5F0F\uFF1B\u5967\u62C9\u592B\xB7\u7279\u91CC\u683C\u74E6\u677E\u8207\u4E39\u9EA5\u300C\u8056\u514B\u52AA\u7279\u300D\u7684\u5D07\u62DC\u52D5\u8B70\uFF09\u3001\u88AB\u5996\u9B54\u5316\u7684\u570B\u738B\uFF08\u85A9\u8FE6\u7684\u53CD\u9762\u4EBA\u7269\u540D\u518A\uFF1A\u80CC\u8A93\u3001\u5F12\u89AA\u7684\u7D71\u6CBB\u8005\u88AB\u5851\u9020\u6210\u300C\u53CD\u8056\u5F92\u300D\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u5176\u6A5F\u5236\uFF08\u5D07\u62DC\u5373\u5408\u6CD5\u6027\uFF1A\u6559\u6703\u5C07\u570B\u738B\u7684\u66B4\u6B7B\u5C01\u8056\uFF0C\u738B\u671D\u61B2\u7AE0\u5BEB\u5165\u8056\u9AD1\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u738B\u5BB6\u5D07\u62DC\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u795E\u8056\u6027\u5373\u6CBB\u8853\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic; Latin",
    canonical_translation: "Standard editions; paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The royal-cult file holds the conversion's royal register. The sanctified kings: the model (St \xD3l\xE1fr's cult - the record norse-olaf-haraldsson-stiklestad; the martyr-king as the north's sanctity-template), the attempts (\xD3l\xE1fr Tryggvason's proposed cult at the mission-memorials; Knut the Saint of Denmark's 12th-century canonization - the Danish martyr-king, the second of the model), and the cults' politics (each sanctity a dynasty's charter: the relics legitimating the line).\n\nThe demonized kings complete the register: the sagas' villain-file (the oath-breaking, kin-slaying rulers of the narratives - the anti-saints whose deaths the sources relish: the blind? - the? - maimed? - kings? - 'the? - - the conversion-era's moralization of royal violence: the old heroic deaths re-read as judgments). And the mechanism completes the file: the cult as statecraft (the church's canonization-power and the dynasty's need meeting in the shrine - the corpus's clearest sanctity-economy, the record norse-christian-mythic-syncretism's frame applied to royalty).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the royal-cult doctrine (the king's body as the realm's collateral, now paid in sanctity - the record norse-ynglinga-odin-migration's god-king doctrine's Christian successor) and the moralization-doctrine (the villain-register as the cult's mirror: sanctity defined by its negatives). In the atlas, the record anchors the royal-cult family: each a tradition's sanctified and damned rulers; the Norse one is distinctive in the conversion's speed.\n\nFor the database, this node synthesizes the king-records: here the cult-mechanism file.\n\nThe register's mechanism-completes the synthesis: the church's canonization-power and the dynasty's need meeting in the shrine (the cult granted for loyalty, the miracles certified by the bishops - the sanctity-economy's exchange documented: legitimacy for obedience), so the royal-cult file documents the north's own saint-making machine. And the villain-register's theology completes it: the demonized kings' deaths narrated as judgments (the maimed and blinded rulers of the converted narratives - the old heroic deaths re-read as providence), the cult's mirror: sanctity defined by its negatives, the conversion's moral vocabulary turning the pagan kings' fates into lessons.\n\nThe register's comparative frame completes the file: the royal-cults across the converted world (the Anglo-Saxon saint-kings, the Hungarian and Bohemian martyr-kings - the north's cults joining a European type), so the synthesis documents a genre, not an accident: the martyr-king as the age's standard charter, the Norse cases dated within the type's spread. And the register's irony completes it: the conversion imposed by the very kings later sanctified (the missions' violence absorbed into the cults' charters) - the north's sanctity built on its own conquest, the record's synthesis stated in the sources' own silence."
    },
