    language: "Latin; Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Older editions; paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The local-hagiography file holds the regional holy-men's legends. St Hallvar\xF0r's legend: the mercy-martyrdom (the young chieftain's son taking the falsely-accused woman aboard his skiff against the mob - 'the? - 'the? - 'the? - - and killed with her, the bodies weighted with a millstone and dropped in the fjord: 'the? - 'bodies? - 'floated? - 'the? - - the miracle that founds the cult: the incorrupt corpse rising, the millstone as the martyr's attribute), and the cult's sites (the skerry of the floating body, the shrine at the? - Oslo's cathedral? - the city's patron-saint: the record's own founding-legend, Oslo's shield bearing the saint with the millstone and the? - the? - axes).\n\nThe local holy-men's register completes the file: the regional wonder-workers beside the great cults (the Icelandic local saints' circle - the records norse-thorlakr-iceland-saint, norse-jon-ogmund-holar, norse-gudmundr-wonder-bishop; the Norwegian local martyrs beside the royal cults - the hagiography's small-scale register).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the mercy-martyrdom doctrine (the sanctity of the defenseless-defender: the corpus's clearest lay-saint) and the local-cult doctrine (the small shrines beside the great ones: the hagiography's geography). In the atlas, the record anchors the local-holy-man family: each a region's own saint; the Hallvar\xF0r one is distinctive in the floating corpse.\n\nFor the database, this node extends the hagiographic corpus to the local register: Norway's protomartyr and the small shrines.\n\nThe legend's documentary frame completes the file: the cult's certification (the floating corpse's witnesses, the shrine's translated relics, the mass and office composed for the local martyr - the hagiography's small-scale apparatus documented beside the great cults' bureaucracy), so the local-saint file documents not only the legend but its institution: the shrine's income, the feast-day's observances, the town's oaths sworn at the altar. And the mercy-doctrine's social reach completes it: the defenseless-defender as the converted ethics' lay-model (the sanctity of the witness-protection performed before the church arrives) - the corpus's clearest statement that the pagan north's own virtues could be sanctified whole.\n\nThe legend's Oslo-charter completes the file: the city's cult-institutions documented (the cathedral's shrine, the feast-day's statute, the seal's imagery - the patron-saint's rights written into the town's charters), so the local-saint file documents a city's self-image: Oslo's founding-legend carried on its seal, the martyr's mercy as the town's identity. And the legend's literary reach completes it: the saint's office in the Norwegian liturgy (the hymns and the readings composed for the local feast) - the protomartyr's cult documented in its books as well as its stones."
    },
