    license: "Public domain (Brodeur 1916; Bellows 1923); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Loki's small-trick catalog: the shape-tricks (the salmon's slip, the fly at the forge, the mare's lure, the seal's swim, the flea's bite), the theft-tricks (Sif's hair, Brisingamen, the? - 'the? - 'knife? - 'at? - the? - feast? - - the servant's killing), the tongue-tricks (the flyting's licenses, the insults' inventions, the false oaths), and the blame-tricks (the charges laid on others: 'the? - 'lies? - 'that? - 'stick? -). The record is the trick-catalog: the corpus's mischief-taxonomy.",
      zh: "\u6D1B\u57FA\u5C0F\u8A6D\u8A08\u76EE\u9304\uFF1A\u5316\u8EAB\u8A6D\u8A08\uFF08\u5316\u9BAD\u9B5A\u6E9C\u8D70\u3001\u935B\u7210\u65C1\u7684\u84BC\u8805\u3001\u8A98\u99AC\u7684\u6BCD\u99AC\u3001\u6CC5\u6C34\u7684\u6D77\u8C79\u3001\u53EE\u54AC\u7684\u8DF3\u86A4\uFF09\u3001\u7ACA\u53D6\u8A6D\u8A08\uFF08\u897F\u8299\u4E4B\u9AEE\u3001\u5E03\u91CC\u8F9B\u5609\u9580\u3001\u5BB4\u5E2D\u4E0A\u7684\u6BBA\u50D5\uFF09\u3001\u820C\u982D\u8A6D\u8A08\uFF08\u5435\u7F75\u7279\u6B0A\u3001\u8FB1\u7F75\u7684\u767C\u660E\u3001\u865B\u507D\u8A93\u8A00\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u6B78\u7F6A\u8A6D\u8A08\uFF08\u5C07\u7F6A\u540D\u683D\u7D66\u4ED6\u4EBA\u2014\u2014\u300C\u9ECF\u8457\u4E0D\u653E\u7684\u8B0A\u8A00\u300D\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u8A6D\u8A08\u76EE\u9304\uFF1A\u8A9E\u6599\u7684\u60E1\u4F5C\u5287\u5206\u985E\u5B78\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Brodeur 1916 (public domain); Bellows 1923 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The trick-catalog holds Loki's small register beside the great episodes (the records norse-loki-genealogy, norse-lokasenna-flyting, norse-loki-bound-serpent). The shape-tricks: the corpus's most complete transformation-register (the salmon's slip through the net; the fly stinging the smith; the mare luring the stallion; the seal's surf-duel; the flea's bite-and-turn) - the trickster's body as the catalog's inventory.\n\nThe theft-tricks: the small larcenies (Sif's midnight shearing; Brisingamen's unclasping; the knife at the feast that kills the servant - the flyting's opening crime) - each theft a door to a larger story (the treasures-cycle, the duel, the binding). The tongue-tricks: the verbal register (the flyting's licenses; the invented insults; the false oaths sworn smoothly - the corpus's best-documented liar). And the blame-tricks: the charges laid on others ('the? - 'lies? - 'that? - 'stick? - - the mischief's transference: the corpus's portrait of scapegoating as craft).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the trick-taxonomy doctrine (the mischief's kinds classified: shape, theft, tongue, blame - the trickster's method-file) and the small-trick engine doctrine (each small trick opening a cycle: the catalog as the myth-world's ignition-system). In the atlas, the record anchors the trick-catalog family: each a tradition's mischief-taxonomy; the Norse one is distinctive in the shape-inventory.\n\nFor the database, this node indexes the trick-records: here the small register.\n\nThe catalog's comparative frame completes the taxonomy: the trickster's register across traditions (the Greek Hermes' thefts, the Chinese Monkey's havoc, the Native American trickster-cycles) - each a mischief-taxonomy; the Norse one is distinctive in the villain-trajectory: Loki's small tricks escalate by rule into the corpus's catastrophe, the catalog documented as a career with a schedule. And the trick's victims completes it: the mischief aimed at the pantheon's own property (the gods' hair, necklace, wife, feast) - the trickster's economy documented as inside-theft rather than outside-attack.\n\nThe catalog's escalation-rule completes the taxonomy: the tricks' gradient documented (the small larcenies opening the treasure-cycle; the tongue-tricks ending in the flyting; the blame-tricks seeding the deaths - each trick's consequence tracked), so the catalog's order is causal: the mischief-chronology built from the trick-records' sequence - the corpus's own argument that the apocalypse is an aggregate of small thefts. And the trick-teller's license completes it: the catalog's delight (the sources relishing the tricks' ingenuity even as they condemn) - the myth-world's ambivalence documented in its own narration.\n\n The file's Sigurd-echo completes it: the trick's inheritance documented (Loki)s otter-deceit seeding the Sigurd-cycle\u2019s curse - the record norse-reginsmal\u2019s charter), the catalog\u2019s small register shown to drive the corpus\u2019s largest tragedy."
    },
