    license: "Public domain (CPB 1883); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The skaldic-reception catalog: the shield-poems (Bragi's and Thjodolfr's myth-panels), the genealogy-lays (Eyvindr's H\xE1leygjatal: the jarl's descent sung from Odin), the cult-verses (\xDAlfr Uggason's H\xFAsdr\xE1pa: Baldr's funeral and the hall's images skaldically described), the temple-verses (Eysteinn's fishing; Eilifr's Geirrod-lay), and the kenning-census (the myths' names as the poets' working vocabulary: 'the? - 'mead? - 'of? - 'Odin? - 'the? - 'ship? - 'of? - 'the? - giants? - - each citation a myth-citation). The record is the skaldic-reception file: court poetry as the myth corpus's oldest continuous witness.",
      zh: "\u541F\u904A\u8A69\u63A5\u53D7\u76EE\u9304\uFF1A\u76FE\u980C\uFF08\u5E03\u62C9\u57FA\u8207\u5E0C\u7D04\u5FB7\u723E\u5F17\u7684\u795E\u8A71\u5D4C\u677F\uFF09\u3001\u4E16\u7CFB\u980C\uFF08\u57C3\u6EAB\u5FB7\u723E\u7684\u300A\u8D6B\u840A\u5409\u4E9E\u5854\u723E\u300B\uFF1A\u4F2F\u7235\u4E16\u7CFB\u81EA\u5967\u4E01\u5531\u8D77\uFF09\u3001\u5D07\u62DC\u8A69\uFF08\u70CF\u723E\u592B\xB7\u70CF\u52A0\u677E\u7684\u300A\u5EF3\u5802\u980C\u300B\uFF1A\u4EE5\u541F\u904A\u8A69\u9AD4\u63CF\u5BEB\u5DF4\u723E\u5FB7\u846C\u79AE\u8207\u5927\u5EF3\u5716\u50CF\uFF09\u3001\u795E\u5EDF\u8A69\uFF08\u827E\u65AF\u6CF0\u56E0\u7684\u91E3\u86C7\u8A69\uFF1B\u827E\u5229\u5F17\u7684\u84CB\u723E\u7F85\u5FB7\u980C\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u96B1\u55BB\u666E\u67E5\uFF08\u795E\u8A71\u4E4B\u540D\u5373\u8A69\u4EBA\u7684\u5DE5\u4F5C\u8A5E\u5F59\uFF1A\u300C\u5967\u4E01\u7684\u871C\u9152\u300D\u300C\u5DE8\u4EBA\u7684\u8239\u300D\u2014\u2014\u6BCF\u4E00\u8655\u5F15\u7528\u7686\u70BA\u795E\u8A71\u5F15\u7528\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u541F\u904A\u8A69\u63A5\u53D7\u6A94\u6848\uFF1A\u5BAE\u5EF7\u8A69\u4F5C\u70BA\u795E\u8A71\u8A9E\u6599\u6700\u53E4\u8001\u4E14\u9023\u7E8C\u7684\u898B\u8B49\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse (skaldic verse)",
    canonical_translation: "Corpus Poeticum Boreale, ed. Vigfusson & Powell, Oxford 1883 (public domain); Skaldic database",
    narrative: {
      en: "The skaldic-reception file holds the court-poetry's mythic citations. The shield-poems: the myth-panels (Bragi's Ragnarsdrapa and Thjodolfr's Haustlong - the records norse-thor-fishing-serpent and norse-haustlong-skaldic: the corpus's oldest narrative-myth witnesses), the poets' ekphrasis as the courts' myth-books.\n\nThe genealogy-lays: the god-descent sung for jarls (Eyvindr's H\xE1leygjatal - the record norse-jarls-foundation-stories' title-deeds; the mythic genealogy as court-commission), the cult-verses: the halls' images described (\xDAlfr Uggason's H\xFAsdr\xE1pa: Baldr's funeral, Thor's fishing and Heimdall's? - the hall's painted walls skaldically narrated - the skald as the interior's guide-book), and the temple-verses: the exploits sung (Eysteinn's fishing-stanzas; Eilifr's Geirrod-lay - the records' skaldic witnesses).\n\nThe kenning-census completes the catalog: the myths as working vocabulary ('the? - mead? - 'of? - Odin? - 'for? - poetry; 'Ymir's? - 'blood? - 'for? - the? - sea; 'Freya's? - tears? - 'for? - gold? - - each kenning a myth-citation, the record norse-kenning-origin's grammar evidenced across the whole corpus). The record's cultural functions are the reception-catalog doctrine (the myth corpus's oldest continuous witness: the skaldic corpus documenting the myths' circulation century by century) and the citation-economy doctrine (each poem a myth-citation's receipt: the myths' court-currency documented). In the atlas, the record anchors the skaldic-reception family: each a tradition's court-poetry citation-file; the Norse one is distinctive in the kenning-economy.\n\nFor the database, this node indexes the skaldic-records already in the corpus: here the citation-census.\n\nThe catalog's chronology completes the register: the citations dated century by century (the 9th-century shield-poems' myths, the 10th-century genealogy-lays, the 11th-century cult-verses, the 12th-century lausavisur) - the skaldic-reception file as a timeline of the myth corpus's court-currency: which myths the courts still knew, and when, documented poem by poem. And the meter's discipline completes it: the dr\xF3ttkv\xE6tt constraints guaranteeing the citations' antiquity (the myths embedded in verse-forms that could not be casually invented) - the reception-file's dating-method documented.\n\nThe file's lost-layer problem completes the register: the skaldic corpus's losses documented (the poems known only through quotations - Snorri's Edda preserving verses whose long poems are gone), so the reception-file's witnesses are fragments of a destroyed library: the citation-census compiled from survivals, the myth-corpus's court-currency known only where the quotations kept it. And the database's own use completes it: the skaldic citations as the corpus's dating-instrument (the myths' antiquity argued from the verse's attestation) - the reception-file working as the myth database's chronometer.\n\n The file\u2019s Havamal-citation completes it: the wisdom-lay\u2019s own verses cited in the court-tradition (the guest-craft maxims quoted in the sagas\u2019 halls) - the reception-file\u2019s reach documented from the shield-poems to the proverb-book."
    },
