    language: "Old Norse iconography (carved stone, wood, parchment)",
    canonical_translation: "Identifications per standard iconographic scholarship; labeled",
    narrative: {
      en: "The picture-register file holds the myth corpus's carved and drawn register. The Altuna stone: the fishing carved (the god, the ox-head bait, the hammer raised - the record norse-thor-fishing-serpent's scene in stone: 'the? - 'whole? - 'fishing? - 'on? - 'one? - face? - - the iconographic case-study). The Gotland picture-stones: the rider on the eight-legged horse (Tj\xE4ngvide's mounted figure above the hall-scene - Odin's Sleipnir-ride carved for the dead: the ride-to-Valhalla as picture), and Ardre's scenes (the hall-images and the smith-scene - the Ardre VIII stone's V\xF6lundr-panels: the record norse-volund-smith's iconographic twin).\n\nThe runestone panels and portals: the Sigurd-scenes and the Ragnar\xF6k-cross (the records' carved material - the dragon-slayer and the doom on Christian stone), and the manuscript-drawings: the 17th-century myth-sheets (the Edda-paper MSS' illustrated pages: the myths drawn for the learned collectors - the corpus's picture-reception's late register). The record's cultural functions are the picture-register doctrine (the myths documented in image independent of the manuscripts: the iconography as a parallel witness-stream) and the image-context doctrine (each carving in its context: the memorial, the church-door, the collector's page - the pictures' functions dated by their settings). In the atlas, the record anchors the picture-register family: each a tradition's myth-image file; the Norse one spans stone to parchment.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the carved-records: here the pictures' file.\n\nThe file's identifications-method completes the register: the picture-record's evidentiary care documented (each carving's reading argued from the attributes: the eight legs, the ox-head, the heart - the iconography's grammar reconstructed attribute by attribute), so the picture-file's method is the database's own: inference from documented detail, with uncertainties labeled. And the manuscript-drawings' provenance completes it: the 17th-century myth-sheets' collectors (the learned Icelanders drawing the gods for the antiquarian market) - the picture-register's late layer documented as scholarship's first illustrated mythology.\n\nThe file's geographic-spread completes the register: the images' distribution documented (the Gotlandic picture-stones, the Swedish runestones, the English crosses, the Norwegian portals - one iconography across the Norse world's coasts), so the picture-file's reach is the diaspora's: the myths carved wherever the Norse settled, the image-register mapping the tradition's territory. And the dating-method completes it: the carvings' styles sequenced (the ornament-chronology used to date the myth-scenes) - the picture-register's chronology documented by style, the images' order argued from their borders.\n\n The file\u2019s Gosforth-junction completes it: the English cross\u2019s panels joining the Swedish stones in one register - the picture-file\u2019s geography spanning the whole Norse world, the images dated and compared across the sea."
    },
