    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "W. C. Green, The Saga of Egil Skallagrimsson, London 1893 (public domain); Islenzk Fornrit II",
    narrative: {
      en: "The verse-wergild file holds the head-ransom's episode. The capture: Egill shipwrecked at the enemy's court ('the? - 'poet? - 'at? - 'the? - mercy? - of? - 'the? - king? - 'whose? - land-spirits? - 'he? - 'had? - cursed? - - the record norse-egill-nithing-pole's curse returning to its author: the poet caught by the king he had driven out).\n\nThe night's composition and the recitation: the head-ransom composed in the dark (the dr\xF3ttkv\xE6tt masterpiece paid for the head - 'the? - 'poem? - 'made? - 'in? - 'one? - night? - 'the? - - the corpus's most famous composition-legend: the death-sentence converted to art in a night's work), the recitation at the king's court (the poem 'that? - 'turned? - 'the? - death? - - the ransom accepted: the king's judgment converted to payment - the corpus's clearest case of poetry as currency), and the compensation-case: the later arbitration (Egill's claim against the crown settled at the law-rock: the wergild's institutional form - the poet's family-claim argued by the poet himself, the record norse-ulf-outlaw-boundary's law-world in the poet's own case).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the verse-wergild doctrine (the poet's head priced in meter - the corpus's currency-theory at its purest: art as legal payment) and the composition-legend doctrine (the night-made masterpiece as the skaldic ethos: the craft's worth proven under sentence). In the atlas, the record anchors the verse-wergild family: each a tradition's poet-ransoms; the Norse one is distinctive in the meter.\n\nFor the database, this node complements the pole-record and the outlaw-record: here the payment-file.\n\nThe file's meter-craft completes the register: the head-ransom's dr\xF3ttkv\xE6tt documented (the poem's internal rhymes and alliteration woven under death-pressure - the composition-legend's technical claim examined: whether the masterwork was made in one night or polished after), so the verse-wergild file documents both the legend and its skepticism: the poem's price debated as the corpus's own crux. And the compensation-case's aftermath completes it: Egill's later settlement with the crown (the wergild's final form: the arbitration's payment and the poet's peace) - the verse-payment's institutional closure documented.\n\nThe file's geography completes the register: the head-ransom's road documented (the shipwreck's shore, the court at York, the return by the northern route - the poet's journey mapped), so the verse-wergild file's episode has a map: the ransom performed in the enemy's capital, the payment carried home by sea. And the poem's survival completes it: the Hofudlausn's transmission (the stanzas preserved in the saga and the skaldic books - the ransom's art outliving the ransom) - the payment's instrument still readable, the corpus's currency documented in its own surviving coin."
    },
