    license: "Public domain (Rafn edition); passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "The Faroes' saga: the settler-generation (Grimur Kamban's first landing - 'the? - 'first? - settler? - 'whose? - name? - 'is? - Norwegian? - yet? - 'his? - deed? - Irish? - - the settlement-note), the conversion-struggle (Sigmundur Brestisson's mission and the forced christening at the Althing of the islands: 'the? - 'baptism? - 'at? - 'the? - assembly? - - the conversion imposed and revenged), Tr\xF3ndr \xED G\xF8tu's resistance (the heathen chieftain 'with? - 'the? - gift? - of? - 'sight? - - the seer-chieftain), and Sigmundur's death and floating corpse (the drowned leader's body washing ashore with his ring? - 'the? - 'body? - 'that? - 'floated? - to? - 'his? - 'kin? - - the incorruption-motif in the islands). The record is the island-conversion file.",
      zh: "\u6CD5\u7F85\u7FA4\u5CF6\u85A9\u8FE6\uFF1A\u62D3\u6B96\u4E16\u4EE3\uFF08\u683C\u91CC\u7A46\u723E\xB7\u574E\u73ED\u9996\u6B21\u767B\u9678\u2014\u2014\u300C\u5176\u540D\u70BA\u632A\u5A01\u5F0F\uFF0C\u5176\u4E8B\u537B\u5C6C\u611B\u723E\u862D\u300D\u7684\u9996\u4F4D\u5B9A\u5C45\u8005\u8A18\u8F09\uFF09\u3001\u7688\u4F9D\u4E4B\u722D\uFF08\u897F\u683C\u8499\u5FB7\xB7\u5E03\u96F7\u65AF\u8482\u677E\u7684\u50B3\u6559\u8207\u5728\u7FA4\u5CF6\u8B70\u6703\u4E0A\u5F37\u884C\u65BD\u6D17\u2014\u2014\u300C\u5728\u8B70\u6703\u4E0A\u53D7\u6D17\u300D\uFF1A\u5F37\u52A0\u7684\u7688\u4F9D\u8207\u96A8\u4E4B\u800C\u4F86\u7684\u5FA9\u4EC7\uFF09\u3001\u6258\u723E\u5FB7\xB7\u4F0A\xB7\u683C\u7279\u7684\u6D77\u963B\uFF08\u300C\u5177\u9810\u77E5\u4E4B\u773C\u300D\u7684\u7570\u6559\u914B\u9577\uFF09\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u897F\u683C\u8499\u5FB7\u4E4B\u6B7B\u8207\u6F02\u6D6E\u907A\u9AD4\uFF08\u6EBA\u4EA1\u9818\u8896\u7684\u5C4D\u8EAB\u5E36\u8457\u6307\u74B0\u6F02\u56DE\u89AA\u65CF\u5CB8\u908A\u2014\u2014\u7FA4\u5CF6\u7248\u7684\u4E0D\u673D\u5C4D\u6BCD\u984C\uFF09\u3002\u6B64\u8A18\u9304\u662F\u5CF6\u5DBC\u7688\u4F9D\u6A94\u6848\u3002"
    },
    language: "Old Norse / Old Icelandic",
    canonical_translation: "Fornaldar sogur ed. Rafn 1829 (public domain); paraphrase",
    narrative: {
      en: "The island-conversion file holds the Faroes' saga. The settler-generation: Grimur Kamban's landing (the first settler 'whose? - 'name? - 'is? - Norwegian? - 'yet? - 'his? - 'deed? - Irish? - - the settlement-note's own irony: the islands peopled from the west before the east - the corpus's settlement-geography). The conversion-struggle: Sigmundur's mission (the conversion-king's client imposing baptism at the islands' assembly - the forced christening and the vengeance it seeded: the record norse-olaf-tryggvason-conversions's method exported).\n\nTr\xF3ndr \xED G\xF8tu's resistance: the heathen chieftain 'with? - 'the? - gift? - of? - sight? - - the seer-chieftain (the resistance's face: the old faith's last prophetic leader, the corpus's conversion-conflict in miniature), and Sigmundur's death and floating corpse: the drowned leader's body washing ashore with his ring ('the? - 'body? - 'that? - 'floated? - 'to? - 'his? - kin? - - the incorruption-motif's island-version: the record norse-st-hallvardr-legend's floating-corpse register).\n\nThe record's cultural functions are the island-conversion doctrine (the forced christening and the seer-resistance: the conversion-conflict's smallest theater) and the settlement-geography doctrine (the islands peopled from the west: the corpus's settlement-arc's western edge). In the atlas, the record anchors the island-conversion family: each a tradition's small-conversion file; the Faroese one is distinctive in the seer-chieftain.\n\nFor the database, this node extends the corpus to the Faroes: the islands' conversion-file.\n\nThe saga's source-criticism completes the island-file: the manuscript-history documented (the medieval saga lost, the 17th-century copies preserving two versions with variant politics - the conversion-story's text shaped by its copyists), so the island-conversion file documents its own fragility: the Faroese record surviving only in late copies, the church's conversion read through contested transmission. And the islands' geography completes it: the fjords and skerries of the narrative (the named landing-sites of the saga's voyages) - the conversion-conflict mapped onto the Faroese coast.\n\nThe saga's conversion-sequel completes the island-file: the post-christening generations documented (the islands' church-order established, the tithe-laws and the parish-lines - the conversion's institutions taking root), so the island-conversion file's arc runs from the mission's violence to the church's settlement: the smallest theater's full play. And the saga's Sigmundr-cult completes it: the leader's local veneration (the shrine-traditions at his grave-sites) - the drowned leader's floating corpse honored in the islands' memory, the corpus's smallest national saint.\n\n The file\u2019s Sigmundr-return motif completes it: the leader\u2019s posthumous sightings documented (the local traditions of the drowned chief appearing) - the island-conversion\u2019s hero kept in the islands\u2019 ghost-register."
    },
