    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive (Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature vol. II) \u2014 modern translation quoted only as paraphrase",
      work: "The Book of the Heavenly Cow (tomb of Seti I, KV 17; earlier copy in tomb of Tutankhamun); canonical trans. Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature vol. II (Berkeley, 1976)",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/ancientegyptianl0000lich"
    },
    license: "Ancient text public domain; Lichtheim translation copyrighted \u2014 passages below are labeled paraphrases",
    summary: {
      en: "Sequel to the Destruction of Mankind: grown weary of ruling blood-soaked humans, the aged sun god rides up on the back of Nut, who becomes a great cow lifted by Shu, and is enthroned in the sky away from humanity; from the darkness of his flight come the stars, and the Greeks' own sky-scape of the Milky Way and constellations is fixed.",
      zh: "\u3008\u6EC5\u4EBA\u985E\u3009\u4E4B\u7E8C\u7BC7\uFF1A\u5E74\u9081\u7684\u592A\u967D\u795E\u53AD\u65BC\u7D71\u6CBB\u6CBE\u8840\u7684\u4EBA\u985E\uFF0C\u9A0E\u4E0A\u5347\u4E0A\u5929\u725B\u4E4B\u80CC\u2014\u2014\u52AA\u7279\u88AB\u8212\u8209\u8D77\u6210\u70BA\u5DE8\u725B\u2014\u2014\u9000\u5C45\u9AD8\u5929\u4E0D\u518D\u81E8\u4E16\uFF1B\u5176\u98DB\u884C\u6697\u5F71\u5316\u70BA\u661F\u8FB0\uFF0C\u5929\u57DF\u79E9\u5E8F\u7531\u6B64\u5B9A\u578B\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "transformation",
      "sacred-kingship",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient funerary-royal corpus, no living community restrictions. The Lichtheim translation is copyrighted, so all passages are condensed paraphrases with mandatory notes, as the project rules require for modern copyrighted translations."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "After the goddess Sekhmet has slaughtered nearly all humankind in her frenzy, Ra reconsiders: men whom he still needs to live have been punished enough. But he has now seen what his creatures are, and the king who once ruled personally grows old and weary on his throne. He tells the assembled gods that he is exhausted and can no longer dwell among mankind; the rebellious Nine Bows have been repelled, but the delight of kingship over them is gone.",
        note: "Paraphrase of The Book of the Heavenly Cow (tomb of Seti I recension), opening column (trans. Lichtheim 1976); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Ra commands Nun to fetch Nut, and the sky-goddess assumes the form of a great cow, Mehueret, upon whose back the god mounts. Yet when she rises she trembles with the height, and Ra calls on the other gods to support her: Shu is set beneath her belly to lift her, and eight gods at each corner steady her legs \u2014 the origin of the eight sustainer-gods of heaven. The cow's belly becomes the sky we see; her four legs become the pillars at the horizon.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Book of the Heavenly Cow (trans. Lichtheim 1976); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "As Ra is borne upward through the primordial darkness, he cries out for light, and from his flight come the stars of the night sky; the constellations, the Fields of Rushes, and the river of stars are fixed as annals of his journey. Ra declares the future of heaven: he will withdraw into the sky itself, no longer reigning directly over men, but ruling through his breath, his Ba, and his moon \u2014 Thoth is created to be his night-deputy and scribe.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Book of the Heavenly Cow (trans. Lichtheim 1976); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "The text closes with the 'annals' Ra decrees: the sky is his palace in the West, the serpent Apophis his enemy, the Nine Bows of mankind his subjects yet kept at a distance, and the judgment of the dead assigned to the gods of the netherworld. The sun's return each morning re-enacts the ascent: he sails in the barque beneath the cow's belly, seen by no one until dawn.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Book of the Heavenly Cow (trans. Lichtheim 1976); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    language: "Egyptian (Middle Egyptian, royal tomb wall)",
    canonical_translation: "Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vol. II: The New Kingdom (Berkeley, 1976), 'The Myth of the Heavenly Cow'",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Book of the Heavenly Cow is the sequel to the Destruction of Mankind, and together the two texts form Egypt's strangest theological drama: a sun god who governs the world directly, loses his patience with it, and abdicates into the sky. The myth is attested from the late Middle Kingdom and is inscribed in full on the walls of the tombs of Seti I, Tutankhamun, Ramesses III, and Ramesses IV in the Valley of the Kings \u2014 royal texts, buried with kings, dealing with the abdication of kingship itself.\n\nBEGINNING: THE WEARY KING. When the Eye of Ra, in the form of the lioness Sekhmet, has waded knee-deep in human blood and been tricked with dyed beer into sparing the rest (that episode is cut separately in this corpus as 'The Destruction of Mankind'), the matter is not simply resolved. The assembled gods congratulate Ra on avenging himself, but the god does not share their relief. He has now learned what his creatures are \u2014 the rebels of the Nine Bows have been repelled, but ruling over them has lost its sweetness. He speaks the weary words of an old king: he is exhausted, his body is old, and he can no longer dwell among mankind. Divine kingship, so grand elsewhere in Egyptian ideology, here becomes a burden.\n\nMIDDLE: THE ASCENT. Ra commands Nun, the primeval water, to fetch his daughter Nut. She comes and is transformed into the great celestial cow, Mehueret, and Ra mounts her back. But no sooner is she aloft than she shudders \u2014 the height of the sky is terror, not triumph \u2014 and Ra commands the gods of the primeval company to assist her. Shu is placed beneath her belly as her eternal support; eight gods are set at her four legs, two to each, to steady her; the four legs become the four pillars of the sky at the horizon, and her belly with its stars becomes the vault we look at every night. The gods whom Ra had threatened with punishment are appeased; the Egyptians of the deserts and the foreigners of the Nine Bows remain below, no longer under the god's eye but under his distance.\n\nEND: THE DARK JOURNEY AND THE STARS. During the ascent the sun passes through absolute darkness, and from that night-flight come the stars and constellations \u2014 the text calls them the 'annals' of the journey, a celestial gazetteer written across the sky. Ra, enthroned at last in the deep of heaven, decrees the new order: he will reside in the western horizon and the deep of the sky; Thoth is created as his deputy to rule the night and record all things; the serpent Apophis remains his enemy; humankind will see him no longer in person but only in his forms \u2014 sun, moon, and barque. The daily renewal of creation continues, but at one remove: the cow's belly is between the god and his people. The myth thus explains both the Milky Road of stars and the theological distance of the solar god in the New Kingdom, and it grounds a ritual function: recited in royal tombs, it enacts the king's own hope of riding the same sky-cow into eternity.\n\nRELATION TO OTHER TRADITIONS. In motif terms the record joins the 'withdrawal of the god' family \u2014 deities who rule, are wounded or disillusioned, and relocate to a distant heaven \u2014 and shares with Nut-centered records the separation of sky from earth as a primordial event."
    },
    functions: [
      "Explains the fixed order of the night sky (stars, constellations, the cow's belly as heaven) as records of the god's ascent",
      "Royal-tomb liturgy: equips the dead king to make the same ascent on the celestial cow into the first heaven",
      "Theology of divine distance: the sun god withdraws from direct rule; Thoth installed as night-deputy",
      "Sequel to the Destruction of Mankind, resolving the theme of divine disillusionment with humankind"
    ],
