    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient literary tale; no living community restrictions. Coordinates are symbolic over the Red Sea mining route, since the enchanted island cannot be located."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Now I shall tell that which has happened to me, to my very self. I was going to the mines of Pharaoh, and I went down on the sea on a ship of 150 cubits long and 40 cubits wide, with 150 sailors of the best of Egypt, who had seen heaven and earth, and whose hearts were stronger than lions. They had said that the wind would not be contrary, or that there would be none. But as we approached the land the wind arose, and threw up waves eight cubits high. As for me, I seized a piece of wood; but those who were in the vessel perished, without one remaining. A wave threw me on an island, after that I had been three days alone, without a companion beside my own heart. I laid me in a thicket, and the shadow covered me.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Petrie, Egyptian Tales First Series (1895), The Shipwrecked Sailor."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Suddenly I heard a noise as of thunder, which I thought to be that of a wave of the sea. The trees shook, and the earth was moved. I uncovered my face, and I saw that a serpent drew near. He was thirty cubits long, and his beard greater than two cubits; his body was as overlayed with gold, and his colour as that of true lazuli. He coiled himself before me. Then he opened his mouth, while that I lay on my face before him, and he said to me, 'What has brought thee, what has brought thee, little one, what has brought thee? If thou sayest not speedily what has brought thee to this isle, I will make thee know thyself; as a flame thou shalt vanish, if thou tellest me not something I have not heard, or which I knew not, before thee.'",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Petrie (1895)."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Then said he to me, 'Fear not, fear not, little one, and make not thy face sad. If thou hast come to me, it is God who has let thee live. For it is He who has brought thee to this isle of the blest, where nothing is lacking, and which is filled with all good after another, until thou shalt be four months in this isle. Then a ship shall come from thy land with sailors, and thou shalt leave with them and go to thy country, and thou shalt die in thy town. I will therefore tell thee of that which is in this isle. I am here with my brethren and my children around me; we are seventy-five serpents, children, and kindred; without naming a young girl who was brought unto me by chance, and on whom the fire of heaven fell, and burnt her to ashes.'",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Petrie (1895)."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "Then he smiled at my speech, because of that which was in his heart, for he said to me, 'Thou art not rich in perfumes, for all that thou hast is but common incense. As for me I am prince of the land of Punt, and I have perfumes. Only the oil which thou sayedst thou wouldest bring is not common in this isle. But, when thou shalt depart from this place, thou shalt never more see this isle; it shall be changed into waves.' \u2014 And, behold, when the ship drew near, according to all that he had told me before, I got me up into an high tree, to strive to see those who were within it. Then he said to me, 'Farewell, farewell, go to thy house, little one, see again thy children, and let thy name be good in thy town; these are my wishes for thee.'",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Petrie (1895), closing (the serpent's farewell and gifts)."
      }
    ],
    language: "Egyptian (Middle Egyptian, Hermitage papyrus 1115)",
    canonical_translation: "W. M. Flinders Petrie, Egyptian Tales, First Series (London, 1895), from the Golenischeff translation",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Tale of the Shipwrecked Sailor is the oldest surviving 'enchanted island' story, framed like a persuasive speech: a returnee from a failed expedition addresses his superior, who has begun to doubt him. 'Wash thee, and turn the water over thy fingers; then go and tell the tale to the majesty' \u2014 and when the master mocks his wandering words, the sailor answers that although a man's mouth may save him, words may also cover his face with confusion. Then he swears his story is of himself, and tells it.\n\nBEGINNING: THE WRECK. Bound for the mines of Pharaoh with 150 sailors 'who had seen heaven and earth, and whose hearts were stronger than lions', the ship is struck by a wind that raises waves eight cubits high. All drown but the narrator, who clings to a plank and is thrown by a wave onto an island. Three days alone, he lies in a thicket and finds the island 'where nothing is lacking': figs and grapes, herbs, berries and grain, melons, fish and birds. He digs a pit, lights a fire, and makes a burnt-offering to the gods \u2014 the first act of a man who knows his survival depends on ritual correctness.\n\nMIDDLE: THE SERPENT'S AUDIENCE. Thunder without a storm: trees shake, the earth moves, and a serpent thirty cubits long appears, its beard over two cubits, its body gilded and its scales true lapis. Coiling before the prostrate sailor, it threatens him: 'As a flame thou shalt vanish, if thou tellest me not something I have not heard.' It takes the man in its mouth \u2014 gently, unharmed \u2014 carries him to its resting-place, and demands his story a second time. The sailor's reply is a full formal speech of his voyage; the serpent relents: 'Fear not, little one... it is God who has let thee live. He has brought thee to this isle of the blest.'\n\nEND: THE PROPHECY AND THE VANISHING ISLAND. The serpent foretells his rescue: four months on the island, then a ship from Egypt, and home to 'die in thy town.' It consoles the shipwrecked man with the wisdom of survival \u2014 'As for thee, if thou art strong, and if thy heart waits patiently, thou shalt press thy infants to thy bosom and embrace thy wife' \u2014 and tells of its own household, seventy-five serpents, kindred and children, and of a young girl brought by chance on whom the fire of heaven fell and burnt her to ashes: the serpent too has lost everything, and knows loss. When the sailor promises to bring oil and incense and sacrifice asses in its honor before Pharaoh, the serpent smiles: 'Thou art not rich in perfumes... As for me I am prince of the land of Punt, and I have perfumes.' Its island needs nothing and will need nothing: when the sailor departs he shall never see it again \u2014 'it shall be changed into waves.' The rescue ship comes exactly as foretold; the serpent loads him with myrrh, cassia, ivory, baboons and apes, and sends him home with the blessing 'let thy name be good in thy town.' The sailor ends his tale urging his superior to report him favorably: 'It was said unto me, 'Become a wise man, and thou shalt come to honour,' and behold I have become such.'\n\nFUNCTION. Recited as a frame-story of persuasion, the tale teaches patience under disaster ('Converse is pleasing, and he who tastes of it passes over his misery') and models the Egyptian confidence that correct speech, correct offering, and endurance will always bring the survivor home \u2014 while the island's sinking into waves keeps the world of marvels strictly separated from the world of report."
    },
    functions: [
      "Persuasive frame-story: teaches a superior to believe a report of marvels and reward the speaker",
      "Moral of endurance: patient heart and correct speech bring the survivor home to die in his own town",
      "Myth of the isle of the blest: an otherworld where 'nothing is lacking', ruled by a serpent-prince of Punt",
      "Earliest known form of the vanishing enchanted island that will recur from Troy to the Arabian Nights"
    ],
