    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient cult retold in Mackenzie 1913; passages verbatim from the retelling."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The chief god was Her-shef, who bears a stronger resemblance to Ptah Tanen than to Horus. He was a self-created Great Father, whose head was in the heavens while his feet rested upon the earth. His right eye was the sun and his left the moon, while his soul was the light that he shed over the world. He breathed from his nostrils the north wind, which gave life to every living being.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Mackenzie, Egyptian Myth and Legend (1913), ch. XIV."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "'Wind' and 'breath' and 'spirit' were believed by many primitive peoples to be identical. Her-shef was therefore the source of universal life. As a 'wind god' he resembles the southern deity Khnumu, who was also called Knef (the Kneph of the Greeks). The Egyptian knef means 'wind', 'breath', and 'spirit' \u2014 'the air of life'.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Mackenzie (1913), ch. XIV."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "shu"
    ]
