    summary: {
      en: "Huaijiang Mountain, in sight of the Hanging Gardens, is held by the god Yingzhao: a horse's body with a human face, tiger-striped and winged, whose cry sounds like a rolling winnow, and who patrols the four seas crying aloud \u2014 the warden whose shout carries around the world's rim.",
      zh: "\u69D0\u6C5F\u4E4B\u5C71\uFF0C\u5BE6\u60DF\u5E1D\u4E4B\u5E73\u5703\uFF1B\u795E\u82F1\u62DB\u53F8\u4E4B\uFF0C\u5176\u72C0\u99AC\u8EAB\u800C\u4EBA\u9762\uFF0C\u864E\u6587\u800C\u9CE5\u7FFC\uFF0C\u5F87\u65BC\u56DB\u6D77\uFF0C\u5176\u97F3\u5982\u69B4\u2014\u2014\u5DE1\u5B88\u56DB\u6975\u4E4B\u795E\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sacred-place",
      "transformation"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Passages are the compiler's condensed paraphrases of the Shanhaijing original."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Huaijiang Mountain, looking up at the emperor's Hanging Gardens, is governed by the god Yingzhao \u2014 a horse's body with a human face, tiger-striped, with a bird's wings, and a voice that rolls like a winnowing-hail. His office is the circuit of the four seas: he patrols the coasts of the world crying aloud, and the book adds that the Yellow River's spring rises from this mountain \u2014 the warden's rounds begin where the great water begins.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Shanhaijing, Xi Ci San Jing, Yingzhao entry (compiler's rendering); not verbatim. / Paraphrase of Shanhaijing, Xi Ci San Jing; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
