    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Passages are the compiler's condensed paraphrases of the Taiping Guangji text. The child-stabbing episode is recorded as the tale's darker layer."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Wei Gu walked in the moonlight outside a temple near Song and found an old man on the steps reading a ledger, with a bag of red threads at his feet; the ledger, the man said, was the book of all marriages, and a single red thread, tied ankle to ankle, joined every pair it named \u2014 however far they ran to escape it.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Taiping Guangji, juan 159, Dinghun Dian entry (compiler's rendering); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "He showed Wei Gu his own entry: a daughter of the vegetable market, a baby in a cradle by the greens. Wei Gu, insulted, sent a servant that night to stab the child; the knife left a scar over the brow and the child lived.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Taiping Guangji, juan 159, the stabbing; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Fourteen years on, the magistrate gave Wei Gu his daughter in marriage \u2014 a beauty, and brow-scarred; she told him at her mirror how she had been a grocer's baby stabbed in the cradle and adopted by an uncle. Wei Gu told her about the old man with the threads; both believed, and the temple of the Old Man Under the Moon has stood in the tale's honor ever since.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Taiping Guangji, juan 159, the marriage; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
