    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient king-atrocity tale via Herodotus; no living community restrictions. Pairs with the Ochus-apis record cut from Plutarch."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "When Cambyses arrived at Memphis, Apis appeared to the Egyptians, whom the Hellenes call Epaphos: and when he had appeared, forthwith the Egyptians began to wear their fairest garments and to have festivities. Cambyses accordingly seeing the Egyptians doing thus, and supposing that they were certainly acting so by way of rejoicing because he had fared ill, called for the officers who had charge of Memphis... They said that a god had appeared to them, who was wont to appear at intervals of long time, and that whenever he appeared, then all the Egyptians rejoiced and kept festival. Hearing this Cambyses said that they were lying, and as liars he condemned them to death.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Macaulay, Herodotus (1890), Book III.27."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "This Apis-Epaphos is a calf born of a cow who after this is not permitted to conceive any other offspring; and the Egyptians say that a flash of light comes down from heaven upon this cow, and of this she produces Apis. This calf which is called Apis is black and has the following signs, namely a white square upon the forehead, and on the back the likeness of an eagle, and in the tail the hairs are double, and on the tongue there is a mark like a beetle.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Macaulay (1890), Book III.28."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "When the priests had brought Apis, Cambyses being somewhat affected with madness drew his dagger, and aiming at the belly of Apis, struck his thigh: then he laughed and said to the priests: 'O ye wretched creatures, are gods born such as this, with blood and flesh, and sensible of the stroke of iron weapons? Worthy indeed of Egyptians is such a god as this. Ye however at least shall not escape without punishment for making a mock of me.'... Apis wounded by the stroke in his thigh lay dying in the temple. Him, when he had brought his life to an end by reason of the wound, the priests buried without the knowledge of Cambyses: but Cambyses, as the Egyptians say, immediately after this evil deed became absolutely mad, not having been really in his right senses even before that time.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Macaulay (1890), Book III.29-30."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "osiris"
    ]
