    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "From the text of Pepi II (1. 975 ff.) we learn the tradition that the wooden sides of the ladder were shaped by an adze wielded by the god Sashsa, that the rungs were made of the strong sinews of Kasut, the Bull of the Sky, and that they were fastened in their places on the sides of the ladder with knotted thongs made from the hide of the god Utes, the son of Hesat. This divine ladder was set up from earth to heaven by Horus and Ra according to one legend, and according to another by Horus and Set.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge, Osiris and the Egyptian Resurrection vol. I (1911), ch. III."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The text of Unas says: 'Ra setteth up the ladder before Osiris, Horus setteth up the ladder before his father Osiris in his going to his spirit. One of them [standeth] on this side, and one of them on that side. Unas is between them.' In the text of Pepi I we find: 'Homage to thee, Ladder of the god! Homage to thee, Ladder of Set! Stand up, Ladder of the god, stand up, Ladder of Set, stand up, Ladder of Horus, whereby Osiris made his way into heaven.'",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge (1911), ch. III, quoting the Pyramid Texts of Unas and Pepi I."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Lo, Khensu, lo, Aahes, Chief of the South-Land, lo, Tetun the Great, Chief of Ta-sti (Nubia), lo, Sept under his trees. They carry the ladder for this Pepi, they set up the ladder for this Pepi, they make firm the ladder for this Pepi. With the assistance of Horus and Set, Osiris stood on the ladder, and with their help he ascended and entered heaven. 'Every spirit and every god opened his hand to Pepi when he was on the ladder.'",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge (1911), ch. III."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "When Osiris ascended the ladder, he 'was covered with the covering of Horus, he wore the apparel of Thoth, Isis was in front of him, Nephthys was behind him, Ap-uat opened the way for him, Shu bore him up, the Souls of An drew him up the steps of the ladder, and Nut gave him her hands.'",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge (1911), ch. III, quoting Pepi I l. 308."
      }
    ],
