    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Archaeological doctrine via Budge 1899; no living community restrictions. Budge's 'ante-natal position' reading is his own inference."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "In the oldest tombs we find the skeleton laid upon its left side, with the limbs bent: the knees are on a level with the breast, and the hands are placed in front of the face. Generally the head faces towards the south, but no invariable rule seems to have been observed as to its orientation. Before the body was laid in the ground it was either wrapped in gazelle skin or laid in loose grass... In burials of this class there are no traces of mummification, or of burning, or of stripping the flesh from the bones.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge, Egyptian Ideas of the Future Life (1899), ch. V."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Now in all classes of tombs belonging to the prehistoric period in Egypt we find offerings in vases and vessels of various kinds, a fact which proves beyond all doubt that the men who made these graves believed that their dead friends and relatives would live again in some place, of the whereabouts of which they probably had very vague ideas, in a life which was, presumably, not unlike that which they had lived upon earth. The flint tools, knives, scrapers and the like indicate that they thought they would hunt and slay their quarry when brought down, and fight their foes... The man who would hunt and fight in the next world must live again; and if he would live again it must be either in his old body or in a new one; if in the old body, it must be revivified.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge (1899), ch. V."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The former are buried in the ante-natal position of a child, and we may perhaps be justified in seeing in this custom the symbol of a hope that as the child is born from this position into the world, so might the deceased be born into the life in the world beyond the grave; and the presence of amulets, the object of which was to protect the body, seems to indicate that they expected the actual body to rise again. The latter, by the mutilation of the bodies and the burning of the dead, seem to show that they had no hope of living again in their natural bodies.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge (1899), ch. V."
      }
    ],
    language: "Archaeological evidence (no text); Egyptian funerary doctrine",
    canonical_translation: "E. A. W. Budge, Egyptian Ideas of the Future Life (London, 1899), ch. V",
    narrative: {
      en: "Egypt's grandest idea \u2014 that the dead rise \u2014 did not begin with a text but with a grave. In the oldest burials of Upper Egypt, at Nakadah and other prehistoric sites, the skeleton is laid upon its left side with the knees at the breast and the hands before the face, wrapped in gazelle skin or loose grass: the ante-natal position of a child. There are no inscriptions (writing was unknown), but there are offerings in vases, flint knives for the hunt, and schist amulets against invisible enemies \u2014 everything a dead man would need to live, hunt, and fight again.\n\nBudge's argument, from the evidence of three burial customs, is that the earliest Egyptians already held two distinct theologies of the corpse. The first kind of grave \u2014 the crouched, unmutilated body \u2014 belongs to people who expected the actual body to rise: born once from the foetal position, born again from the grave in the same body, revivified. The second kind \u2014 bodies stripped of flesh, bones cast in wild confusion or 'jointed' to save space, sometimes with the head separated, sometimes burnt \u2014 belongs to people who seemingly had no hope of living again in the natural body, and whose conception of a resurrection, if any, must have been of a spiritual body only. The third kind marks the transition: clay casings over folded bodies.\n\nWhat the dynastic Egyptians then did was to reverse the second custom and universalize the first. By the 4th Dynasty every funerary text assumes the body will be buried whole \u2014 a reversal to which we owe the great anti-decay spells ('O flesh of Pepi, rot not, decay not, stink not') and, in time, the whole apparatus of embalming, the Ka statues, the Book of the Dead, and the promise of Osiris: that the god who had been a mortal and had risen from the dead would make his followers rise likewise. The belief in a future life, Budge concludes, is older than the belief in God as Egypt formulated it: its beginnings must be as old as the oldest human remains found in the valley \u2014 traces of bitumen on prehistoric bones showing that the attempt to preserve the dead, and the hope it embodied, was there at the very beginning.\n\nTHE DATING QUESTION. Budge is careful about chronology: the interval between these graves and the reign of Mena, the first historical king, 'must have been very considerable', representing thousands of years \u2014 yet the beliefs handed on through that interval were not wiped out and were scarcely modified, surviving 'in an almost unchanged state at the latest period of Egyptian history'. Even the College of Heliopolis, editing the ancient funeral texts, did not dare to delete the savage allusions their ancestors had written: objectionable passages were merely softened or left, out of 'their own reverence for the written word'. The theology of the resurrection was thus, in the strictest sense, prehistoric \u2014 older than the dynasty, older than writing, older than the gods as Egypt named them; and the later religions of Osiris and of Ra did not invent it but inherited it, clothing an immemorial grave-hope in ever more splendid garments."
    },
