    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Both Plutarch and Diodorus agree in assigning a divine origin to Osiris, and both state that he reigned in the form of a man upon the earth. This being so it is clear that the Egyptians generally believed that a god made himself incarnate, and that an immediate ancestor of the first Pharaoh of Egypt was a being who possessed two natures, the one human and the other divine.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge, Osiris and the Egyptian Resurrection vol. I (1911), ch. I."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "As a man he performed the good works which his divine nature indicated to him, he abolished cannibalism, he improved the manners and morals of men, he taught them to live according to law, to worship the gods, and to practise the arts of agriculture. Filled with love for man he set out to travel over the whole world so that he might teach all non-Egyptians to embrace his beneficent doctrine, and enjoy the blessings which accrue to God-fearing and law-abiding peoples. This god-man was hated by his brother, who by a cunning device inveigled him into a box, which he closed and sealed with lead, and thus killed him.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge (1911), ch. I."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The divine part of Osiris did not die, it was only the mortal body, which he put on when he came from the abode of the gods to reign upon earth, that suffered death. In the divinity and immortality of the god-man Osiris lay the strength of the power with which he appealed to the minds and hopes of the Egyptians for thousands of years, and we shall see in the course of the following pages that both these conceptions of Osiris are of purely African origin, and that they were in existence long before the Dynastic Period in Egypt.",
        note: "Verbatim excerpt of Budge (1911), ch. I."
      }
    ],
    language: "Egyptian texts read through classical sources",
    canonical_translation: "E. A. W. Budge, Osiris and the Egyptian Resurrection, vol. I (London, 1911), ch. I",
    narrative: {
      en: "What exactly did Egypt believe had happened to Osiris? Budge's first chapter frames the answer as a two-natures doctrine. Plutarch and Diodorus, whatever their confusions, agree on the essential points: Osiris was of divine origin and 'reigned in the form of a man upon the earth' \u2014 from which Budge draws the far-reaching conclusion that 'the Egyptians generally believed that a god made himself incarnate, and that an immediate ancestor of the first Pharaoh of Egypt was a being who possessed two natures, the one human and the other divine.'\n\nAs a man, this god-man 'performed the good works which his divine nature indicated to him' \u2014 abolished cannibalism, improved the manners and morals of men, taught law, worship and agriculture \u2014 and then, 'filled with love for man', set out to teach the whole world. His brother's cunning box killed only what was mortal in him. The body that went into the river and floated to the reed marshes was the mortal body 'which he put on when he came from the abode of the gods to reign upon earth'; the divine part did not die. Hence the possibility that Plutarch records and the Pyramid Texts confirm: that Osiris returned from the Other World, spoke, and appeared 'in a form which Horus could recognize' \u2014 a spirit, or his own body raised, Budge leaves open, but with 'the power of speech and thought'.\n\nThe payoff, for Budge, is historical: 'in the divinity and immortality of the god-man Osiris lay the strength of the power with which he appealed to the minds and hopes of the Egyptians for thousands of years' \u2014 and both conceptions, the divine man and the risen body, are, he argues, 'of purely African origin', older than the Dynastic Period. The narrative material Plutarch gathered can be substantiated, piece by piece, 'by texts written three thousand years before the Christian Era', though the classical writers 'are arranged in wrong order' and joined in ways their informants could no longer understand. The god-man of Egypt is thus the through-line from the predynastic grave to the Passion Play of Abydos: a god who wore a body, died in it, and rose to rule the dead \u2014 and whose royal successors on earth each put on, at their coronation, the same two natures.\n\nTHE PHARAONIC CONSEQUENCE. The doctrine has a political edge that Budge makes explicit only by implication: if an immediate ancestor of the first Pharaoh was a god incarnate, then every Pharaoh who sat on the throne renewed the incarnation \u2014 the living king as Horus, the dead king as Osiris, both wearing the two natures. This is why the Osiris funerary texts are royal texts at their oldest, and why the god's rise tracks the king's: the Passion Play at Abydos rehearses annually the passion of the first god-man, the corn-mummy of Denderah buries and raises him in grain, and the ancestor stele of every village eventually puts his figure where the family's dead used to sit. The classical testimony, checked clause by clause against texts three thousand years older, thus confirms rather than distorts the native theology \u2014 it is the native theology, seen through Greek eyes, 'arranged in wrong order' but substantiated in nearly every part. For Budge the two-natures Osiris is the master-key of Egyptian religion: a god who wore a body, died in it, rose from it, and left the risen body as the pattern for every mummy laid in the tombs of Egypt."
    },
