    license: "Public domain (first published 1929)",
    summary: {
      en: "Pursued by the hunter Akiti, the lion-coloured leopard eats the roots of a magic plant and becomes invulnerable \u2014 but at every poisoned arrow that strikes him a dark mark appears, until his body carries so many arrow-marks that among the trees he looks like the mingling of sun and shadow.",
      zh: "\u88AB\u7375\u4EBA\u963F\u57FA\u63D0\u8FFD\u6355\u7684\u8C79\u539F\u8207\u7345\u540C\u8272\u3001\u8EAB\u7121\u6591\u7D0B\uFF1B\u7260\u670D\u98DF\u4ED9\u8349\u4E4B\u6839\u8B8A\u5F97\u5200\u69CD\u4E0D\u5165\uFF0C\u4F46\u6BCF\u4E2D\u4E00\u7BAD\u4FBF\u7559\u4E00\u9053\u9ED1\u75D5\u3002\u7BAD\u75D5\u7D2F\u7A4D\u6EFF\u8EAB\uFF0C\u8C79\u7A7F\u884C\u6797\u9593\u6642\u6070\u4F3C\u65E5\u5149\u8207\u6A39\u5F71\u4EA4\u932F\u2014\u2014\u8C79\u7D0B\u7531\u6B64\u800C\u4F86\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "transformation",
      "boon-curse"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Living Yoruba tradition; animal etiology. Archival record of a 1929 colonial-era collection."
    },
    persons: [],
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "At one time the Leopard was coloured like a lion, and he had no dark markings; but he was pursued by Akiti, the renowned hunter, and feared that he might be slain. To avoid this he ate the roots of a certain magic plant, which had the effect of making him invulnerable to any of the hunter's weapons.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Ogumefu, Yoruba Legends, tale XIX (1929); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Soon afterwards Akiti saw him slipping through the dense undergrowth, and though he shot his poisoned arrows, the Leopard escaped \u2014 but where each arrow struck him, there appeared a dark mark. Now, though hunters still pursue him, he is rarely caught, and his body is covered with the marks of the arrows, so that as he goes among the trees he looks exactly like the mingling of the sun and shadow.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Ogumefu, Yoruba Legends, tale XIX (1929); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
