    license: "Public domain (translation 1896)",
    summary: {
      en: "Having made the islands, mountains and trees, Izanagi and Izanami ask who shall be lord of the universe and produce the resplendent Sun-Goddess Oho-hiru-me, then the Moon-god, then the leech child who cannot stand, and finally the violent Susanoo, whom they expel to the Nether Land. Variant writings make the sun and moon born from Izanagi's copper mirrors, and reorder the leech child's place in the sequence.",
      zh: "\u4F0A\u8036\u90A3\u5C90\u3001\u4F0A\u8036\u90A3\u7F8E\u9020\u570B\u65E2\u7562,\u6B32\u751F\u6CBB\u7406\u5929\u4E0B\u4E4B\u8CB4\u5B50,\u9042\u751F\u5927\u65E5\u5B41\u8CB4(\u5929\u7167\u5927\u795E)\u3001\u6708\u795E,\u5FA9\u751F\u4E0D\u80FD\u7AD9\u7ACB\u4E4B\u6C34\u86ED\u5B50,\u53CA\u6027\u60C5\u66B4\u623E\u4E4B\u7D20\u6214\u55DA\u5C0A,\u5176\u7236\u6BCD\u9010\u4E4B\u5F80\u6839\u570B\u3002\u4E00\u66F8\u53E6\u8B02\u65E5\u6708\u81EA\u4F0A\u8036\u90A3\u5C90\u624B\u4E2D\u767D\u9285\u93E1\u5316\u751F\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "miraculous-birth",
      "sibling-creators"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient Japanese court myth (Nihongi, Book I); passages are condensed paraphrases of public-domain translations, presented for comparative mythology."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "After producing the sea, the rivers and the mountains, and the ancestors of trees and herbs, Izanagi and Izanami consulted together: they had produced the Great-Eight-Island country with its mountains, rivers, herbs and trees \u2014 why not now produce someone who should be lord of the universe? So they together produced the Sun-Goddess, Oho-hiru-me no muchi, whose resplendent lustre shone through all six quarters. Rejoicing, the parents said that though they had many children, none equalled this wondrous infant; she ought not to be kept long in this land, so they sent her up to heaven by the ladder of heaven to be entrusted with the affairs of heaven. Next they produced the Moon-god, Tsuki-yumi, whose radiance was next to the sun's; he was to be the Sun-Goddess's consort and share her government, and him also they sent up to heaven.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book I \xA716 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Next they produced the leech child, which even at three years of age could not stand upright; they placed it in the rock-camphor-wood boat of heaven and abandoned it to the winds. Their next child was Sosa no wo (Susanoo), of fierce temper and given to cruel acts, continually weeping and wailing, bringing many people of the land to untimely death and withering the green mountains. His parents therefore addressed him: it was not meet that he should reign over the world; he must depart far away to the Nether Land \u2014 and at length they expelled him.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book I \xA716\u201317 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "In one writing it is said: Izanagi declared that he wished to procreate the precious child who was to rule the world. He took a white-copper mirror in his left hand, and a deity was produced from it, Oho-hiru-me; a mirror in his right hand produced Tsuki-yumi; and while turning his head and looking askance, a god was produced who was Sosa no wo. The two mirror-born deities were bright and beautiful in nature and were made to shine down on heaven and earth, but Susanoo's nature was to love destruction, so he was sent down to rule the Nether Land.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book I \xA717 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "Another writing places the leech child after the sun and moon, and explains his deformity: because, when the parents first went round the pillar, the female deity was the first to utter a cry of pleasure, the law of male and female was broken. This Susanoo was of a wicked nature, always fond of wailing and wrath, so that many of the people died and the green mountains withered; his parents told him he must govern the far-distant Nether Land. Their next child, in this version, was the bird-rock-camphor-wood boat of heaven, in which they placed the leech child and abandoned it to the current; their next child was Kagutsuchi, the fire god, by whom his mother Izanami was burnt.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book I \xA718\u201319 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
