    license: "Public domain (translation 1896)",
    summary: {
      en: "Taka-mi-musubi casts the coverlet of the true couch over the August Grandchild Ama-tsu-hiko-hiko-ho-no-ninigi and sends him down through the eight-fold clouds of heaven to the Peak of Takachiho in Hiuga. Amaterasu bestows the three treasures \u2014 the curved jewel, the eight-hand mirror and the sword Kusanagi \u2014 with five attendant gods of the first Be corporations, entrusts him with the rice-ears of the sacred garden, and consecrates the mirror with the prayer that looking on it be as looking on her. The road-god Saruta-hiko, seven hands in the nose and glowing from mouth and posteriors, meets them at the crossways, is questioned by the unabashed Uzume, and becomes the Grandchild's harbinger to Ise.",
      zh: "\u9AD8\u7687\u7522\u9748\u5C0A\u4EE5\u771F\u5E8A\u8986\u887E\u88F9\u74CA\u74CA\u6775\u5C0A,\u4EE4\u5176\u95E2\u5929\u78D0\u5EA7,\u675D\u516B\u91CD\u96F2\u800C\u964D\u65BC\u65E5\u5411\u9AD8\u5343\u7A57\u4E4B\u5CF0\u3002\u5929\u7167\u5927\u795E\u6388\u4E09\u7A2E\u795E\u5668\u2014\u2014\u516B\u5C3A\u74CA\u52FE\u7389\u3001\u516B\u54AB\u93E1\u3001\u8349\u8599\u528D\u2014\u2014\u5E76\u4EE5\u5929\u5152\u5C4B\u547D\u3001\u5E03\u5200\u7389\u547D\u3001\u5929\u923F\u5973\u547D\u3001\u77F3\u51DD\u59E5\u547D\u3001\u7389\u5C4B\u547D\u4E94\u90E8\u795E\u70BA\u5C6C;\u53C8\u6388\u9F4B\u5EAD\u7A3B\u7A57,\u56D1\u5176\u5B50\u5B6B\u6C38\u70BA\u8466\u539F\u5343\u4E94\u767E\u79CB\u4E4B\u4E3B\u3002\u5929\u5152\u5C4B\u547D\u5949\u93E1\u795D\u8FAD:\u898B\u6B64\u93E1\u7576\u5982\u898B\u543E\u3002\u6642\u733F\u7530\u6BD8\u53E4\u5927\u795E\u5019\u65BC\u5929\u516B\u8862,\u9F3B\u9577\u4E03\u624B,\u80CC\u9AD8\u4E03\u5C3A,\u53E3\u5C3B\u7686\u6709\u5149;\u5929\u923F\u5973\u547D\u8892\u80F8\u800C\u554F\u4E4B,\u733F\u7530\u6BD8\u53E4\u4E43\u70BA\u524D\u5C0E,\u5C07\u8D74\u4F0A\u52E2\u4E94\u5341\u9234\u5DDD\u4E0A\u6D41\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sacred-kingship",
      "ascension",
      "miraculous-birth",
      "prayer",
      "magic-gifts"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient Japanese court myth (Nihongi, Book II); the descent of the imperial ancestor is the charter myth of Japanese sacred kingship; passages are condensed paraphrases of public-domain translations, presented for comparative mythology."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Taka-mi-musubi took the coverlet which was on his true couch, cast it over his August Grandchild Ama-tsu-hiko-hiko-ho-ninigi, and made him descend. The August Grandchild left his heavenly rock-seat, clove his way with an awful path-cleaving through the eight-fold clouds of heaven, and descended on the Peak of Takachiho of So in Hiuga. He took his stand on a level part of the floating sand-bank, traversed the desert land of Sojishi from the Hill of Hitawo in search of a country, and came to Cape Kasasa in Ata-no-nagaya, where a man of that land, Koto-katsu-kuni-katsu Nagasa, told him there was a country and begged him to roam through it at his pleasure.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book II \xA79 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Amaterasu gave the August Grandchild the three treasures, the curved jewel of Yasaka gem, the eight-hand mirror, and the sword Kusanagi, joining to him as attendants Ame no Koyane, first ancestor of the Nakatomi; Futo-dama, first ancestor of the Imibe; Ame no Uzume, first ancestor of the Sarume; Ishi-kori-dome, first ancestor of the mirror-makers; and Tamaya, first ancestor of the jewel-makers \u2014 gods of five Be. She commanded him: this Reed-plain-1500-autumns fair-rice-ear Land is the region which her descendants should be lords of; let him proceed thither and govern it, and may his dynasty, like heaven and earth, endure for ever. She also gave over to him the rice-ears of the sacred garden of which she partook in the Plain of High Heaven.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book II \xA711\u201312 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Amaterasu took the precious mirror in her hand, gave it to Ame no Oshi-ho-mimi and uttered a prayer: my child, when thou lookest upon this mirror, let it be as if thou wert looking on me; let it be with thee on thy couch and in thy hall, a holy mirror. The Koyane and Futo-dama were commanded to guard it well. At the descent itself, one sent in advance returned and reported that at the eight-cross-roads of heaven dwelt a god the length of whose nose was seven hands and of whose back more than seven fathoms, with light shining from his mouth and posteriors, and eyeballs like an eight-hand mirror with a ruddy glow like the winter-cherry. None of the eighty-myriad deities dared confront him.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book II \xA712\u201313 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "So Uzume was specially commanded to question him, being superior in the power of her looks. She bared her breasts, pushed the band of her garment down below her navel, and confronted him with a mocking laugh. The god of the crossways asked what this behaviour meant; she replied by asking who he was to remain in the road by which the child of Amaterasu was to make his progress. He answered: hearing that the child of Amaterasu was about to descend, he had come respectfully to meet and attend upon him \u2014 his name was Saruta-hiko. Asked whether he would go before or she, he chose to go before and be the harbinger, directing the Grandchild to the Peak of Kushifuru of Takachiho and himself to the upper waters of the River Isuzu at Sanada in Ise. Uzume attended him, and for having discovered him received the designation Sarume no Kimi, from which the male and female Lords of Sarume were both styled Kimi.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book II \xA713 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "japanese-ninigi",
      "amaterasu",
      "japanese-uzume",
      "japanese-sarutahiko",
      "japanese-takamimusubi"
    ]
