    period: "Zhou-era elegy tradition, recorded in Hulbert's History of Korea (1905); episode dated c. 1120 BC",
    source: {
      archive: "Project Gutenberg",
      work: "Homer B. Hulbert, The History of Korea, Vol. 1 (1905), ch. 2, eBook #52127",
      url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127"
    },
    license: "Public domain (1905)",
    summary: {
      en: "Though made King of Cho-sun without vassalage, Ki-ja is permitted to send envoys to worship at the tombs of the fallen Shang. He visits the site of the ancient capital himself, finds it sown with barley, and weeps, composing an elegy on the spot before going on to swear allegiance to the new emperor \u2014 an act the Cho-so history calls his weakness, for he had sworn never to do so.",
      zh: "\u6B66\u738B\u5C01\u7B95\u5B50\u65BC\u671D\u9BAE\u800C\u4E0D\u81E3\u3002\u7B95\u5B50\u5617\u9063\u4F7F\u796D\u6BB7\u5893\uFF0C\u53C8\u89AA\u5F80\u6BB7\u589F\uFF0C\u898B\u5BAE\u5BA4\u6BC0\u58DE\u3001\u751F\u79BE\u9ECD\uFF0C\u60B2\u800C\u4F5C\u300A\u9EA5\u79C0\u6B4C\u300B\uFF0C\u5F8C\u4E43\u671D\u5468\u3002\u53F2\u7A31\u6B64\u70BA\u5176\u5F31\u9EDE\uFF0C\u84CB\u66FE\u8A93\u4E0D\u4ED5\u5468\u4E5F\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "parable-teaching",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Classic Chinese elegy tradition (Maixiu ge) embedded in Korean annals; no living-community restriction."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "From the very first Korea was an independent kingdom, for though Emperor Mu-wang made Ki-ja King of Cho-sun he neither demanded nor received his allegiance as vassal, and even allowed Ki-ja to send envoys to worship at the tombs of the fallen dynasty.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Hulbert, History of Korea, Vol. 1, ch. 2 (1905); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "It is said that Ki-ja himself visited the site of the ancient Shang capital, but when he found it sown with barley he wept and composed an elegy on the occasion, after which he went and swore allegiance to the new Emperor.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Hulbert, History of Korea, Vol. 1, ch. 2 (1905); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The work entitled Cho-so says that he mounted a white cart drawn by a white horse and went to the new capital and swore allegiance; and it adds that in this he showed his weakness, for he had sworn never to do so.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Hulbert, History of Korea, Vol. 1, ch. 2 (1905); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "kija"
    ]
