    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The twins perform. They set fire to a house with themselves inside and dance out of the flames unhurt; they kill a dog and bring it back to life; they cut themselves open, take out their hearts, and put them back, and go on dancing. Each trick is announced by the one who is about to die, who tells the other, and then it is done.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Popol Wuj, Part 3 (Brasseur 1861; Tedlock 1996); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The lords of the place of fear are beside themselves. They crowd the benches to watch, calling for more: kill each other, burn yourselves, do it again. The twins oblige with faces of sorrow and feats of laughter, until One Death and Seven Death themselves begin to boast: if the boys can die and return, so can the Lords of Death.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Popol Wuj, Part 3 (Brasseur 1861; Tedlock 1996); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "And so they ask for their turn, which is exactly what the twins have danced toward. The boys tell them: it is right that it should be tried, and the lords prepare to die in front of everyone, certain of their resurrection, having watched two boys do it three times without harm.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Popol Wuj, Part 3 (Brasseur 1861; Christenson 2003); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
