    license: "Public domain (Brasseur 1861 French edition); modern translations only paraphrased",
    summary: {
      en: "After wars and wanderings, the K'iche' lords settle on the spur of rock called Q'umarkaj \u2014 rotten cane \u2014 and build the council houses of the three great lineages. There the count of days, the god bundles, and the tribute of the subject towns are gathered, and the K'iche' kingdom takes the form it still had at the coming of the Spanish.",
      zh: "\u6B77\u7D93\u6230\u4E8B\u8207\u9077\u5F99\u5F8C\uFF0C\u57FA\u5207\u8AF8\u4E3B\u5B9A\u90FD\u65BC\u540D\u70BA\u5EAB\u99AC\u723E\u5361\u8D6B\uFF08\u8150\u7AF9\uFF09\u7684\u77F3\u5D17\uFF0C\u5EFA\u4E09\u5927\u65CF\u7CFB\u4E4B\u8B70\u4E8B\u4E4B\u5C4B\u3002\u65E5\u6578\u3001\u795E\u5305\u8207\u5C6C\u9091\u4E4B\u8CA2\u9042\u805A\u65BC\u6B64\uFF0C\u57FA\u5207\u738B\u570B\u6210\u5F62\uFF0C\u76F4\u81F3\u897F\u73ED\u7259\u4EBA\u5230\u4F86\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sacred-kingship",
      "sacred-place",
      "culture-hero"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Paraphrase of published translation; no living-community restriction."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The lords come to the spur of rock that is called Rotten Cane, Q'umarkaj, and there they settle, because the rock is high and the canyons around it are deep. They build the houses of the three lineages side by side: the house of Jaguar Quitze, the house of Jaguar Night, and the house of Not-Right-Now, each with its council bench and its bundle of the god.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Popol Wuj, Part 4 (Brasseur 1861; Tedlock 1996); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "There they keep the count of days and the tribute of the towns that have submitted, and there the mother of the lineage keeps the fire. The rock becomes the city of the K'iche' kingdom, and from it the lords go out to war and return to the benches to decide what has been taken.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Popol Wuj, Part 4 (Brasseur 1861; Tedlock 1996); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "This is the city that the Spanish will burn: its benches are remembered in the Popol Wuj as the place where the lineage speeches were given and the days were counted, and where the last kings, taken by Pedro de Alvarado, were questioned and put to death.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Popol Wuj, Part 4 (Brasseur 1861; Christenson 2003); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "balamquitze",
      "balamacab",
      "mahucutah",
      "tohil"
    ]
