    license: "Public domain (translation 1882)",
    summary: {
      en: "Seeking a chief empress, Jimmu is told of a maiden called the august child of a Deity: the Great-Master-of-Things deity of Miwa admired the beautiful Seya-datarahime, transformed himself into a red-painted arrow, pierced her, and became a handsome youth who married her; their daughter is I-suke-yori-hime. Jimmu's courtier Ohokume, watching seven maidens play on the Takasazhi moor, picks out I-suke-yori-hime standing first and reports her in song; she answers in astonishment at his slit eyes, and the Heavenly Sovereign goes to her house on the River Sawi to sleep one night before bringing her into the palace.",
      zh: "\u795E\u6B66\u5929\u7687\u6C42\u6B63\u540E\u3002\u4E09\u8F2A\u5C71\u7684\u5927\u7269\u4E3B\u795E\u6155\u52E2\u591C\u9640\u591A\u826F\u6BD4\u8CE3\u4E4B\u7F8E\uFF0C\u5316\u70BA\u4E39\u5857\u77E2\uFF0C\u9042\u6210\u7F8E\u7537\u5B50\u8207\u5979\u6210\u5A5A\uFF0C\u6240\u751F\u5973\u5373\u4F0A\u9808\u6C23\u4F59\u7406\u6BD4\u8CE3\uFF0C\u6545\u7A31\u795E\u5973\u3002\u5927\u4E45\u7C73\u547D\u65BC\u9AD8\u5009\u91CE\u898B\u4E03\u5C11\u5973\u904A\u6232\uFF0C\u5531\u6B4C\u8FA8\u51FA\u7ACB\u65BC\u6700\u524D\u8005\uFF0C\u5929\u7687\u9042\u5E78\u5176\u6CB3\u80CC\u4E4B\u5BB6\u4E00\u591C\uFF0C\u7D0D\u5165\u5F8C\u5BAE\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "divine-marriage",
      "miraculous-birth",
      "transformation",
      "sacred-kingship"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient Japanese court myth (Kojiki); the arrow-conception episode is paraphrased from the translator's Latin rendering of the original; passages are condensed paraphrases of public-domain translations, presented for comparative mythology."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "When Kamu-yamato-ihare-biko, ruling at Kashibara, sought a beautiful maiden to make his Chief Empress, his commander Ohokume told him of a beauteous girl in Yamato who was called the august child of a Deity, and explained the name. The Princess Seya-datarahime, daughter of Mizokuhi of Mishima, was admired for her beauty by the Great-Master-of-Things, the Deity of Miwa. The god changed himself into an arrow painted red, transfixed her by the privy, and startled her: she fled, then brought the arrow home and laid it beside her bed. Suddenly it became a handsome young man, who swiftly joined the beautiful girl to himself in marriage; their daughter was named Hoto-tatara-i-susugi-hime - the same as Hime-tatara-i-suke-yori-hime. So therefore she is called the august child of a Deity.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Kojiki, Chamberlain trans. pp. 232-233 (1882); the original's arrow-courtship lines are here translated in Latin and rendered back into English; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Seven beauteous maidens were playing on the moor of Takasazhi, and I-suke-yori-hime stood among them. Ohokume, seeing her, spoke to the Heavenly Sovereign in a song pointing her out among the maidens; the Sovereign, reading in his heart that the girl standing in the very front was the one, replied with a song of his own. When Ohokume carried the Sovereign's decree to her, she sang back in astonishment at the courtier's slit eyes, and he sang an answer explaining himself. So the maiden promised she would respectfully serve the Heavenly Sovereign.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Kojiki, Chamberlain trans. pp. 233-234 (1882); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The maiden's house stood on the bank of the River Sawi. The Heavenly Sovereign made a progress to her dwelling and slept there one august night; afterwards I-suke-yori-hime came and entered the palace, and the Sovereign sang at her arrival. The children born of this marriage were Hiko-ya-wi, Kamu-ya-wi-mimi, and Kamu-nuna-kaha-mimi - the last to reign as the second sovereign of the line.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Kojiki, Chamberlain trans. pp. 234-235 (1882); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "japanese-jimmu",
      "japanese-isukeyorihime",
      "japanese-omononushi",
      "japanese-seyadatarahime"
    ]
