    license: "Public domain (translation 1882)",
    summary: {
      en: "After Jimmu's death his elder half-brother Tagishi-mimi marries the Empress I-suke-yori-hime and plots to slay her three young sons. The mother laments and betrays the plot through songs; the eldest prince Kamu-ya-wi-mimi takes up a weapon but trembles so that he cannot strike, and his younger brother Kamu-nuna-kaha-mimi begs for the weapon and slays Tagishi-mimi himself. Praised for the deed as Take-nuna-kaha-mimi, he is ceded the sovereignty by the elder brother, who resolves to serve him as a priest: thus the valiant slayer, not the firstborn, reigns as the Emperor Suizei.",
      zh: "\u795E\u6B66\u5929\u7687\u5D29\u5F8C\uFF0C\u7570\u6BCD\u5144\u624B\u7814\u8033\u547D\u5A36\u7687\u540E\u4F0A\u9808\u6C23\u4F59\u7406\u6BD4\u8CE3\uFF0C\u8B00\u6BBA\u4E09\u4F4D\u5E74\u5E7C\u7687\u5B50\u3002\u6BCD\u540E\u54C0\u615F\uFF0C\u4EE5\u6B4C\u793A\u8B66\u3002\u9577\u5B50\u795E\u516B\u4E95\u8033\u547D\u6301\u5175\u6B32\u8A85\u4E4B\uFF0C\u7136\u624B\u8DB3\u986B\u6296\u4E0D\u80FD\u4E0B\u624B\uFF1B\u5F1F\u795E\u6E3E\u540D\u6CB3\u8033\u547D\u4E5E\u528D\u89AA\u5F80\u8A85\u624B\u7814\u8033\uFF0C\u9042\u7A31\u5EFA\u6CBC\u6CB3\u8033\u547D\u3002\u9577\u5144\u4EE5\u5F1F\u52C7\u800C\u8B93\u4F4D\uFF0C\u81EA\u4EFB\u796D\u7940\u8F14\u4F50\uFF0C\u5F1F\u5373\u4F4D\u70BA\u7D8F\u9756\u5929\u7687\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "taboo-transgression",
      "sacred-kingship",
      "boon-curse",
      "betrayal"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient Japanese court myth (Kojiki); passages are condensed paraphrases of public-domain translations, presented for comparative mythology."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "After the decease of the Heavenly Sovereign Kamu-yamato-ihare-biko, who had lived one hundred and thirty-seven years and been buried on the Kashi spur by Mount Unebi, his elder half-brother Tagishi-mimi wedded the Empress I-suke-yori-hime. Pending his design he plotted how he might slay his three younger brethren, the sons of Jimmu; and their august mother, lamenting, made the plot known to her children through a song, singing it once and then again.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Kojiki, Chamberlain trans. pp. 235-236 (1882); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Hearing the song, her august children knew their danger and resolved to slay Tagishi-mimi first. The elder prince Kamu-ya-wi-mimi said to his younger brother Kamu-nuna-kaha-mimi: do you take a weapon and go in and slay Tagishi-mimi. The elder prince took a weapon, went in, and made ready to slay him; but his arms and legs trembled so that he was unable. Then the younger brother begged for the weapon his elder brother held, went in himself, and slew Tagishi-mimi. In praise of the deed the younger brother was again named Take-nuna-kaha-mimi, the Valiant of Nunakaha.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Kojiki, Chamberlain trans. pp. 236-237 (1882); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Kamu-ya-wi-mimi then resigned the sovereignty to the younger brother: I could not slay the foeman, he said, but Thine Augustness was at once able to slay him. Though I be the elder, it is not right that I should be the superior; wherefore do you be the superior and rule all beneath the heaven, while I become a priest and respectfully serve you. So the slayer of Tagishi-mimi, though the younger, took the throne and ruled the Empire - the sovereign the chronicles count as Emperor Suizei, who dwelt at the palace of Takawoka in Kadzuraki.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Kojiki, Chamberlain trans. pp. 237-239 (1882); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
