    license: "Public domain (Tozzer 1907; Cogolludo 1688)",
    summary: {
      en: "When the sun or moon is in eclipse, it is believed that an evil being is biting them. A great noise is made to frighten the animal away, and the children are struck so that they will cry. Cogolludo recorded the same tradition in 1688: at eclipses the Indians make their dogs howl, pinch them, and beat the boards, benches and doors, saying that the moon is dying or is being stung by a kind of ant called Xulab.",
      zh: "\u65E5\u8755\u6216\u6708\u8755\u4E4B\u6642\uFF0C\u4EBA\u5011\u76F8\u4FE1\u6709\u90AA\u7269\u6B63\u54AC\u566C\u65E5\u6708\u3002\u65BC\u662F\u5236\u9020\u5DE8\u97FF\u4EE5\u9A45\u5176\u7378\uFF0C\u4E26\u62CD\u6253\u5B69\u7AE5\u4EE4\u5176\u557C\u54ED\u3002\u79D1\u6208\u67F3\u591A1688\u5E74\u5373\u8A18\u6B64\u4FD7\uFF1A\u8755\u6642\u5370\u7B2C\u5B89\u4EBA\u4F7F\u72AC\u54C0\u568E\u3001\u64F0\u5176\u8EAB\u3001\u64CA\u677F\u51F3\u9580\u6249\uFF0C\u8A00\u6708\u4EAE\u5782\u6B7B\uFF0C\u6216\u6B63\u88AB\u4E00\u7A2E\u540D\u70BA\u8607\u62C9\u5E03\u7684\u87FB\u53EE\u54AC\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sun-capture",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Published public-domain ethnography; paraphrase/quote only."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "When the sun or moon is in eclipse, it is believed that an evil being is biting them. A great noise is made to frighten the animal away. The children are struck so that they will cry. No trace of this idea can be found among the Lacandones; the belief that a great noise must be made is found among the Mexicans, and its origin may possibly be traced to them.",
        note: "Condensed from Tozzer (1907), Part III; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Cogolludo (1688) records the same tradition: 'In the eclipses of Sun and Moon they use, by tradition of their ancestors, to make their dogs howl or cry out, pinching their body or ears, and give blows on the boards, benches and doors. They say the Moon is dying, or is being stung by a kind of ant that they call Xulab.'",
        note: "Condensed from Cogolludo, Historia de Yucathan (1688), Bk. IV, ch. 4, as quoted in Tozzer (1907); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
