    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "His friends hearing of this tragedy went immediately and performed for him the last act of kindness, by burying his remains under the same tree. On hearing of the King's death, his personal friends followed his example, and died with him: Biri committed suicide at Koso (where the King died), Omiran did the same, his cousin Omo Sinda committed suicide at Papo, Babayanmi at Sele, Obei at Jakuta, and Oya his favourite wife at Ira.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Johnson, The History of the Yorubas, Part II ch. I (1921); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: 'Thus ended the life of this remarkable personage, who once ruled over all the Yorubas and Popos. He was afterwards deified, and is still worshipped by all of the Yoruba race as the god of thunder and lightning. In every Yoruba and Popo town to this day, whenever there is a flash of lightning followed by a peal of thunder, it is usual to hear from the populace shouts of "Ka biye si" (welcome to your majesty, long live the King). Deified heroes and heroines are never spoken of as dead, but as having disappeared. Thus the saying: "Oya wole ni ile Ira, Sango wole ni Koso" \u2014 Oya disappeared in the town of Ira, Sango disappeared at Koso.',
        note: "Paraphrase of Johnson, The History of the Yorubas, Part II ch. I (1921); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
