    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "An old woman lived alone in her hut, rarely leaving her chimney-corner. She was much distressed at having no children; in her grief, one day she took an egg, wrapped it up carefully in a cotton cloth, and put it in a corner of her hut. She looked at it every day with great anxiety; one morning, however, she found the shell broken, and a lovely tiny creature was stretching out its arms to her.",
        note: "Quoted from Charnay (1887) reproducing Stephens (1843); lightly condensed; not verbatim throughout."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The old woman was in raptures; she took it to her heart, gave it a nurse, and was so careful of it that at the end of a year the baby walked and talked as well as a grown-up man, but he stopped growing. She exclaimed that the baby should be a great chief, and one day told him to go to the king's palace and engage him in a trial of strength. The dwarf begged hard not to be sent on such an enterprise, but the old woman insisted, and he was obliged to obey.",
        note: "Condensed from Charnay (1887) reproducing Stephens (1843); not verbatim throughout."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The king smiled and asked him to lift a stone of three arobes; the child returned crying to his mother, who sent him back, saying: 'If the king can lift the stone, you can lift it too.' The king did take it up, but so did the dwarf. His strength was tried in many other ways, but all the king did was as easily done by the dwarf. Wroth at being outdone by so puny a creature, the prince told the dwarf that unless he built a palace loftier than any in the city, he should die.",
        note: "Condensed from Charnay (1887) reproducing Stephens (1843); not verbatim throughout."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "The affrighted dwarf returned to the old woman, who bade him not despair, and the next morning they both awoke in the palace which is still standing. The king, amazed, sent for the dwarf to collect two bundles of cogoiol, with one of which he would strike the dwarf on the head and consent to be struck in return. The old woman placed a tortilla on his head. The king broke his whole bundle on the dwarf's head without hurting him in the least; the dwarf struck, and at the second blow the king's skull was broken to pieces. The spectators immediately proclaimed the victorious dwarf their sovereign.",
        note: "Condensed from Charnay (1887) reproducing Stephens (1843); not verbatim throughout."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "itzamna"
    ],
    language: "Yucatec Maya oral tradition",
    canonical_translation: "Stephens, Incidents of Travel in Yucatan (1843), via Charnay (1887)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The dwarf legend is the signature folk narrative of Uxmal, told to John Lloyd Stephens at the ruins in 1841-43 and retold by Desir\xE9 Charnay from his pages. A childless old woman, alone in her hut, grieves that she has no children; one day she takes an egg, wraps it in a cotton cloth, and sets it in a corner of her hut. It does not change until one morning the shell is broken and a tiny, lovely creature stretches out its arms to her. She gives it a nurse and tends it so carefully that within a year the baby walks and talks like a grown man \u2014 but then he stops growing. In her joy the old woman declares the child will be a great chief, and sends him to the king's palace to engage the sovereign in a trial of strength.\n\nThe trials follow the folk-tale pattern of the outwitted king. The king bids the dwarf lift a stone of three arobes; the child runs home crying, but his mother sends him back with the words 'If the king can lift it, you can lift it too' \u2014 and the dwarf lifts what the king lifts, and matches every feat. Furious at being outdone by a puny creature, the king sets a deadly condition: build a palace loftier than any in the city overnight, or die. The old woman forbids despair, and the next morning the dwarf awakes in the completed palace \u2014 the House of the Dwarf, the Casa del Adivino or Pyramid of the Magician, the tallest and steepest structure at Uxmal, whose enormous rounded pyramid still dominates the site.\n\nThe final ordeal closes the tale as a succession myth: the king must break a bundle of cogoiol wood over the dwarf's head and take a blow in return, with the ordeal held before all the grandees of the state. The old woman places a tortilla on the dwarf's head; the king shatters his whole bundle to no effect; when his own word binds him to the return blow, the dwarf strikes once and then a second time, and the king's skull breaks to pieces. The spectators proclaim the victorious dwarf their sovereign, and the old woman vanishes \u2014 only to reappear, in the tale's coda, as the water-selling woman by the subterranean river at Mani, whose serpent devours the innocent babies.\n\nAs a corpus record the episode is the Maya counterpart of the 'unfinished palace in a night' cycle (parallels exist in Irish and South American tale types) fused with a kingship-transfer myth: the dwarf's victory founds a new dynasty of the diviner's house. The Casa del Adivino's own architecture \u2014 built over and enclosing an older inner temple \u2014 was read by travelers as the material signature of the legend's claim that the dwarf's palace rose in a single night over an older world."
    },
    functions: [
      "Founding legend of the House of the Dwarf / Pyramid of the Magician at Uxmal",
      "Succession myth: the smallest challenger replaces the proud king",
      "Etiology of the Casa del Adivino's enclosed older inner temple"
    ],
