    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Over a half thousand years ago there lived in a northern village, near Ping Yang, a wood-cutter named Keel Wee. Arriving in the woods at a clearing, he spied at some distance off two fairy-like beings, dressed in the costume of the Chow dynasty of two thousand years ago; they sat on stones and played the game of go-ban. Coming near, the woodman took a respectful attitude, and the two fairies seemed by eye-winks to invite him to look on.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales, 'The Woodman and the Mountain Fairies' (c. 1913); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Forgetting himself and his manners, he stretched forth his right hand to move one of the pieces. At once the fairy nearest to him gave him a crack on the fingers for his impudence, and jerked his arm away; then, without saying a word, he took out from his wallet something that looked like a persimmon seed and put it in the woodman's mouth. Hour after hour the game proceeded; Keel Wee became more and more wrapped up in the game, for he had forgotten that with mountain spirits there is no night or morning, nor passing of the hours \u2014 in fairy-land there is no time.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales, 'The Woodman and the Mountain Fairies' (c. 1913); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Suddenly the timber of his axe seemed to turn to dust and his chin fell; he tumbled over, upsetting the checker-board. When he looked up, the fairies were gone, and the stones they had used as seats were warm to his touch. He put his hand to his breast and found that he had grown a long white beard; the iron axe-head was rusty and half buried in the ground, his hat rotted away, his joints stiff, his clothes a mass of rags, and his hair had burst its topknot string and hung down his back grayish-white.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales, 'The Woodman and the Mountain Fairies' (c. 1913); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "Instead of the two grinning idol posts that had stood before every Korean hamlet since the days of Kija, there was a line of high thick poles with iron wire stretching for miles; his face was flattened against something hard which he could see through, and a young man shouted, 'Here, you mountain daddy, let that glass alone.' A house on wheels came right at him on two lines of shining iron, and the conductor called, 'Hello, old goblin, where did you get your pumpkin mouth? Look out or you'll swallow the moon. Get out of the way of the trolley.' Catching sight of a dignified gentleman in flowing white clothes, Keel Wee bowed his head low; as he did so the stone put in his mouth by the fairies dropped out, and his tongue was loosed. The gentleman cried, 'Heavens! man, are you a beggar-spirit of the mountains? Your speech sounds like the dialect spoken in these parts five hundred years ago; the head of such a family, a wood-cutter, is reported to have gone up into the mountains and was eaten up by a tiger. Yonder in the graveyard are buried ten or more generations of his descendants.' Next morning, all that was seen of Keel Wee was a mass of dust, rags, some bones, and much long white hair; when they buried him, there sprang up around his grave strange flowers that bloomed only on the high mountains.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales, 'The Woodman and the Mountain Fairies' (c. 1913); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
