    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Seventeenth-century colonial record of a Yucatec prophecy; paraphrase of the Spanish original."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "In the end of the thirteenth age, being in his puhanga of Ytza and the city named Tancab, there shall come the sign of a God who is in the heights, and the Cross shall manifest itself to the world, by which the orb was lighted. There will be a division among the wills when this sign is brought in the time to come. The priests, before they come within a league and a quarter, shall see the Cross which appears to them and dawns from pole to pole.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Cogolludo, Historia de Yucathan (1688), lib. II, prophecy of Chilam Balam; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The cult of the vain gods shall cease. Your Father comes, you Ytzalanos; and your brother comes, you Tantunites. Receive your bearded guests from the East, who come bringing the sign of God. God is one, and merciful. The time of our life comes. Do not fear the world. Let us raise his sign on high, raise it to adore and to see it: the Cross we must raise. In opposition to the lie it appears today, against the first tree of the world. This is the sign of a God of the heights; this the Ytzalanas adore.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Cogolludo (1688), prophecy of Chilam Balam; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Those who believe shall be lighted in the age to come. Behold if it imports you what I say, warns and charges your interpreter and master of credit, Balam by name; and with this I have finished saying what the true God commanded me, that the world may hear it. Soon after the coming of the Spaniards, the Indians, seeing how much reverence was paid to the Cross, held certain what their Prophet had told them.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Cogolludo (1688); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "chilambalam"
    ],
    language: "Seventeenth-century Spanish record of a Yucatec prophecy",
    canonical_translation: "Cogolludo, Historia de Yucathan (1688)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Cogolludo preserves, in his 1688 history, the fullest colonial version of the prophecy cycle that Yucatec tradition attached to the coming of the Spaniards. The great priest of Tixcacayom, Chilam Balam (the 'jaguar priest'), is made to speak in the first person of the end of the thirteenth age: the sign of a God of the heights will appear, the Cross will dawn 'from pole to pole', the cult of the vain gods will cease, and bearded guests from the East \u2014 the Ytzalanos' father, the Tantunites' brother \u2014 must be received with good will because they carry the sign of God. The priest commands that his people raise the sign on high and adore it, in opposition 'to the lie' and 'against the first tree of the world' \u2014 a striking formulation in which the new cross displaces the old world-tree as the true axis of the world.\n\nThe historic kernel the chronicler gives is the episode of the woven mantle: before the strangers arrived, the prophet had a tribute-mantle woven in the pattern that the new tribute would take, and had it offered to the idols for safekeeping; he also had a cross made of stone and set in the courts of the temples, saying that this was the true tree of the world and that many peoples would come to venerate it. When Montejo entered Yucat\xE1n and the Indians saw the reverence paid to the Cross, they 'held certain what their Prophet had told them.'\n\nCogolludo reports several such prophecies from different priests (one from Ah Na Puc Tun about the last age purified by fire), and argues \u2014 with theological ingenuity \u2014 that the gift of true prophecy might have been given even to gentile priests, citing the example of Balaam: the prophetic word does not justify the prophet but serves the people who must hear it. As a corpus record this is the Maya 'return of the culture hero' motif transposed into colonial eschatology: the bearded god of the east whom the codices called Kukulcan reappears as the expected bearded strangers, and the prophecy becomes the ritual script with which the Maya of Yucat\xE1n narrated the catastrophe that overtook them.\n\nThe comparative value of the episode lies in its double framing. Read from within, it is a Maya prophecy: a priest foresees the foreigners, commands the reception of their sign, and orders a woven mantle as the pattern of the tribute to come. Read from without, it is a missionary construction: Cogolludo selects, translates, and theologically glosses the material to show that God announced the Gospel to the Indians through their own priests, citing Balaam as his warrant. Both readings are true, and the corpus record keeps both. For the Motif Atlas the record is the Maya contribution to the return-of-the-god cluster: the culture hero who promised to come from the east - Kukulcan, the bearded lord - is the figure whose return the bearded strangers are made to fulfill, in a colonial mirror image of the Popol Wuj expectation of the true dawn."
    },
    functions: [
      "Colonial-era eschatology: the conquest narrated as the fulfillment of a native prophecy",
      "Explains the stone crosses found in Yucatec temple courts as pre-Christian tokens",
      "Authorizes submission to the conquerors as obedience to the prophetic word"
    ],
