    narrative: {
      en: "In the early sixth century the kingdom of Silla wavered over the new religion from the west. King Beopheung had come so completely under the influence of the monks that the old aristocracy feared their power would soon swallow the ancient offices of state; the courtiers used every means to induce the king to moderate his views, and at last won from him a reluctant assent to the execution of the high priest, Ichadon (Yi Cha-don). Tradition says that Ichadon had himself foreseen the bargain and chosen it: brought to the place of execution, he exclaimed that when they slew him his blood would flow not red like blood but white as milk, and then they would know that Buddhism was true. When the head was severed from the trunk, his blood flowed white like milk. None could gainsay the evidence, and from that day Buddhism advanced with rapid steps in Silla; the following year the king made a law against the killing of animals, and the temple at Heungnyun-sa rose on the site of the old sacred grove. The episode is Korea's founding martyrdom \u2014 a miracle of proof modeled on the lot-flower and white-blood wonders of the wider Buddhist world, and retold in the Samguk Sagi annals and, with richer legendary detail, in the Samguk Yusa's Feats of the Monk Ichadon. In the Yusa version, Ichadon had proposed the stratagem himself to the king: if they kill me and my blood turns white, then Your Majesty may build the temples without fear \u2014 and after his death his face showed no change, and a great dragon and a shower of flowers attended his burial on Mt. Cheonseong. The officials who had opposed the faith read the sign and confessed; the king proclaimed that no man should henceforth hinder the law of the Buddha, and the ten temple compounds of the royal foundation rose in the capital. Later generations of Korean monks kept the feast of the martyr, and his shrine became one of the stations of the Silla Buddhist year. Whether read as history, hagiography, or politics dressed as portent, the story fixes the moment when the sacred kingship of Silla and the Dharma made their pact: the martyr's white blood purchased, once and for all, the freedom of the sangha in Korea, and the boundary between the old blood-sacrifice of the aristocracy and the new compassion-ethic of the monasteries was drawn in a color no one could deny."
    },
