    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Instantly the young man took up paper and brush, and wrote: A memorandum. Desiring to enjoy the spring scenery, I came to Kang Hal Loo. There I saw for the first time my heaven-sent bride. Meeting for the first time, I pledge myself for one hundred years; to be her faithful husband. Should I change, show this paper to the magistrate.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Allen, Korean Tales, ch. 7 (1889); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Never, she bravely cried. A thousand deaths first. The man was fairly beside himself with wrath, and ordered her chained and thrown into prison at once; she passed the night in bowing before Heaven and calling on the gods and her husband to release her, while her mother scolded her for clinging to a faithless husband and the neighbors assured her she had done just right and would yet be rewarded.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Allen, Korean Tales, ch. 7 (1889); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "To the surprise of all, the beggar claimed that as the other guests each had a fair gee sang to sing a wine song while they drank, he should be treated likewise; and in return for his food and wine he wrote: The oil that enriches the food of the official is but the life blood of the down-trodden people. The Ussa took out his official seal; at sight of the King's seal terror blanched the faces of the half-drunken men, the wicked host was caught crawling under the house and bound with chains, and Chun Yang, borne in shackles to answer charges, heard his own voice and fainted in his arms.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Allen, Korean Tales, ch. 7 (1889); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
