    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "The kumiho (nine-tailed fox) motif is central to Korean folklore; this is the Sino-Korean ur-variant (Daji); no living-community restriction."
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "Korean folklore's most feared shape-shifter, the kumiho or nine-tailed fox-woman, has its ur-portrait in the annalistic legend of Tal-geui, the concubine of the last Shang emperor. Wonderful in her beauty, she is surpassing so when she smiles: whoever she smiles upon is fascinated as by a serpent, and forced to comply with whatever request she makes. Pondering on this, the statesman Pi-gan decides that she must be a fox in human shape, for it is well known that if an animal tastes of water that has lain twenty years in a human skull it acquires the power to assume human shape at will. He sets inquiries on foot, and discovers that she makes a monthly visit to a certain mountain, ascending alone and leaving her train of attendants at the foot. Armed detectives follow her unseen and watch her enter a cave near the summit, from which she emerges accompanied by a pack of foxes who leap about her and fawn upon her in evident delight. When she leaves, the spies enter, put the foxes to the sword, and cut from each body the piece of white fur always found on the breast of the fox. When Tal-geui next meets the emperor, dressed in a sumptuous white fur robe, she shudders but does not yet guess the truth; a month later, entering the cave, she beholds the festering remains of her kindred. On her way home she plans her revenge, and adorning herself in all her finery she enters the imperial presence and exerts her fascination to the utmost. 'I hear,' she says at last, 'that there are seven orifices in the heart of every good man. I fain would put it to the test. I would that I might see the heart of Pi-gan.' His soul revolts from the act, yet he has no power to refuse; and as the executioner's knife is plunged into the victim's breast, Pi-gan cries that she is no woman but a fox in disguise, and charges her to resume her natural shape. Instantly hair springs forth upon her face, her nails grow long, and bursting from her garments she stands revealed \u2014 a white fox with nine tails. With one parting snarl at the assembled court she leaps from the window and makes good her escape. The story is, in effect, the Chou-dynasty fall of Shang as moralized by Korean historiography, and it fixes the grammar of the kumiho tale: a woman too beautiful, a smile that binds, a beast that gained its shape from drinking skull-water, the white breast-fur as tell, and the heart of the righteous man demanded as proof of virtue. In later Korean tellings the fox sheds her tails one by one as she approaches the human, eats liver, wears the shape of a bride or a schoolgirl, and is unmasked by dogs, mirrors or her own tail showing beneath the skirt; Im Bang's seventeenth-century collections record fox-women at the Hill Pagoda and wild-cat doubles at the chestnut table, and the motif remains a fixture of modern film and drama. Read as myth-history, Tal-geui's legend teaches the same boundary lesson as her sisters in every land: the beautiful stranger may be the beast, and the wise man's own death may be the price of proving it."
    },
    functions: [
      "Explains the origin and detection-signs of the kumiho (fox-woman) figure",
      "Frames political downfall through the moralized figure of the dangerous female stranger",
      "Marks the boundary between human and animal shape-shifting in Korean lore"
    ],
