    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ancient Japanese court myth (Nihongi); Aston dates this transmission of writing 120 years later; passages are condensed paraphrases of the public-domain Aston translation, presented for comparative mythology."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The King of Pekche sent A-chik-ki with two quiet horses as tribute. So they were fed in stables on the acclivity of Karu, and A-chik-ki was appointed to have charge of their foddering. Therefore the place where the horses were kept was named Mumaya-saka, Stable-hill. Moreover, A-chik-ki was able to read the classics, and so the Heir Apparent made him his teacher. Hereupon the Emperor inquired of A-chik-ki, saying: 'Are there other learned men superior to thee?' He answered and said: 'There is Wang-in, who is superior.' Then Areda wake, ancestor of the Kimi of Kodzuke, and Kamu-nagi wake were sent to Pekche to summon Wang-in.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book X, sec. 15 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Wang-in arrived, and straightway the Heir Apparent took him as teacher and learnt various books from him. There was none which he did not thoroughly understand. Therefore the man called Wang-in was the first ancestor of the Fumi no Obito, the chiefs of writing. In the same year King Ahwa of Pekche died, and the Emperor sent for Prince Tyen-chi and addressed him: 'Do thou return to thy country and succeed to the royal Dignity.' Accordingly he further granted to him the territory of Eastern Han and so dismissed him. Meanwhile, Kidzu no Sukune of Heguri and Tada no Sukune of Ikuba had been sent to Kara with choice troops to assail Silla and open a way for Sotsuhiko, whose return had been long delayed: the King of Silla was afraid, confessed his guilt, and they brought away with them the people of Kungwol and Sotsuhiko.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book X, secs. 15-16 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "In the twentieth year, autumn, ninth month, Achi no Omi, ancestor of the Atahe of the Aya of Yamato, and his son Tsuga no Omi immigrated to Japan, bringing with them a company of their people of seventeen districts. Earlier, in the fourteenth year, the King of Pekche had sent as tribute a seamstress named Maketsu, first ancestress of the seamstresses of Kume; and the Lord of Yutsuki came from Pekche to offer his allegiance, saying that, coming with one hundred and twenty districts of the people of his own land, the men of Silla had prevented them and forced them to remain in the land of Kara. Katsuraki no Sotsuhiko was sent to bring the men of Yutsuki from Kara, but three years passed and he did not come.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Nihongi, Book X, secs. 14 and 20 (trans. Aston 1896); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "japanese-ojin"
    ]
