    persons: [],
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "A tale of the creation of man from earth is told in the New Hebrides. Takaro made from mud ten figures of men. When they were finished, he breathed upon them \u2014 upon their eyes, their ears, their mouths, their hands, their feet \u2014 and thus the images became alive. But all the people he had made were men, and Takaro was not satisfied.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Dixon, Oceanic Mythology, 'Melanesia' ch. I (1916); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "So he told them to light a fire and cook some food, then ordered them to stand still while he threw a fruit at one of them \u2014 and lo, one of the men was changed into a woman. Takaro sent the woman to dwell by herself in a house. One by one he sent the nine men to her, first to ask for fire, then for water; each she greeted as her elder brother, her younger brother, her kinsman \u2014 all but the last, and him she called her husband. So Takaro said to him, 'Take her as your wife, and you two shall live together.'",
        note: "Paraphrase of Dixon, Oceanic Mythology, 'Melanesia' ch. I (1916); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
