    summary: {
      en: "In the dainas the Sun wears silver shoes and dances on the silver mount, sails a silver sea, is herself a silver apple; at evening she crowns the linden with gold, the oak with silver, each little willow with a ring \u2014 and weeps because the golden apple has fallen from the tree.",
      zh: "\u9054\u4F0A\u7D0D\u65AF\u4E2D\u4E4B\u592A\u967D\u8457\u9280\u5C65\u3001\u821E\u9280\u5C71\u3001\u6CDB\u9280\u6D77\uFF0C\u5176\u8EAB\u4EA6\u5982\u9280\u860B\u679C\uFF1B\u65E5\u66AE\u6642\u4EE5\u91D1\u51A0\u8D08\u83E9\u63D0\u3001\u9280\u51A0\u8D08\u6A61\u6A39\u3001\u6212\u6307\u8D08\u67F3\u679D\u2014\u2014\u53C8\u56E0\u91D1\u860B\u679C\u96E2\u6A39\u800C\u6CE3\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sun-capture",
      "world-renewal",
      "transformation"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Daina."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "We think of the sun as golden, and rightly; yet in the dainas she wears silver shoes and dances on the silver mount, sails over a silver sea, scatters gifts of silver, sows silver, or is herself a silver apple, a boat of silver, bronze, and gold, or half gold and half silver \u2014 all the shadings of her various stations in the sky. Her hundred brown horses are her rays; in the east, where she rises, lives a gold and diamond steed.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Gray, Baltic Mythology, Mythology of All Races III (1918), Part V; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "When she sets she gives a golden crown to the linden, a silver coronet to the oak, and a golden ring to each little willow. She weeps bitterly because the golden apple has fallen from the tree \u2014 a myth of sunset \u2014 but 'God' will make her another of gold, brass, or silver. She herself is an apple sleeping in an apple-garden, decked with apple-blossoms: the fleecy clouds of dawn.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Gray, Baltic Mythology, Mythology of All Races III (1918), Part V; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
