    license: "Public domain (translation 1873)",
    summary: {
      en: "There was a woman who was a huaca, named Cavillaca, a most beautiful virgin sought after by all the principal idols, but she would never show favour to any. Once she sat down to weave a mantle at the foot of a lucma tree, when the wise Coniraya turned himself into a very beautiful bird, went up into the lucma tree, took some of his generative seed and made it into the likeness of a ripe and luxurious lucma, which he allowed to fall near the beautiful Cavillaca. She took it and ate it with delight, and was made pregnant without other contact with man. When nine months were completed she bore a son, herself remaining a virgin, and suckled the child for a whole year without knowing whose it was. When the child began to crawl, Cavillaca demanded that the huacas and principal idols should assemble, and that it should be declared whose son the child was. Each adorned himself in the richest clothes to appear brighter than the rest, that she might select him for her spouse. In the assembly at Anchicocha, in the lowest place of all, sat Coniraya Uiracocha in his beggar's rags. Cavillaca declared that as none would speak, she would let the child go, and his father would be the one to whom he crawls; she loosed the child, who, passing by all the others, went to Coniraya in his rags and dirt, rejoiced, laughed, and rested at his feet. Cavillaca, in shame - 'What disgrace is this, that a lady such as I am should be made pregnant by a poor and filthy creature' - snatched up the child and fled towards the sea-shore; Coniraya put on magnificent golden robes and pursued her crying loving words, his splendour illuminating the whole country; but the disdainful Cavillaca would not turn her head, calling him a creature so vile and filthy. She came to the sea coast of Pachacamac, entered the sea with her child, and was turned into a rock; the two rocks may still be seen, mother and child.",
      zh: "\u7576\u6642\u6709\u4E00\u4F4D\u5973\u6027\u74E6\u5361,\u540D\u5361\u7DAD\u5229\u4E9E\u5361,\u7D55\u4E16\u7F8E\u4EBA,\u773E\u795E\u7686\u6C42\u4E4B,\u5979\u537B\u4E00\u6982\u4E0D\u5141\u3002\u4E00\u65E5\u5979\u5728\u76E7\u514B\u746A\u6A39\u4E0B\u7E54\u5E03,\u667A\u8005\u7684\u5EAB\u5C3C\u62C9\u4E9E\u5316\u4F5C\u7F8E\u9CE5\u98DB\u4E0A\u6A39\u7AEF,\u5C07\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u7CBE\u6DB2\u5316\u4F5C\u4E00\u9846\u719F\u900F\u80A5\u7F8E\u7684\u76E7\u514B\u746A\u679C,\u843D\u5230\u7F8E\u4EBA\u8FD1\u65C1\u3002\u5361\u7DAD\u5229\u4E9E\u5361\u62FE\u800C\u98DF\u4E4B,\u7518\u7F8E\u7570\u5E38,\u9042\u672A\u7D93\u7537\u5973\u4EA4\u63A5\u800C\u611F\u5B55\u3002\u5341\u6708\u671F\u6EFF,\u8655\u5973\u751F\u5B50,\u89AA\u81EA\u54FA\u4E73\u4E00\u5E74,\u4E0D\u77E5\u5176\u7236\u70BA\u8AB0\u3002\u5B69\u5B50\u6703\u722C\u4E4B\u5F8C,\u5979\u53EC\u8ACB\u8AF8\u74E6\u5361\u9F4A\u805A,\u5BA3\u544A:\u8AB0\u662F\u5B69\u5B50\u7684\u7236\u89AA\u3002\u8AF8\u795E\u5404\u68B3\u6D17\u76DB\u88DD,\u6B32\u4EE5\u6700\u5149\u9BAE\u4E4B\u59FF\u88AB\u9078\u70BA\u592B\u3002\u5728\u5B89\u5947\u79D1\u67E5\u7684\u96C6\u6703\u4E0A,\u5EAB\u5C3C\u62C9\u4E9E\u4EE5\u4E5E\u4E10\u8964\u8938\u4E4B\u8EAB\u5750\u5728\u6700\u672B\u4F4D\u3002\u5361\u7DAD\u5229\u4E9E\u5361\u74B0\u8996\u7121\u4EBA\u61C9\u7B54,\u4FBF\u8AAA:\u300C\u4F60\u5011\u65E2\u90FD\u4E0D\u80AF\u8A8D,\u6211\u4FBF\u653E\u4E86\u9019\u5B69\u5B50\u2014\u2014\u4ED6\u722C\u5411\u8AB0,\u8AB0\u4FBF\u662F\u4ED6\u7684\u7236\u89AA\u3002\u300D\u5B69\u5B50\u722C\u904E\u8AF8\u795E,\u9015\u5954\u90A3\u8964\u8938\u9AAF\u9AD2\u7684\u5EAB\u5C3C\u62C9\u4E9E,\u5B09\u7B11\u6B61\u547C,\u4F9D\u504E\u5176\u8DB3\u3002\u5361\u7DAD\u5229\u4E9E\u5361\u7F9E\u61A4\u4EA4\u52A0:\u300C\u6211\u9019\u822C\u5C0A\u8CB4\u4E4B\u8EAB,\u7ADF\u70BA\u6B64\u5351\u8CE4\u6C61\u7A62\u4E4B\u7269\u6240\u5B55!\u300D\u62B1\u8D77\u5B69\u5B50\u8F49\u8EAB\u5954\u5411\u6D77\u6FF1\u3002\u5EAB\u5C3C\u62C9\u4E9E\u5FFD\u6362\u4E0A\u71E6\u7136\u91D1\u888D,\u8FFD\u5979\u547C\u559A\u611B\u8A9E,\u5149\u8F1D\u7167\u5FB9\u5C71\u91CE;\u7F8E\u4EBA\u537B\u982D\u4E5F\u4E0D\u56DE,\u7F75\u4ED6\u662F\u7A62\u7269\u3002\u5979\u9003\u81F3\u5E15\u67E5\u5361\u99AC\u514B\u6D77\u6FF1,\u62B1\u5B50\u5165\u6D77,\u5316\u70BA\u7901\u77F3\u3002\u81F3\u4ECA\u6D77\u908A\u7336\u898B\u5169\u77F3:\u6BCD\u8207\u5B50\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "miraculous-birth",
      "transformation",
      "trickster",
      "petrification",
      "virgin-birth"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "The Cavillaca cycle is the Huarochiri masterpiece; the conception scene is retold soberly as the source has it."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "At that time they also say that there was a woman who was a huaca. Her name was Cavillaca, and she was a most beautiful virgin, who was much sought after by the huacas, or principal idols, but she would never show favour to any of them. Once she sat down to weave a mantle at the foot of a lucma tree, when the wise Coniraya succeeded in approaching her in the following manner: he turned himself into a very beautiful bird, and went up into the lucma tree, where he took some of his generative seed and made it into the likeness of a ripe and luxurious lucma, which he allowed to fall near the beautiful Cavillaca. She took it and ate it with much delight, and by it she was made pregnant without other contact with man.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "When the nine months were completed she conceived and bore a son, herself remaining a virgin; and she suckled the child at her own breast for a whole year without knowing whose it was. At the end of the year, when the child began to crawl, Cavillaca demanded that the huacas and principal idols of the land should assemble, and that it should be declared whose son was the child. Each one adorned himself in the best manner possible, combing, washing, and dressing in the richest clothes, each desiring to appear brighter and better than the rest in the eyes of the beautiful Cavillaca, that so she might select him for her spouse and husband.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "They say that, in this assembly, in the lowest place of all, sat the god Coniraya Uiracocha in his beggar's rags. When she found that all were silent, she said: 'As none of you will speak, I shall let the child go, and doubtless his father will be the one to whom he crawls.' So saying, she loosed the child, who, passing by all the others, went to where was his father Coniraya in his rags and dirt, and when the child reached him, it rejoiced and laughed, and rested at his feet. This conduct caused Cavillaca great shame and annoyance, and she snatched up the child, exclaiming: 'What disgrace is this that has come upon me, that a lady such as I am should be made pregnant by a poor and filthy creature.' Then she turned her back and fled away towards the sea-shore.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "Coniraya Uiracocha desired the friendship and favour of the goddess, so, when he saw her take her flight, he put on magnificent golden robes, and, leaving the astonished assembly of gods, he ran after her, crying out: 'O my lady Cavillaca, turn your eyes and see how handsome and gallant am I'; and they say that his splendour illuminated the whole country. Yet the disdainful Cavillaca would not turn her head, but rather increased her speed, saying she had been made pregnant by a creature so vile and filthy. She came to the sea coast of Pachacamac, where she entered the sea with her child, and was turned into a rock. They say that the two rocks may still be seen, which are mother and child.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "cuniraya",
      "cavillaca"
    ],
    language: "Spanish record of a Quechua tradition",
