    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Warrau oral tradition recorded 1915 (Roth); archival record, read critically."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "A man told his wife he was going to another village to work for the headman; she was so importunate to come that he yielded and insisted on her traveling in male attire \u2014 hair cut short, breasts hidden with cotton and hog-tooth neck-chains, a strip of bark covering her nakedness. The others at the settlement wondered why the new-comer would never bathe with them, and set watch; discovering it was a woman, they determined on killing the husband so as to secure possession of the wife.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7106; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "They tried twice, but each time something went wrong with their plans. The third time they tied him in a corial and let it drift out to sea, but the sea cast it back on shore, where a tiger, scenting him, gnawed through the ropes and set him free \u2014 for pure selfishness, telling his captive he now intended punishing him. 'Don't do that; haven't I been punished enough in losing my wife?' pleaded the man, and the tiger let him go. The house-master who received him was really a Spirit; he told the man to shut his eyes, and when he opened them again another Spirit was present to guide him to his wife.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7106; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The guilty couple, recognizing the husband, chopped him up with an ax \u2014 but the Spirit friend restored him to life. They killed him a second time; the Spirit again made him whole. A third time the keeper burned the body and scattered the ashes; this made no difference, for the Spirit collected the ashes together in a palm leaf and made them into a living person again. The resurrected husband, acting under advice, then destroyed his faithless wife as well as her paramour: their friends tried to piece the bits together and 'make them alive', but could not. It is only Spirits who can do such things.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7106; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
