    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Carib oral tradition recorded 1915 (Roth); archival record, read critically. Origin-of-death cluster parallel."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "In the olden times, there was no contention, all were happy, and no one became sick or died. It was then that the Yurokons used to come and live among us as our friends and associates; they were short people like ourselves. One Yurokon in particular used to come and drink paiwarri with my people regularly once a month. The last time he came, he appeared as a woman with a baby at the breast.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7108; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The Caribs gave her of the pepper-pot, into which she dipped the cassava; it was so hot that it burned the inside of her mouth and 'heart', and this made her ask for water. Leaving her baby up at the house, she went down to the waterside and quenched her thirst. On her return her little child was nowhere to be seen, because during her absence some worthless woman among the company had thrown it into the boiling cassiri pot. By and by Yurokon went to stir the cassiri with the usual paddle-spoon, and, while she stirred, the body of her baby rose to the surface.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7108; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "She wept, and then, turning on the people, upbraided them: 'Why have you punished me in this way? I have never had a bad mind against any of you, but now I will make you pay me. In future your children shall all die, and this will make you weep as I am weeping. And when children are born to you, you shall suffer pain and trouble at their birth. Furthermore, I will give you great trouble when you go out to catch fish.' \u2014 for in those days the Caribs only had to bail the water out of the streams and pick up the fish left exposed at the bottom, putting the water back to breed fish once more. Yurokon altered all this, made fish-poisoning the toil it now is, killed the worthless Indian who had thrown her boy into the cassiri \u2014 and pain, misery, and death entered the world.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7108; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [],
    narrative: {
      en: "The Caribs of the Pomeroon district tell that in the olden times there was no contention: all were happy, and no one became sick or died. In those days the Yurokons \u2014 the hairy little forest spirits, short people like the Caribs themselves \u2014 used to come and live among men as friends and associates. One Yurokon in particular came regularly once a month to drink paiwarri with the narrator's people. The last time he came he appeared as a woman with a baby at the breast. The Caribs gave her pepper-pot, into which she dipped her cassava; it burned her mouth and 'heart', and she asked for water, was told there was none, and went down to the water-side with her calabash, leaving the baby at the house. While she was gone some worthless woman among the company threw the child into the boiling cassiri pot. When she returned and stirred the drink with the paddle-spoon, the body of her baby rose to the surface. She wept, then turned on the people with the curse that re-ordered the world: 'Why have you punished me in this way? I have never had a bad mind against any of you, but now I will make you pay me. In future your children shall all die, and this will make you weep as I am weeping. And when children are born to you, you shall suffer pain and trouble at their birth. Furthermore, with regard to you men, I will give you great trouble when you go out to catch fish.' So fishing, which in those days meant simply bailing out a stream and picking the fish from the exposed bed before letting the water back to breed again, became the toil of poisoning pools with roots; and sickness, the pain of childbirth, and death entered the world \u2014 a Guianese parallel to the world-wide origin-of-death tales in which a spirit's child is killed and the loss is repaid in the only coin mortals have."
    },
    functions: [
      "Origin-of-death tale: explains mortality, the pain of childbirth, and the labour of fish-poisoning as a single compensation for the murdered spirit-child",
      "Codes hospitality: guests \u2014 even disguised spirits \u2014 must not be wronged, for the wronged spirit's curse binds the whole community",
      "Sanctions food-processing taboos around menstruation, pregnancy, and drinking-feasts within which such 'worthless women' offences were policed",
      "Anchors the ritual authority of the piai, whose spirit-friends are the Yurokons now estranged from men"
    ],
