    license: "Scholarly documentation of public-domain folk material; passages are paraphrase",
    summary: {
      en: "When God made the first house he set his own dog at the gate to keep the Devil out; the dog sleeps when it should watch, the Devil slips in \u2014 and ever since, the tales say, the household dog barks at what men cannot see, doing God's watch as best a dog can.",
      zh: "\u795E\u9020\u7B2C\u4E00\u5EA7\u5B85\u6642\uFF0C\u89AA\u81EA\u7F6E\u72AC\u65BC\u9580\u4EE5\u62D2\u9B54\uFF1B\u72AC\u8A72\u5B88\u671B\u6642\u537B\u7761\u53BB\uFF0C\u9B54\u6F5B\u5165\u2014\u2014\u81EA\u6B64\uFF0C\u6545\u4E8B\u8AAA\uFF0C\u5BB6\u72AC\u671D\u4EBA\u6240\u4E0D\u898B\u4E4B\u7269\u800C\u5420\uFF0C\u7AED\u76E1\u72AC\u529B\u4EE3\u795E\u5B88\u671B\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "creation-chaos",
      "taboo-transgression",
      "ritual-origin"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Etiological tale; reconstructed framing flagged."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "When God made the first house he set his own dog at the gate, for the Devil walks abroad at night and a house unguarded is a house the Devil enters. The dog sleeps when it should watch, the Devil slips in and does his mischief in the dark \u2014 and when the owners wake, the loss is blamed on the dog's failure, not the Devil's cunning.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the origin-of-dog tale as documented in Vellius, Lithuanian Mythological Tales (1979); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "This is why, the tales say, a dog barks at what men cannot see: it remembers its first duty, standing where God stood it, and its bark at the empty yard is the oldest prayer of the household. (The framing of this record is reconstructed from the motif as documented in the collection; the tale's wording varies from teller to teller.)",
        note: "Paraphrase of the documented motif; framing reconstructed (Vellius 1979); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
