    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Grunau is a late and doubtful source (Machal's caveat); interpretive reconstruction flagged."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "According to the historian Simon Grunau, writing in the early part of the sixteenth century, a triad of gods received adoration at Romowe: Perkunas, deity of thunder, with his perpetual fire of oak; Potrympus, deity of rivers and springs \u2014 and hence of vegetation and good fortune \u2014 with a pot containing a serpent carefully fed by the priests; and Patollus, deity of the underworld.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the plate-note summary in Machal, Mythology of All Races III (1918), after Grunau; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Each god had his emblem in the evergreen oak: before Patollo stood the heads of a man, a horse, and a cow \u2014 the dead and the beasts he receives. We have, however, no certain evidence that the ancient Prussians possessed idols of their gods at all, and in many respects the statements of Grunau are open to grave doubt; the triad stands as the best-remembered shape of a religion recorded only by its destroyers. (Interpretive reconstruction flagged.)",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Machal (1918) caveat and material; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "perkunas"
    ]
