      {
        id: "1",
        text: "In the beginning the snake was made gentle and harmless, a creature of the households \u2014 the same being the Lithuanians fed with milk as the guardian \u017Ealtys. But the Devil, envious of a beast so favoured, gave the field-snake its venom, and from that hour the same shape walked abroad in two fortunes: the house-snake honoured as a god, the field-snake feared as the Devil's beast.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the etiological tale as documented in Vellius, Lithuanian Mythological Tales (1979); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The tale explains why the same creature is worshipped and killed: it is not the scales that decide but the side of the creation the beast serves. The house-snake's milk-dish stands at the hearth to this day in the old stories, and the field-snake's bite is the Devil's signature \u2014 one animal, two creators, as in all the Baltic making. (Framing reconstructed from the motif as documented.)",
        note: "Paraphrase of the documented motif; framing reconstructed (Vellius 1979); not verbatim."
      }
