    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "The name-giving curse episode; the tears of blood prefigure the dynastic bloodshed of the civil war."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The Sinchi received his prize with great satisfaction, asking in a loud voice if this was the child of Mama Micay, who ought to have been his wife. Titu Cusi Hualpa, though but a child, replied boldly that he was the son of Mama Micay and of the Inca Rocca. Tocay was indignant when he had heard those words, and ordered those who brought the child as a prisoner to take him out and kill him.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Sarmiento, History of the Incas, ch. 21 (Markham 1907); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The boy, when he heard such a sentence passed upon him, was so filled with sadness and fright, that he began to weep from fear of death. He began to shed tears of blood and with indignation beyond his years, in the form of a malediction he said to Tocay and the Ayamarcas: 'I tell you that as sure as you murder me there will come such a curse on you and your descendants that you will all come to an end, without any memory being left of your nation.'",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Sarmiento, History of the Incas, ch. 21 (Markham 1907); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The Ayamarcas and Tocay attentively considered this curse of the child together with the tears of blood. They thought there was some great mystery that so young a child should utter such weighty words, and that the fear of death should make such an impression on him that he should shed tears of blood. They were in suspense divining what it portended, whether that the child would become a great man. They revoked the sentence of death, calling the child Yahuar-huaccac, which means 'weeper of blood', in allusion to what had taken place. Before this they all said to the child that he should turn his face to Cuzco and weep over it, because those curses he had pronounced, would fall on the inhabitants of Cuzco, and so it happened.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Sarmiento, History of the Incas, ch. 21 (Markham 1907); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
