    license: "Public domain (translation 1907)",
    summary: {
      en: "As Viracocha was now very old, he nominated as his successor his bastard son Inca Urco, without regard to the order of succession, because he was very fond of his mother. This Inca was bold, proud, and despised others, so that he aroused the indignation of the warriors, more especially of the legitimate sons, and of the valiant captains Apu Mayta and Vicaquirau. These took order to prevent this succession, secretly setting their eyes on the third of the legitimate sons named Cusi, afterwards called Inca Yupanqui, because they believed that he was mild and affable, and showed signs of high spirit and lofty ideas. While they were consulting how it should be managed, the Chancas of Andahuaylas marched upon that city. Inca Viracocha, from fear of them, fled from Cuzco, and went to a place called Caquia Xaquixahuana, where he shut himself up, being afraid of the Chancas. Here he died after some years, deprived of Cuzco, of which his son Cusi had possession for several years before his father's death. This Inca was industrious, and inventor of cloths and embroidered work called Viracocha-tocapu, and amongst us brocade. He was buried in Caquia Xaquixahuana, and Gonzalo Pizarro, having heard that there was treasure with the body, discovered it and a large sum of gold; he burnt the body, and the natives collected the ashes and hid them in a vase. This, with the Inca's guauqui called Inca Amaru, was found by the Licentiate Polo, when he was Corregidor of Cuzco.",
      zh: "\u7DAD\u62C9\u79D1\u67E5\u5E74\u4E8B\u5DF2\u9AD8,\u4E0D\u6309\u7E7C\u4F4D\u6B21\u5E8F,\u7ADF\u7ACB\u5BF5\u5983\u4E4B\u5B50\u3001\u5EB6\u51FA\u7684\u5370\u52A0\xB7\u70CF\u723E\u5EAB\u70BA\u5132\u3002\u70CF\u723E\u5EAB\u9A55\u6A2A\u50B2\u6162,\u8F15\u8996\u4ED6\u4EBA,\u6FC0\u8D77\u6B66\u4EBA\u2014\u2014\u5C24\u5176\u662F\u5AE1\u5B50\u5011\u8207\u731B\u5C07\u963F\u666E\xB7\u99AC\u96C5\u5854\u3001\u7DAD\u5361\u57FA\u52DE\u2014\u2014\u7684\u61A4\u6168\u3002\u4ED6\u5011\u5BC6\u8B00\u963B\u6B62\u9019\u6A01\u7E7C\u627F,\u6697\u4E2D\u5C6C\u610F\u4E09\u5B50\u5EAB\u897F(\u5373\u5F8C\u4F86\u7684\u5370\u52A0\xB7\u5C24\u6F58\u742A),\u56E0\u4ED6\u6EAB\u548C\u53EF\u89AA,\u53C8\u986F\u9732\u51FA\u9AD8\u9060\u4E4B\u5FD7\u3002\u6B63\u7576\u4ED6\u5011\u8B00\u5283\u4E4B\u969B,\u5B89\u9054\u74E6\u4F0A\u5229\u4E9E\u65AF\u7684\u660C\u5361\u4EBA\u9032\u903C\u5EAB\u65AF\u79D1\u3002\u7DAD\u62C9\u79D1\u67E5\u61FC\u6575,\u68C4\u57CE\u800C\u9003,\u9000\u81F3\u5361\u57FA\u4E9E-\u590F\u57FA\u590F\u74DC\u7D0D,\u9589\u9580\u81EA\u5B88;\u6578\u5E74\u5F8C\u5BA2\u6B7B\u65BC\u65AF,\u5EAB\u65AF\u79D1\u65E9\u5DF2\u5728\u5176\u5B50\u5EAB\u897F\u624B\u4E2D\u3002\u9019\u4F4D\u5370\u52A0\u738B\u52E4\u65BC\u5236\u4F5C,\u662F\u300C\u7DAD\u62C9\u79D1\u67E5-\u6258\u5361\u666E\u300D\u7E54\u91D1\u9326\u7DDE\u7684\u767C\u660E\u8005\u3002\u4ED6\u846C\u65BC\u590F\u57FA\u590F\u74DC\u7D0D;\u8CA2\u85A9\u6D1B\xB7\u76AE\u85A9\u7F85\u805E\u8AAA\u96A8\u846C\u6709\u5BF6,\u6398\u5893\u5F97\u5927\u6279\u9EC3\u91D1,\u7ADF\u5C07\u907A\u9AD4\u711A\u6BC0\u3002\u571F\u4EBA\u6536\u62FE\u9AA8\u7070,\u85CF\u65BC\u7515\u4E2D;\u9023\u540C\u9019\u4F4D\u5370\u52A0\u738B\u7684\u74DC\u70CF\u57FA\u300C\u5370\u52A0\xB7\u963F\u746A\u9B6F\u300D,\u4E00\u4F75\u88AB\u5F8C\u4F86\u4EFB\u5EAB\u65AF\u79D1\u884C\u653F\u9577\u5B98\u7684\u6CE2\u7F85\u5148\u751F\u5C0B\u7372\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "taboo-transgression",
      "sacred-kingship",
      "pride-punished"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "The flight that opened the way for Pachacuti; the body-burning by Pizarro is the conquest's violation of the ancestor cult."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "As Viracocha was now very old, he nominated as his successor his bastard son Inca Urco, without regard to the order of succession, because he was very fond of his mother. This Inca was bold, proud, and despised others, so that he aroused the indignation of the warriors, more especially of the legitimate sons... and of the valiant captains Apu Mayta and Vicaquirau. These took order to prevent this succession to the Incaship... They secretly set their eyes on the third of the legitimate sons named Cusi, afterwards called Inca Yupanqui.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Sarmiento, History of the Incas, ch. 25 (Markham 1907); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "While they were consulting how it should be managed, the Chancas of Andahuaylas, thirty leagues from Cuzco, marched upon that city. Inca Viracocha, from fear of them, fled from Cuzco, and went to a place called Caquia Xaquixahuana, where he shut himself up, being afraid of the Chancas. Here he died after some years, deprived of Cuzco, of which his son Cusi had possession for several years before his father's death.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Sarmiento, History of the Incas, ch. 25 (Markham 1907); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "He was buried in Caquia Xaquixahuana and Gonzalo Pizarro, having heard that there was treasure with the body, discovered it and a large sum of gold. He burnt the body, and the natives collected the ashes and hid them in a vase. This, with the Inca's guauqui, called Inca Amaru, was found by the Licentiate Polo, when he was Corregidor of Cuzco.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Sarmiento, History of the Incas, ch. 25 (Markham 1907); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [],
    language: "Spanish record of Quechua testimony",
    canonical_translation: "Sir Clements Markham, History of the Incas (Hakluyt Society, 1907)"
