    lat: 7,
    lon: -58.8,
    period: "Arawak oral tradition; recorded 1915 (Roth)",
    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "Walter E. Roth, An Inquiry into the Animism and Folk-lore of the Guiana Indians (33rd Annual Report, Bureau of American Ethnology, 1915), ch. X 'Spirits of the Bush: Associated with Particular Plants', \xA7163A",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/cu31924104074665"
    },
    license: "Public domain (report 1915)",
    summary: {
      en: "There was a family of two sisters and two brothers. Cutting firewood, the sisters split a log and found inside a pretty little whip; each made another exactly like it, hung the three whips in a little banab in their field, and took their own jug of drink there on three occasions. The brothers, suspecting, sent the little hummingbird to make inquiries, then asked the sisters to explain \u2014 and to let them look on while the girls sported with the whips. At the banab the women showed their manner of play, but it was soon evident they knew neither how to sing, to dance, nor to whip properly; constrained after repeated entreaties, they handed the whips over to their brothers, who showed them how the real thing ought to be done \u2014 and 'called' their sisters Kumaka-plait and Ite-fiber, the elder and younger, transforming them into the kumaka thread and ite fiber out of which the Arawaks have ever since made their makuari whips.",
      zh: "\u5BB6\u6709\u4E8C\u59B9\u4E8C\u5144\u3002\u59CA\u59B9\u4F10\u85AA\u5256\u6728\uFF0C\u5F97\u4E00\u7F8E\u97AD\u65BC\u6728\u4E2D\uFF1B\u5404\u4EFF\u88FD\u4E00\u97AD\uFF0C\u61F8\u4E09\u97AD\u65BC\u7530\u4E2D\u5C0F\u68DA\uFF0C\u4E09\u5EA6\u81EA\u651C\u9152\u5F80\u3002\u5144\u7591\uFF0C\u9063\u8702\u9CE5\u5075\u4E4B\uFF0C\u9042\u8A70\u5176\u59B9\uFF1B\u59B9\u4E0D\u8A31\u5144\u57F7\u97AD\uFF0C\u60DF\u8A31\u5176\u89C0\u3002\u59B9\u4E4B\u6B4C\u821E\u8207\u97AD\u7686\u4E0D\u7CBE\uFF1B\u81F3\u518D\u56DB\u61C7\u6C42\uFF0C\u4E43\u4EE5\u97AD\u4ED8\u5144\uFF0C\u5144\u793A\u5176\u771F\u6CD5\u2014\u2014\u4E14\u300E\u547C\u300F\u5176\u59B9\u70BA\u5EAB\u746A\u5361\u7DE8\u8207\u4F0A\u7279\u7E34\uFF0C\u5316\u4E4B\u70BA\u5EAB\u746A\u5361\u7E36\u8207\u4F0A\u7279\u7E34\u2014\u2014\u963F\u62C9\u74E6\u514B\u4EBA\u81F3\u4ECA\u5236\u746A\u5EAB\u963F\u5229\u97AD\uFF0C\u7528\u6B64\u4E8C\u6750\u4E5F\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "ritual-origin",
      "transformation",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Arawak oral tradition recorded 1915 (Roth); archival record, read critically."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "There was a family of two sisters and two brothers. Going out one day to cut firewood, the sisters proceeded to the forest and cut the timber; on splitting a log, they found inside a pretty little whip. After closely examining it, each girl proceeded to make another exactly like it. Then they proceeded to their provision field, put up a little banab, and hung inside it the three whips; when they reached home they made some drink, two jugsful altogether, one for their two brothers and one for themselves \u2014 they took their portion to the banab, where they left it, and did this on three occasions.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7163A; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The brothers, suspecting that something was wrong, sent the little hummingbird to make inquiries: the bird flew into the banab, saw the jug of drink there and the three whips hanging up, and reported accordingly. The sisters, admitting the whips, refused to deliver their charge over to anyone \u2014 but they let the brothers come and look at them sporting with them. The women did their play, but it was soon evident that they knew neither how to sing, to dance, nor to whip properly with them; after repeated entreaties they finally handed the whips over to their brothers, who now showed them how the real thing ought to be done.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7163A; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Furthermore, they 'called' their sisters Kussaro-banna and Koro-botio \u2014 the elder and the younger \u2014 that is, they transformed them into kumaka [Bromelia] thread and ite fiber, the two materials out of which the Arawaks have ever since made their makuari whips.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Roth 1915, \xA7163A; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
