    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The hapless Korobona now lives in the woods alone; another babe there hides from view, for if her fault her brothers knew, blood only could atone. One, passing by, the infant's cry heard, and upon her came; then told her brethren, hunting near, and soon she saw the four appear, all wild with rage and shame. Two of them dragged their sister home; two turned the child to slay: they with an arrow pierced him through, and left him where he lay.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Brett 1880, 'Legend of Korobona'; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "'The child is dead,' the slayers said \u2014 but knew not that the care she gave revived that hapless child. He grew far more than other babes in wisdom and in size, and, still concealed in some thick tree, till he his mother's form could see, would shun all other eyes. With food she daily sought the woods where he was doomed to stay; but Korobona quite forgot that some her track might know \u2014 her track, by those small footprints shown. Each brother then, her secret known, prepared the shaft and bow; they screened her from his foes, cut him into pieces small, and she cursed their cruelty: 'Vile slayers of the innocent! The woes you fear will now be sent \u2014 and come through you, not me!'",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Brett 1880, 'Legend of Korobona'; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "A mighty wonder happened then, for that great change which waits all men touched not the serpent child. At length that heap, with flowers bedecked, began with life to heave; she seemed these words to hear: 'Thy son shall now avenge his murder done: O mother, cease to grieve!' And first a head and shoulders rose, slow growing from that mound: a mighty form appeared, well armed, with limbs complete and sound \u2014 white down adorned his short black hair, his skin like copper shone, more fair than with Waraus had been, his cheeks and brow besmeared with vermilion like blood. Thus the first Carib stern arose. No Warau could his strength withstand; he slaughtered those who dared his club, forced the rest to flee, took their daughters for wives, and as his children still increased, they took the Warau's place \u2014 invincible, from Wahma sprung.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Brett 1880, 'Legend of Korobona'; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
