    license: "Public domain (Liliuokalani 1897)",
    summary: {
      en: "Born is a child to Po-wehiwehi, cradled in the arms of Po-uliuli, dweller in the land of Poho-mi-luamea - the sacred scent from the gourd stem proclaims itself, and the seven waters just float. Born is the child of the hilu fish and swims. Born is the porpoise in the sea there swimming; born is the shark and the goatfish; the eel and the crab; the octopus and the sting ray; the bonito and the albacore; mackerel, mullet - each born in the sea there swimming, while land creatures wait their pairing across the divided nights.",
      zh: "\u6CE2\u7DAD\u5E0C\u7DAD\u5E0C\u4E4B\u5B50\u8A95\u751F\uFF0C\u6416\u65BC\u6CE2\u70CF\u91CC\u70CF\u91CC\u4E4B\u61F7\uFF0C\u5C45\u65BC\u6CE2\u970D\u7C73\u76E7\u963F\u6885\u4E9E\u4E4B\u5883\uFF1B\u846B\u8606\u8396\u9593\u8056\u9999\u81EA\u9673\uFF0C\u4E03\u6C34\u6F02\u6D6E\u3002\u5E0C\u76E7\u9B5A\u4E4B\u5B50\u8A95\u751F\u800C\u6E38\u3002\u6D77\u4E2D\u4F9D\u6B21\u8A95\u751F\uFF1A\u9F20\u6D77\u8C5A\u3001\u9BCA\u3001\u5C71\u7F8A\u9B5A\u3001\u9C3B\u8207\u87F9\u3001\u7AE0\u9B5A\u8207\u9B5F\u3001\u9C39\u8207\u9BAA\u3001\u9BD6\u8207\u9BD4\u2014\u2014\u51E1\u4F8B\u7686\u300E\u5728\u6D77\u4E2D\u6E38\u800C\u8A95\u300F\uFF1B\u9678\u4E0A\u751F\u9748\u5247\u5F85\u5404\u591C\u914D\u5C0D\u800C\u751F\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "creation-chaos",
      "sibling-creators",
      "miraculous-birth"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Chant-two catalogue paraphrase."
    },
    persons: [],
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Born is a child to Po-wehiwehi, cradled in the arms of Po-uliuli; a wrestler, a pusher; dweller in the land of Poho-mi-luamea. The sacred scent from the gourd stem proclaims itself; the stench breaks forth in the time of infancy; he is doubtful and stands swelling; he crooks himself and straddles. The seven waters just float; born is the child of the hilu fish and swims. The hilu fish rests with spreading tail-fin; a child of renown for Po-uliuli, a little one for Po-wehiwehi: Po-uliuli the male, Po-wehiwehi the female.",
        note: "Paraphrase of The Kumulipo, chant two (Liliuokalani 1897); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Born is the I'a, born the porpoise in the sea there swimming; born is the shark, born the goatfish, in the sea there swimming; born is the Mau and the Maumau; the Nana and the Mana; the Nake and the Make; the Napa and the Nala; the Pala and the Kala; the eel and the crab in the sea there swimming; born is the Kalakala and the Huluhulu sea slug; the Halahala and the Palapala; born is the octopus, born the sting ray in the sea there swimming; born is the Ao and the milkfish; born is the bonito, born the albacore in the sea there swimming; born is the mackerel and the Akule fish; born is the mullet and the Anae in the sea there swimming.",
        note: "Paraphrase of The Kumulipo, chant two (Liliuokalani 1897); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Man for the narrow stream, woman for the broad stream - the refrain that opens each division of the chant: every sea creature born in its night has its counterpart born on land in a night to come, the pairing of sea and shore, water-thing and land-thing, running through sixteen chants until the human line of the chiefs appears. The Kumulipo sings the birth of the cosmos as a genealogy, so that the chief born at its end may claim the whole chain of life from the first darkness as his ancestors.",
        note: "Paraphrase of The Kumulipo, chant two and frame (Liliuokalani 1897; Beckwith 1951); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
