      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Tamosi, the Mighty Maker (whom no mortal eye can see), made, that man might be partaker of His gifts, a wondrous tree. Though on earth huge trees have grown, none like that was ever known. High o'er all its head uprearing, cloudlike mass of brilliant green! On its noble branches bearing fruits, which none before had seen: each a different kind would bear, beauteous clusters, high in air! Lower down \u2014 its trunk surrounding \u2014 plantains grew, bananas sweet: all choice plants were there abounding which we now in gardens meet.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Brett 1880, 'The First Cultivation'; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Golden maize, so fresh and fair, waved its plumy tresses there. Sweet cassava one might find there, with the bitter, 'neath the fruits; yams, potatoes, every kind, where widely spread its mighty roots. There was found, in pristine state, all that men now cultivate. 'Twas Maipuri, that way roaming (whom some white men 'tapir' call), from the river's margin coming, who observed it first of all \u2014 daily through the forest stealing, he devoured its fruits which fell, its existence still concealing: what he fed on none could tell.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Brett 1880, 'The First Cultivation'; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Men, who saw him fat and sleek, sent forth scouts the truth to seek. First, Woodpecker: he kept tapping (from long habit) each old tree; shrewd Maipuri heard him rapping, and another way went he. But the Rat, with silent toil, tracked his steps \u2014 then shared the spoil \u2014 till some food, delicious, sticking to his lips was found, and mankind, become suspicious, made him show that feeding ground. All exclaimed: 'O noble tree! Precious gift of Tamosi!' Then an oracle commanded: 'Cut it down!' \u2014 they wondered all; yet sharp stones, as fate demanded, caused it in ten months to fall, crashing, thundering to the ground. Then, a fair division making, for his field each man provides, slips and cuttings freely taking from its branches, roots, and sides: 'Gaining thus at once precious fruits and daily bread.'",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Brett 1880, 'The First Cultivation'; not verbatim."
      }
