    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "A negotiated theology: Garcilaso presents the Inca conquest of the coast as a religious peace treaty; the fox-and-ocean cult detail is unique."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "He replied that his people had no need of another lord... that they had no wish to put away their gods, for these gods, he said, were very powerful. Among them was Pachacamac, whom they believed to be the creator and preserver of the universe, and therefore necessarily a greater god than the sun... such was the veneration in which they held him, that they used not even to look upon his image, but the King and the priests entered the temple to worship, walking backwards, and went out so as to avoid raising their eyes. They also worshipped Rimac, a god that spoke and gave replies to those who sought him. Their other objects of worship were the fox, which they respected for his cunning and sagacity, and the ocean, because it supplied them with fish... the sun less than any, as they needed no more heat than they already experienced.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Garcilasso de la Vega, Royal Commentaries (Markham 1871); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The Yuncas agreed to worship the sun like the Yncas, and to build a separate temple... but that no human sacrifices should be made, because to kill a man was contrary to the laws of nature, and the custom was to be entirely abolished. It was agreed that the idols should be removed from the temple of Pachacamac... Pachacamac was henceforth to be worshipped in the heart, and no statue to him was to be set up; because, as no one had ever seen him, it was impossible to know his figure, or to make an image of him. It was also arranged, for the greater honour and glory of the valley of Pachacamac, that a house of chosen virgins of the sun should be established... The Yncas agreed to hold in high veneration the oracle Rimac.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Garcilasso de la Vega, Royal Commentaries (Markham 1871); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
