    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "They commonly acknowledge a supreme Lord and Author of all things, which they of Peru called Viracocha, and gave him names of great excellence, as Pachacamac, or Pachayachachic, which is the Creator of heaven and earth: and Vsapu, which is admirable. Him they did worship, as the chiefest of all, whom they did honour in beholding the heaven.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Acosta, Natural and Moral History, book 5 ch. 3 (Hakluyt Society 1880); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "It hath caused great admiration in me, that although they had this knowledge, yet had they no proper name for God. If we shall seek into the Indian tongue for a word to answer to this name of God... we shall not find any in the Cuscan tongues. So as such as preach or write to the Indians use our Spanish name Dios... yet in truth they had some little knowledge, and therefore in Peru they made him a rich temple, which they called Pachacamac, which was the principal Sanctuary of the realm. And as it hath been said, this word of Pachacamac is, as much to say, as the Creator, yet in this temple they used their idolatries, worshipping the devil and figures.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Acosta, Natural and Moral History, book 5 ch. 3 (Hakluyt Society 1880); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "They likewise made sacrifices and offerings to Viracocha, which held the chief place amongst the worships which the Kings Yncas made. Hereof they called the Spaniards Viracochas, for that they hold opinion they are the sons of heaven, and divine.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Acosta, Natural and Moral History, book 5 ch. 3 (Hakluyt Society 1880); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
