    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "The Cavillaca cycle is the most-studied narrative of the Huarochiri manuscript; the sexual content is retold soberly as the source has it."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "At that time they also say that there was a woman who was a huaca. Her name was Cavillaca, and she was a most beautiful virgin, who was much sought after by the huacas, or principal idols, but she would never show favour to any of them. Once she sat down to weave a mantle at the foot of a lucma tree, when the wise Coniraya turned himself into a very beautiful bird, went up into the lucma tree, took some of his generative seed and made it into the likeness of a ripe and luxurious lucma, which he allowed to fall near the beautiful Cavillaca. She took it and ate it with much delight, and by it she was made pregnant without other contact with man.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "When she found that all were silent, she said: 'As none of you will speak, I shall let the child go, and doubtless his father will be the one to whom he crawls, and at whose feet he rests.' So saying, she loosed the child, who, passing by all the others, went to where was his father Coniraya in his rags and dirt, and when the child reached him, it rejoiced and laughed, and rested at his feet. This conduct caused Cavillaca great shame and annoyance... Then she turned her back and fled away towards the sea-shore.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "As he ran, he met a condor, to whom he said: 'Brother, tell me whether you encountered a woman with such and such marks?' The condor answered: 'I saw her very near this place...' Coniraya, rejoicing at the good news, blessed the condor: 'You shall live for ever... I further declare that he who kills you shall himself be killed.' ... A lion gave him good news and was blessed; the foxes that told him the goddess was far off were cursed... Thus he rewarded and granted privileges to all the animals that gave him news that accorded with his wishes, and cursed all those whose tidings were not agreeable to him.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "In those days it is said that there were no fishes in the sea, but that this Urpi-huachac reared a few in a small pond. Coniraya was enraged that Urpi-huachac should be absent in the sea, visiting Cavillaca; so he emptied the fishes out of her pond into the sea, and thence all the fishes now in the sea have been propagated... While she was pretending to comb his head she was forming a rock over which she might hurl him when he was off his guard. He knew this through his great wisdom, and went back to the land of Huarochiri, where he wandered about for a long time, playing tricks both to whole villages and to single men or women.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Avila, Narrative of the Errors of Huarochiri, ch. 2 (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "cuniraya",
      "cavillaca",
      "pachacamac"
    ],
    language: "Spanish record of Quechua tradition",
    canonical_translation: "C. R. Markham, Narrative of the Rites and Laws of the Yncas (1873)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Cavillaca cycle is the Huarochiri manuscript's narrative showpiece, and it does what the courtly Inca texts never attempt: it portrays the Creator as a disreputable wanderer and a goddess as a woman with her own unyielding judgement. Cuniraya Viracocha - whose name the people still invoked with a coca leaf in hand - appears in the form of the poorest of Indians, in rags, reviled by those who do not know him as a lousy wretch. His power is casual and immense: terraces rise at a word, canals flow at the throw of a hollow cane. He is also a trickster with a specialist's knowledge of huacas, and he invents deceits wherever he visits.\n\nThe virgin huaca Cavillaca is courted by every great idol and refuses them all. Weaving beneath a lucma tree, she is approached by the bird-disguised Cuniraya, whose seed he fashions into a ripe fruit and drops beside her. She eats it with delight, conceives, and bears a son in virginity, suckling him for a year without knowing whose he is. When the child can crawl she summons the assembly of the gods to Anchicocha, each of them groomed and dressed to be chosen. Since none will claim the child, she sets him loose: his father will be the one he crawls to. The child passes the whole adorned assembly and goes to the ragged figure in the lowest place, laughing, and rests at his feet.\n\nCavillaca's shame is the emotional engine of the rest of the cycle. Snatching up the child - 'What disgrace, that a lady such as I am should be made pregnant by a poor and filthy creature' - she flees toward the sea. Cuniraya dons golden robes in an instant, his splendour lighting the whole country, and pursues her with loving words; she will not turn her head, calling him a creature so vile and filthy. At the coast of Pachacamac she enters the sea with the child and both are turned into rock - the two stones offshore that the people still showed to Avila as mother and child.\n\nThe pursuit along the shore is a chain of animal judgements that etiologically ordains the relations between Andean people and animals. The condor's good news earns it eternal life, freedom of the wilderness, the right to eat the dead, and a taboo: who kills a condor dies. The lion's good news makes it the executioner of evildoers and ordains the festival use of its skin, with a llama sacrificed by whoever wears it. The falcon is given the hummingbird for breakfast and a place on dancers' heads. The two foxes and the parrots, bearers of bad news, are cursed to night-wandering, stench, persecution and noisy betrayal. Avila verified in his parish that each decree still governed conduct: condors untouchable even when dying of old age, villages turning out wholesale at a cry of 'fox!', falcon skins at every festival.\n\nAt Pachacamac the cycle doubles its sexual plot with the daughters of Urpi-huachac, wife of the god Pachacamac, guarding a great serpent. Cuniraya stills the serpent by his wisdom and takes the elder daughter; the younger escapes by turning into a dove, giving her mother her name, Urpi-huachac, 'mother of the doves'. Revenge follows: no fish yet live in the sea, only in the goddess's pond, and the enraged Cuniraya empties it into the ocean, stocking all the seas of the world from a single Andean pond. She pursues him with the offer of a head-combing; he accepts, and she builds a rock under her hands to hurl him from. He sees through it, excuses himself, and returns to the highlands, where he wanders playing tricks on villages and single men and women to the end of the story as we have it.\n\nThe cycle's theology is pointed: the creator of the Andes is not a triumphant sky-king but a poor man whom the dignified reject, and the goddess who rejects him is herself immortalized as sea-rock - the myth encodes the coastal valley's memory of contact between highland trickster-religion and the old Pachacamac pantheon, with Urpi-huachac's fishes and Pachacamac's wife as the local establishment the wanderer defeats. In motif terms it is a virgin-birth plus trickster-pursuit plus animal-decree chain; in Andean terms it is a myth of how the world's fish, the coast's rocks, the condor's sanctity and the fox's shame all got their character at once, on a single disastrous day of love."
    },
