    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Ritual calendar from a parish priest's compilation; the source of the modern Inti Raymi revival (1944) - a contested reconstruction, not a continuous ceremony."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "They went to Curicancha in the morning, at noon, and at night, bringing the sheep that were to be sacrificed on that day, which they carried round the idols and huacas called Punchao Ynca, and Pachayachachi, and Chuqui-yllayllapa... the Priests said: 'O Creator, and Sun, and Thunder, be for ever young! do not grow old. Let all things be at peace! let the people multiply, and their food, and let all other things continue to increase.'",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Molina, Fables and Rites, month of Hauca (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Four hundred men of war assembled in the middle of the square... one hundred faced towards Colla-suyu, one hundred to the westward towards Chincha-suyu, another hundred towards Anti-suyu, and the last hundred towards the south. They cried 'Go forth all evils', and each party set out at great speed along its appointed road, handing the cries on from lineage to mitimaes... until at the great rivers they bathed themselves and washed their clothes and arms.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Molina, Fables and Rites, Situa festival (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The high priest then said in a loud voice so that all might hear: 'Take heed how you eat this sancu; for he who eats it in sin, and with a double will and heart, is seen by our father the Sun, who will punish him with grievous troubles. But he who with a single heart partakes of it, to him the Sun and the Thunder will show favour, and will grant children and happy years, and abundance.'",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Molina, Fables and Rites, Situa festival (Markham 1873); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [],
    language: "Spanish record of Quechua liturgy",
    canonical_translation: "C. R. Markham, Narrative of the Rites and Laws of the Yncas (1873)",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Inca sacred year, as Molina took it down from elderly witnesses who had seen it under Huayna Capac, Huascar and Manco Inca, ran from one May to the next on a lunar-solar reckoning with the solstice festival at its heart. The year opened in Hauca, the month of rest, when the whole court retired to pleasure-houses and the flocks of the Sun and the Creator were driven in procession three times a day around their images, while the priests prayed that the gods 'be for ever young'. The golden sheep-figures, curi-napa and collque-napa, were carried on litters in memory - the witnesses said - of the flock that came out of the founding cave with the ancestors.\n\nJune was the month of canals. July, the sowing month, imposed its fast: the Tarpuntae priests ate nothing but boiled maize and herbs from sowing until the shoot stood a finger high, and the whole city sang the yahuayra in red robes, praying the Creator for a full harvest, the Sun for warmth, Thunder for rain without hail.\n\nAugust brought the year's great exorcism, the Situa, timed to the first rains and their fevers. Huacas were summoned to Cuzco from Quito to Chile; strangers, the deformed, even dogs were driven from the city. Four hundred warriors, a hundred to each quarter, faced the four suyos and cried 'O sicknesses, disasters, misfortunes, and dangers, go forth from the land!'; then they relay-ran the shout along the four highways, hand to hand, lineage to mitimaes, until at the great rivers - the Apurimac, the Vilcanota, the river of Cusipampa - the runners bathed themselves and their weapons, so that rivers of great volume would carry the evils to the sea. Everyone in the city shook their mantles from their doorways and bathed at dawn; then came the pancurcu torch-play, the sancu pudding smeared on doorposts and faces, and the yahuar-sancu, blood-sprinkled, eaten under a public oath of loyalty to Creator, Sun, Thunder and Inca, with not a crumb allowed to fall.\n\nThe third quarter of the year belonged to the young: the Ccapac Raymi of November armed the knights of royal blood with slings, breeches and pierced ears, racing at Anahuarqui and receiving the golden ear-plugs at Raurana, while their ancestors' mummies watched from the square. December opened with the chocanaco sling-battle between Hanan and Hurin, then the moro-urco, the four-coloured cable wound in a spiral 'like a snake'; and the month closed with mojocati, when the year's ashes, food, clothes and gold were thrown into the dammed river and swept to Ollantaytambo and the sea, so that the Creator would not find his people ungrateful.\n\nJanuary to March were working months; April harvested the sacred fields, beginning at Sausiru, the farm where Mama Huaco had sown the first maize, and the new knights carried the crop singing the yaravi. Through it all, the embalmed Incas sat on their golden benches in the great square, fed from the feast, warmed with sancu, washed in their own baths. The liturgical year thus moved the whole social body - crops, youths, ancestors, sicknesses, and the year's own refuse - through a cycle of renewal that ended where it began, at the doors of the Sun.\n\nAfter the conquest the cycle was broken; the festivals were suppressed by the 1570s idolatry campaigns that produced Molina's own report. The modern Inti Raymi celebrated in Cuzco since 1944 is a theatrical reconstruction of the June solstice feast - an invented tradition in Hobsbawm's sense - and should not be confused with the colonial-era ritual calendar recorded here, though both now compete to represent the Andean sacred year."
    },
