    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Then all the rivers dried up, and the fish stayed in the lakes and little brooks, and there was abundance of fish. Makunaima made fishing-hooks out of beeswax, but caught nothing, for when the fish bit, they bit the fishing-hook in two. So he met a man who was fishing for Aimar\xE4.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Koch-Gr\xFCnberg 1924, Mythe 5; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "'What shall we do? We will steal his hook! I will transform myself into an Aimar\xE4 and steal the hook from him. If he catches me and hauls me out, I shall cheat him: if he strikes me on the head, I shall make a mess and pretend to die. Brother, ask him for this fish to eat! I shall become the biggest Aimar\xE4 there is.' He sprang into the water, transformed himself into the biggest Aimar\xE4 and bit; the man hauled him out, struck him on the head, drew the hook from his mouth. The brother begged the fish, knew him by his closed eyes, took the fish with him \u2014 and it transformed itself again into Makunaima.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Koch-Gr\xFCnberg 1924, Mythe 5; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The man went away to work on the other side, making manioc cakes for the journey. 'We will transform ourselves into crickets and sit in his back-basket!' They transformed themselves into crickets and sat in his basket, and so came to the other side, where Makunaima and the eldest brother still live to-day. In the basket Makunaima conjured wounds on his body and said: 'I am tired of these wounds! I will pull them off!' He pulled them off, threw them on the path and transformed them into stones: 'Remain here! To all people who walk this path you shall cling!' \u2014 that is why people who walk this path suffer from wounds to this day.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Koch-Gr\xFCnberg 1924, Mythe 5; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
