    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Khusrau Parwiz like other paladins, while yet his father lived, was young and bold, and had for mate Shirin who was to him as his bright eyes. He cared for none beside among the fair, but parted from her for a while when he came to be king and had to roam the world unrestingly, for all his work was then to fight Bahram Chubina, while the Fair wept day and night o'er his defect in love.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Khusrau Parwiz sec. 56 (Warner 1923); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "It was so that one day he willed to hunt, and all things were prepared as in the times of former Shahs: three hundred steeds caparisoned with gold, a thousand and eight score afoot, seven hundred falconers with royal falcons, three hundred keepers of the cheetahs, and two thousand minstrels all on camel-back, crowned with gold.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Khusrau Parwiz sec. 56 (Warner 1923); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Now Shirin, on hearing that the host, preceded by the mighty Shah, hath come, put on a yellow vest musk-scented, and made her visage like pomegranate-blooms; she left her jocund hall, went on the roof, and waited till Khusrau Parwiz arrived, then let the tear-drops fall upon her cheeks. All tears and beauty, eagerly she cried thus in the olden tongue: O Shah! Great Lion! where is that love of thine? Where are the tears of blood once stanched by looking on Shirin? Where are our loves, our troth, our bonds, our oaths?",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Khusrau Parwiz sec. 56 (Warner 1923); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
