    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Living Buddhist narrative tradition (Kah-gyur); paraphrase of public-domain 1882 translation only; nothing restricted reproduced."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "On the fifteenth of the month, Manohara, daughter of Druma the Kinnari king, came with five hundred kinnari companions to the Brahmasabha pool to bathe, dancing and singing so sweetly that even the beasts of the forest were enchanted - the Rishi who told the hunter of it said the sound gave him delight for seven whole days. The hunter hid in the thicket with the Amogha-chain he had won from the Naga, and cast it: Manohara bounded and shrieked, her companions fled, and the hunter secured her by her own head-jewel, the jewel by which she flew heavenwards, which she had left with him as a pledge of good faith.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Schiefner/Ralston, Sudhana Avadana (1882); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The hunter offered her to Prince Sudhana, who looked once and was struck by the dart of passion, like a moth falling into the flame. When mountaineers rebelled and eight generals had failed, the Purohita urged the king to send his son to war and forbid him a last sight of Manohara. Sudhana left her head-jewel with his mother: give it back only if her life is in danger. The campaign was won without a fight, for the god Vaisravana, delayed on his celestial road above the marching prince, sent the yaksha general Panchika to raise a phantom host so vast the mountaineers opened their gates at once.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Schiefner/Ralston, Sudhana Avadana (1882); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "The king dreamed that a vulture tore open his belly and wound his entrails around the city. The Purohita read it as mortal danger and prescribed the Brahmans' mystic cure: a tank filled with the blood of young roes, into which the king must descend while four Veda-learned Brahmans licked his feet and anointed them with the fat of a non-human being - and Manohara was that non-human being. Learning what the bath meant, she fled to the queen, who gave back the head-jewel; and as she was summoned she rose into the air, saying that like a cow freed from its bonds she would flee.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Schiefner/Ralston, Sudhana Avadana (1882); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "She flew first to the Rishi, telling him his directions had caused her capture, and left with him a ring and the way home: three black mountains, and three and three beyond, then Himavant, a cavern beneath three peaks, the king of birds carrying the traveler over Vajraka, ram-like creatures, the iron men, the iron-lipped Rakshasi to be pinned with a wedge, the whirlpool of sixty fathoms, the demon rivers, then the Kinnari king's capital. Sudhana, armed with the nectar remedy, a monkey, spells, bow, jewel, poison and antidote, three wedges and a lyre, crossed them all and dropped his ring into a water-carrier's jug - the first to be poured over the restored princess. It fell into Manohara's lap.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Schiefner/Ralston, Sudhana Avadana (1882); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "5",
        text: "Before her father Druma, Sudhana sliced seven golden stems like a plantain and shot one arrow uninjured through seven palm-trees, seven kettledrums and seven boars, standing unmoved afterward like Sumeru. Set among a thousand identical kinnaris, Manohara stepped forward at his truthful verse; Druma gave her back with a jar of gold-dust, warning only that men are fickle. Later, homesick, the pair flew back through the air to Hastinapura; Sudhana told all, was invested with royal power, and for twelve years offered sacrifice without hindrance.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Schiefner/Ralston, Sudhana Avadana (1882); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "sudhana",
      "manohara",
      "druma",
      "phalaka",
      "indra"
    ]
