    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "My mother was the one who told me that the girl arose; she put her hands into the wood ashes; she threw up the wood ashes into the sky. She said to the wood ashes: 'The wood ashes which are here, they must altogether become the Milky Way. They must white lie along in the sky, that the stars may stand outside of the Milky Way, while the Milky Way is the Milky Way, while it used to be wood ashes.'",
        note: "Quote from Specimens of Bushman Folklore (Bleek & Lloyd 1911), p. 73 (public domain)."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The Milky Way lying comes to its place... that it may descend nicely... The Stars shall also stand nicely around. They shall sail along upon their footprints, which they, always sailing along, are following... The Stars had been setting; they had, again, been coming out; they had, sailing along, been following their footprints. They become white, when the Sun comes out.",
        note: "Quote from Specimens of Bushman Folklore (Bleek & Lloyd 1911), p. 75 (public domain)."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Condensed paraphrase, same locus; not verbatim. She first gently threw up wood ashes into the sky, that she might presently throw up |hum roots; while she felt that she was angry with her mother, because her mother had not given her many |hum roots, that she might eat abundantly; for, she was in the hut. The red roots made red stars and the white roots white stars; the peel of the |khissi root she threw up became locusts. And the girl was at last shot by her husband.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of Specimens of Bushman Folklore (Bleek & Lloyd 1911), pp. 76-77; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
