    license: "Public domain (Schiefner/Ralston translation 1882)",
    summary: {
      en: "A crow cries agreeably to a woman whose husband is far away; she vows a golden cap if he returns safe. He does, the crow presents itself, and she pays the promised cap - with which the crow struts about until a falcon, drawn by the gold, tears off its head: 'a possession which has no necessary cause will be taken away.'",
      zh: "\u70CF\u9D09\u5411\u5BE1\u5C45\u5A66\u4EBA\u6085\u9CF4\uFF0C\u5A66\u4EBA\u8A31\u9858\u592B\u6B78\u5247\u8D08\u91D1\u51A0\u3002\u592B\u5B89\u7136\u6B78\u4F86\uFF0C\u70CF\u9D09\u8A0E\u5F97\u91D1\u51A0\uFF0C\u6234\u4E4B\u98DB\u63DA\uFF1B\u56E0\u91D1\u51A0\u62DB\u4F86\u9DC2\u9DF9\uFF0C\u5544\u53BB\u5176\u9996\u2014\u2014\u300C\u7121\u56E0\u800C\u5F97\u4E4B\u7269\uFF0C\u5FC5\u906D\u4EBA\u596A\u3002\u300D"
    },
    motifs: [
      "parable-teaching",
      "boon-curse"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Kah-gyur fable literature; living Buddhist tradition treated respectfully."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The crow uttered agreeable sounds, and the woman promised a golden cap if her husband returned home safe. He did return, the crow appeared with an eye to the reward, and she gave it the cap; the crow put it on and flew hither and thither - but on account of the golden cap a falcon tore off its head. A deity sang: a possession which has no necessary cause will be taken away; the gold on the crow's head was looked after by a robber.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Schiefner/Ralston, The Crow with the Golden Cap (1882); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
