    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The Porcupine said: 'Do you really want me to go to the Man who eats bushes? He will come and swallow all the sheep, as they stand in the kraal... A Man who devours things he is; he walks along eating up the bushes as he passes... The Mantis said to her: 'You must go to your other father, the All-devourer, that he may help me eat up these sheep, and drink this soup.'",
        note: "Quote from D. F. Bleek, The Mantis and His Friends (1924), pp. 34-35 (public domain)."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The All-devourer followed the Porcupine's spoor; as he went he ate up the bushes. He climbed up, finishing off the bushes, while his shadow glided up to the Mantis's hut. It fell upon the Mantis. The Mantis looked at the sun, he asked where the clouds were, for the sun seemed to be in clouds... 'Is Father holding fire in his hand, for a fire is waxing red yonder?' The Porcupine answered him: 'It is the Man coming there, whose tongue is red.'",
        note: "Quote from D. F. Bleek, The Mantis and His Friends (1924), pp. 35-36 (public domain)."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "She heated the spear, she laid it burning hot to her son's ear-root; her son sat still. She heated the spear again, she said to her son: 'Remember, Grandfather's tongue is like this. You must sit firmly when you go to Grandfather.'... The All-devourer scorched the young Mantis's temple with his tongue... Young Kwammang-a looked hard at the other, he signed to him to hold his spear fast, and he held his own well.",
        note: "Quote from D. F. Bleek, The Mantis and His Friends (1924), pp. 38-39 (public domain), minor elisions."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "They ran away, while their fathers poured forth; the sheep also poured forth, the buckets poured forth; his father sat on the bed; the pots poured forth; the things poured down. His Grandfather doubled up and died. The children said: 'O bushes, we have cut you out! You shall truly become bushes, you shall grow at your place... the place shall be right again and these sheep shall wander over it... that the people may get dry bushes and be able to warm themselves.'",
        note: "Quote from D. F. Bleek, The Mantis and His Friends (1924), pp. 39-40 (public domain)."
      }
    ],
