    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "San oral tradition recorded in Afrikaans by G. R. von Wielligh; presented as an archival record."
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "'Kou, the eldest of Night's three daughters, married a son of the 'Hotnotsgod (the Mantis); they built huts of bushes. Never taking off her tortoise-shell necklace, she could catch tortoises as she pleased \u2014 but she grew so fond of tortoise flesh that she wanted nothing else, and, like the slow, lazy tortoise, she grew slow and lazy herself. A short walk to fetch tortoises took her nearly the whole day; she did nothing more for husband or child. Her husband scolded, then beat her \u2014 as well have scolded a tortoise, or rattled the pebbles in the shells. He had to do everything alone, even catch her tortoises.\n\nOne day, while he was away hunting, a bobbejaan came and asked the child where her mother was: he wanted the thong that bound the tortoise shells on her neck, for it was made of the skin of his brother. The child would not tell; the bobbejaan threatened to come next day and tear the necklace from 'Kou's neck by force. When the father came home the child begged him to stay; next day the bobbejaan came as promised, and the husband threatened him with bow and arrow. The bobbejaan warned that he would return with a commando of his brothers and break the huts to flinders.\n\nThe husband hurried out to gather food and made ready for the attack. As promised, the bobbejaan came with a great company of his family. The Mantis, watching quietly from a bush, changed his son into a great bird and the child into the Echo. The bird rose and made a great wind with its wings; thick dust arose, and the Echo made a great noise. As the bird wheeled, a terrible whirlwind arose, hurling stones and pieces of wood; here a bobbejaan took a stone on the head, there another a blow with a stick, and the sand and dust blinded them so that they scattered and fled.\n\nFrom far off they called back: 'Wait, we will never leave 'Kou in peace \u2014 over her grave we will walk, and the stones of it we will turn and roll forever.' But they slunk away. And 'Kou grew lazier by the day: she fell asleep, and slept, and slept to this very day. In her sleep she became a great mountain, never to wake again. When the bobbejaans saw her become mountains they were only glad; in revenge they walk on the mountains and roll the stones, as they swore. 'Kou, the mountains, is not dead: she only rests in an eternal sleep."
    },
    functions: [
      "Origin of mountains as the sleeping body of Night's eldest daughter.",
      "Explains the bobbejaan's habit of turning and rolling stones on high places.",
      "Charter for the echo as a child-spirit that answers from the rocks."
    ],
