    motifs: [
      "resurrection",
      "taboo-transgression",
      "transformation"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "San oral tradition recorded in Afrikaans by G. R. von Wielligh; presented as an archival record."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Condensed paraphrase of von Wielligh Deel I No. 10 (1919); not verbatim. The Rain-Beast is the great rain-maker of the Bushmen; his dwelling-place is beside the great waters. In drought the people fetch him, lead him round, and show him all the places where they want rain; to put him in a good mood they dance about him, clap hands, sing, praise him and beg him to let it rain so the fields may grow green and the game and honey-bees return. He is tame if no one vexes him, and changes himself into a cow so they can milk her.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of von Wielligh, Boesman-Stories Deel I (1919), No. 10; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Condensed paraphrase, same locus; not verbatim. Once there was drought in the mountains where the bobbejaans lived; their leader fetched the Rain-Beast, who changed into a cow so the bobbejaan could have milk on the journey, and was milked wherever he thirsted. After he had shown every dry place he brought the Rain-cow back, and everywhere it rained beautifully. But instead of thanking her, he killed her \u2014 only to get the milk-udder to take with him.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of von Wielligh, Boesman-Stories Deel I (1919), No. 10; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Condensed paraphrase, same locus; not verbatim. A burning thirst and gnawing hunger seized him: the green karkoere dried to dust at his touch, the fountain dried as he bent to drink, the shade shifted away from him as he walked toward it, the eggshell of water vanished at his lips. The people bade him run back and cry 'Choro, Choro, live again as you were!' and then return the milk-udder; and the Rain-cow sprang up and at once let rain fall where they stood, a flood on which banks of locusts floated. The flood rose till he sat to his neck in a tree packed with locusts, and the Rain-Beast left him there until the next day.",
        note: "Condensed paraphrase of von Wielligh, Boesman-Stories Deel I (1919), No. 10; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
