    license: "Public domain",
    summary: {
      en: "The Fire-man's shining head lights the twilight world and takes the hunters' game; the women urge his death. As he crosses the water the arrows bring him down, and the children throw his head into the sky \u2014 where it becomes the Sun, seeking its body forever. The body, headless, rots into a crab and still walks the shores at dusk.",
      zh: "\u706B\u4EBA\u660E\u9996\u7167\u5FAE\u660E\u4E4B\u4E16\uFF0C\u4E14\u596A\u7375\u8005\u4E4B\u7372\uFF1B\u5A66\u4EBA\u616B\u607F\u6BBA\u4E4B\u3002\u5176\u6D89\u6C34\u6642\u7BAD\u4E0B\u5982\u96E8\uFF1B\u5B69\u7AE5\u64F2\u5176\u9996\u4E0A\u5929\u2014\u2014\u9042\u70BA\u65E5\uFF0C\u6C38\u5C0B\u5176\u8EC0\u3002\u7121\u9996\u4E4B\u8EC0\u673D\u5316\u70BA\u87F9\uFF0C\u81F3\u4ECA\u9EC3\u660F\u6CBF\u5CB8\u800C\u884C\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sun-capture",
      "culture-hero",
      "transformation"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "San oral tradition recorded in Afrikaans by G. R. von Wielligh; presented as an archival record."
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "Danser is the Fire-man, the fire-maker. He first walked as a man; light and warmth streamed from his head, for he was the one who placed fire in the stones, in the wood and in the clouds. Where he walked or went there was always light and warmth, so he always had a great company around him: the hunters in his neighbourhood could see where the game was, and those seeking veld food could find it by the light of Danser's head.\n\nBut sometimes he was too lazy to walk and sat at ease; when the hunters begged him to go further so they could see the game, he mocked them, asking why they did not go and make light themselves. He also had a way of taking the shot game from the hunters, and the hunters grew angry \u2014 for the women are discontented when their men come home without food and have nothing for the hungry children crying for it. So the women advised their men to kill Danser and carry his head round on a long pole, so that everyone might see.\n\nOne hunter who liked the women's talk said: 'Good, let us shoot Danser with poisoned arrows, to carry his head on a long pole so we can get warmth and light.' A second said: 'All very well; but whose eyes are tough enough to bear that strong light, and whose hands to cut off that warm head?' A third asked: 'And where do we get a pole that will not burn with the heat?' A fourth said: 'You are bad at making plans! I will tell you what to do: we wait till Danser walks through the water \u2014 something he does regularly \u2014 then we dart at him with poisoned arrows; if he falls into the water his light and warmth are gone.' A fifth answered: 'You think you are clever, but you are very stupid. Tell me, what good is it to us if Danser's light and warmth are put out? Then we sit in the same old darkness and cold.' But all cried: 'It is nothing! Once he is away from us he can plague and torment us no more.' Lion, leopard, wolf, jackal and cat \u2014 men of the old race \u2014 wanted nothing to do with the murder; they can see well enough in the dark.\n\nThe others kept to their plan. They watched Danser, and just as he stepped through the water they shot, all together; the arrows struck him, he fell forward into the water, and dead was the light and gone the warmth. They ran up, quickly cut off Danser's head under the water, dragged out the body and set the head down on the bank to see what would come of it. But the head and the body would not die: the head's eyes looked round and turned from side to side, and the body stood up and walked about without its head. The head could see and speak but could not move; the body could move but could not see, hear, smell or speak. The head called to the body, but the body did not hear; the body searched for the head but could not find it, and instead of coming nearer walked ever further away.\n\nThe murderers went away. Then a girl sent her children to throw the head of Danser into the air so that there should be light and warmth, warning them to beware of the headless body, which would murder them if it caught them. The children stepped carefully; the head was already drying and giving light, and it was so hot when they took hold of it that they threw it into the air at once. Then the mothers cried: 'Wind, Wind, come and take the head high into the blue sky, that we may have light and warmth!' A strong whirlwind came and carried the head above the clouds and let it stay there. The head of Danser the Fire-man became the Sun.\n\nAll the people rejoiced that there was now light and warmth over the whole earth: 'O Sun, stay there and give us light and warmth every day... Great is the Sun, who can see all people at once. Now there is light to forbid evil: whoever does evil, steals or murders will be seen, and the Sun will burn him with his heat.' But the head still plays one trick: it seeks for its body. In the morning it climbs, seeking, higher and higher at the mountains on the east side, and when it is high enough sinks again to the mountains on the west, and dives into the deep waters of the sea to seek there too \u2014 for the body wasted and rotted and has taken the shape of a Crab. And the body, still headless, walks up and down along the waters at evening, seeking its head."
    },
    functions: [
      "Cosmogony of the sun as the severed head of the fire-being.",
      "Explains the crab's shape and its dusk patrolling of the shore.",
      "Frames the day-night alternation as the head's endless search."
    ],
