    summary: {
      en: "The Moon lets moonwater fall so 'Kaggen's new shoes stay wet and unwearable; when the Sun burns them by his fire, the Owl \u2014 the Mantis's other daughter \u2014 flings the coals and glowing ash into the sky, crying 'Coals, become Stars! Ash, become the Milky Way!' A whirlwind carries them above the clouds. The Sun curses her: by day she is blind and mocked; by night she flies among her stars \u2014 and evil-doers multiply.",
      zh: "\u6708\u964D\u6708\u6C34\uFF0C\u4EE4\u87B3\u8782\u65B0\u978B\u5E38\u6FD5\u4E0D\u53EF\u7A7F\uFF1B\u65E5\u4EE5\u5176\u706B\u711A\u4E4B\u3002\u689F\u2014\u2014\u87B3\u8782\u4E4B\u53E6\u4E00\u5973\u2014\u2014\u64F2\u70AD\u8207\u71BE\u7070\u4E0A\u5929\uFF0C\u547C\u300C\u70AD\u5316\u70BA\u661F\uFF01\u7070\u5316\u70BA\u9280\u6CB3\uFF01\u300D\u65CB\u98A8\u8209\u4E4B\u904E\u96F2\u3002\u65E5\u5492\u5176\u665D\u76F2\u53D7\u5632\uFF1B\u591C\u5247\u7FD4\u65BC\u5DF1\u661F\u4E4B\u9593\u2014\u2014\u800C\u60E1\u884C\u8005\u81EA\u6B64\u6ECB\u9577\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "transformation",
      "culture-hero",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "San oral tradition recorded in Afrikaans by G. R. von Wielligh; presented as an archival record."
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "The Moon came out of the right-foot shoe of the 'Hotnotsgod 'Kaggen. He made himself another pair of shoes in place of it. But the Moon did not like it that 'Kaggen should tramp on his masters, the shoes, by walking in them; so she let moonwater fall to keep the shoes wet, so that 'Kaggen could never put them on.\n\nThe 'Hotnotsgod called his other daughter, she who later became the Owl, and told her to place the wet shoes beside the Fire to dry. This again the Sun did not like, that 'Kaggen should dare put his dirty shoes beside the Fire; so he made the fire flame fiercely, so that the shoes were burnt. When the 'Hotnotsgod sent for his shoes they were burnt, and he scolded the Owl not a little.\n\nThe Owl herself was angered by it, and she took the coals and threw them into the sky, crying out: 'Come, coals, become Stars, that we may have light by night when the Moon and Sun sleep!' And to the glowing ashes she said: 'Come, ashes, become the Milky Way, to help the Stars give light. Give light, that the people may see by night and need not sit at home.' Then a great whirlwind came and carried the coals and ashes high above the clouds: the glittering coals became the flickering Stars, and the glowing ash became the Milky Way, which stands like a lit arch over the earth. Then there was light enough to walk by night.\n\nWhen the Sun rose next day behind the mountains he saw the Owl sitting and asked her: 'Who has filled the sky with coals and ashes?' The Owl answered: 'I; for it is so dark by night that no one can see to walk.' Then the Sun said: 'Do you know that you have done a great evil? Now the people will walk about by night to do evil. I made the nights so dark that people could not see their hands before their eyes, and then they had to stay at home and sleep \u2014 the day is long enough to hunt and go about, to talk and make merry; but now you have opened the door for evil. I shall strive hard to get the Moon out of the sky, so that no moonshine comes; by day I shall wipe the Stars away before me. But as for you, my sister: henceforth you shall fly about by night with starlight; for if I see you by day I shall burn you to ashes as I did the shoes. Everyone who sees you will mock you for that foolish deed. Go, out of my sight, and prowling in the night! And you shall find that the Stars will give you sadly little light \u2014 just enough for evildoers to do evil. The Stars are too small to give you warmth; so by night you will sit shivering and shaking with cold.'\n\nThe Owl flew away to hide herself; for wherever she flies by day she is mocked. And now she sits pondering what she has done \u2014 whether it was right or wrong. Where she sits she calls out 'Ho, hoa' \u2014 which in the Bushman tongue means 'uplift, uplifted' \u2014 asking herself whether she did evil by throwing the coals and ashes up into the sky or lifting them up. The Sun blinded the Owl by day; so from that day she never saw her brother the Sun again. She delights now in the Stars and the Milky Way, or Heaven-road, which she herself made. And so it came about, as the Sun foretold: from then on lion, leopard, wolf, jackal and aardvark prowl again by night to murder and rob; from then on the evildoers multiplied, for they had now light enough by night to see, while others could not make them out from afar."
    },
    functions: [
      "Origin of the stars and Milky Way as flung coals and ashes.",
      "Explains the owl's day-blindness and its 'ho-ho' cry as self-questioning.",
      "Explains night crime as the consequence of opening the dark to light."
    ],
