    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "When the seven days were passed there came along two merchants, Trapusha and Bhallika, with five hundred waggons; and following the advice of a deva they came to the Buddha and offered him food sweetened with honey. Each of the four great kings of the cardinal points brought him a bowl in which to take the food, and not wishing to offend any of them he took the four bowls and transformed them into one. Then the Buddha said to the merchants, go for a refuge to the Buddha, to the truth and to the church that will hereafter exist; whatever wish you may have made when you made me this offering, it will be granted unto you.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
