    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The founder of the Bon-pa religion was Gshen-rabs, or Gshen-rabs mi-bo, called also excellent Mahapurusha, glorious Mahapurusha, whose compassion shines forth like the rays of the sun; in his right hand he holds the iron hook of mercy, and in his left the mudra of equality; on his head is the mitra jewel. It may possibly be that this iron hook of mercy, with which Gshen-rabs fishes people out of the ocean of transmigration, has something to do with the swastika cross, which is also a hooked cross.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884), summarizing Schiefner's Bon sutra; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "In former times, as a bodhisattva, he says, I have obtained perfect freedom by walking in the way of perfect charity; and he took upon himself the task of teaching the holy law to all humanity in the ten regions of the thousand millions of continents, for which purpose he took the form of the holy white Naga Hundred-thousand. He taught the four truths of Gshen-rabs, the five perfections - charity, morality, patience, steadfastness and meditation - the five exoteric perfections, and the nine branches of the gyung-drung: in all the 142 rules of deliverance which are the foundation and root for humanity.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
