    license: "Public domain (Rockhill translation 1884)",
    summary: {
      en: "The first king of Tibet, Chiya-khri Tsenpo - Nyatri Tsenpo - is claimed in Tibetan historiography as a son of King Prasenajit of Kosala, the Buddha's lifelong friend; twelve chiefs of the tribes of Southern and Central Tibet elected him in the hope of ending the internecine wars ravaging the country. He settled in the Yarlung country and built the castle of Phyi-dbang-stag-stse, known as the Ombo-blang-gang, ruled according to law, organized an army, and was glorified by the five principal sages in records of gold and turquoises.",
      zh: "\u5410\u8543\u9996\u738B\u8076\u8D64\u8D0A\u666E\uFF0C\u85CF\u6587\u53F2\u66F8\u7A31\u5176\u70BA\u4F5B\u9640\u7D42\u8EAB\u646F\u53CB\u3001\u62D8\u85A9\u7F85\u6CE2\u65AF\u533F\u738B\u4E4B\u5B50\uFF1B\u5410\u8543\u4E2D\u90E8\u5357\u90E8\u5341\u4E8C\u90E8\u914B\u9577\u5171\u8209\u4E4B\uFF0C\u5180\u6B62\u5167\u4E82\u3002\u5B9A\u90FD\u96C5\u7931\uFF0C\u5EFA\u96CD\u5E03\u62C9\u5EB7\u57CE\u5821\uFF0C\u4F9D\u6CD5\u800C\u6CBB\uFF0C\u6574\u8ECD\u8B77\u571F\u885B\u7586\uFF0C\u4E94\u5927\u667A\u8005\u4EE5\u91D1\u8207\u7DA0\u677E\u77F3\u70BA\u4E4B\u8A18\u529F\u2014\u2014\u5982\u7D50\u7E69\u8A18\u4E8B\u4E4B\u907A\u98A8\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sacred-kingship",
      "foundational-legend",
      "miraculous-birth"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Historical-legendary material of the living Tibetan nation; paraphrase of public-domain translation."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The first king of Tibet was Chiya-khri Tsenpo, a son of King Prasenadjit of Kosala; he was elected by the twelve chiefs of the tribes of Southern and Central Tibet, who hoped by this means to put an end to the internecine wars which were ravaging the country. He took up his residence in the Yarlung country and built a castle at Phyi-dbang-stag-stse, which became known as the U-bu bla-sgang or Ombo-blang-gang; he ruled according to law and the kingdom was in happiness, and he organised an army to protect his person, to quell troubles in the country and to keep off foreign enemies.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The five principal sages glorified the king in records in gold and turquoises - a remark which seems to confirm what the Chinese say about the Tibetans making use of a species of quippus. Tibetan historians, in the reign of Ral-pa-chan, deliberately carried the genealogy of their monarchs back, if not to the Buddha himself, at least to one of his friends and protectors: as in Europe families are proud to claim descent from the Virgin Mary, so the first Tibetan monarch claims descent from Prasenajit, the life-long friend of the Buddha Gautama.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
