    license: "Public domain (Rockhill translation 1884)",
    summary: {
      en: "When the T'ang emperor Thai-tsung refused his suit, Srong-btsan sgam-po assembled a great army, marched into Szechuan and subdued every tribe allied to China - and in 641 the emperor yielded and gave him Princess Wen-ch'eng, known in Tibetan history as Kong-cho, who brought Buddhist images and taught the 'red-faced men' the ways of the Middle Kingdom. After the marriage he built a walled city and a palace on Mount Dmar-po-ri (the later Potala), abolished the painted faces, put off felt and skins for brocade and silk, sent the sons of his chiefs to the imperial schools, and introduced silkworms, mulberries, wine-making, water-mills, paper and ink - and a law-code whipping quarrelers, beheading murderers, taxing thieves eightfold, mutilating adulterers and tearing out the tongues of perjurers.",
      zh: "\u5510\u592A\u5B97\u62D2\u5A5A\uFF0C\u677E\u8D0A\u5E72\u5E03\u9042\u8D77\u5927\u8ECD\u5165\u56DB\u5DDD\uFF0C\u964D\u670D\u8AF8\u9644\u5510\u90E8\u843D\uFF1B\u8C9E\u89C0\u5341\u4E94\u5E74\uFF08641\uFF09\u5510\u5BA4\u7D42\u4EE5\u6587\u6210\u516C\u4E3B\u8A31\u5AC1\uFF0C\u85CF\u53F2\u7A31\u4E4B\u300C\u516C\u4E3B\u300D\u3002\u516C\u4E3B\u651C\u4F5B\u50CF\u5165\u85CF\uFF0C\u6559\u5316\u300C\u7D05\u9762\u4E4B\u4EBA\u300D\uFF1B\u677E\u8D0A\u5E72\u5E03\u9042\u9077\u90FD\u62C9\u85A9\uFF0C\u65BC\u7D05\u5C71\u5EFA\u5BAE\uFF08\u5373\u5F8C\u4E16\u5E03\u9054\u62C9\u5BAE\uFF09\uFF0C\u5EE2\u8D6D\u9762\u4E4B\u4FD7\uFF0C\u812B\u6C08\u88D8\u800C\u8863\u9326\u7E61\uFF0C\u9063\u8CB4\u65CF\u5B50\u5F1F\u5165\u570B\u5B78\uFF0C\u4E26\u81EA\u4E2D\u539F\u5F15\u5165\u8836\u6851\u3001\u9020\u9152\u3001\u6C34\u78E8\u3001\u7D19\u58A8\uFF0C\u7ACB\u6CD5\uFF1A\u9B25\u6BC6\u8005\u97AD\u3001\u6BBA\u4EBA\u8005\u8A85\u3001\u76DC\u8005\u516B\u500D\u511F\u3001\u6DEB\u8005\u5211\u9010\u3001\u5984\u8A9E\u8005\u62D4\u820C\u3002"
    },
    language: "Tibetan and Chinese chronicles",
    canonical_translation: "W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha, London 1884 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: "Srong-btsan sgam-po, born about 600 AD, ascended the throne of Tibet in his thirteenth year and was recognised by the neighbouring states; his rule extended over the whole of Tibet, to the north as far as Khoten and to the east to China. Thai-tsung, the second emperor of the great T'ang dynasty, desiring doubtlessly to be on amicable terms with his warlike neighbour, sent a friendly mission to Srong-btsan, who in 634 sent a return mission and requested that the emperor would give him in marriage a princess of his family. The emperor having refused, Srong-btsan got together a great army and advanced into Szechuan, subduing all the tribes which opposed him and which were allies of the Chinese; in 641 Thai-tsung granted the request and gave him in marriage the princess Wen-ch'eng of the imperial house, known in Tibetan history as Kong-cho, or 'princess'.\n\nTibetan works are unanimous in affirming that Buddhism was established in the country before the advent of Wen-ch'eng, but her influence was unquestionably very great in helping to spread it; the Tibetan historian Buston says that 'in the commencement the Chinese Tcechana were the guides of the Tibetans in Buddhism'. According to the T'ang annals it was after the marriage that Srong-btsan built a walled city and erected inside its walls a palace for her residence - the event chronicled by the Tibetans as his removing his capital to Lhasa and building the palace on Mount Dmar-po-ri. As the princess disliked their custom of painting their faces red, Srong-btsan ordered his people to put a stop to the practice; he also discarded his felt and skins, put on brocade and silk, and gradually copied Chinese civilisation, sending the children of his chiefs and rich men to request admittance into the national schools to be taught the classics.\n\nHe furthermore introduced into Tibet from China silkworms and mulberry-trees, and asked the emperor for persons knowing how to make wine, water-mills, paper and ink, all of which were sent him with the calendar. He established commercial relations with the Chinese, the Minak, Hindustan, Nepal, the Hor and Guge, and extended his rule over half of Jambudvipa. A high tribunal was established to see that all laws were respected, to keep under the arrogance of the mighty and to protect the oppressed: the authors of quarrels were whipped, the murderer was put to death, the thief was made to restore eight times the value of the stolen property, the adulterer was mutilated and exiled, liars and perjurers had their tongues torn out.\n\nThe Nepalese and Chinese princesses had no children, so the king married four other women, one of whom, called Khri-cham, belonging to one of the Mon tribes which lived among the mountains between Tibet and India, bore him a son, Gung-ri gung-tsan. Srong-btsan died leaving the child-king Mang-srong under the regency of his great minister Gar; and it was in his reign that Tibet first became known among the Chinese as Thu-fan - the name which, softened through Thub-phod, 'the able ones', may be the origin of the very word Tibet.",
      notes: "Cultural function: the marriage of Songtsen Gampo and Wen-ch'eng is the founding myth of Sino-Tibetan cultural exchange; the Jowo statue she brought remains the holiest image in Lhasa. The princess as Buddhist patroness sits beside the Nepalese Bhrikuti as one of the two 'Taras' of the king."
    },
    functions: [
      "Founding charter of the Tibetan capital: Lhasa and the Dmar-po-ri palace arise from the marriage alliance",
      "Charter of Tibetan law: the punishments of the high tribunal (whipping, beheading, eightfold restitution, mutilation, tearing out the tongue) are attributed to his reign",
      "Explains cultural transfer from China - silkworms, mulberries, wine, water-mills, paper, ink, the calendar, the schools",
      "Marks the turn from Bon to Buddhism in the royal cult through the princesses' images and the Nepalese connection"
    ],
