    tradition: "tibetan",
    category: "myth-religion",
    region: {
      en: "Lake of Khoten (Li-yul), later the city of Yudan (symbolic pin at Khotan, Xinjiang)",
      zh: "\u4E8E\u95D0\uFF08\u674E\u57DF\uFF09\u4E4B\u6E56\uFF0C\u5F8C\u70BA\u4E8E\u95D0\u57CE\uFF08\u4EE5\u65B0\u7586\u548C\u7530\u70BA\u8C61\u5FB5\u5B9A\u4F4D\uFF09"
    },
    lat: 37.11,
    lon: 79.92,
    period: "Tibetan Li-yul annals (Li-yul-gyi lo-rgyus-pa, Tengyur vol. 94), translated c. 9th century; Rockhill's English 1884",
    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884), ch. VIII, translated from the Li-yul annals of the Tengyur",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/thelifeofbuddha00rockuoft"
    },
    license: "Public domain (Rockhill translation 1884)",
    summary: {
      en: "In the days of the Buddha Kashyapa, Rishis who visited Li-yul were badly treated, and the vexed Nagas turned the dry country into a lake. When Shakyamuni visited with a great number of disciples he enveloped the whole lake in rays of light, from which came 363 water-lilies each with a lamp in its centre; the rays united into one which circled the lake three times to the right and disappeared in the water. Then the Buddha told Shariputra and Vaishravana to cut open the lake black as the Samangasarana mountain; Shariputra pierced it with the butt-end of his staff and Vaishravana with the end of his pike, and the Buddha foretold that within the space the light had encircled a great city would be built - the 363 lilies prefiguring the 363 Buddhist viharas of Khoten.",
      zh: "\u8FE6\u8449\u4F5B\u4E16\uFF0C\u4ED9\u4EBA\u81F3\u674E\u57DF\u70BA\u6C11\u6240\u4FAE\uFF0C\u9F8D\u738B\u6012\u800C\u4F7F\u9678\u5730\u5316\u70BA\u5927\u6E56\u3002\u91CB\u8FE6\u725F\u5C3C\u5055\u5927\u773E\u89AA\u81F3\uFF0C\u4EE5\u5149\u660E\u904D\u8986\u5168\u6E56\uFF0C\u5149\u4E2D\u6E67\u51FA\u4E09\u767E\u516D\u5341\u4E09\u6735\u84EE\u82B1\uFF0C\u82B1\u5FC3\u5404\u6709\u4E00\u71C8\uFF1B\u773E\u5149\u5408\u4E00\uFF0C\u7E5E\u6E56\u53F3\u65CB\u4E09\u531D\u800C\u6C92\u3002\u4F5B\u547D\u820D\u5229\u5F17\u8207\u6BD7\u6C99\u9580\u5929\u300C\u947F\u958B\u6B64\u9ED1\u5982\u5C71\u4E4B\u6E56\u300D\u2014\u2014\u5C0A\u8005\u4EE5\u6756\u5C3E\u523A\u4E4B\uFF0C\u5929\u738B\u4EE5\u77DB\u7AEF\u7834\u4E4B\u2014\u2014\u4E26\u6388\u8A18\uFF1A\u5149\u6240\u7E5E\u4E4B\u8655\uFF0C\u5C07\u5EFA\u5927\u90FD\u57CE\u4E8E\u95D0\uFF1B\u4E09\u767E\u516D\u5341\u4E09\u84EE\uFF0C\u5373\u9810\u793A\u4E09\u767E\u516D\u5341\u4E09\u5EA7\u4F3D\u85CD\u3002"
    },
    language: "Tibetan (translation from the Khotanese or Turkic)",
    canonical_translation: "W. W. Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha, London 1884 (public domain)",
    narrative: {
      en: `In the days of the Buddha Kashyapa, Li-yul was frequented by some Rishis, but they were badly treated by the people of the country, so they departed; then the Nagas were vexed, and from a dry country they converted Li-yul into a lake. When Shakyamuni was in the world he visited Li-yul in company with a great number of his disciples; the Buddha enveloped the whole of Li-yul, which was then a lake, with rays of light, and from out these rays there came 363 water-lilies, in the centre of each one of which was a lamp. Then these rays of light united into one, which circled around the lake three times, going to the right, and then disappeared in the water.

After that the Blessed One said to the Arya Shariputra and to Vaishravana, 'Cut open this lake which is as black as the Samangasarana Parvata'; then the Arya Shariputra made an opening for the lake - pierced it - with the butt-end of his staff, and Vaishravana did likewise with the end of his pike. After this the Blessed One remained for seven days for the welfare of mankind in a temple to the left-hand side of the great figure on the Gogircha mountain, where there is now a little chaitya.

While there, Ananda asked the Blessed One about what had just occurred, and the Buddha replied: from the fact that Shariputra has pierced the lake with the butt-end of his staff and Vaishravana with the end of his pike, the lake will hereafter dry up, and after my death it will be a land called Li-yul. In days to come, within the space which the light encircled three times, there will be built a great city with five towers, called U-then. The 363 lilies represented the number of Buddhist viharas which would be built in this country - an explanation which, for some unaccountable reason, the Tibetan text itself omitted, though the mountain's prodigy is preserved by Huen Thsang.

The legend belongs to a small group of Tibetan translations from the Khotanese language - the Annals of Li-yul, the Prediction of Li-yul, the Prediction of the Arhat Sanghavardhana, and the Gogringa Vyakarana preserved in the Bkah-hgyur and Bstan-hgyur - and Rockhill, editing them into chronological order and past tense, notes that these works probably reached Tibet in translation from Khotenese or Djagatai Turki. The annals supply the chronology of the prophecy's fulfilment: King Adjatasatru reigned thirty-two years, the Buddha passing away five years after his accession; from Adjatasatru to Dharmagoka there were ten generations of kings, Dharmagoka reigning fifty-four years, and two hundred and thirty-four years after the death of the Buddha there was a king of India called Dharmagoka, who in the first place had put to death many beings but had later become a righteous man through the Arhat Yasha, had confessed his sins and vowed to sin no more. At that time the lake had dried up, but Li-yul was still uninhabited - the country waiting, as the annals frame it, for the child who would be suckled by the earth to grow up and claim it.",
        "notes": "Cultural function: the Khotanese Buddhist annals recast the nation's origin as a fulfilled Buddha-prediction - the standard move Rockhill notes for countries adopting Buddhism - binding city-site, monastery-count and lake-draining to the person of the historical Buddha."`
    },
