    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Durdarga, wishing to atone for all his sins, invited a Bhikshu called Shringaka, learned in the Tripitaka, and was told that as a penance he must entertain all the Bhikshus of Jambudvipa and daily confess his sins before them; the king invited all the Bhikshus throughout India, and they, rejoicing, gathered together in Kaugambi to the number of 200,000.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "On the night of the fifteenth day of the month the Bhikshus called upon Shringaka to repeat the Pratimoksha Sutra, but he answered, what can the Pratimoksha do for you? What is the good of a looking-glass for a man whose nose and ears are cut off? Then an Arhat called Surata arose and cried with a lion's voice, why speak you thus? I am whole as the Sugata ordained - and the enraged disciple of Shringaka seized a door-bar and killed the Arhat; the Arhat's disciple killed Shringaka, and all the Bhikshus, dividing into two camps, killed each other.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "At dawn the king saw all the Bhikshus lying dead, and his eyes were obscured with tears; he rushed to the vihara calling their names and pressed their corpses to his breast, crying, alas, Tripitaka! thou didst possess the treasure of the Dharma of the Sugata! And as the shades of night were closing around the blessed law, the Trayastrimcat Devas were defeated by the Asuras and fled, and transmigrating passed among the Asandjasattva Devas.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rockhill, The Life of the Buddha (1884); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
