    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Gang is eight leagues in length and four in breadth; men, women, children, and troops are there; thou wilt have wealth, the foe will still toil on. No eagles soar above its battlements, none dreameth of such heights! There are provisions, palace, treasure, crown, and majesty, command, and throne and host. The country round about is called Bihisht, where all is pleasure, peace, and happiness.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Great War sec. 16 (Warner 1905); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Whenas Afrasiyab had heard these words they pleased him and, relying on his fortune, he entered Gang-bihisht exultingly with all his arms and implements of war. He went about the city and beheld not e'en a hand-breadth of waste ground therein; there was a palace lifted to the sky, built by himself, a king whose word was law.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Great War sec. 16 (Warner 1905); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Afrasiyab for his own part at Gang abandoned quiet, banqueting, and sleep, arranged his catapults upon the walls, and fitted up the towers to stand a siege. He bade magicians bring up mighty stones upon the walls, and he kept a troop of smiths at work to fashion claws of steel on every side and bind them to long spears to grapple any that adventured nigh, or, if not that, to make him shun the hold.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Great War sec. 17 (Warner 1905); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "afrasiyab",
      "kai-khusrau"
    ]
