    license: "Public domain (translation 1905)",
    summary: {
      en: "Afrasiyab, beholding such terror and defeat \u2014 no Jahn, no brother, no land, no throne, no realm, no gems, no treasure \u2014 farewells his throne of sovereignty: when shall I ever look on thee again upon a day of pleasure, ease, and joy? Now when he built the palace in the hold he had made a secret passage underground, and not a soldier of the army wotted that there was such a by-way underneath the castle: he made choice of ten score chiefs, and vanished by that secret souterrain, emerging he betook him to the waste, while all his kingdom wondered after him, none knowing where he was. Khusrau approached the palace, trod down his foe's star, and assumed the throne.",
      zh: "\u5821\u7834\u4E4B\u969B\uFF0C\u963F\u5F17\u62C9\u897F\u4E9E\u535C\u767B\u6A13\u4E00\u671B\u2014\u2014\u6230\u58EB\u6B7B\u50B7\u6B86\u76E1\u3001\u5144\u5F1F\u4FF1\u5931\u3001\u6C5F\u5C71\u8CA1\u5BF6\u6210\u7A7A\u2014\u2014\u4ED6\u6CE3\u5225\u5FA1\u5EA7\uFF1A\u300C\u5E7E\u6642\u624D\u80FD\u518D\u4EAB\u6B61\u6109\u4E4B\u65E5\uFF1F\u300D\u539F\u4F86\u4ED6\u7BC9\u5BAE\u6642\u9810\u7559\u4E86\u4E00\u689D\u5730\u4E0B\u5BC6\u9053\uFF0C\u5168\u8ECD\u7121\u4E00\u4EBA\u77E5\u66C9\uFF1B\u4ED6\u7387\u5169\u767E\u540D\u914B\u9577\u81EA\u5BC6\u9053\u9041\u5165\u8352\u91CE\uFF0C\u8209\u570B\u9A5A\u7591\u3001\u83AB\u77E5\u6240\u8E64\u3002\u970D\u65AF\u52DE\u5165\u57CE\u3001\u8E0F\u788E\u6575\u661F\uFF0C\u767B\u4E0A\u90A3\u7A7A\u7F6E\u7684\u738B\u5EA7\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "trickster",
      "quest-immortality",
      "sacred-place"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Epic literary source; no living community restriction."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Afrasiyab, beholding matters thus, such terror and defeat, no Jahn, no brother, no land, no throne, no realm, no gems, no treasure, cried out in very earnest, seared and sore: how wantonly heaven's vault hath dealt with us! He came down woeful from the palace-roof, farewelled his throne of sovereignty, and said: when shall I ever look on thee again upon a day of pleasure, ease, and joy?",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Great War sec. 22 (Warner 1905); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Now when he built the palace in the hold he made a secret passage underground, and not a soldier of the army wotted that there was such a by-way underneath the castle. He made choice of ten score chiefs, and vanished by that secret souterrain; emerging he betook him to the waste, while all his kingdom wondered after him, none knowing where he was because he vanished so suddenly.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Great War sec. 22 (Warner 1905); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Khusrau approached the palace, trod down his foe's star, and assumed the throne amid the paladins with golden helms. They made abundant quest, but failed to trace that chief of nobles; then the Shah inquired of Jahn and Garsiwaz about their king: how did he go and whither hath he gone? The conquering Shah said: now that my foe hath vanished from the throne his name and purpose matter not a jot.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Firdausi, Shahnama, Great War sec. 22 (Warner 1905); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "afrasiyab",
      "kai-khusrau",
      "garsiwaz"
    ]
