    period: "Recorded 1890s in Selangor; published Skeat, Malay Magic, 1900",
    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "W. W. Skeat, Malay Magic (1900), ch. I, Gabriel kills the serpent Sakatimuna",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/malaymagicbeingi00skeauoft"
    },
    license: "Public domain (1900)",
    summary: {
      en: "God's word comes in secret to Gabriel: 'Take me down the iron staff of the Creed which dangles at the gate of heaven, and kill me this serpent Sakatimuna.' Gabriel does so; the serpent breaks asunder, its head shooting above the heavens, its tail penetrating beneath the earth \u2014 and its body becomes the world: the rainbow from its sword, grass from the hair of its body, trees from the hair of its head, rain from its tears, dew from its sweat, and genii of every kind from its remains.",
      zh: "\u795E\u8AED\u5BC6\u544A\u8FE6\u4F2F\u5217\uFF1A\u300E\u53D6\u4E0B\u61F8\u65BC\u5929\u9580\u4E4B\u9435\u6756\uFF0C\u70BA\u6211\u8A85\u6BBA\u5DE8\u86C7\u85A9\u5361\u8482\u7A46\u7D0D\u3002\u300F\u8FE6\u4F2F\u5217\u5F9E\u547D\uFF1B\u86C7\u8EAB\u65B7\u88C2\u2014\u2014\u9996\u90E8\u76F4\u4E0A\u8AF8\u5929\uFF0C\u5C3E\u90E8\u4E0B\u7A7F\u5927\u5730\u2014\u2014\u5176\u8EC0\u5316\u6210\u842C\u8C61\uFF1A\u8679\u9713\u51FA\u81EA\u5176\u528D\uFF0C\u8349\u51FA\u81EA\u8EAB\u6BDB\uFF0C\u6A39\u51FA\u81EA\u982D\u9AEE\uFF0C\u96E8\u51FA\u81EA\u5176\u6DDA\uFF0C\u9732\u51FA\u81EA\u5176\u6C57\uFF0C\u7CBE\u602A\u81EA\u5176\u9918\u8EC0\u800C\u751F\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "dismembered-giant",
      "creation-chaos",
      "world-serpent"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Dismembered-primordial-being cluster (compare Purusha, Ymir, Pangu, Tiamat); Skeat notes the anthropomorphic serpent as likely an Indian Naga reminiscence \u2014 flagged as interpretive. Cross-cluster: the same serpent-name Sakatimuna appears slain by Sang Sapurba in the Malay Annals."
    },
    language: "Malay",
    canonical_translation: "Skeat, Malay Magic (1900), English",
    narrative: {
      en: "Among the cosmogonic excerpts that Skeat collected from Malay charm-books and magicians in Selangor, the most dramatic is the killing of the primordial serpent Sakatimuna \u2014 a serpent remembered (Skeat notes, citing Newbold and Leyden) as having ravaged the country of Menangkabau in Sumatra about the twelfth century, and the same monster-name that the Malay Annals gives as slain by Sang Sapurba's champion.\n\nBEGINNING: THE COMMISSION. God's word comes in secret to Gabriel: 'Take me down the iron staff of the 'Creed' which dangles at the gate of heaven, and kill me this serpent Sakatimuna.' The weapon is itself sacred \u2014 a staff inscribed with the confession of faith, hanging at heaven's gate; the snake must be killed from above.\n\nMIDDLE: THE BREAKING. Gabriel does as commanded, and the serpent 'brake asunder, the head and forepart shooting up above the heavens, and the tail part penetrating downwards beneath the earth.' The world is thus split open at the moment of the serpent's death: a being who spanned both heaven and earth is cut at the middle, and its two halves become the bounds of the upper and lower worlds.\n\nEND: THE ANATOMY OF THE WORLD. The rest of the charm describes the transformation of the serpent's parts into the things of the world \u2014 'the rainbow from the serpent's sword with its hilt and cross-piece, grass from the hair of its body, trees from the hair of its head, rain from its tears, and dew from its sweat,' while most of its anatomy turns into the good and evil genii that crowd Malay popular belief. Skeat draws out the remarkable anthropomorphism: this is 'a serpent in little more than name,' likely a reminiscence of the Indian Naga \u2014 and he compares the seated human figure with folded arms on every Malay kris hilt.\n\nFUNCTIONS. The myth (1) explains natural phenomena (rain, dew, grass, trees, the rainbow) from a slain primordial body; (2) fixes the vertical axis of the Malay cosmos (head above heaven, tail below earth); (3) peoples the spirit-world (genii from the serpent's remains); and (4) links the Malay folk cosmology to the historical dragon-slayer tradition of Menangcabow (Sang Sapurba in the Annals, with the sword of one hundred and ninety notches in the Sultan of Pagaruyung's title-list) \u2014 the same monster, slain twice: once at creation, once at the founding of kingship. The charm's geography deserves a final note: because the serpent's head shoots above the heavens and its tail below the earth, the Malay world inherits a vertical ladder along which prayer, smoke and spirit-travel all move \u2014 the same axis the Kayan tree-ladder and the Ka'bah-as-navel-tree express in their own idioms. The serpent's notional death also explains why the genii born from its remains are ambivalent: they are the leftovers of a primordial killing, neither wholly good nor wholly bad, and the charm-books' invocations address them as kin rather than strangers. The Menangcabow connection closes the loop in time as well as space: a serpent that ravaged the highlands in the twelfth century, was slain by a champion whose sword still shows the notches, and was killed once more at the beginning of the world in the charm-books' memory \u2014 the same monster met twice, once by kings and once by creation.",
      zh: "\u65AF\u51F1\u7279\u5728\u96EA\u862D\u83AA\u6240\u63A1\u4E4B\u99AC\u4F86\u7B26\u5492\u66F8\u5B87\u5B99\u8AD6\u4E2D\uFF0C\u6700\u6232\u5287\u8005\u70BA\u8A85\u6BBA\u539F\u521D\u5DE8\u86C7\u85A9\u5361\u8482\u7A46\u7D0D\u2014\u2014\u6B64\u86C7\u540D\u4EA6\u898B\u65BC\u300A\u99AC\u4F86\u7D00\u5E74\u300B\uFF08\u8607\u9580\u7B54\u81D8\u7C73\u5357\u52A0\u4FDD\u5341\u4E8C\u4E16\u7D00\u4E4B\u5BB3\u7378\uFF1B\u4E09\u8607\u723E\u5DF4\u52C7\u58EB\u6240\u8A85\u8005\u540C\u540D\uFF09\u3002\u795E\u8AED\u5BC6\u544A\u8FE6\u4F2F\u5217\u57F7\u5929\u9580\u4E4B\u9435\u6756\uFF08\u523B\u6709\u4FE1\u4EF0\u7BB4\u8A00\uFF09\u8A85\u86C7\uFF1B\u86C7\u65B7\u70BA\u5169\u622A\u2014\u2014\u9996\u4E0A\u8AF8\u5929\uFF0C\u5C3E\u4E0B\u7A7F\u5730\u2014\u2014\u4E16\u754C\u65BC\u86C7\u6B7B\u4E4B\u523B\u88AB\u5288\u958B\u3002\u86C7\u8EC0\u5316\u6210\u842C\u8C61\uFF1A\u8679\u51FA\u81EA\u528D\u3001\u8349\u51FA\u8EAB\u6BDB\u3001\u6A39\u51FA\u982D\u9AEE\u3001\u96E8\u51FA\u6DDA\u3001\u9732\u51FA\u6C57\uFF1B\u5176\u9918\u907A\u9AB8\u5316\u70BA\u5584\u60E1\u7CBE\u602A\u3002\u65AF\u51F1\u7279\u6307\u51FA\u5176\u5F37\u70C8\u64EC\u4EBA\u7279\u5FB5\uFF0C\u8B02\u8FD1\u4F3C\u5370\u5EA6\u90A3\u4F3D\u3002\u529F\u80FD\uFF1A\u91CB\u81EA\u7136\u73FE\u8C61\u3001\u7ACB\u5B87\u5B99\u7E31\u8EF8\u3001\u751F\u7CBE\u602A\u4E16\u754C\u3001\u4E26\u8207\u7C73\u5357\u52A0\u4FDD\u5C60\u9F8D\u738B\u6B0A\u50B3\u7D71\uFF08\u7D00\u5E74\u4E2D\u4E09\u8607\u723E\u5DF4\u8A85\u86C7\u3001\u5DF4\u52A0\u723E\u5BB9\u738B\u5217\u528D\u4E00\u767E\u4E5D\u5341\u7F3A\u53E3\uFF09\u76F8\u929C\u2014\u2014\u540C\u4E00\u602A\u7269\uFF0C\u6B7B\u65BC\u5275\u4E16\uFF0C\u53C8\u6B7B\u65BC\u738B\u6B0A\u4E4B\u59CB\u3002"
    },
