    license: "Public domain (kakawin narrative); paraphrase",
    summary: {
      en: "The Arjunawiwaha ('Arjuna's Wedding'), the first great Javanese court epic, opens with the gods in terror: the demon-king Niwatakawaca cannot be conquered until a mortal ascetic's power is ripened. Arjuna, resting between wars, is sent to Mount Indrakila to perform tapa \u2014 standing on one leg, taking only wind for food \u2014 until the fire of his asceticism endangers the world itself and the gods must send messengers to cool him.",
      zh: "\u300A\u963F\u5468\u90A3\u5A5A\u79AE\u300B\u2014\u2014\u722A\u54C7\u9996\u90E8\u5049\u5927\u5BAE\u5EF7\u53F2\u8A69\u2014\u2014\u958B\u7BC7\u5373\u8AF8\u795E\u60F6\u6050\uFF1A\u9B54\u738B\u5C3C\u74E6\u5854\u5361\u74E6\u67E5\u4E0D\u53EF\u514B\uFF0C\u9664\u975E\u4E00\u4F4D\u51E1\u4EBA\u82E6\u4FEE\u8005\u4E4B\u529B\u81FB\u65BC\u5713\u719F\u3002\u963F\u5468\u90A3\u65BC\u6230\u6687\u5949\u547D\u8D74\u56E0\u9640\u7F85\u5409\u62C9\u5C71\u884C\u300E\u5854\u5E15\u300F\u82E6\u4FEE\u2014\u2014\u7368\u817F\u800C\u7ACB\u3001\u50C5\u4EE5\u98A8\u70BA\u98DF\u2014\u2014\u76F4\u81F3\u5176\u82E6\u4FEE\u4E4B\u706B\u5371\u53CA\u4E16\u754C\uFF0C\u8AF8\u795E\u4E0D\u5F97\u4E0D\u9063\u4F7F\u70BA\u5176\u964D\u6EAB\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "ascension",
      "quest-immortality",
      "theophany",
      "asceticism"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Court kakawin (c. 1030 CE); composed for King Airlangga, read as his mirror. Paraphrase of the kakawin narrative."
    },
    language: "Old Javanese",
    canonical_translation: "Based on the kakawin narrative as summarized in standard editions; paraphrase only",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Arjunawiwaha, composed by Mpu Kanwa around 1030 CE for the court of King Airlangga, is the earliest fully surviving Javanese court kakawin, and it opens not with a battle but with a theology of power.\n\nBEGINNING: THE GODS' TERROR. The demon-king Niwatakawaca of Mount Mamertha has accumulated such tapa-power that no god can harm him; the gods' counselor (Indra) perceives that only a human ascetic, ripened by austerity, can match him \u2014 and only one mortal is worthy: Arjuna, the Pandawa archer, who has retired between wars. A divine mission descends to ask Arjuna to become, for the moment, a hermit.\n\nMIDDLE: THE TAPA. On Mount Indrakila Arjuna performs the full ascetic discipline: hair matted, body smeared, standing on the tips of his toes, eating nothing but wind, until his radiance scorches the worlds. The gods, alarmed that their own instrument may burn the cosmos, send first the apsaras to tempt him back into flesh and then Indra himself to test his resolve with a hunting-deity disguise and an assault of illusions; Arjuna, unmoved in both temptations, is recognized as a genuine master of desire.\n\nEND: THE MISSION ASSIGNED. Pleased, the gods grant Arjuna boons of weapons, and Indra assigns him the conquest of Niwatakawaca \u2014 with the crucial counsel that the demon's single vulnerability (his heart hidden beneath his false magic) can be learned only from within the demon's palace, by a woman planted at his side. The war of the kakawin is thus won before it is fought: the hero's ascetic power is to be spent through seduction and information rather than arrows.\n\nFUNCTIONS. The narrative (1) teaches that royal power in Java is founded on tapa (ascetic heat), the same force demons use, held under discipline; (2) models the courtier-king (Airlangga) as Arjuna \u2014 powerful, controlled, victorious through wisdom and alliance rather than raw force; (3) supplies the comparative tapa-motif (heat versus heaven) parallel to the Indian sources it adapts; and (4) anchors the whole Javanese tradition of reading epic as statecraft. The choice of Arjuna for the role is itself the kakawin's argument: the archer who has already renounced the rewards of victory in the Indian epic is here sent back into asceticism at the height of his powers, so that the poem can insist that discipline is not the refuge of the weak but the completion of strength. The Indrakila episode also fixes the kakawin's characteristic balance of Sanskrit inheritance and Javanese courtliness: the demon-king plot, the nymphs' temptation and the boons of weapons are recognizably Indian, but the temper of the telling \u2014 the emphasis on ceremony, self-command and intelligence as the marks of the victor \u2014 is the court of Airlangga looking into the epic as into a mirror. Read this way, the tapa is the poem's training manual for kings: the sovereign who cannot burn the world with his own heat has not yet earned the right to aim it at an enemy.",
      zh: "\u300A\u963F\u5468\u90A3\u5A5A\u79AE\u300B\u7531\u574E\u74E6\u65BC\u7D041030\u5E74\u70BA\u827E\u723E\u6717\u52A0\u4E4B\u671D\u6240\u4F5C\uFF0C\u70BA\u73FE\u5B58\u6700\u65E9\u722A\u54C7\u5BAE\u5EF7\u980C\u8A69\u3002\u5176\u958B\u7BC7\u4E43\u6B0A\u529B\u795E\u5B78\uFF1A\u9B54\u738B\u5C3C\u74E6\u5854\u5361\u74E6\u67E5\u82E6\u4FEE\u4E4B\u529B\u7121\u795E\u80FD\u6575\uFF1B\u552F\u51E1\u4EBA\u82E6\u4FEE\u4E4B\u71B1\u65B9\u53EF\u76F8\u6297\u2014\u2014\u800C\u552F\u4E00\u914D\u5F97\u4E0A\u8005\uFF0C\u5373\u6230\u6687\u9000\u96B1\u4E4B\u795E\u5C04\u624B\u963F\u5468\u90A3\u3002\u4ED6\u5949\u795E\u547D\u8D74\u56E0\u9640\u7F85\u5409\u62C9\u5C71\u884C\u5854\u5E15\uFF1A\u7ACB\u65BC\u8DB3\u5C16\u3001\u4EE5\u98A8\u70BA\u98DF\uFF0C\u5176\u5149\u8292\u7ADF\u707C\u71D2\u4E16\u754C\uFF1B\u8AF8\u795E\u5148\u9063\u5929\u5973\u8A98\u60D1\u3001\u5F8C\u7531\u56E0\u9640\u7F85\u89AA\u8A66\u5E7B\u8C61\uFF0C\u963F\u5468\u90A3\u4E0D\u52D5\u5982\u5C71\uFF0C\u9042\u88AB\u8A8D\u53EF\u70BA\u6B32\u671B\u4E4B\u771F\u4E3B\u3002\u795E\u4E43\u8CDC\u5176\u795E\u5175\uFF0C\u6388\u4EE5\u8A0E\u9B54\u4E4B\u547D\uFF0C\u4E26\u6388\u5BC6\u7B56\uFF1A\u9B54\u738B\u552F\u4E00\u5F31\u9EDE\u552F\u6709\u6F5B\u5165\u5176\u5BAE\u3001\u7531\u6795\u908A\u5973\u5B50\u63A2\u5F97\u3002\u529F\u80FD\uFF1A\u7ACB\u300E\u738B\u5BA4\u4E4B\u529B\u6E90\u65BC\u82E6\u4FEE\u300F\u4E4B\u722A\u54C7\u795E\u5B78\u3001\u4EE5\u827E\u723E\u6717\u52A0\u70BA\u963F\u5468\u90A3\u4E4B\u93E1\u50CF\u3001\u63D0\u4F9B\u8DE8\u6587\u5316\u4E4B\u82E6\u4FEE\u6BCD\u984C\u3001\u4E26\u5960\u5B9A\u722A\u54C7\u300E\u53F2\u8A69\u5373\u653F\u8853\u300F\u4E4B\u89E3\u7D93\u50B3\u7D71\u3002"
    },
