    period: "Javanese legend attached to the Prambanan temple (temple name recorded by Worsfold 1893); older oral tradition",
    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "W. Basil Worsfold, A Visit to Java (1893), ch. V, the temple of Loro-Jonggrang at Brambanan; Javanese oral legend",
      url: "https://archive.org/stream/avisittojavawit00worsgoog/avisittojavawit00worsgoog_djvu.txt"
    },
    license: "Public domain (1893)",
    summary: {
      en: "To win the princess Roro Jonggrang, the ogre-prince Bandung Bondowoso must build a thousand temples in one night; the demons work at supernatural speed until the princess, fearing the dawn, orders her maids to pound rice and kindle fires \u2014 the cocks crow a false morning, the last temple lacks its idol, and the cheated prince turns the deceiver herself into stone: the Durga image in the northern cell of the temple still called Loro Jonggrang, 'the maiden of beautiful form.'",
      zh: "\u70BA\u5A36\u516C\u4E3B\u62C9\u7F85\xB7\u937E\u683C\u6717\uFF0C\u7F85\u524E\u738B\u5B50\u842C\u4E39\xB7\u90A6\u591A\u6C83\u7D22\u9808\u4E00\u591C\u5EFA\u6210\u5343\u5EA7\u795E\u5EDF\uFF1B\u7FA4\u9B54\u4EE5\u5996\u901F\u65BD\u5DE5\uFF0C\u516C\u4E3B\u61FC\u9ECE\u660E\u5C07\u81F3\uFF0C\u5BC6\u4EE4\u5BAE\u5973\u8202\u7C73\u751F\u706B\u2014\u2014\u96C4\u96DE\u8AA4\u9CF4\u800C\u5047\u6668\u81F3\uFF0C\u6700\u5F8C\u4E00\u5EDF\u7121\u50CF\uFF0C\u88AB\u6B3A\u7684\u738B\u5B50\u5C07\u6B3A\u4EBA\u8005\u5316\u70BA\u77F3\uFF1A\u4ECA\u65E5\u5EDF\u5317\u6BBF\u4E2D\u90A3\u5C0A\u675C\u723E\u8FE6\u50CF\uFF0C\u4ECD\u540D\u300E\u62C9\u7F85\xB7\u937E\u683C\u6717\u300F\u2014\u2014\u300E\u7F8E\u9AD4\u4E4B\u5973\u300F\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "trickster",
      "transformation",
      "taboo-transgression",
      "idol-contest"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Best-known Central Javanese foundation legend; paraphrase of the oral cycle with the temple-name etymology from Worsfold."
    },
    language: "Javanese",
    canonical_translation: "Based on the Javanese oral legend and the temple-name note in Worsfold (1893); paraphrase only",
    narrative: {
      en: "The legend of Roro Jonggrang is Central Java's most famous etiological myth, fixed to the great Saivite temple of Prambanan \u2014 whose name, as Worsfold records, 'is simply the native name given to the particular Durga represented in the shrine, and means literally the Maiden with beautiful hips.'\n\nBEGINNING: THE WAR AND THE WOOING. In the wars between the kingdoms of Pengging and Boko, the ogre-prince Bandung Bondowoso overthrows Pengging and demands the hand of Roro Jonggrang, daughter of the defeated king, as the price of peace. She cannot refuse and cannot accept; she sets an impossible bride-price: a thousand temples to be built in a single night.\n\nMIDDLE: THE DEMONS BUILD. The prince commands his legions of ogres and spirits, and they work at supernatural speed \u2014 quarries open, walls rise, temples multiply through the dark hours toward the thousandth. As dawn approaches and the count nears its end, the princess acts: she orders the village women to pound rice in mortars and to kindle cooking fires on the eastern heights, so that the cocks, deceived by light and noise, crow before their time.\n\nEND: THE STONE MAIDEN. The demons scatter in terror at the false dawn; the work stops at nine hundred and ninety-nine temples, and the last one is left with no idol in its cell. Bandung Bondowoso, cheated, names the deception and curses his deceiver: Roro Jonggrang is turned to stone, seated forever in the shrine of the great temple \u2014 the image of Durga that the temple's own name commemorates. The thousandth temple, the people say, is the princess herself.\n\nFUNCTIONS. The legend (1) explains the skyline of Prambanan and the Durga image's name; (2) encodes the cock-crow false-dawn motif (parallel to European rooster tales) as female resistance to a forced marriage; (3) fixes the moral ambiguity at the legend's heart \u2014 the princess lies, the prince curses, and both are caught in stone and story; and (4) binds modern Javanese identity to the monuments of the Hindu past through a story told in every school and wayang performance. The legend's geography is exact: the temple whose northern cell holds the stone Durga is the great Saivite complex at Prambanan, whose central temple still carries the name Loro Jonggrang, and the bandung of the ogre-prince's name is remembered in the plain to the west. Schoolchildren and dalangs alike tell the tale with the same beats \u2014 the impossible bride-price, the supernatural work-crew, the pounding mortars and fires on the eastern hills, the cock's false crow, the curse \u2014 and the wayang stages it as a standard lakon with the ogre-legions as shadow spectacle. The story's moral balance is deliberately uncomfortable: the princess lies to save her freedom, the prince curses her for a deception his own wager invited, and both are fixed forever in the landscape \u2014 one as stone, one as the name of the ruin where the stone sits. Javanese tellers rarely resolve the discomfort; the legend keeps it, as the temple keeps the image.",
      zh: "\u62C9\u7F85\xB7\u937E\u683C\u6717\u50B3\u8AAA\u70BA\u4E2D\u722A\u54C7\u6700\u8457\u540D\u91CB\u6E90\u795E\u8A71\uFF0C\u9644\u65BC\u666E\u862D\u5DF4\u5357\u6FD5\u5A46\u5927\u795E\u5EDF\u2014\u2014\u6C83\u65AF\u798F\u5FB7\u8A18\u5176\u5EDF\u540D\u300E\u5373\u6BBF\u4E2D\u6240\u7940\u675C\u723E\u8FE6\u4E4B\u571F\u540D\uFF0C\u610F\u70BA\u7F8E\u9AD4\u4E4B\u5973\u300F\u3002\u50B3\u8AAA\uFF1A\u842C\u4E39\xB7\u90A6\u591A\u6C83\u7D22\u738B\u5B50\u7834\u5F6D\u91D1\u570B\uFF0C\u7D22\u6557\u738B\u4E4B\u5973\u62C9\u7F85\xB7\u937E\u683C\u6717\u70BA\u59BB\uFF1B\u516C\u4E3B\u65E2\u4E0D\u80FD\u62D2\u53C8\u4E0D\u80FD\u8A31\uFF0C\u4E43\u5B9A\u5929\u50F9\u5F69\u79AE\uFF1A\u4E00\u591C\u5EFA\u6210\u5343\u5EA7\u795E\u5EDF\u3002\u738B\u5B50\u9A45\u7FA4\u9B54\u4EE5\u5996\u901F\u7BC9\u5EDF\uFF0C\u9ECE\u660E\u5C07\u81F3\u3001\u5927\u529F\u5C07\u6210\u4E4B\u969B\uFF0C\u516C\u4E3B\u5BC6\u4EE4\u6751\u5A66\u8202\u7C73\u3001\u6771\u5DBA\u751F\u706B\uFF0C\u96C4\u96DE\u53D7\u6B3A\u800C\u63D0\u524D\u5831\u66C9\uFF1B\u7FA4\u9B54\u9A5A\u6563\uFF0C\u5DE5\u7A0B\u6B62\u65BC\u4E5D\u767E\u4E5D\u5341\u4E5D\u5EDF\uFF0C\u672B\u5EDF\u7121\u50CF\u3002\u738B\u5B50\u53D7\u9A19\uFF0C\u6012\u800C\u5C07\u516C\u4E3B\u5316\u70BA\u77F3\uFF0C\u6C38\u5750\u5927\u795E\u5EDF\u5317\u6BBF\u2014\u2014\u5373\u5EDF\u540D\u6240\u7D00\u4E4B\u675C\u723E\u8FE6\u50CF\u3002\u6751\u4EBA\u4E91\uFF1A\u7B2C\u4E00\u5343\u5EA7\u5EDF\uFF0C\u5C31\u662F\u516C\u4E3B\u81EA\u5DF1\u3002\u529F\u80FD\uFF1A\u91CB\u666E\u862D\u5DF4\u5357\u5EDF\u7FA4\u8207\u675C\u723E\u8FE6\u50CF\u4E4B\u540D\u3001\u4EE5\u300E\u5047\u6668\u96DE\u9CF4\u300F\u6BCD\u984C\uFF08\u53EF\u6BD4\u6B50\u6D32\u96C4\u96DE\u50B3\u8AAA\uFF09\u5BEB\u5973\u6027\u5C0D\u903C\u5A5A\u4E4B\u62B5\u6297\u3001\u523B\u756B\u50B3\u8AAA\u6838\u5FC3\u7684\u9053\u5FB7\u66D6\u6627\uFF08\u516C\u4E3B\u8A2D\u8A50\u3001\u738B\u5B50\u65BD\u5492\u3001\u4E8C\u8005\u7686\u56F0\u65BC\u77F3\u8207\u6545\u4E8B\uFF09\u3001\u4E26\u7D93\u7531\u8AB2\u5802\u8207\u54C7\u63DA\u6232\u5C07\u722A\u54C7\u8A8D\u540C\u7E6B\u65BC\u5370\u5EA6\u6559\u53E4\u8E5F\u3002"
    },
