    license: "Public domain (oral tradition); paraphrase",
    summary: {
      en: "The rejected suitor king of AntanginJagar, cursed and transformed, pays out his rage in magic: Princess Sekar Taji, lost on the wedding morning, is turned into a golden snail and cast into a river; a fisher-widow nets the snail and keeps it as a treasure, for the snail secretly cooks and cleans her hut. When the widow spies on the gift, the woman inside is caught \u2014 and the long recognition begins that will carry the princess back to her name, her ring, and her waiting Panji.",
      zh: "\u5B89\u5766\u91D1\u8CC8\u52A0\u723E\u7684\u88AB\u62D2\u6C42\u5A5A\u8005\u61F7\u6028\u5F04\u6CD5\uFF1A\u5A5A\u6668\u8D70\u5931\u7684\u745F\u5361\u723E\xB7\u9054\u5409\u516C\u4E3B\u88AB\u5316\u70BA\u91D1\u87BA\u3001\u62CB\u5165\u6CB3\u4E2D\uFF1B\u6F01\u5A66\u6492\u7DB2\u5F97\u87BA\uFF0C\u8996\u70BA\u5BF6\u7269\u6536\u85CF\u2014\u2014\u87BA\u7ADF\u6697\u81EA\u70BA\u5979\u716E\u98EF\u6383\u5C4B\u3002\u6F01\u5A66\u7ABA\u7834\u5176\u79D8\uFF0C\u87BA\u4E2D\u4EBA\u73FE\u5F62\u2014\u2014\u6F2B\u9577\u7684\u76F8\u8A8D\u4E4B\u65C5\u7531\u6B64\u958B\u59CB\uFF0C\u516C\u4E3B\u7D42\u5C07\u5C0B\u56DE\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u540D\u5B57\u3001\u6212\u6307\uFF0C\u4EE5\u53CA\u7B49\u5F85\u5979\u7684\u6F58\u57FA\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "transformation",
      "trickster",
      "foundational-legend"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Panji-cycle offshoot; the animal-bride motif attached to Java's great romance."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Denied at the wedding morning, the rejected king works his grudge into magic: Sekar Taji is transformed into a golden snail and thrown into the river, her name lost with her shape. A fisher-widow nets the shining snail and keeps it caged as treasure; unknown to her, the woman inside slips out by night to cook and sweep the hut. Discovered at last, the princess tells her name and story, and the recognition sets the whole Panji cycle's search back on its road.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Javanese Keong Emas legend; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
