    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "A person accused of a crime who asserts innocence is in some cases acquitted by solemnly swearing to it, and is sometimes obliged to go through a kind of ordeal: a cock's throat is cut, the accused puts a little rice into his mouth and wishes it may become a stone if he is guilty of the crime charged.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Marsden, History of Sumatra (1784); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "In more important instances they put a small leaden or tin image into the middle of a dish of rice garnished with musket bullets, and the man, kneeling down, prays that his crop of paddy may fail, his cattle die, and that himself may never taste salt \u2014 which is regarded as necessary to existence \u2014 if he does not declare the truth.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Marsden (1784); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
