    license: "Public domain (1784)",
    summary: {
      en: "Marsden records, from the converging testimony of English residents at Natal and Tappanooly, the old Batak custom of the prisoner's feast: Charles Miller's journal notes a man's skull hanging in the raja's hall 'placed there as a trophy, it being the skull of an enemy they had taken prisoner, whose body, according to the custom of the Battas, they had eaten about two months before' \u2014 a rite of absorbing the enemy's strength, Marsden argues, attested by old writers and recent witnesses alike.",
      zh: "\u99AC\u723E\u65AF\u767B\u64DA\u7D0D\u5854\u723E\u8207\u5854\u5E15\u52AA\u5229\u82F1\u570B\u5C45\u6C11\u4E4B\u4E00\u81F4\u8B49\u8A00\uFF0C\u8A18\u9304\u5DF4\u5854\u514B\u4FD8\u865C\u4E4B\u5BB4\u53E4\u4FD7\uFF1A\u67E5\u723E\u65AF\xB7\u7C73\u52D2\u65E5\u8A18\u898B\u62C9\u8CC8\u5EF3\u4E2D\u61F8\u4E00\u4EBA\u9871\u2014\u2014\u300E\u4F5C\u70BA\u6230\u5229\u54C1\u61F8\u65BC\u6B64\uFF1B\u6B64\u4E43\u6240\u64D2\u4E4B\u6575\u4E4B\u9871\uFF0C\u4F9D\u5DF4\u5854\u514B\u4E4B\u4FD7\uFF0C\u5176\u8EAB\u65BC\u7D04\u5169\u6708\u524D\u5DF2\u98DF\u300F\u2014\u2014\u99AC\u723E\u65AF\u767B\u8AD6\u66F0\uFF1A\u6B64\u4E43\u5438\u6536\u6575\u529B\u4E4B\u5100\uFF0C\u53E4\u66F8\u8FD1\u8B49\u7686\u76F8\u5408\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "ritual-origin",
      "cannibal-giant",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Historical ethnography of a discontinued rite; presented as eighteenth-century testimony, framed within its colonial context of reporting. No present-day community is accused."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Charles Miller, in the journal quoted by Marsden, writes: 'In the house where the raja receives strangers we saw a man's skull hanging up, which the raja told us was placed there as a trophy, it being the skull of an enemy they had taken prisoner, whose body, according to the custom of the Battas, they had eaten about two months before.'",
        note: "Paraphrase of the testimony quoted in Marsden, History of Sumatra (1784); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Marsden gathers the converging accounts of the residents of Natal and Tappanooly and concludes that the experience of later days agrees with the uniform testimony of old writers \u2014 presenting the practice as a rite of the period, framed within the colonial context of its reporting.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Marsden (1784); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
