    sensitivity_flag: "living_tradition_low",
    sensitivity_guidance: "Living Sasak royal-ritual memory; paraphrase respectfully, note the colonial narrating frame.",
    provenance: {
      record_status: "complete",
      compiled: "2026-11-15",
      compiled_by: "DHG508 fieldwork compilation",
      review_status: "draft - pending human review"
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "On the top of the mountain the Rajah fell into a trance, and the great spirit appeared to him with a face like burnished gold, and said: 'Oh Rajah! much plague and sickness and fevers are coming upon all the earth, upon men and upon horses and upon cattle; but as you and your people have obeyed me and have come up to my great mountain, I will teach you how you and all the people of Lombock may escape this plague.'",
        note: "Paraphrase of Wallace, The Malay Archipelago (1869), the Lombok kris narrative; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The great spirit commanded that twelve sacred krisses should be made, and that to make them every village and every district must send a bundle of needles \u2014 a needle for every head in the village. When any grievous disease appeared in any village, one of the sacred krisses should be sent there; and if every house had sent the right number of needles, the disease would immediately cease; but if the number had not been exact, the kris would have no virtue.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Wallace (1869); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "All made haste to collect the needles with the greatest accuracy, fearing that if one were wanting the whole village would suffer; the bundles were marked with the names of their villages and kept by the Rajah in a camphor-wood chest, and the best steelworker of Mataram forged the twelve krisses at the palace in the sight of all men. And when any sickness appeared, sometimes it went away and the kris was taken back with great honour \u2014 and sometimes it did not, and then everybody was convinced there had been a mistake in the number of needles sent.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Wallace (1869); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
