    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Pilgrimage scene in Wallace's narrative; part of the kris-myth cycle."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "On the day before that appointed for starting, all the chiefs, both great and small, came to Mataram, the abode of the king, with their horses and their servants, and the bearers of their sirih boxes, and their sleeping-mats, and their provisions; and they encamped under the tall waringin-trees that border all the roads about Mataram, and with blazing fires frightened away the ghouls and evil spirits that nightly haunt the gloomy avenues.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Wallace, The Malay Archipelago (1869); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "In the morning a great procession was formed to conduct the Rajah to the mountain: the royal princes mounted their black horses whose tails swept the ground, and all, even the Rajah, were bare-legged to above the knee, everyone attended by servants bearing his sirih and betel boxes \u2014 the king ascending to do honour to the great spirit who had bid him undertake the journey.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Wallace (1869); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
