    tradition: "indonesian",
    category: "myth",
    region: {
      en: "Minahassa peninsula, North Sulawesi",
      zh: "\u5317\u8607\u62C9\u5A01\u897F\u7C73\u7D0D\u54C8\u85A9\u534A\u5CF6"
    },
    lat: 1.5,
    lon: 124.8,
    period: "Minahassa ancestral tradition; recorded in nineteenth-twentieth century ethnography",
    source: {
      archive: "Minahassa oral tradition (recorded in colonial-era ethnography)",
      work: "The ancestral myth of Toar and Lumimu'ut (Minahassa tradition)",
      url: "https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/la-galigo"
    },
    license: "Oral tradition; paraphrase",
    summary: {
      en: "The Minahassa begin with a mother and a son: Lumimu'ut, the first woman of the land, and her son Toar, who grew to be a giant. Separated so that neither knew the other's origin, mother and son met as strangers on the mountain, wed, and their children became the first peoples of the peninsula \u2014 the sibling-creator pair of North Sulawesi, whose boundary-taboo is told before every genealogy.",
      zh: "\u7C73\u7D0D\u54C8\u85A9\u4EE5\u4E00\u5C0D\u6BCD\u5B50\u958B\u7BC7\uFF1A\u5927\u5730\u521D\u5973\u76E7\u7C73\u7A46\u7279\u8207\u5176\u5B50\u6258\u963F\u723E\u2014\u2014\u9577\u6210\u5DE8\u4EBA\u3002\u6BCD\u5B50\u76F8\u9694\u3001\u4E92\u4E0D\u77E5\u4F86\u6B77\uFF0C\u7ADF\u5728\u5C71\u4E0A\u5982\u964C\u751F\u4EBA\u76F8\u9047\u800C\u7D50\u5408\uFF1B\u5176\u5B50\u5B6B\u9042\u70BA\u534A\u5CF6\u6700\u65E9\u7684\u65CF\u7FA4\u2014\u2014\u5317\u8607\u62C9\u5A01\u897F\u4E4B\u300E\u5144\u59B9\u5275\u4E16\u5C0D\u300F\uFF0C\u5176\u7981\u5FCC\u6545\u4E8B\u5728\u6BCF\u4E00\u4EFD\u8B5C\u7CFB\u4E4B\u524D\u88AB\u8B1B\u8FF0\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "sibling-creators",
      "miraculous-birth",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Minahassa ancestry charter; the incest-recognition pair is treated at tradition level."
    },
    language: "Minahasan (Tondano/Tombulu traditions)",
    canonical_translation: "Based on the standard Minahassa ancestral account; paraphrase only",
    narrative: {
      en: "The Minahassa peoples of the northern peninsula tell their beginnings as a story of two people who did not know what they were to each other.\n\nBEGINNING: THE FIRST WOMAN AND HER SON. Lumimu'ut is the first woman of the Minahassa country; her son Toar grows in the wilds to marvellous size and strength. The two live apart, and the tale keeps them apart by a device: neither is ever told the origin of the other \u2014 the son does not know his mother, the mother does not know her grown son.\n\nMIDDLE: THE MEETING ON THE MOUNTAIN. Mother and son meet as strangers on the mountain, exchange the courtesies of two young unmarried people, and wed. Only afterwards, when the signs of kinship are told to them by the old ones \u2014 in the standard tellings, by the story of the twin stones or the staffs of Lumimu'ut \u2014 do they understand what they have done.\n\nEND: THE CHILDREN AND THE DIVISION. The children of the union are the first peoples of the peninsula; their mother, understanding now the true relation of all the houses, gathers her descendants and divides them \u2014 the division told of in the companion record of the stone of Watu Pinawetengan \u2014 and the Minahassa nations set out from the mountain each to their own lands.\n\nFUNCTIONS. The myth (1) provides the sibling-creator pair of North Sulawesi; (2) uses the not-knowing device to keep the ancestral union within the law; (3) grounds the Minahassa nations' division of the land in the mother's act of ordering; and (4) makes genealogy-telling itself the ritual that repairs the broken knowledge. The Minahassa tellings keep the recognition mechanism visible rather than mysterious: in the standard forms, the tokens that reveal the kinship are Lumimu'ut's own \u2014 the twin staffs or stones by which a mother may know her son, held apart by the same device that kept the origin untold. The not-knowing is thus presented as an institution with a purpose: the first union is lawful because the law was not yet knowable, and the knowledge that arrives afterward is converted into the division of the children, the mother ordering her descendants to their lands. The stone of Watu Pinawetengan, with its old inscriptions, is the charter's monument: the place of division where Tonsea, Tondano, Tombulu and Tontemboan received their allotments. In Minahassa memory the myth does its work at every genealogy-recitation, where telling the descent is itself the rite that repairs the first broken knowledge \u2014 the community listening to the origin it once could not be told.",
      zh: "\u5317\u8607\u62C9\u5A01\u897F\u534A\u5CF6\u7C73\u7D0D\u54C8\u85A9\u4EBA\u4EE5\u5176\u958B\u7AEF\u70BA\u4E00\u5C0D\u4E92\u4E0D\u77E5\u8EAB\u4EFD\u8005\u4E4B\u6545\u4E8B\u3002\u958B\u7BC7\uFF1A\u521D\u5973\u76E7\u7C73\u7A46\u7279\u8207\u5176\u5B50\u5728\u8352\u91CE\u4E2D\u751F\u9577\uFF0C\u6210\u70BA\u529B\u5927\u7121\u7AAE\u4E4B\u5DE8\u4EBA\uFF1B\u4E8C\u8005\u5206\u5C45\uFF0C\u4E14\u6545\u4E8B\u4EE5\u4E00\u8A2D\u8A08\u4F7F\u4E4B\u76F8\u9694\uFF1A\u8AB0\u4E5F\u4E0D\u88AB\u544A\u77E5\u5C0D\u65B9\u4E4B\u4F86\u6B77\u3002\u4E2D\u6BB5\uFF1A\u6BCD\u5B50\u5982\u964C\u751F\u4EBA\u65BC\u5C71\u4E0A\u76F8\u9047\uFF0C\u884C\u672A\u5A5A\u9752\u5E74\u4E4B\u79AE\u800C\u7D50\u5408\uFF1B\u53CA\u9577\u8005\uFF08\u6A19\u6E96\u6558\u8FF0\u4E2D\u70BA\u5B7F\u751F\u77F3\u6216\u76E7\u7C73\u7A46\u7279\u4E4B\u6756\u7684\u6545\u4E8B\uFF09\u9EDE\u660E\u89AA\u7DE3\u4E4B\u5FB5\uFF0C\u4E8C\u4EBA\u65B9\u77E5\u6240\u70BA\u3002\u7D50\u5C3E\uFF1A\u7D50\u5408\u4E4B\u5B50\u5B6B\u5373\u534A\u5CF6\u6700\u65E9\u4E4B\u65CF\u7FA4\uFF1B\u6BCD\u89AA\u65E2\u660E\u8AF8\u5BB6\u771F\u5BE6\u95DC\u4FC2\uFF0C\u4E43\u805A\u5176\u5F8C\u88D4\u800C\u5206\u4E4B\uFF08\u5176\u5206\u5730\u4E4B\u4E8B\u898B\u300E\u74E6\u5716\xB7\u76AE\u7D0D\u97CB\u6ED5\u7518\u77F3\u300F\u7BC7\uFF09\uFF1B\u7C73\u7D0D\u54C8\u85A9\u5404\u65CF\u9042\u81EA\u8056\u5C71\u800C\u51FA\uFF0C\u5404\u8D74\u5176\u571F\u3002\u529F\u80FD\uFF1A\u4F9B\u7D66\u5317\u8607\u62C9\u5A01\u897F\u4E4B\u5144\u59B9\u5275\u4E16\u5C0D\u3001\u4EE5\u300E\u4E0D\u77E5\u60C5\u300F\u7DAD\u7E6B\u7956\u6E90\u7D50\u5408\u4E4B\u5408\u6CD5\u6027\u3001\u4EE5\u6BCD\u89AA\u4E4B\u79E9\u5E8F\u884C\u70BA\u5960\u7ACB\u7C73\u7D0D\u54C8\u85A9\u6C11\u65CF\u5206\u5730\u4E4B\u61B2\u7AE0\u3001\u4E26\u4F7F\u8B5C\u7CFB\u8B1B\u8FF0\u672C\u8EAB\u6210\u70BA\u4FEE\u5FA9\u6240\u65B7\u77E5\u8B58\u4E4B\u5100\u5F0F\u3002"
    },
