    sensitivity_guidance: "Living sacred drama; paraphrase only; note that the kris-trance climax is a managed ritual event, not spectacle.",
    provenance: {
      record_status: "complete",
      compiled: "2026-11-15",
      compiled_by: "DHG508 fieldwork compilation",
      review_status: "draft - pending human review"
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The barong, the shaggy lion-dragon kept in the temple shed as a village treasure, dances against Rangda the widow-witch \u2014 fangs, pendulous breasts and the mane of white hair \u2014 whose leyak followers swarm behind her. The kris dancers of the village dance between the two powers; at the climax Rangda's power enters them and they turn the daggers on their own breasts in trance \u2014 the blades bending, unable to pierce \u2014 until incense breaks the trance and the dancers are led back to themselves. The play does not end in victory: Rangda retreats toward the graveyard, the barong stands between, and the village has tested its boundary \u2014 the destructive force met, entered, and returned outside the line where the community's protection stops.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Balinese Barong-Rangda drama; not verbatim. / Paraphrase of the Barong-Rangda drama; not verbatim. / Paraphrase of the Barong-Rangda drama; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "rangda"
    ]
