    id: "indonesian-timor-crocodile",
    title: {
      en: "The Crocodile That Became Timor",
      zh: "\u5316\u70BA\u5E1D\u6C76\u4E4B\u9C77"
    },
    tradition: "indonesian",
    category: "myth",
    region: {
      en: "Timor island",
      zh: "\u5E1D\u6C76\u5CF6"
    },
    lat: -9.3,
    lon: 124.8,
    period: "Timorese oral legend; recorded in twentieth-century collections",
    source: {
      archive: "Timorese oral tradition",
      work: "The legend of the crocodile and the boy (Timor island-origin legend)",
      url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2530/pg2530.txt"
    },
    license: "Oral tradition; paraphrase",
    summary: {
      en: "A crocodile, stranded far from the sea, is saved by a small boy who carries it back to the water; the grateful reptile promises to repay him, and years later comes for the boy \u2014 to carry him not to death but on a last voyage. The crocodile grows old swimming the seas, and when it dies its body becomes the island of Timor, shaped like a crocodile in the map; the Timorese, descendants of the boy, still call the crocodile grandfather and observe its taboos.",
      zh: "\u4E00\u9C77\u96E2\u6D77\u64F1\u6DFA\uFF0C\u70BA\u4E00\u5B69\u7AE5\u6240\u6551\uFF0C\u88AB\u80CC\u8FD4\u6C34\u4E2D\uFF1B\u611F\u6069\u7684\u9C77\u5141\u8AFE\u5831\u7B54\u3002\u591A\u5E74\u4E4B\u5F8C\uFF0C\u9C77\u4F86\u5C0B\u90A3\u5B69\u5B50\u2014\u2014\u4E0D\u662F\u7D22\u547D\uFF0C\u800C\u662F\u9080\u4ED6\u540C\u884C\u6700\u5F8C\u4E00\u7A0B\u3002\u9C77\u6E38\u904D\u5927\u6D77\uFF0C\u5782\u5782\u8001\u53BB\uFF1B\u6B7B\u5F8C\u5176\u8EAB\u5316\u70BA\u5E1D\u6C76\u5CF6\u2014\u2014\u5730\u5716\u4E0A\u8F2A\u5ED3\u5982\u9C77\u3002\u5CF6\u6C11\uFF08\u7537\u5B69\u4E4B\u5F8C\u88D4\uFF09\u81F3\u4ECA\u7A31\u9C77\u70BA\u7956\u7236\uFF0C\u5B88\u5176\u7981\u5FCC\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "transformation",
      "sacred-place",
      "origin-of-death",
      "animal-transformation",
      "helpful-animal"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Island-origin legend shared by Timorese communities on both sides of the island."
    },
    language: "Timorese",
    canonical_translation: "Based on the Timorese oral legend; paraphrase only",
    narrative: {
      en: "The best-known origin legend of Timor tells how the island itself is a relative.\n\nBEGINNING: THE RESCUE. A crocodile, caught far from the sea on dry land, is found by a small boy; instead of killing it, he carries it on his back to the water and releases it. The crocodile promises to repay the service.\n\nMIDDLE: THE CALL AND THE VOYAGE. Years pass; the crocodile returns \u2014 for the boy, now grown, is called by it. It carries him not to death but on a last voyage across the seas, as its guest and friend, and the two travel together as the crocodile grows old.\n\nEND: THE ISLAND. When the crocodile dies its vast body stays above the water and hardens into land: the island of Timor, which the map shows shaped like a crocodile, with the mountain ridges for its spines. The boy's descendants are the Timorese, who call the crocodile grandfather, ask its pardon before entering its rivers, and treat the animal as kin rather than vermin.\n\nFUNCTIONS. The legend (1) makes the island a relative, with all the obligations of kinship toward crocodiles; (2) explains the island's shape as a body; (3) polices a shared ecology (the courtesy owed to crocodiles in river and sea); and (4) supplies the comparative island-from-animal motif of the eastern archipelago. The legend's ecology is its point: Timor's rivers and coasts are crocodile country, and the myth converts a lethal neighbour into a relative with a name, a genealogy and an etiquette. Fishermen and river-crossers acknowledge the grandfather crocodile, ask pardon before entering his waters, and distinguish the crocodile that takes a man from the grandfather who would not \u2014 a legal distinction inside a kinship. The island's shape seals the reading: on the map Timor's long ridged outline suggests the crocodile floating west to east, the mountain chain for its spines, so that geography confirms genealogy. Eastern-Indonesian parallels \u2014 islands from animals, canoes turned to reefs, ancestors as sharks or serpents \u2014 surround the Timor tale, but its warmth is distinctive: the island is not a defeated monster but a dying grandfather who carried the boy's line home. The legend also carries the eastern archipelago's oldest register of obligations: the boy's rescue of the stranded crocodile is an act of pity between unequals, and the crocodile's repayment \u2014 the last voyage, the island, the descendants \u2014 converts a debt between two individuals into a standing covenant between two peoples, the boy's line and the crocodile's. Timorese ritual speech still keeps the terms of that covenant in view: the crocodile is addressed as elder, not hunted as vermin, and the tellings insist that the few crocodiles who break the peace are not the grandfather but his lawless descendants. Read as charter, the myth does what island-origin legends do \u2014 it makes the land itself the body of an ancestor \u2014 but it does so with the unusual tenderness of a rescue repaid, which is why the tale survives so uniformly on both sides of the island's colonial border.",
      zh: "\u5E1D\u6C76\u6700\u8457\u540D\u4E4B\u7531\u4F86\u50B3\u8AAA\u8B02\u6B64\u5CF6\u672C\u8EAB\u5373\u89AA\u65CF\u3002\u958B\u7BC7\uFF1A\u4E00\u9C77\u96E2\u6D77\u64F1\u65BC\u9678\u5730\uFF0C\u88AB\u5B69\u7AE5\u767C\u73FE\uFF1B\u7537\u5B69\u4E0D\u5FCD\u6BBA\u4E4B\uFF0C\u80CC\u4E4B\u8FD4\u6C34\u800C\u91CB\u3002\u9C77\u5141\u8AFE\u5831\u6069\u3002\u4E2D\u6BB5\uFF1A\u591A\u5E74\u5F8C\u9C77\u6B78\u4F86\u2014\u2014\u5524\u90A3\u5DF2\u6210\u5C11\u5E74\u7684\u7537\u5B69\uFF1B\u4E0D\u662F\u7D22\u547D\uFF0C\u800C\u662F\u9080\u4ED6\u4F5C\u5BA2\uFF0C\u540C\u904A\u6700\u5F8C\u4E00\u7A0B\uFF1B\u4EBA\u8207\u9C77\u540C\u6E38\u56DB\u6D77\uFF0C\u9C77\u6F38\u8001\u3002\u7D50\u5C3E\uFF1A\u9C77\u6B7B\uFF0C\u5176\u5DE8\u8EAB\u7559\u65BC\u6C34\u4E0A\u786C\u5316\u70BA\u9678\u2014\u2014\u5373\u5E1D\u6C76\u5CF6\uFF1B\u5730\u5716\u4E0A\u5176\u8F2A\u5ED3\u5982\u9C77\uFF0C\u5C71\u810A\u70BA\u5176\u68D8\u523A\u3002\u7537\u5B69\u4E4B\u5F8C\u88D4\u5373\u5E1D\u6C76\u4EBA\uFF0C\u81F3\u4ECA\u7A31\u9C77\u70BA\u7956\u7236\uFF0C\u5165\u6CB3\u4E0B\u6D77\u524D\u5148\u8ACB\u9C77\u6055\u7F6A\uFF0C\u8996\u4E4B\u70BA\u89AA\u800C\u975E\u5BB3\u7378\u3002\u529F\u80FD\uFF1A\u4EE5\u89AA\u5C6C\u95DC\u4FC2\u91CB\u5CF6\uFF08\u5C0D\u9C77\u8CA0\u89AA\u65CF\u4E4B\u7FA9\uFF09\u3001\u4EE5\u5C4D\u8EAB\u91CB\u5CF6\u5F62\u3001\u898F\u8A13\u4EBA\u9C77\u5171\u5C45\u4E4B\u751F\u614B\u79AE\u4FD7\u3001\u4E26\u70BA\u6771\u90E8\u7FA4\u5CF6\u300E\u52D5\u7269\u5316\u5CF6\u300F\u6BCD\u984C\u4F9B\u7D66\u5E1D\u6C76\u4E4B\u5F62\u614B\u3002"
    },
