    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "An old man, while staying in a firth to fish for salmon, lost his son, who died at some distance up the country; in his grief he could not persuade himself to leave his son's grave, and therefore put up his winter-house on the spot. In this lonely abode they were once surprised by seeing three men entering the house, one of them tall and long-nosed, the other smaller and with a flat nose, and the last of very small stature and white as snow. After passing the evening talking with the host the short-nosed man before starting asked for a piece of sole-leather, and the white one wanted a piece of walrus-tooth; the old man saw the departing visitors out, but when they left him stood dumfoundered at seeing them bounding off in the shape of a reindeer, a fox, and a hare \u2014 it is said that the hare had need of something for a new tooth.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Henry Rink, Tales and Traditions of the Eskimo (1875), tale 106 'The Visiting Animals'; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: []
