    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "And Dzunnun fled like a runaway slave, and went on board a ship fully laden, and they drew lots, and he was of the losers; and the fish swallowed him, and he was worthy of blame. Had he not been of those who glorify God, he had remained in its belly until the day when men shall be raised from the dead.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Qur'an 37:140-144 (Rodwell 1861); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "And Yunus, remember him, when he went away in anger and supposed We had no power over him; and he cried out in darkness, 'There is no God but Thou: glory be to Thee! I have done amiss.' So We heard him and delivered him from his grief, and thus do We deliver the faithful. And We cast him on the bare shore, and he was sick; and We caused a gourd-tree to grow up over him.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Qur'an 21:87-88 and 37:145-146 (Rodwell 1861); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "And We sent him on a mission to a hundred thousand men, or to a greater number; and they believed, and We gave them the enjoyment of this life for a season. Why hath there not been one city, which on its believing might have reaped the blessing of its faith? \u2014 the people of Yunus believed not until they had seen a grievous chastisement; and only Jonah was an exception.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Qur'an 10:98 (Rodwell 1861); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "yunus"
    ]
