    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Sira tradition of a living religion; paraphrased from public-domain Arabic/English sources."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The Prophet sent letters to the kings of the earth, sealed and delivered by riders: to Heraclius in Constantinople, to Khosrau the Persian, to the Negus of Abyssinia, to Muqawqis in Egypt, to the Ghassanid and the Himyarite princes. To each, the same invitation: 'In the name of God, the Merciful, the Compassionate. From Muhammad, the servant of God and His Messenger, to Heraclius the great of Byzantium: Peace to him who follows the guidance. I invite you with the invitation of Islam... If you turn away, then upon you be the sin of the peasants.'",
        note: "Paraphrase of Ibn Ishaq/Ibn Hisham, Sira (Muir 1877); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Khosrau tore the letter and said, 'A slave of my slaves writes to me?' The Prophet was told and said: 'May God tear his kingdom.' Heraclius assembled the patriarchs of his realm and asked of the claimant's lineage, his following, his wars; the traders of Quraysh answered him, and Heraclius weighed the signs. The Negus read the letter and wept, and said: 'Of a truth this is the spirit of truth.' Muqawqis sent gifts, among them two slave-girls, one of whom, Mary, became the wife of the Prophet.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Ibn Ishaq/Ibn Hisham, Sira (Muir 1877); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "muhammad"
    ]
