    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Sacred text of a living tradition; translations are interpretations, and only public-domain translations are quoted with the Arabic as primary. 'Six days' in the Qur'an (41:9-12) is compared by exegetes with the Biblical week; the Qur'an does not say God rested."
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "The Qur'an speaks of creation not as a story told once but as a set of sayings scattered through the revelation, each answering a different need. To the Meccans who worshipped many gods it says: ask yourselves who made the heavens and the earth in six days \u2014 'Ask them: is it they who created the heavens and the earth, or are they stronger in creation?' (52:35-36). The earliest account runs: 'Your Lord is God, who created the heavens and the earth in six days, and then mounted the throne; He makes the night enfold the day, pursuing it ceaselessly; the sun and the moon and the stars are made subject to His command' (7:54). A second passage gives the order: 'Say: do you disbelieve in Him who created the earth in two days, and set up equals to Him? He made therein firm mountains and blessed it, and measured therein its sustenance in four days, alike for those who ask. Then He mounted to the heaven, while it was smoke, and said to it and to the earth: come ye, willingly or unwillingly. They said: we come willingly. And He completed them seven heavens in two days, and revealed in every heaven its command; and We adorned the lowest heaven with lamps, and made it a guard' (41:9-12).\n\nThe keynote of the account is measure: the earth is a carpet spread out, the mountains are pegs driven in to keep it from shifting under the inhabitants; the heaven is raised without pillars that you can see, and the water cycle is the mercy by which the dead land is revived. The spirit is breathed into man of God's own breath; the angels are made of light, and jinn of smokeless fire; and the first man is taught the names of all things. Creation is an act of command \u2014 'Be' \u2014 not of labour: when the unbelievers taunt God with weariness after creating the heavens and the earth, the answer is that a day with the Lord is as a thousand years of your reckoning, and that weariness does not touch Him; the Qur'an says expressly that He created in six days and 'did not feel wearied' (50:38).\n\nIn the exegetical tradition the days are variously reckoned; al-Tabari reports that they are days of the unseen order, not the days of this earth; al-Biruni, the eleventh-century astronomer, discusses the question in his Chronology and notes that the six days of the Qur'an are not six solar days but six periods, so that the age of the world is a matter of the divine command rather than of the sun's circuit. The throne upon the water is a proof-text for the theologians of the divine superiority: the water existed first, beneath the throne, and the heavens and the earth were brought forth from it in the appointed measure. The seven heavens are the storeys of a tower of command, each with its own law; the lowest is adorned with lamps \u2014 the stars \u2014 and guarded against the satans who seek to listen. In the popular qisas the whole of creation is gathered into a single week of wonders, the week ending with the creation of Adam on Friday, the day of the great assembly; the theologians hold instead that the days are six periods and that their order is a mystery of the command.\n\nCulturally, the creation sayings of the Qur'an function as the ground of worship: the daily prayers turn to the East because the sun and the moon are made subject to command; the fast of Ramadan is timed by the moon because the moon is one of the measured things; and the sabbath-like rest is absent precisely because God does not rest \u2014 the world runs on His command without weariness, and the Muslim's week has no day of divine repose.",
      zh: "\u53E4\u862D\u7D93\u4EE5\u6563\u898B\u5404\u7AE0\u7684\u8A00\u8FF0\u8B1B\u5275\u9020\uFF1A\u300E\u4F60\u5011\u7684\u4E3B\u662F\u9020\u5929\u5730\u65BC\u516D\u65E5\u7684\u4E3B\uFF0C\u7136\u5F8C\u5347\u65BC\u5BF6\u5EA7\uFF1B\u591C\u8FFD\u9010\u665D\uFF0C\u7D61\u7E79\u4E0D\u7D55\uFF1B\u65E5\u6708\u661F\u8FB0\u7686\u53D7\u5176\u547D\u300F\u3002\u5730\u5982\u6BEF\u92EA\u5C55\uFF0C\u5C71\u70BA\u91D8\u93AE\uFF0C\u5929\u7121\u67F1\u800C\u5347\uFF1B\u300E\u4E3B\u9020\u4E4B\u800C\u6BEB\u7121\u5026\u610F\u300F\u3002\u4E03\u91CD\u5929\u5404\u6709\u5176\u5F8B\uFF1B\u6700\u8FD1\u4E4B\u5929\u98FE\u4EE5\u71C8\u3002\u300E\u5176\u5BF6\u5EA7\u539F\u5728\u6C34\u4E0A\u300F\u3002\u7D93\u6CE8\u5BB6\u89E3\u516D\u65E5\u70BA\u4E3B\u4E4B\u6642\u671F\uFF0C\u975E\u5730\u4E0A\u4E4B\u65E5\u3002"
    },
