    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Sacred text of a living tradition; the flood of Nuh is a cardinal narrative shared with the Biblical deluge; presented in public-domain translation."
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "Noah is the first of the great warners of the Qur'an. He is sent to his people, who worship the idols Wadd, Suwa, Yaghuth, Ya'uq and Nasr; he calls them night and day, and they put their fingers in their ears. He says: 'I am to you a plain warner: serve God and fear Him and obey me; He will forgive you your sins and respite you to an appointed term; but the term of God when it comes is not deferred \u2014 did you but know it.' The chiefs answer: 'You are no more than a man like us; you wish to turn us from what our fathers worshipped; bring the punishment you promise if you speak truth.'\n\nThen comes the command of God: 'Build the Ark under Our eyes and Our revelation, and do not speak to Me for the wrongdoers; they shall be drowned.' Noah builds in the open, and the people laugh as they pass: 'What do you build?' \u2014 'An ark; you will know.' The waters come. 'Load into it of every species two by two, and your family, except those against whom the word has gone before; and do not speak to Me for those who have done wrong.' The fountains of the great deep are burst open and the windows of heaven opened, and the earth's valleys stream together. Noah calls to his son, who had kept apart: 'Embark with us, my son, and be not with the unbelievers.' The son answers: 'I will take refuge on a mountain that will save me from the water.' 'There is no safety this day from the command of God, except for him on whom He has mercy' \u2014 and the wave passes between the two, and the son is among the drowned.\n\nThe Ark runs with them between waves like mountains. Noah calls to his son who is riding apart: 'Embark with us.' 'I will take refuge in a mountain.' 'There is no refuge today from the command of God except for him on whom He has mercy.' And the wave comes between them. When the command comes and the oven \u2014 the tannur \u2014 boils over, the sign of the end, the earth's valleys stream together: 'We opened the gates of heaven with water pouring down, and made the earth to gush forth with fountains, and the waters met, as was the matter decreed. And We bore him on a vessel of planks and nails, which ran under Our eyes \u2014 a reward for him who had been disbelieved. And We left it a sign: is there then any that will be mindful?'\n\nThe flood rises and covers all, and the Ark is carried until it rests, the tradition says, on Judi \u2014 the mountain of the north Mesopotamian border. Noah calls to his Lord: 'My Lord, my son is of my family, and Thy promise is true.' The answer is the severest lesson of the story: 'He is not of thy family; he hath wrought a work that is not right. Ask Me not for what thou knowest not; I admonish thee lest thou become one of the ignorant.' 'My Lord, I take refuge with Thee, lest I ask Thee that whereof I have no knowledge.' Then comes the end: 'Depart in peace, Nuh, and blessings on thee and on the nations that spring from those with thee; and blessings on some of them, but the rest shall be suffered to go astray.' The story closes with the formula of peace: 'Peace on Nuh among all creatures' \u2014 and with the promise that this is a warning to the world: the flood is both judgement and renewal, the washing of the earth for a new people.\n\nCulturally the story stands behind the Qur'anic doctrine of the messengers: every prophet tells his people the same three things \u2014 worship God alone, fear the Day, follow the guide \u2014 and every people answers with the same refusal. The flood of Nuh is the archetype of the destroyed generations (Aad, Thamud, Lut's people, the people of Shu'ayb), and its remembrance is built into the calendar of Muslim practice: the fast of Ashura, the tenth of Muharram, is kept in the tradition in memory of the day the Ark came to rest.",
      zh: "\u52AA\u54C8\u662F\u53E4\u862D\u7D93\u7B2C\u4E00\u4F4D\u5927\u8B66\u8AA1\u8005\u3002\u5176\u6C11\u62DC\u74E6\u5FB7\u3001\u7D20\u74E6\u3001\u8449\u5DEB\u65AF\u3001\u8449\u6B50\u514B\u8207\u5948\u65AF\u723E\uFF1B\u4ED6\u65E5\u591C\u547C\u559A\uFF0C\u4ED6\u5011\u4EE5\u6307\u585E\u8033\u3002\u4E3B\u8AED\uFF1A\u300E\u9020\u821F\u65BC\u6211\u9452\u8207\u6211\u555F\u793A\u4E4B\u4E0B\uFF1B\u52FF\u70BA\u4E0D\u7FA9\u8005\u4EE3\u6C42\uFF0C\u4ED6\u5011\u5FC5\u88AB\u6DF9\u6B7B\u3002\u300F\u773E\u4EBA\u8B4F\u7B11\uFF1B\u821F\u6210\uFF0C\u300E\u5404\u985E\u6210\u96D9\u800C\u8F09\uFF0C\u4F60\u7684\u5BB6\u7737\u2014\u2014\u60DF\u6B64\u524D\u5DF2\u88AB\u5224\u6C7A\u8005\u9664\u5916\u300F\u3002\u5927\u6DF5\u6CC9\u6E90\u88C2\uFF0C\u5929\u9580\u958B\uFF0C\u5C71\u8C37\u5954\u6D41\u3002\u5176\u5B50\u62D2\u767B\uFF1A\u300E\u6211\u5C07\u907F\u65BC\u4E00\u5C71\u300F\u2014\u2014\u300E\u4ECA\u65E5\u4E3B\u4E4B\u547D\u4EE4\uFF0C\u975E\u8499\u6148\u8005\u7121\u4EBA\u53EF\u907F\u300F\u2014\u2014\u5DE8\u6D6A\u7D93\u65BC\u7236\u5B50\u4E4B\u9593\u3002\u821F\u8F09\u65BC\u6CE2\u5CF0\u5982\u5C71\u4E4B\u9593\uFF0C\u505C\u65BC\u6731\u8FEA\u5C71\u3002\u52AA\u54C8\u6C42\u5176\u5B50\uFF1B\u4E3B\u8AED\uFF1A\u300E\u4ED6\u975E\u4F60\u7684\u5BB6\u7737\uFF1B\u52FF\u554F\u4F60\u4E0D\u77E5\u9053\u7684\u4E8B\u3002\u300F\u7D50\u8A9E\uFF1A\u300E\u5E73\u5B89\u6B78\u65BC\u52AA\u54C8\uFF0C\u5728\u773E\u4E16\u754C\u4E4B\u9593\u3002\u300F\u963F\u8212\u62C9\u65E5\u4E4B\u9F4B\uFF0C\u76F8\u50B3\u7D00\u5FF5\u65B9\u821F\u9760\u5CB8\u4E4B\u65E5\u3002"
    },
