    license: "Arabic original public domain (9th century CE); original English paraphrase",
    summary: {
      en: "Carried by Buraq from the Kaaba to 'the farthest mosque' of Jerusalem, Muhammad leads the prophets in prayer; then from the Rock he is taken up through the heavens: he meets Adam, Yahya and Isa, Yusuf, Idris, Harun, Musa and Ibrahim; sees Paradise and Hell; receives the command of fifty prayers, negotiated down to five. In the morning he tells his story in Mecca; Abu Bakr believes him at once, and the mosque of Jerusalem and the five prayers are the legacy of the night.",
      zh: "\u4E58\u5E03\u62C9\u514B\u7531\u5929\u623F\u81F3\u8036\u8DEF\u6492\u51B7\u300E\u9060\u5BFA\u300F\uFF1B\u5148\u77E5\u7387\u773E\u5148\u77E5\u79AE\u62DC\uFF1B\u5F8C\u81EA\u78D0\u77F3\u767B\u4E03\u91CD\u5929\uFF1A\u9047\u963F\u4E39\u3001\u8449\u6D77\u96C5\u8207\u723E\u6492\u3001\u512A\u7D20\u798F\u3001\u6613\u5FB7\u91CC\u65AF\u3001\u54C8\u502B\u3001\u7A46\u85A9\u8207\u6613\u535C\u62C9\u6B23\uFF1B\u898B\u6A02\u5712\u8207\u706B\u7344\uFF1B\u53D7\u4E94\u5341\u62DC\u4E4B\u547D\uFF0C\u7D93\u6298\u8877\u5B9A\u70BA\u4E94\u62DC\u3002\u6668\u8FD4\u9EA5\u52A0\u8FF0\u5176\u4E8B\uFF1B\u963F\u5E03\xB7\u4F2F\u514B\u723E\u5373\u523B\u76F8\u4FE1\uFF1B\u8036\u8DEF\u6492\u51B7\u4E4B\u5BFA\u8207\u4E94\u756A\u62DC\u4E43\u90A3\u4E00\u591C\u4E4B\u907A\u7522\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "ascension",
      "prophetic-vision",
      "theophany",
      "prayer"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Canonical hadith of a living tradition; the Night Journey is a cardinal miracle. Presented as a paraphrase of the Arabic original; no doctrinal commentary."
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "In the year of sorrows, after the deaths of Khadija and Abu Talib, the Prophet was taken by night from the Sacred Mosque of Mecca to 'the farthest mosque' \u2014 al-Aqsa, in Jerusalem \u2014 on a white steed, Buraq, 'long as a mule and higher than a donkey, whose stride reached as far as the eye could see.' At Jerusalem he tethered Buraq at the Rock, and led the prophets of God in prayer: the meeting of the envoys of the old covenant with the Seal.\n\nThen he was taken up. From the first heaven, guarded by an angel, he met Adam; in the second, Yahya and Isa, cousins and relatives of the Word; in the third, Yusuf, given half of beauty; in the fourth, Idris; in the fifth, Harun with his white beard; in the sixth, Musa, weeping \u2014 because, he said, a young man will enter Paradise after me, and more of his people than of mine; in the seventh, Ibrahim, sitting with his back against the Bait al-Ma'mur, the House of the Frequented, where seventy thousand angels pray every day and do not return. And above the seventh heaven he saw Sidrat al-Muntaha, the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary, whose fruits are like great jars and its leaves like elephants' ears; and beyond it the divine majesty: 'And I was given three things: the five prayers, the last verses of Surah al-Baqarah, and the pardon of my people who die not joining aught with God.'\n\nThe command that came down was fifty prayers a day. On the way back Musa stopped him: 'Your people will not bear it; go back and ask for less.' Between the Throne and the Prophet the toll went down to five, and Musa still said, 'Go back'; but the Prophet said: 'I am ashamed before God; I will not go back again.' Then a voice said: 'Five prayers, and they are as fifty: the word is not changed before Me.'\n\nIn the morning he told the story in Mecca, and the Quraysh mocked: a caravan returns from Syria in ten days, and Muhammad says he went to Jerusalem by night and back before dawn. Abu Bakr answered at once: 'If he says so, it is true' \u2014 and was called al-Siddiq, the Verifier. The tradition adds the answer of the Prophet: as he rode, caravans passed him; he told the Quraysh where they were, and the news, checked, confirmed him. The legacy of the night is twofold: the five daily prayers, whose number was settled in the ascent, and the sanctity of Jerusalem, whose farthest mosque is the qibla of the first years and the third sanctuary of Islam.\n\nThe Isra and Mi'raj stand in the cross-tradition company of the seer's ascensions \u2014 Ezekiel's chariot-throne, Arda Viraf's tour of heaven and hell, Er's vision at the end of Plato's Republic \u2014 but with its own centre: the Prophet does not see his own soul's fate; he intercedes, and the number of prayers is the outcome. In Sufism the Mi'raj becomes the model of the mystic's own ascent; in popular devotion the 27th of Rajab is kept as the Night of the Ascension; and in the Qur'an the night is covered by a single verse \u2014 'Glory to Him who carried His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose precincts We have blessed, to show him of Our signs' (17:1) \u2014 with the ascent alluded to in the Sura of the Star: 'He saw him in another descent, at the Lote-Tree of the Boundary, near which is the Garden of Refuge.'",
      zh: "\u6182\u50B7\u4E4B\u5E74\uFF0C\u5148\u77E5\u591C\u9593\u4E58\u767D\u9A38\u5E03\u62C9\u514B\u2014\u2014\u300E\u5176\u5954\u99B3\u53CA\u76EE\u529B\u6240\u53CA\u300F\u2014\u2014\u81EA\u9EA5\u52A0\u7981\u5BFA\u81F3\u8036\u8DEF\u6492\u51B7\u9060\u5BFA\u3002\u7E6B\u9A42\u65BC\u78D0\u77F3\uFF0C\u7387\u773E\u5148\u77E5\u79AE\u62DC\u3002\u96A8\u5F8C\u767B\u9704\uFF1A\u7B2C\u4E00\u5929\u9047\u963F\u4E39\uFF1B\u7B2C\u4E8C\u5929\u9047\u8449\u6D77\u96C5\u8207\u723E\u6492\uFF1B\u7B2C\u4E09\u5929\u9047\u512A\u7D20\u798F\uFF1B\u7B2C\u56DB\u5929\u9047\u6613\u5FB7\u91CC\u65AF\uFF1B\u7B2C\u4E94\u5929\u9047\u54C8\u502B\uFF1B\u7B2C\u516D\u5929\u9047\u7A46\u85A9\u2014\u2014\u56E0\u898B\u5176\u5F8C\u9032\u6A02\u5712\u8005\u66F4\u591A\u800C\u6CE3\uFF1B\u7B2C\u4E03\u5929\u9047\u6613\u535C\u62C9\u6B23\uFF0C\u501A\u65BC\u65E5\u65E5\u6709\u4E03\u842C\u5929\u4F7F\u79AE\u62DC\u7684\u300E\u5E38\u8A2A\u4E4B\u6BBF\u300F\u3002\u5176\u4E0A\u898B\u6975\u5883\u4E4B\u9178\u68D7\u6A39\uFF0C\u679C\u5982\u5927\u7515\u3001\u8449\u5982\u8C61\u8033\uFF1B\u53D7\u8CDC\u4E09\u4E8B\uFF1A\u4E94\u756A\u62DC\u3001\u9EC3\u725B\u7AE0\u672B\u6578\u7BC0\u3001\u53CA\u4E0D\u4EE5\u7269\u914D\u4E3B\u800C\u4EA1\u8005\u7684\u8D66\u5BA5\u3002\u964D\u547D\u4E94\u5341\u62DC\uFF1B\u7A46\u85A9\u5C62\u52F8\u6C42\u6E1B\uFF0C\u81F3\u4E94\u62DC\u800C\u5148\u77E5\u8A00\uFF1A\u300E\u6211\u65BC\u4E3B\u524D\u7F9E\u6127\uFF0C\u4E0D\u5FA9\u5F80\u77E3\u3002\u300F\u6709\u8072\u66F0\uFF1A\u300E\u4E94\u62DC\u540C\u65BC\u4E94\u5341\u62DC\uFF0C\u4E3B\u524D\u4E4B\u8A00\u4E0D\u8B8A\u3002\u300F\u6668\u8FD4\u9EA5\u52A0\u8FF0\u4E4B\uFF0C\u53E4\u840A\u6C0F\u8B4F\u4E4B\uFF1B\u963F\u5E03\xB7\u4F2F\u514B\u723E\u5373\u4FE1\uFF0C\u88AB\u7A31\u70BA\u300E\u8AA0\u4FE1\u8005\u300F\u3002\u90A3\u4E00\u591C\u7684\u907A\u7522\uFF1A\u4E94\u756A\u62DC\u8207\u8036\u8DEF\u6492\u51B7\u7684\u8056\u6240\u5730\u4F4D\u3002"
    },
