    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Early Islamic historiography; the first caliph slain by Muslims is treated with restraint in all traditions."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The malcontents gathered in Egypt and Kufa marched to Medina and besieged the house of Uthman. Ali sent his sons to keep the door, and blood was shed before it. The rebels broke in at last; and the old caliph, seated with the Book before him, was struck, and his blood ran over the page where he had read: 'They will say, art thou the one whom God and His Messenger blessed? \u2014 and God will suffice thee against the mockers.'",
        note: "Paraphrase of al-Tabari via Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 17; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "It was the copy of the Qur'an which his own commission had gathered that lay before him, and the tale is told that he read to the end of the verse. His body was buried at night by his kin; Ali washed his hands, saying, 'God knows that I was innocent of his blood.' The Caliphate of Medina had seen its first civil blood, and the wound of the community was opened.",
        note: "Paraphrase of al-Tabari via Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 17; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "uthman",
      "ali"
    ]
