    period: "Event 711 CE; English: W. Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 37 (Spanish conquest); S. Ockley (1890)",
    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "William Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 37; Simon Ockley, History of the Saracens (1890)",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/caliphateitsrise00muir"
    },
    license: "Public domain (English editions 1890-91; Arabic originals 10th-11th centuries)",
    summary: {
      en: "Tariq ibn Ziyad crosses from Morocco to the rock that bears his name with seven thousand Berbers, and burns the boats: 'Where can you flee? The sea is behind you, the enemy in front.' Roderick the Visigoth comes against him with the hosts of Spain; the battle lasts a week, and the Visigothic kingdom falls. The rock keeps the commander's name; and Spain passes to the Muslims for eight hundred years.",
      zh: "\u5854\u91CC\u514B\xB7\u672C\xB7\u9F4A\u4E9E\u5FB7\u7387\u4E03\u5343\u67CF\u67CF\u723E\u4EBA\u81EA\u6469\u6D1B\u54E5\u6E21\u81F3\u4EE5\u4ED6\u70BA\u540D\u7684\u5CA9\u5730\uFF0C\u4E26\u711A\u5176\u821F\uFF1A\u300E\u4F60\u5011\u80FD\u9003\u5F80\u4F55\u8655\uFF1F\u6D77\u5728\u4F60\u8EAB\u5F8C\uFF0C\u6575\u5728\u4F60\u9762\u524D\u3002\u300F\u897F\u54E5\u7279\u738B\u7F85\u5FB7\u91CC\u514B\u7387\u897F\u73ED\u7259\u5927\u8ECD\u8FCE\u64CA\uFF1B\u6230\u4E8B\u6301\u7E8C\u4E00\u9031\uFF0C\u897F\u54E5\u7279\u738B\u570B\u50BE\u8986\u3002\u5CA9\u7559\u7D71\u5E25\u4E4B\u540D\uFF1B\u897F\u73ED\u7259\u6B78\u7A46\u65AF\u6797\u516B\u767E\u5E74\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "foundational-legend",
      "sacred-kingship",
      "culture-hero"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Andalusi historiography as received in Muir/Ockley from Arabic sources."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed from Morocco to the great rock of the strait with seven thousand Berbers, and burned the boats behind him. He spoke to them, in the speech the historians have kept: 'Whither can you flee? The sea is behind you, the enemy before you; you have nothing left but truth and courage.' And the rock took his name \u2014 Jabal Tariq, Gibraltar.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Arabic accounts via Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 37; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Roderick, the last Visigothic king, came against him with the hosts of Spain. The battle lasted a week on the river of the south; the king fled or was drowned in the Guadalquivir, and his kingdom fell with him. The cities of Spain opened their gates one after another, and for eight hundred years the land of the Visigoths was the land of the Muslims, and its capital became Cordoba.",
        note: "Paraphrase of the Arabic accounts via Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 37; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
