    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "The caliph Abd al-Malik sent al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf to Kufa without an army. He rode into the mosque hooded, on a mule, and sat; then he unmasked and spoke: 'I am he who trims the feather and sharpens the point; I am the punishment of God among you.' And he governed the city and then the East by terror for ten years, it is said, and slew more men than the wars of his time had taken.",
        note: "Paraphrase of al-Tabari via Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 31; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The anecdote lore keeps his sayings: that he corrected the reading of the mosque by the rod; that he called the men of Kufa 'the dregs of the Arabs'; and that at his death the caliph said of him: 'I raised him when he was light, and he made himself heavy.'",
        note: "Paraphrase of al-Tabari via Muir, The Caliphate (1891), ch. 31; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
