    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "A king set his three sons to learn trades. One learned archery, and became the finest bowman of the age; one learned to make arrows, and his wares filled the market; and the third learned to heal the bodies and souls of men. War came, and the archer's art stood idle, and the arrow-maker's stock was seized for the king's men; only the healer was wanted, in war and in peace alike.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rumi, Masnavi (Whinfield 1887), Book 1, 'The King and his Three Sons'; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Rumi's moral is of the science of the saints: the trade that is never dearer for need nor cheaper for peace \u2014 the healing of the soul, which every age requires, and which no misfortune can take from the man who has learned it.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Rumi, Masnavi (Whinfield 1887), Book 1; not verbatim."
      }
    ],
