    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Islamicate folk-tale of the Nights; the multi-narrator cycle is the Nights' signature device; paraphrased from the public-domain Burton translation."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "A hunchback jester chokes on a fishbone at a tailor's supper, and each host of the night believes himself the murderer \u2014 the tailor, the Christian broker, the Muslim steward, the Jewish physician of the town. Each buys off the dawn with the best story he can tell, and the tales are told in turn to the governor, who spares each teller for the sake of the tale.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Burton, Thousand Nights and a Night, vol. 1 (1885), 'Tale of the Hunchback'; not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "The barber of Kashgar, last of the tellers, and the talker of six brothers, is brought in; he examines the dead man, feels his gullet, and draws out the bone with the instruments of his trade \u2014 and the hunchback revives. The tale is the great example of the story-cycle: five tales hung on one corpse, and a corpse that is not dead.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Burton, Thousand Nights and a Night, vol. 1 (1885); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
