    lat: 33,
    lon: 40,
    period: "Qisas al-Anbiya tradition (Arabic, c. 8th-11th centuries CE); paraphrase from Arabic",
    source: {
      archive: "Wikisource (Arabic; al-Kisa\u2019i, Qisas al-Anbiya, Leiden ed. 1922)",
      work: "Qisas al-Anbiya / tafsir legends (al-Tabari, al-Kisa'i); passages paraphrased from Arabic",
      url: "https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/%D9%82%D8%B5%D8%B5_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%86%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D9%84%D9%84%D9%83%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%A6%D9%8A"
    },
    license: "Arabic original public domain; paraphrase original",
    summary: {
      en: "The legend fills the motive of the first murder: Adam's children were born in twin pairs, boy and girl, and the law of the time was that each brother should marry his own twin-sister from another pair \u2014 not his own. Abel's twin was the fairer; Cain refused to take his own and demanded his brother's; Adam bade them offer a sacrifice, and the fire from heaven decided. Cain slew, and the first city was built on the first grave.",
      zh: "\u50B3\u8AAA\u88DC\u8DB3\u9996\u5B97\u8B00\u6BBA\u4E4B\u52D5\u6A5F\uFF1A\u963F\u4E39\u5B50\u5973\u7686\u70BA\u96D9\u751F\u2014\u2014\u4E00\u7537\u4E00\u5973\uFF1B\u7576\u6642\u4E4B\u6CD5\uFF0C\u5404\u5144\u5F1F\u7576\u5A36\u53E6\u4E00\u5C0D\u7684\u96D9\u751F\u59CA\u59B9\uFF0C\u800C\u975E\u81EA\u5DF1\u7684\u3002\u4E9E\u4F2F\u7684\u96D9\u751F\u59B9\u8C8C\u7F8E\uFF1B\u8A72\u96B1\u62D2\u5A36\u5DF1\u59B9\u800C\u6C42\u5176\u5F1F\u4E4B\u59B9\uFF1B\u963F\u4E39\u547D\u4E8C\u4EBA\u737B\u796D\uFF0C\u5929\u706B\u5B9A\u596A\u3002\u8A72\u96B1\u6BBA\u4E4B\uFF1B\u9996\u5EA7\u57CE\u7BC9\u65BC\u9996\u500B\u58B3\u5893\u4E4B\u4E0A\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "origin-of-death",
      "taboo-transgression",
      "sibling-creators"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Qisas legend of a living tradition; the twin-marriage motif parallels origin-of-death and sibling clusters; paraphrased from the Arabic original; motif tagging is interpretive."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Adam's children, the legend says, were born in twin pairs, boy and girl together; and the law of that first age was that each brother should marry his twin-sister from another pair, but not his own. Abel's twin was the fairer of the two; and Cain refused to take his own sister and demanded his brother's.",
        note: "Paraphrase of al-Tabari, Tarikh (Arabic); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Adam bade them settle it by an offering: each to bring a sacrifice, and the fire from heaven to take the acceptable one. The fire came down on Abel's lamb and left Cain's sheaf; and Cain said to his brother, 'I will slay thee.' And he slew him \u2014 and the legend adds that the first city of the world was built by the line of Cain on the first grave of the world.",
        note: "Paraphrase of al-Tabari, Tarikh (Arabic); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "adam"
    ]
