    summary: {
      en: "Lieh Tz\u016D asks Kuan Yin how the perfect man can walk through solid bodies unobstructed, pass through fire unburnt, and scale the highest heights without fear. Kuan Yin answers that he is in a condition of absolute purity \u2014 man can attain to formlessness and vanquish death \u2014 and tells the parable of the drunken man who falls out of a cart and does not die, for his spirit is in a condition of security; and if such security is to be got from wine, how much more from God.",
      zh: "\u5217\u5B50\u554F\u95DC\u5C39\u66F0\uFF1A\u81F3\u4EBA\u6F5B\u884C\u4E0D\u7A92\uFF0C\u8E48\u706B\u4E0D\u71B1\uFF0C\u884C\u4E4E\u842C\u7269\u4E4B\u4E0A\u800C\u4E0D\u6144\u2014\u2014\u662F\u7D14\u6C23\u4E4B\u5B88\u4E5F\uFF0C\u975E\u77E5\u5DE7\u679C\u6562\u4E4B\u5217\u3002\u9189\u8005\u4E4B\u589C\u8ECA\uFF0C\u96D6\u75BE\u4E0D\u6B7B\uFF1B\u9AA8\u7BC0\u8207\u4EBA\u540C\uFF0C\u800C\u72AF\u5BB3\u8207\u4EBA\u7570\uFF0C\u5176\u795E\u5168\u4E5F\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "quest-immortality",
      "transformation",
      "parable-teaching",
      "primeval-waters",
      "bodhisattva"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Canonical text of the Taoist canon (Nanhua zhenjing); the invulnerability passage is the charter of later Daoist immortality-arts claims. Included as a comparative text."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: 'Lieh Tz\u016D asked Kuan Yin, saying, "The perfect man can walk through solid bodies without obstruction. He can pass through fire without being burnt. He can scale the highest heights without fear. How does he bring himself to this?" "It is because he is in a condition of absolute purity," replied Kuan Yin. "It is not cunning which enables him to dare such feats. Be seated, and I will tell you.'
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: '"All that has form, sound, and colour, may be classed under the head thing. Man differs so much from the rest, and stands at the head of all things, simply because the latter are but what they appear and nothing more. But man can attain to formlessness and vanquish death. And with that which is in possession of the eternal, how can mere things compare?'
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: `"A drunken man who falls out of a cart, though he may suffer, does not die. His bones are the same as other people's; but he meets his accident in a different way. His spirit is in a condition of security. He is not conscious of riding in the cart; neither is he conscious of falling out of it. Ideas of life, death, fear, etc., cannot penetrate his breast; and so he does not suffer from contact with objective existences. And if such security is to be got from wine, how much more is it to be got from God. It is in God that the Sage seeks his refuge, and so he is free from harm."`
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "liezi"
    ]
