    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell, vol. 4 (trans. Chalmers, 1901)",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/complete-cowell-jataka-six-volumes-in-one"
    },
    license: "Public domain (translation 1901)",
    summary: {
      en: "Ummadant\u012B, of more than mortal beauty won by a half-robe given to a monk, is branded a witch by the dazed royal soothsayers and married to the commander Ahip\u0101raka instead. At the Kattika festival she scatters flowers on the passing king, who loses his wits; Ahip\u0101raka stages a hollow-tree oracle to spare him sin, and then, offering her as a free gift, refuses the king's relief until the king renounces the wrong\u2014not for all the world would he do injustice\u2014and so is cured of his infatuation.",
      zh: "\u9B31\u6469\u9054\u5973\u56E0\u5E03\u65BD\u534A\u8863\u8207\u6BD4\u4E18\u800C\u5F97\u7D55\u4E16\u5BB9\u8C8C\uFF1B\u76F8\u58EB\u5011\u898B\u4E4B\u5931\u5E38\uFF0C\u8AA3\u70BA\u5996\u5A66\uFF0C\u9042\u5AC1\u7D71\u5E25\u963F\u7F85\u6CE2\u7F85\u8FE6\u3002\u7BC0\u6176\u4E4B\u591C\u5979\u5411\u5DE1\u904A\u4E4B\u738B\u64F2\u82B1\uFF0C\u738B\u9042\u795E\u9B42\u985B\u5012\u3002\u7D71\u5E25\u8A2D\u7A7A\u5FC3\u6A39\u795E\u8AED\u4E4B\u8A08\u4EE5\u8B77\u738B\u514D\u7F6A\uFF0C\u4E26\u4EE5\u59BB\u76F8\u8D08\uFF1B\u738B\u8A00\u300C\u7E31\u5F97\u5168\u4E16\uFF0C\u4E0D\u884C\u4E0D\u7FA9\u300D\uFF0C\u7D42\u65B7\u75F4\u5FF5\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "trickster",
      "oracle-divination",
      "sacred-kingship",
      "miracle-cure"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Canonical Jataka of a living tradition; retold freely; no ritual secrecy."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: `The Master told this of a backsliding Brother who, pierced with love's shafts for a woman seen on his alms round, wasted to a wild-deer leanness and gave up instruction and meditation. Of old, in Aritthapura in the kingdom of the Sivis, king Sivi made over his realm to his son, prince Sivi, who made his friend Ahip\u0101raka commander-in-chief. In the same city dwelt the merchant Tiritavaccha, worth eighty crores, whose daughter Ummadant\u012B was of more than mortal loveliness: all worldlings who beheld her were intoxicated as with strong drink. Sent for by the king to be tested by the fortune-tellers, she so unhinged the brahmins that some put their morsels on their heads and some threw them at the wall; furious, they told the king she was a witch, and she was not sent for\u2014and she conceived a grudge, saying, "Should I ever see the king, I shall know what to do." Her father gave her to Ahip\u0101raka, her husband's darling and delight.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 527 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: `Her beauty was the fruit of an old merit: once, a poor girl of Benares, laboring three years for a scarlet robe, met by the river a monk of Buddha Kassapa robbed of his garments and dressed in broken boughs; she tore her new robe in two and gave him half, then, seeing him blaze like the newly-risen sun, gave him the other half too, praying, "May I in some future existence be of such surpassing beauty that no one who sees me may have power to control himself, and no other woman be more beautiful." Born now in Aritthapura, at the Kattika festival her husband warned her not to show herself to the king; but when the king's chariot came first to their door in the moonlit procession, she stood at the window and threw flowers over him with all the charm of a sylph. The king, maddened with passion, failed even to recognise the house, asked his charioteer whose it was and whether the lady were unwed, and turning back from the festival lay on his couch chattering of Ummadant\u012B: "I would be Ahip\u0101raka one night or haply twain, and Ummadant\u012B thus enjoyed, he might o'er Sivi reign."`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 527 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: 'Told that the king had gone only to his door, Ummadant\u012B called him a pot-bellied fellow with huge teeth, and Ahip\u0101raka saw that if the king did not get her he would die. "My duty is to restore him to life, if it can be done without sin on king or me." So he schooled a stout knave to sit within a hollow tree, a sacred shrine, and when the general made his offering and prayed to know why the king lay chattering, the knave delivered the lesson: your king is not sick but infatuated with your wife Ummadant\u012B; if he gets her he will live, otherwise he will die. Repeating this as from the yakkha of the shrine, the servant secured the purpose, and Ahip\u0101raka told the king the strange thing the yakkha had said. The king, shamed that his vileness was known to the very yakkhas, took his stand in righteousness\u2014"Fallen from grace no godhead shall I win, and all the world will hear of my great sin"\u2014and the two, in alternate stanzas, the general offering her as a free gift, the king pleading for his friend and his own honor, disputed the gift till dawn.',
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 527 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: `"Whoso shall wrong his neighbour nor repent, saying, 'See here a lord omnipotent,' will ne'er be found to live out half his days, and gods will view his conduct with dispraise," said the king. "I would not by an unjust act e'en heaven itself attain, no, not if, Ahip\u0101raka, the whole world I should gain." Like the bull that leads the herd, so should the chief steer a direct course; father and chief and guardian of the land, he took his stand as champion of its rights, to his own will no more subservient: do righteously to parents, wife, children, friends, in war and travel, town and village, to brahmins and ascetics, to beasts and birds\u2014thus heaven at last. When the king had thus been taught the law by his commander-in-chief, he got rid of his infatuation for Ummadant\u012B. Of that time \u0100nanda was the charioteer Sunanda, S\u0101riputta was Ahip\u0101raka, Uppalava\u1E47\u1E47\u0101 was Ummadant\u012B, and the Master was king Sivi.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 527 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
