    title: {
      en: "Kusa-J\u0101taka (The Ugly Prince and the Radiant Bride)",
      zh: "\u83C5\u8349\u738B\u5B50\u672C\u751F"
    },
    category: "religious",
    tradition: "buddhist",
    period: "Composed c. 3rd c. BCE-2nd c. CE, Pali; Jataka translation 1901 (Chalmers)",
    region: {
      en: "Kus\u0101vat\u012B in the Malla country and S\u0101gala in Madda (symbolic pin)",
      zh: "\u672B\u7F85\u570B\u62D8\u820D\u5A46\u63D0\u57CE\u8207\u6469\u9054\u570B\u5A11\u4F3D\u7F85\u57CE\uFF08\u8C61\u5FB5\u6027\u5B9A\u4F4D\uFF09"
    },
    lat: 32.5,
    lon: 74.5,
    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell, vol. 4 (trans. Chalmers, 1901)",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/complete-cowell-jataka-six-volumes-in-one"
    },
    license: "Public domain (translation 1901)",
    summary: {
      en: "Granted by Sakka a wise but ugly son, king Okk\u0101ka's queen seeks a bride as lovely as the golden image her son himself has wrought\u2014and finds Pabh\u0101vat\u012B, whose radiance lights a dark room. Hid from her by day, Kusa is discovered at a lotus-pool; she flees home, and he follows as a humble cook in her father's palace for seven months, until Sakka's ruse brings seven suitors with armies and her father threatens to hack her in seven pieces\u2014whereupon she reveals the 'slave' as the lion-voiced king, who routs them all and marries off her seven sisters to the seven kings.",
      zh: "\u5929\u5E1D\u8CDC\u8207\u667A\u6167\u800C\u8C8C\u919C\u4E4B\u5B50\u83C5\u8349\uFF1B\u5176\u81EA\u9444\u91D1\u50CF\u904D\u5C0B\u5929\u4E0B\uFF0C\u7D42\u5F97\u5BB9\u5149\u53EF\u7167\u6697\u5BA4\u4E4B\u5A46\u85AE\u8DCB\u63D0\u3002\u56E0\u5176\u4F8B\u7981\u665D\u898B\uFF0C\u8C61\u7CDE\u99AC\u7CDE\u6232\u4E4B\u3001\u9A0E\u8C61\u7ABA\u4E4B\u3001\u84EE\u6C60\u64D2\u4E4B\uFF0C\u5979\u7D42\u9041\u56DE\u6BCD\u570B\u3002\u83C5\u8349\u5316\u540D\u5165\u5BAE\u70BA\u5E96\u4E01\u4E03\u6708\uFF1B\u5E1D\u91CB\u8A08\u4F7F\u4E03\u738B\u4F86\u6C42\u5A5A\uFF0C\u7236\u6012\u6B32\u5206\u5C38\u4E03\u6BB5\u9063\u4E4B\uFF0C\u5979\u4E43\u63ED\u7A7F\u300C\u8CE4\u5F79\u300D\u5BE6\u70BA\u7345\u543C\u4E4B\u738B\u3002\u738B\u5927\u7834\u4E03\u8ECD\uFF0C\u4EE5\u5176\u4E03\u59B9\u914D\u4E03\u738B\uFF0C\u592B\u5A66\u548C\u6A02\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "transformation",
      "sacred-kingship",
      "karma-retribution"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Canonical Jataka linked by commentators to Beauty-and-the-Beast tale-types; retold freely as scripture of a living tradition; no ritual secrecy."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: `The Master told this of a backsliding Brother smitten by a fair lady: through love of a woman, mighty sages of old lost their power and came to misery. Of old, in the Malla kingdom, king Okk\u0101ka's realm lacked an heir; dancing women were exposed in vain, and queen-consort S\u012Blavat\u012B herself was exposed with religious sanction. Her virtue heated Sakka's throne; the god, disguised as an aged brahmin, took her hand before the jeering crowd, caused a hut to appear, and carried her to the heaven of the Thirty-three, where on the seventh day she knew him and asked a son. "Not merely one, lady: one wise but ugly, the other handsome but a fool; which first?" "The wise one." Sakka gave her kusa grass, a heavenly robe, sandal-wood, a coral-tree flower and a Kokanada lute, and bore her back to the king's couch; in ten months she bore a son named for the grass, Kusa, and later a second, Jayampati. Kusa, so wise that untaught he mastered every art, at sixteen was to receive the kingdom\u2014but he had fashioned of gold a female image of matchless beauty, and vowed to wed only a woman like it.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 531 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: `The councillors bore the image through India; everywhere the people admired the divine nymph, but nowhere could they match her to any daughter\u2014till at S\u0101gala in Madda the humpbacked nurse, sent for water, struck the golden image, her runaway foster-child, on the cheek; finding it gold, she boasted her Pabh\u0101vat\u012B, eldest of the Madda king's seven daughters, worth a sixteenth part more. The envoys rejoiced, the marriage was contracted, and queen S\u012Blavat\u012B made a family observance the condition: no wife might see her husband by daylight till she had conceived. So Kusa reigned with Pabh\u0101vat\u012B, meeting her only in the dark, lest his bride see his radiance. Yet he longed to see her, and his mother's devices told against her: in the elephant-stall he pelted her with dung, in the stable with dirt; when his handsome brother rode by she saw only the impertinent elephant-keeper\u2014Kusa himself, betraying his joy with gesticulating hands; in the lotus-pool, hidden by a leaf, he seized her hand: "I am king Kusa." She cried, "A goblin is catching hold of me," swooned, and fled home to her father that day; and Kusa, too proud to hold her, let her go, vowing to win her back.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 531 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: `Her scorn had old roots: in a former birth, when his sister-in-law gave his cake to a paccekabuddha, he snatched it back in anger\u2014hence his ugliness\u2014and prayed that wherever she dwelt he might carry her off as bride. In sorrow he took a thousand pieces, his five weapons and his Kokanada lute, traveled a hundred leagues a day, and entered S\u0101gala as minstrel, then apprentice potter, basket-maker and gardener, each time making work for her hand, which she threw down in rage while her sisters laughed. As the king's cook he surpassed himself, and climbing to the princesses' dwellings was reviled by Pabh\u0101vat\u012B: "Kusa, for thee by day and night to bear this burden is not right; haste back to Kus\u0101vat\u012B; thy ugly form I loth to see." "Bound by thy beauty's spell, Pabh\u0101vat\u012B, my native land has little charm; Madda's fair realm is ever my delight, my crown resigned, to live in thy dear sight," he answered. The humpbacked slave, bribed with a golden necklace, praised him through thirteen stanzas\u2014"Esteem him not by outward form or height, Pabh\u0101vat\u012B; great glory his, so do whate'er is pleasing in his sight"\u2014till her mistress cowed her with threats of a cut tongue.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 531 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: `After seven months of servitude Sakka intervened, sending seven kings, each privately, word that Pabh\u0101vat\u012B had cast off king Kusa and was to be wed. All seven arrived, discovered the trick, and invested the city; Madda's ministers counseled surrender, but the king swore to slay her and send a piece of her to each suitor, and the executioner was summoned. Her mother, lamenting her beauty doomed to the vultures, cried, "Where's he that crushes hostile realms? Kusa the wise would free us from our woes." "The conqueror who crushes all his foes\u2014lo, here is he," said Pabh\u0101vat\u012B, pointing through the window at the pot-bellied cook; her father humbled himself before the disguised king, and Pabh\u0101vat\u012B groveled in the mud at Kusa's feet and begged pardon. Kusa, lion-like, mounted a war-elephant with Pabh\u0101vat\u012B behind, and roared thrice: warriors, horse and foot broke and fled, and Sakka, glad, gave him the gem Verocana. He took the kings alive, and bestowed Pabh\u0101vat\u012B's seven sisters on them; riding home in one car, neither outshone the other, both alike fair, and in peace and plenty they lived happily. The humpback of that tale was Khujjuttar\u0101, Pabh\u0101vat\u012B was R\u0101hula's mother, and king Kusa was the Master.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 531 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
