    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Kuveni had served Vijaya faithfully: she had given him the yakkhas' kingdom, done him a woman's service, and borne him a son and a daughter. But when the Pandya princess arrived at Mahatittha with her train, Vijaya said to the yakkhini: 'Go thou now, dear one, leaving the two children behind; men are ever in fear of superhuman beings.' Seized with fear of the yakkhas herself, she besought him again and again; he promised a thousand pieces for an offering to her, and at last she took her two children and departed for Lankapura, fearing what evil might come of it.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Mahavamsa, ch. 7 (Geiger 1912); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "She set the children down outside the yakkha city and went in alone. The yakkhas recognized her at once, took her in their terror for a spy of the men who now dwelt in the island, and great was the stir among them; one who was violent killed the yakkhini with a single blow of his fist. Her mother's brother, a yakkha, came out and found the children: 'Here has your mother been slain, and they will slay you also if they see you; therefore flee swiftly!' Fleeing with speed they came to Sumanakuta. When the brother grew up he took his sister to wife, and multiplying with sons and daughters they dwelt, with the king's leave, in the mountain region; from these are sprung the Pulindas.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Mahavamsa, ch. 7 (Geiger 1912); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "Vijaya died with no heir, and for a year the ministers ruled from Upatissagama awaiting the prince. In Sihapura his brother Sumitta sent his youngest son Panduvasudeva, who came in the disguise of mendicant monks with thirty-two sons of ministers and landed at the mouth of the Mahakandara river. A soothsayer had foretold the prince's coming and that one sprung of his house would establish the religion of the Buddha in Lanka. Panduvasudeva, lacking a consort, was not yet consecrated; so when the golden maiden Bhaddakaccana \u2014 youngest daughter of the Sakya Pandu, fair as a woman of gold, wooed by seven kings \u2014 was placed by her father on a ship with thirty-two women-friends, she too landed robed as a nun, was recognized by a minister who had heard the soothsayer's word, and was consecrated queen; her companions were given to the prince's followers, and the monarch lived happily.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Mahavamsa, ch. 8 (Geiger 1912); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "vijaya-lanka",
      "kuveni"
    ]
