    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Tiger had a big pot of meat, boiled it, left it, and kept a bone-hole where he threw what he finished. Anansi took his wife and three children and said the five of them would go to the house and get into the pot and eat the meat; when they heard Tiger coming, the five of them went into the hole.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Beckwith, tale 8 (1924); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Tiger came and said, 'Not a creature has eaten this meat but Brar Nansi!' \u2014 then began to eat. The first bone he threw into the hole knocked one of the children; as the child started to holler, Anansi said, 'Shut your mouth, sir, don't cry!' Another bone, another child knocked \u2014 'Shut your mouth, sir!' Then the last child, then the mother, each bruised and each hushed, until the final bone knocked Anansi in the head. 'Let us all holler now in the hole!' they cried together \u2014 'Yee! yee-e-e!' \u2014 and Tiger, terrified by the chorus in his bone-hole, ran and left his house; Anansi and wife and children came out, took all the meat, and ran him out of his house on account of that bone-hole.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Beckwith, tale 8, closing (1924); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
