    summary: {
      en: "Confucius finds Lao Tz\u016D, freshly washed and hair hanging to dry, looking like a lifeless body \u2014 I was wandering in the unborn, says Lao Tz\u016D. The perfect Negative is majestically passive, the perfect Positive powerfully active; their interaction results in the harmony by which all things are produced. There may be a First Cause, but we never see his form. Heaven is naturally high, the earth naturally solid \u2014 do they cultivate these attributes?",
      zh: "\u5B54\u5B50\u898B\u8001\u8043\uFF1B\u8001\u8043\u65B0\u6C90\uFF0C\u65B9\u5C07\u88AB\u9AEE\u800C\u4E7E\uFF0C\u6179\u7136\u4F3C\u975E\u4EBA\u3002\u66F0\uFF1A\u543E\u904A\u5FC3\u65BC\u7269\u4E4B\u521D\u2014\u2014\u81F3\u9670\u8085\u8085\uFF0C\u81F3\u967D\u8D6B\u8D6B\uFF1B\u4EA4\u901A\u6210\u548C\u800C\u7269\u751F\u7109\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "creation-chaos",
      "theophany",
      "parable-teaching"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Canonical text of the Taoist canon (Nanhua zhenjing); the yin-yang cosmogony in Laozi's mouth. Included as a comparative text."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: 'Confucius went to see Lao Tz\u016D. The latter had just washed his head, and his hair was hanging down his back to dry. He looked like a lifeless body; so Confucius waited awhile, but at length approached and said, "Do my eyes deceive me, or is this really so? Your frame, Sir, seems like dry wood, as if it had been left without that which informs it with the life of man." "I was wandering," replied Lao Tz\u016D, "in the unborn."'
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: '"The perfect Negative principle is majestically passive. The perfect Positive principle is powerfully active. Passivity emanates from heaven above; activity proceeds from earth beneath. The interaction of the two results in that harmony by which all things are produced. There may be a First Cause, but we never see his form. His report fills space. There is darkness and light. Days come and months go. Work is being constantly performed, yet we never witness the performance. Life must bring us from somewhere, and death must carry us back. Beginning and end follow ceaselessly one upon the other, and we cannot say when the series will be exhausted. If this is not the work of a First Cause, what is it?"'
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: '"Not so," answered Lao Tz\u016D. "The fluidity of water is not the result of any effort on the part of the water, but is its natural property. And the virtue of the perfect man is such that even without cultivation there is nothing which can withdraw from his sway. Heaven is naturally high, the earth is naturally solid, the sun and moon are naturally bright. Do they cultivate these attributes?"'
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "confucius",
      "laozi"
    ]
