    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Canonical text of the Taoist canon (Nanhua zhenjing); the spurious-but-canonical Robber Ch\xEA dialogue is Zhuangzi's great trickster-narrative. Included as a comparative text."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Robber Ch\xEA had a band of followers nine thousand strong. He ravaged the whole empire, plundering the various nobles and breaking into people's houses. He drove off oxen and horses. He stole men's wives and daughters. Family ties put no limit to his greed. He had no respect for parents nor for brothers. He neglected the worship of his ancestors. Wherever he passed, the greater States flew to arms, the smaller ones to places of safety. All the people were sore distressed."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: `The latter had just encamped to the south of T'ai-shan, and was engaged in devouring a dish of minced human liver. Confucius alighted from his chariot, and advancing addressed the doorkeeper as follows:--"I am Confucius of the Lu State. I have heard of the high character of your captain." When Robber Ch\xEA heard who it was, he was furious. His eyes glared like stars. His hair raised his cap from his head as he cried out, "What! that crafty scoundrel Confucius of Lu? Go, tell him from me that he is a mere word-mongerer. That he talks nonsense about W\xEAn Wang and Wu Wang. That what he says is mostly rhodomontade. That he consumes where he does not sow, and wears clothes he does not weave. That his lips patter and his tongue wags. Tell him that he deserves the worst, and that if he does not take himself off his liver shall be in my morning stew."`
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: `"Ch'iu! come here," cried Robber Ch\xEA in a great rage. "There is no State bigger than the empire. Yao and Shun both got this, yet their descendants have not territory enough to insert an awl's point. T'ang and Wu Wang both sat upon the Imperial throne, yet their posterity has been obliterated from the face of the earth. Was not this because of the very magnitude of the prize? ... And now you come forward preaching the old dogmas of W\xEAn Wang and palming off sophistries without end, in order to teach future generations. You are the biggest thief I know of; and if the world calls me Robber Ch\xEA, it most certainly ought to call you Robber Ch'iu.`
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: `"Ch'iu! all your teachings are nothing to me. Begone! Go home! Say no more! Your doctrine is a random jargon, full of falsity and deceit. It can never preserve the original purity of man. Why discuss it further?" Confucius made two obeisances and hurriedly took his leave. On mounting his chariot, he three times missed hold of the reins. His eyes were so dazed that he could see nothing. His face was ashy pale. ... "He did," said Confucius. "I am a man who has cauterized himself without being ill. I hurried away to smooth the tiger's head and comb out his beard. And I very nearly got into the tiger's mouth."`
      }
    ],
