    source: {
      archive: "Internet Archive",
      work: "The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell, vol. 4 (trans. Chalmers, 1901)",
      url: "https://archive.org/details/complete-cowell-jataka-six-volumes-in-one"
    },
    license: "Public domain (translation 1901)",
    summary: {
      en: "King Arindama forgets his friend Sonaka for forty years, singing his reward song through all Benares, while Sonaka, a withered sal-leaf falling before him, becomes a Silent Buddha. Fifty years on he teaches the reward-song's refrain to a wood-gathering boy, who sings it back before the king; led to the park, the king insults the shaven recluse and is rebuked, then hears the parable of the crow that feasted on a floating carcass until the Ganges swept it to the sea, and renounces his throne the same day.",
      zh: "\u963F\u6797\u9054\u6469\u738B\u56DB\u5341\u5E74\u4F86\u904D\u5531\u300C\u898B\u6211\u6545\u53CB\u8005\u8CDE\u5343\u9802\u51A0\u300D\u4E4B\u6B4C\uFF0C\u800C\u8607\u90A3\u8FE6\u898B\u843D\u8449\u609F\u7121\u5E38\uFF0C\u5DF2\u6210\u7368\u89BA\u4F5B\u3002\u5F8C\u6559\u62FE\u67F4\u5C0F\u5152\u7E8C\u5531\u5176\u6B4C\u8A5E\uFF1B\u738B\u5FAA\u6B4C\u81F3\u5FA1\u82D1\uFF0C\u65A5\u5243\u9AEE\u884C\u8005\u70BA\u53EF\u6190\u87F2\u800C\u906D\u53CD\u8A70\uFF1B\u805E\u300C\u70CF\u9D09\u98DF\u6D6E\u5C4D\u96A8\u6D41\u81F3\u6D77\u300D\u4E4B\u55BB\uFF0C\u7576\u65E5\u6368\u738B\u4F4D\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "parable-teaching",
      "transformation",
      "wilderness-wandering"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Canonical Jataka of a living tradition; retold freely; no ritual secrecy."
    },
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: `The Master told this concerning the Perfection of Renunciation: "not now only, but of old also the Tath\u0101gata verily left the world and made the Great Renunciation." Of old, the Magadha king reigned in Rajagaha, and on the day the Bodhisatta was born to his chief queen as prince Arindama, a son was born to the royal chaplain and named young Sonaka. The two boys grew up alike as twins and studied at Taxila; wandering to Benares they lodged in the royal park, and at a brahmins' almsgiving the seats assigned them\u2014white cloth for one, red woollen rug for the other\u2014told Sonaka that that day his friend would be made king of Benares and would offer him the post of commander-in-chief. On the seventh day after the king's death the festal car stopped at the park gate for any worthy man to mount; the chaplain sprinkled the Bodhisatta king on the spot and led him into the city with a vast escort, and in the greatness of his glory the king forgot all about Sonaka.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 529 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: `But when the king was gone, Sonaka sat again on the stone seat, and a withered leaf of a sal tree fell from its stalk in front of him. "Even as this leaf, so will my body fall into decay," he cried, and acquiring supernatural insight by reflecting on the impermanence of all things he attained that instant to the state of a Silent Buddha\u2014his layman's marks vanishing, the ascetic's appearing\u2014and set out for the cave of Nandam\u016Bla. The king, after forty years, remembered Sonaka, and offered a thousand pieces to whoever should see him with his own eyes and a hundred to whoever should hear of him, singing his aspiration: "A thousand crowns for one that sees my friend and playmate dear; a hundred lo! I give if one of Sonaka should hear." The song passed from mouth to mouth through harem, city and country till all sang it. Fifty years on, Sonaka, moved by the king's longing, came back through the air and taught the refrain to a seven-year-old boy gathering sticks in the park.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 529 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: `Bathed and dressed in his best, the boy had a drum beaten through the city, and before all the people the king sang first and the boy\u2014five tangled locks he wore\u2014answered: "The thousand give to me who saw, who heard a hundred more: I'll tell thee news of Sonaka, thy playfellow of yore. Within this realm, in thine own park is many a big sal tree... and at their foot lo! Sonaka in meditation lies, filled with the Arhat's holy calm, when human passion dies." The king started in full force and found his friend at rest, passionless and saintly; but given up to evil passion, he insulted him without saluting: "His parents dead, with shaven head, clad in monk's robe I see, a wretched Brother in a trance, stretched here beneath this tree." "He is no wretched wight who in his every action, Sire, has aye attained to right; nay rather wretched those who right neglect and practise ill, for evil doer evil doom is destined to fulfil."`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 529 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: `Proving himself king of Kasi, Arindama asked if the sage had met with anything deserving blame, and Sonaka told the eight blessings of the homeless monk; but the king cut him short: "I have no need of monkish blessings... how to gain both worlds at once, to me, I prithee, tell." Then came the parable: a foolish crow, seeing a vast carcass floating past on the Ganges' tide, boarded it for goodly store of food and stayed eating and drinking till the river swept him headlong to the sea, where the monster fish came ravening round and devoured him. "So thou and all that greedily pleasures of sense pursue are deemed as wise as was this crow, till ye all lusts eschew." "In pity once, nay even twice, utter the warning word, but keep not on repeating it like a slave before his lord"\u2014and the sage vanished through the air. The king resolved, "To-morrow one may die, who knows? I'll be ordained to-day," gave his sixty thousand villages, elephants, horses, cars and maidens to his son Dighavu, and was destined for the Brahma world; of that time the son was young Rahula and the king the Master.`,
        note: "Paraphrase of Jataka 529 (Chalmers 1901); not verbatim."
      }
    ],
    persons: [
      "rahula"
    ]
