    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "Caught with Brer Wolf closing in, Brer Rabbit asked how he could show his politeness: 'I want you to say grace, Brer Wolf, and say it quick, because I am getting mighty weak. Fold your hands under your chin, Brer Wolf, and shut your eyes, and say: Bless us and blind us, and put us in the crack where the Ole Boy can't find us. Say it quick, Brer Wolf, because I am failing mighty fast.'",
        note: "Paraphrase of Harris, tale XXVII (1883); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "Brer Wolf put up his hands and shot his eyes and began, 'Bless us and blind us' \u2014 but he did not get any farther: just as he took up his hands, Brer Rabbit fetched a wiggle, lit on his feet, and left a blue streak behind him. 'Oh, I just knew Brother Rabbit would get away!' the little boy declared; and Uncle Remus said that was right \u2014 put your dependence in Brer Rabbit and you won't be far out of the way.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Harris, tale XXVII, ending (1883); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
