    license: "Public domain (published 1929)",
    summary: {
      en: "On the day of the great feast Croyance, pitying her dead drudges once more, bought tablettes \u2014 'not salted or seasoned, they are sweet, and can do no harm just this once.' But the baker had salted the pistache nuts before stirring them into the rapadou; and as the zombies tasted the salt, they knew that they were dead, made a dreadful outcry, arose, and turned their faces toward the mountain. No one dared to stop them, for they were corpses walking in the sunlight; and at their own village in the twilight they shuffled past fathers, wives, mothers who screamed and begged them to stay, up the path to the graveyard, and each before his own empty grave clawed the stones and earth to enter it again \u2014 and as their cold hands touched the earth of their own graves, they fell and lay there, rotting carrion.",
      zh: "\u5927\u7BC0\u4E4B\u65E5\uFF0C\u514B\u9B6F\u74E6\u6602\u7D72\u518D\u5EA6\u6190\u61AB\u9019\u4E9B\u4EA1\u8005\u82E6\u5DE5\uFF0C\u8CB7\u4F86\u82B1\u751F\u7CD6\u2014\u2014\u300E\u6C92\u9E7D\u6C92\u6599\uFF0C\u662F\u751C\u7684\uFF0C\u5C31\u9019\u4E00\u6B21\u7121\u59A8\u300F\u3002\u8AB0\u77E5\u7CD6\u8CA9\u5728\u62CC\u5165\u7CD6\u6F3F\u524D\u5148\u7D66\u82B1\u751F\u52A0\u4E86\u9E7D\uFF1B\u9084\u9B42\u5C4D\u4E00\u5617\u5230\u9E7D\uFF0C\u4FBF\u77E5\u81EA\u5DF1\u5DF2\u6B7B\uFF0C\u767C\u51FA\u53EF\u6016\u7684\u54C0\u865F\uFF0C\u8D77\u8EAB\u8F49\u5411\u7FA4\u5C71\u3002\u7121\u4EBA\u6562\u6514\u2014\u2014\u90A3\u662F\u884C\u8D70\u5728\u65E5\u5149\u4E0B\u7684\u5C4D\u9AD4\u3002\u8584\u66AE\u6642\u5206\uFF0C\u4ED6\u5011\u8E0F\u8457\u788E\u6B65\u7D93\u904E\u8A8D\u51FA\u89AA\u4EBA\u3001\u54ED\u558A\u633D\u7559\u7684\u6751\u4EBA\uFF0C\u8D70\u4E0A\u901A\u5F80\u5893\u5712\u7684\u5C0F\u5F91\uFF0C\u5728\u5404\u81EA\u7A7A\u58B3\u524D\u5228\u6398\u77F3\u571F\u6B32\u5165\uFF1B\u51B0\u51B7\u7684\u624B\u89F8\u5230\u81EA\u5BB6\u58B3\u571F\u7684\u4E00\u523B\uFF0C\u4ED6\u5011\u5012\u4E0B\uFF0C\u6A6B\u9673\u70BA\u8150\u8089\u3002"
    },
    motifs: [
      "resurrection",
      "taboo-transgression"
    ],
    sensitivity: {
      restricted: false,
      note: "Sequel of the Hasco narrative; the salt-taste rule is folklore-level. Source is Seabrook's popular-ethnography; the melodrama is his, the folk logic (salt releases the dead) is the tellers'."
    },
    persons: [],
    passages: [
      {
        id: "1",
        text: "As Croyance sat with her savory dried herring and biscuit baked with salt and soda, and her provision of clairin in the tin cup by her side, she pitied the zombies who had worked so faithfully for Joseph in the cane fields and who now had nothing, while all the other groups around were feasting; and as she pitied them a woman passed, crying, 'Tablettes! Tablettes pistaches! Trois pour dix cobs!' \u2014 tablettes are a sort of candy, in shape and size like cookies, made of brown cane sugar, sometimes with pistaches, which in Haiti are peanuts.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Seabrook, The Magic Island, ch. 'Black Sorcery' (1929); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "2",
        text: "And Croyance thought, 'These tablettes are not salted or seasoned, they are sweet, and can do no harm to the zombies just this once.' So she untied the corner of her kerchief, took out a coin \u2014 a gourdon, the quarter of a gourde \u2014 and bought some of the tablettes, which she broke in halves and divided among the zombies, who began sucking and mumbling them in their mouths. But the baker of the tablettes had salted the pistache nuts before stirring them into the rapadou, and as the zombies tasted the salt, they knew that they were dead, and made a dreadful outcry, and arose and turned their faces toward the mountain.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Seabrook (1929); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "3",
        text: "No one dared to stop them, for they were corpses walking in the sunlight, and they themselves and all the people knew that they were corpses; and they disappeared toward the mountain. When later they drew near their own village on the slopes of Morne-au-Diable \u2014 these dead men and women walking single file in the twilight, with no soul leading them or daring to follow \u2014 the people of their village, holding their hamboche in the market-place, saw them drawing closer, and recognized among them fathers, brothers, wives and daughters whom they had buried months before. Most of them knew at once the truth, that these were zombies who had been dragged dead from their graves; but others hoped that a blessed miracle had taken place on this Fete Dieu, and rushed forward to take them in their arms and welcome them.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Seabrook (1929); not verbatim."
      },
      {
        id: "4",
        text: "But the zombies shuffled through the market-place, recognizing neither father nor wife nor mother; and as they turned leftward up the path leading to the graveyard, a woman whose daughter was in the procession of the dead threw herself screaming before the girl's shuffling feet and begged her to stay \u2014 but the grave-cold feet of the daughter and the feet of the other dead shuffled over her and onward. As they approached the graveyard they began to shuffle faster and rushed among the graves, and each before his own empty grave began clawing at the stones and earth to enter it again; and as their cold hands touched the earth of their own graves, they fell and lay there, rotting carrion.",
        note: "Paraphrase of Seabrook (1929); not verbatim."
      }
    ]
