    sensitivity_flag: "living_tradition_high",
    sensitivity_guidance: "Vodou is a living religion; initiatory knowledge and some song cycles are restricted and are not reproduced. This record paraphrases published scholarly sources only.",
    provenance: {
      record_status: "complete",
      compiled: "2026-11-14",
      compiled_by: "DHG508 fieldwork compilation",
      review_status: "draft - pending human review"
    },
    narrative: {
      en: "In Haitian Vodou the creator, Bondye \u2014 a Kreyol rendering of the French Bon Dieu, the Good God \u2014 stands at the apex of the cosmos, and stands there as a distant power. He set the world in motion, established its moral order, and then withdrew from the direct management of human affairs. The peasant adage that this distance is precisely why the lwa matter runs through the whole theology: Bondye is too great, too high, too occupied with the ends of things to be troubled with every small case of a farmer's child or a fisherman's boat, and so prayer is properly addressed to the intermediaries. The intermediaries are the lwa \u2014 the ancestral and cosmic powers, many of them named individuals with histories, tastes, tempers, and whole biographies of marriage and feud \u2014 a crowded pantheon carried across the Middle Passage from Fon, Yoruba, Kongo and other African sources and renewed generation by generation by Haitian experience. Some are old African gods in Kreyol dress; some are the spirits of revolutionary heroes; some are powers of the road, the sea, the market, the graveyard \u2014 the in-between places where the living and the dead trade. The cosmos itself is mapped as a system of roads. Between the world of the living and the world of the dead runs the Grand Chemin, the great road; the dead travel it under the care of Baron Samedi and the Gede, and the living send word along it by service, song and sacrifice. Every ceremony dramatizes a journey: the processionals that enter the peristyle from the mystery house, the tracing of v\xE8v\xE8 signs on the ground, the poteau-mitan center-pole up which the lwa are called to descend. The peristyle floor is a map; the dance is a form of travel; the drummer is a driver of the road. Syncretism in this system is structural, not accidental. Bondye merges with the Christian God and shares his distance; the lwa wear Catholic faces \u2014 Legba with Saint Peter or Saint Lazarus, Ezili with the Mater Dolorosa, Ogou with Saint James \u2014 and the Catholic calendar of saints' feasts, Good Friday and All Souls organizes the Vodou year. Leslie Desmangles reads this double registry as a theology in its own right rather than a camouflage: the African powers wearing Catholic faces made the religion survivable under the Code Noir, and in time the two registers fused into one grammar in which the same power is known by two names and honored at two altars. The cultural function of the whole structure is to make the African gods governable in a colony and a republic that burned them: the distant creator keeps the state's God out of the way of the ancestors' business, and the roads keep every petition answerable in this world. The Grand Chemin deserves its own close attention, because it is the image that most distinguishes this cosmology from its Old World sources. In the African homelands the worlds of the living and the dead are adjacent villages, connected by boundaries that are tended \u2014 the sacred grove, the river crossing, the family tomb inside the compound wall. In Haiti the crossing became the road: a long, dangerous, traveled distance, made by the Middle Passage and renewed by the regime of mobility that the plantation imposed \u2014 the forced march of the coffle, the flight of the maroon along the mountain paths, the constant traffic of the dead in a colony where death was everywhere at work. The road-cosmos is therefore a historical cosmology: it maps the specific geography of survival in Saint-Domingue, in which everyone, living or dead, was a traveler between two worlds, and in which every ceremony was a way-station. The Comparative value for the Motif Atlas is that this image joins the descent-myths and underworld-roads of the Greek, Sumerian, Norse and Maya traditions with an ocean-crossing account of the same structure: the road between the worlds is the Middle Passage itself, remembered cosmologically, and every ritual journey on it is at once a liturgy and a history lesson."
    }
