    narrative: {
      en: "Ezili Freda Dahomey is the lwa of love, beauty and luxury: perfumed, jeweled, pale in the iconography, quarrelsome \u2014 and cursed never to be satisfied in love. Her very name carries her origin: Freda Dahomey, the lady from Dahomey, and Karen McCarthy Brown reads in her the creolized memory of a Dahomean noblewoman of the slave-trade era, a woman who may once have owned slaves herself and who in Haiti was enslaved. The luxury of her cult is her remembered station \u2014 perfume, sweets, lace, white rum, the finest white and pink things in the household \u2014 and her tears are her remembered fate: she weeps, the tradition says, because she is destined always to love and never to be loved enough. Her mythic biography is a romance of jealousy, played out among the pantheon's most powerful men: she is the wife or lover of three rivals \u2014 the iron soldier Ogou, the serpent Damballa, and the mortal trickster Ti-Jean, who won her for a single night and lost her forever. The triangle is the engine of her service-songs, which recount her fits of pique, her errands of vengeance against rival women, her shopping for the finest cloth in the market of heaven. Her emblem is the heart pierced by a sword \u2014 the emblem that Catholic Haiti knows as the Mater Dolorosa \u2014 and her day is Thursday, her color white and pink, her posture fainting elegance. Those who serve her keep her altar immaculate: she cannot abide tobacco smoke, and above all she cannot abide the smell of pork, which the tradition reads as the smell of the suffering of her people \u2014 the smell of the ship, the smell of the cane. She has her dark twin, and the pair makes a single theology of love. Ezili Danto is black, scarred, the warrior-mother who fights with a child strapped to her back and a knife in her teeth, her speech folded with the hard bends of a rough life; where Freda weeps at romance gone wrong, Danto aches for the children of Haiti; where Freda's service is a garden of white things and sweet perfumes, Danto's is a dark kitchen of iron, pepper and machete. Together they are the two faces of love \u2014 the longing and the labor \u2014 and the devotee who serves one learns to serve both, for the same heart wears both faces in a country that has known both the fantasy of luxury and the discipline of survival. The ritual economics of her cult make the theology legible. Ezili is the most expensive of the lwa to serve: her perfumes, her white cakes, her imported lace, her pink and white candles \u2014 the altar of a rich Ezili house in Haiti is a small museum of the dream of luxury, and the poor devotee who buys one candle for her is buying, consciously, a share in the fantasy. Brown's ethnography shows the other side with equal care: in the Brooklyn apartment altar of Mama Lola, Ezili Freda's things stand beside Ezili Danto's machete and the family's Ogou flags, and the three of them constitute a single statement about love and iron and survival \u2014 the fantasy, the labor and the fight, each with its own price. In the comparative grid Ezili stands with the Aphrodite-and-Ares pair of the Greek cycle and with the Mesopotamian Inanna in her modes of desire and war; the Haitian particularity is that the pair are two names of one goddess, and that the history of slavery is written into the seam between them. For the Motif Atlas the record anchors the cluster of love-goddess-with-war-goddess and supplies the Afro-Atlantic case in which the split is explained by history rather than by theology."
    }
