    narrative: {
      en: "Ogou Feray \u2014 Ogun Ferraille, Ogun of the Irons \u2014 is the lwa of iron and war in Haitian Vodou, the Yoruba road-opener and blacksmith-god carried to Saint-Domingue and fused there with the iron-powers of the Fon and Kongo ancestors. His emblems are a machete crossed on his shrine, an iron bar, the red flag; his colors are red and blue, his animal the red rooster, his Saint the warrior-saint James in his iconography of battle. In the Haitian telling he is pre-eminently the spirit of the warriors of the revolution of 1791 to 1804 \u2014 the power that marched with the maroons and the freedmen of the North Plain, that stood beside Dessalines at the crack of independence \u2014 and at the same time the workers' lwa of forge, field and factory: the same iron that fights also feeds, the same blade that harvests cane also harvests enemies. When he comes in service he arrives like a soldier: standing at attention in the doorway of the peristyle, drinking rum neat, smoking a cigar, his voice hoarse with command, his feet planted. He demands payment for his work \u2014 a small coin, a splash of rum, a cigar \u2014 and he keeps accounts: the Haitian proverb that Ogou is owed remembers every unpaid debt of the revolution's dead, and the service-song that lists his battles is a ledger of national memory. But he is also a healer, and the two offices are one: iron's own medicine for iron's own wounds, the cuts and fevers of labor treated with heated blades, cauterizing iron, the red steel laid on red flesh. Brown's telling makes the politics of this explicit and personal. In Mama Lola's family Ogou is the ancestor-spirit of a lineage of rebel men \u2014 a great-grandfather who fought in the cacos, a grandfather who died under the American occupation \u2014 and the family service to him is at once a remembering and an accounting: what was promised to the men, what was owed to the lwa, what has been paid and what remains. The iron soldier of the revolution lives on in the iron of the household, and the household keeps his books. The comparative grid confirms how much of this is old iron in new hands: the Yoruba Ogun of the homeland is the hunter and road-opener who first cleared the paths with his blade, the blacksmith whose forge civilized the forest, the orisha who insists on being fed before any serious work begins; the Haitian Ogou keeps every one of these offices and adds the political one, because in Saint-Domingue and after, iron was the metal of the slaves \u2014 the machete that cut cane and cut masters, the chains that had to be broken, the tools that were forbidden and were seized. In the Petro register of the hot rites he appears as Ogoun Badagris, the bloody dreadful one whose voice is thunder, and in the Rada register he is the cooler soldier of the calendar saint; the two registers share a single shrine and a single ledger. For the corpus of comparative myth he supplies the Afro-Atlantic form of the culture-hero of iron: the first clearers of the road, the smiths of the social contract, the warriors whose pay is always partly owed."
    }
