    narrative: {
      en: "Damballa Wedo is the serpent-lwa of Haitian Vodou, and he is old \u2014 the oldest in the ritual order of the Rada rites. Ancient, slow, white, and speechless, he does not speak in service: he hisses, and his horse \u2014 the possessed celebrant \u2014 moves like a great snake along the ground, slithering rather than walking, tasting the air. He is the power of the waters: rain, rivers, the primal sea from which the world was made; in the Fon mythology he descends from the creator pair Mawu-Lisa, and in Haiti he keeps that seniority. He is served first among the Rada lwa, with the whitest and coolest offerings \u2014 white flour, white eggs, cool water, white sheets \u2014 because heat is not his element and the coolness of the deep water is. His wife, or co-wife in different tellings, is Ayida Weddo, the rainbow: the serpent that bridges the sky to the earth and carries the waters home. Where Damballa is the still deep water, Ayida is the arc of color that the water makes climbing back to the sky, and their union is the movement of creation itself \u2014 the water falling and returning, the world breathing. Their paired emblem \u2014 two serpents about the center-pole \u2014 is one of the oldest signs of the religion, drawn first on the floor of every service. The colonial record preserves the same deity under the name of the Vaudoux serpent. Moreau de Saint-M\xE9ry, writing in the 1790s, describes the non-venomous serpent kept in a box upon the altar of the sect, before which the King and Queen take their oath and the whole community swears by the serpent's power; the serpent in the box is, in the living theology, the same power that made the world \u2014 a god too old to argue, kept near at hand in a small wooden ark. The continuity is exact and documentable: the Arada serpent of 1790 and the Damballa of the present-day peristyle are one lineage, and the colonial archive itself \u2014 hostile as it is \u2014 becomes evidence of survival. What the colonial eye read as superstition is the memory of a cosmology in which the world itself was made by the serpent and the rainbow, and in which every rain is the old agreement renewed. In the comparative grid Damballa and Ayida stand with the world-serpents of every mythology \u2014 the Midgard serpent beneath the sea, the Chinese Yinglong and the coiling dragons of the flood-tales, the Rainbow Serpent of Australia and Melanesia \u2014 but with a Haitian particularity: the world-serpent here is not a threat to be fought but a peace to be kept. The waters that he governs are the waters of origin, and his service is a renewal of the original calm rather than a defeat of chaos; the rainbow that Ayida spans is the sign that the flood has an end. The colonial sources, seeing only the box and the oath, could not read this; the living liturgy reads it every time the paired serpents are drawn on the floor of the peristyle and the oldest drums begin to cool the air of the yard. For the comparative corpus the Haitian serpent supplies the one case in which the world-serpent is both creator and comforter, and in which the oldest god is the quietest one in the room."
    }
