    narrative: {
      en: "The Marassa are the lwa of twins in Haitian Vodou \u2014 Marasa Twims, Marasa Etwa Dosa, the sacred doubled children \u2014 and they are served first at every ceremony, from twin-plates and twin-spoons that no other lwa may touch, seated on the ground like children at a picnic and offered the plainest food of the compound. Their theology is the theology of double birth: twins are not ordinary children but spirits walking among mortals, close to the source of things, dangerous to cross and glorious to please. They are petulant, easily offended, prone to strike a household with the childhood diseases when slighted, and so tenderly feared that the whole service pauses until they are fed and pleased. In the Fon-Yoruba homeland the same powers are the Ibeji of the Yoruba and the hohovi of the Fon: twin-births as miracles or calamities, twin-deaths not as ends but as returns to the wild twin-country from which the children came, twin-cults of little paired figures carved, fed, and kept on the family shelf. The Haitian Marassa carry this memory whole across the Middle Passage: the twin-plates, the first-served portion, the childish fury, the small chairs set for guests who are always invisible and always expected. In the Afro-Atlantic comparative grid the Marassa stand with the Yoruba Ibeji, with the Hero Twins of the Maya Popol Vuh, and with every tradition's doubled founders: the twin as creator, twin as trickster, twin as omen. The Haitian form is distinct \u2014 children rather than heroes, hunger rather than conquest \u2014 but the structural rhyme is exact: two born together, honored with doubled things, feared with doubled fear. Their ritual function in the diaspora is a social one as much as a cosmic one: to serve the Marassa is to serve every doubled and divided household in a society created by forced separations, to honor the sibling pair as the smallest unit of survival, and to keep the first portion of every feast for the smallest and most easily wounded of the powers. The ritual technology of the cult is precisely calibrated to its theology: twin-plates that no single food may be served on, twin-spoons, twin-cups, two small chairs, two portions of everything, and the rule that the Marassa are fed before any other lwa \u2014 the first portion of the first dish, on the ground, with the house children standing around as if at the feeding of real children. When the twins are angry, the whole household knows it at once: the children sicken, the food spoils, the services stall, and the remedy is a double feast as quickly as it can be cooked. The Marassa therefore function as the household conscience of the religion \u2014 the lwa whose displeasure is immediate, domestic, and repairable, and whose cult teaches the primary Afro-Atlantic ethic that the doubled and the divided must be honored together. In the corpus of world myth they stand with every sacred-twin tradition, from the Ashanti twins to the Popol Vuh, supplying the Haitian case in which twinship is treated not as a heroic epoch but as a permanent domestic arrangement that must be kept in good order at every meal."
    }
