    narrative: {
      en: "The Gede family are the lwa of the cemetery and of everything that crosses it: Baron Samedi, the top-hatted master of the dead; Baron La Croix, keeper of the cross at the tomb's head; Gede Nibo, the young dandy of the cemetery paths, patron of those who die young and unshriven; Gede Frey, the great protector of children; and the Gede as a laughing, swarming crowd behind them. They come in service with skull-painted faces, black coats, dark glasses, a cane in one hand and a rum bottle in the other; they sing the filthiest songs in the peristyle and dance the banda, the obscene two-step that makes the whole yard laugh \u2014 because the Gede know how the story ends and know how it does not, and their laughter is the laughter of those who have read the ledger. Their work is double. They guard the graves of the dead, and they superintend the fertility of the living \u2014 the same power over the gates of entry and exit: a dying child is carried to the Gede; a woman who cannot conceive petitions the Baron; the funeral is negotiated with the cross at the tomb's head, the price discussed, the gate opened or kept shut. They are the psychopomps and the gynecocrats of the Afro-Atlantic crossing, and their cult, in the Kongo-inflected rites, preserves the African grammar of the land of the dead as a crowded village just beyond the fence. Brown's telling holds the two moods together. At her altar-service the Gede arrive drunk, obscene, pinching the guests, demanding cigarettes and rum and telling the sort of jokes that no other lwa would tell; and then, when the serious work begins, the same figures make the most sober contracts of all: who shall live, who shall die, who shall be remembered and how. The cemetery's laughter is not denial; it is the professional manner of the only specialists who deal daily with the crossing, and who insist that the crossing be conducted with style \u2014 a top hat, a good song, a fair price, and a laugh at the gate. The Gede's festival season is a study in this doubleness: on the feasts of the dead \u2014 All Souls and the days of the cemetery \u2014 the peristyle fills with black-clad, skull-faced dancers, and the services run all night, part funeral and part carnival, with the banda danced until the drums give out. The economics of the cult are equally explicit: the Gede are paid for everything \u2014 a coin for a prayer, a bottle for an audience, a substantial fee for the contract at the tomb \u2014 and they are famous for collecting; a service without payment is a service the Baron will remember. In the comparative grid they stand with every psychopomp of world mythology \u2014 Hermes, the Egyptian Anubis and Osiris, the Norse Hel \u2014 but with the Afro-Atlantic particularity that the lord of the dead is also the patron of the unborn, and that his cult is the one place in the religion where death and fertility are negotiated at the same table. For the corpus of comparative myth the Haitian Gede supply the fullest case of death as a professional service: contracted, priced, celebrated, and always, in the end, laughed with rather than at."
    }
