    narrative: {
      en: "The earliest written account of the ceremony at Bois Ca\xEFman is that of Antoine Dalmas, a planter of the Northern Plain, who wrote within a few years of the events he described: on the night of August 14, 1791, the principal leaders of the rising \u2014 Boukman Dutty among them \u2014 gathered in a wooded place called Bois Ca\xEFman near Cap-Fran\xE7ais, where a pig was sacrificed and an oath taken over its blood to destroy the power of the whites. Dalmas wrote as an enemy of the revolution, and his account was meant to horrify; but his testimony that such a meeting took place is not seriously doubted, and every later source builds on his report or quarrels with it. What the ceremony actually contained \u2014 the exact liturgy, the deity invoked, the share of each participant \u2014 is beyond recovery, and honest scholarship keeps the uncertainty open. What is certain is what followed. A week later the Northern Plain was in flames: the insurrection that began the Haitian Revolution \u2014 the only successful slave revolution in modern history \u2014 took its order and its courage from the networks of vodou priests and Congolese and Arada elders who had organized it. The rising was coordinated across plantations by runners and by drum, and its leadership was thick with ritual specialists; the plantation world's secret assembly was the school of its politics. Boukman's speech, as remembered in the oral tradition, casts the theology of the rising: the God of the whites demands crime, but our God asks only good works; and the remembered storm that broke that night \u2014 rain that covered the first fires \u2014 is read in the tradition as the ancestors themselves answering. In Haitian memory Bois Ca\xEFman functions as a founding scene on the order of a covenant: the oath over blood, the first fires of the North, and the birth of a nation whose independence in 1804 declared that the color question had been answered in favor of the enslaved. Historians debate the details \u2014 Geggus doubts the pig, Fick doubts the planter's numbers, and the oral tradition keeps its own counsel \u2014 but the memory has never been in doubt, and the oath, the storm and the covenant are rehearsed in song on the revolution's anniversaries to this day. For the comparative corpus Bois Ca\xEFman supplies the Afro-Atlantic form of the covenant scene: the oath sworn over blood in the wood, the divine sanction claimed by the weakest party, and the fire that follows as the sign that the covenant has been accepted. Parallels are exact and instructive \u2014 the covenant of the Hebrew exodus, the Maccabean oath at Modin, the secret oath-societies of the Kongo and the Anjelo coast that the slave-trade carried to the Caribbean \u2014 and the Haitian case is the only one that succeeded all the way to statehood. The sensitivity of the material is proportionate to its importance: Bois Ca\xEFman is invoked by Haitians as the nation's baptism, contested by scholars as a planter's story, and claimed by the living religion as proof that the ancestors were present at the founding; the honest record keeps all three claims in view and adds none of its own. In the Motif Atlas the record anchors the cluster of oath-over-blood foundings and links the Haitian revolution to the worldwide grammar of covenant and exodus."
    }
