    narrative: {
      en: "Every Vodou ceremony begins with writing. On the peristyle floor, in flour or cornmeal or ash, the celebrant traces the v\xE8v\xE8 of the lwa to be called \u2014 the signature-script of Legba's crossroads, Damballa's paired serpents, Ezili's pierced heart, the boat of Agou\xE9, the crossed iron of Ogou. The sign is the invitation: until it is drawn, the ground is ordinary ground; once it is drawn, the ground is the spirit's address, and the drums that follow are read by the spirits who arrive to find their names written. The practice is described by M\xE9traux in his classic account as the technical heart of the service \u2014 a liturgy of drawing, singing and rhythm in which the visual, the verbal and the musical are one act. Robert Farris Thompson traces the practice to Kongo. In the old homeland, cosmic graphs are drawn on the ground, on the bodies of the initiated, on the walls of tombs \u2014 writing that makes a place a crossing of worlds; the Kongo cosmogram, the diamond-with-cross that maps the living and the dead on the four quarters of a single field, is the master-sign of this art. In Haiti the Kongo sign-writing fused with the Fon and Yoruba emblem-languages and became the choreography of the ceremony: the dance is danced on the signature, the singers sing the signature into voice, and the signature is erased before the service ends \u2014 the writing is for the moment of the crossing, not for the museum, and the same hand that draws it sweeps it away. The v\xE8v\xE8 belongs to the grammar of the road-cosmos: each emblem is a door, and the ceremony is a sequence of doors opened in order. Legba's is drawn first, that the other gates may open; the paired serpents next, that the oldest powers may come cool; the crossed iron of Ogou, that the work may be done; the boat of Agou\xE9, that the crossing may be made. To read a v\xE8v\xE8 is to read the itinerary of a service, and the liturgy of drawing is thus the written liturgy of the religion \u2014 the part of the sacred that leaves a mark, and the part that leaves none, both at once. The pedagogy of the v\xE8v\xE8 is worth stating plainly, because it explains why the practice survives so tenaciously: a v\xE8v\xE8 can be learned by watching a hand draw it, without books, without literacy, without any of the institutions of writing that the colony denied the enslaved; it is a script designed by and for a population from whom reading was forbidden. The flour that carries it is food; the ash that carries it is the residue of the fire; both are humble materials, renewed every service, and the ephemerality is doctrinal \u2014 the writing belongs to the moment of contact between worlds and must not outlast it. In the comparative grid the practice stands with the ground-drawings of the Kongo, the sand-figures of West African divination (the sikidy of Madagascar, the If\xE1 marks of the Yoruba), and the chalk diagrams of European cunning-folk; the Haitian v\xE8v\xE8 is the point at which all of these stream together into one liturgical art. For the Motif Atlas the record anchors the cluster of sacred sign-writing \u2014 the diviner's marks, the cosmograms, the signatures of the gods \u2014 and gives the Afro-Atlantic case in which writing itself is a rite of hospitality: the gods arrive because someone took the trouble to spell their names on the floor."
    }
