    narrative: {
      en: "High John de Conquer, in Zora Neale Hurston's telling, was not originally a root at all but a spirit \u2014 the great trickster-hero who came across the Middle Passage with the enslaved and never left. He had no temples, no altars, no priesthood; he had songs in the fields and a name on the lips of the people, and his business in the world was the winning side of things. He is the invisible presence of the plantation narratives: the one who laughed last and laughed best, the one who was not fooled when everyone else was fooled, the one whose victories never required a throne because they were always won from below. Hurston frames him as the answer to a theological problem of the slave quarters: a God too distant to intervene, a master's religion that demanded submission \u2014 and somewhere in between, a power that belonged entirely to the people and fought entirely for them. His relics are roots. The High John the Conqueror root \u2014 a dried, gnarled, root-shaped curio carried in a conjure-bag \u2014 is his relic among hoodoo practitioners, and its uses in the folklore are the uses of the god: chewed before going to court, for the winning word; carried for luck in any contest; buried under the doorstep of a house to bring the luck inside; anointed and sung to in the rites of the practitioners, who treat the root as a living thing with a name. The root's virtues are High John's virtues \u2014 luck, eloquence, the turn of the losing hand into the winning one \u2014 and the theology of the charm is the theology of the hero: that the power was given to the weak and that it works by laughing. Hurston's essay closes with her own claim about his function: he was the culture-hero of hope of a people who could not afford despair. He gave them no commandments and no promise of heaven; he gave them a sense of the winning side, and proof of his presence was the laughter itself \u2014 the laughter of the enslaved at the master's expense, with the master none the wiser, which she reads as High John walking the rows. In the comparative grid of the Motif Atlas he stands with the culture-heroes of fire-theft and civilizing gift, and his closest kin in the corpus are Brer Rabbit and Anansi \u2014 the same trickster, the same winning side, the same laughter \u2014 but Hurston's High John is the only one of the family who is explicitly a god, and the only one whose relic is chewed for luck rather than eaten for dinner. The root itself deserves a note, because it is the classic case of a charm that is also a hagiography. The High John the Conqueror root of the practitioners is botanically the tuber of a morning-glory relative \u2014 Ipomoea \u2014 whose gnarled shape suggests a figure, and whose folklore name records a masculine power. Practitioners treat it accordingly: it is fed, anointed, addressed by name, carried in the left-hand bag that custom assigns to the powers of luck, and retired with honors when its work is done. The charm-market of the 20th century commercialized the root heavily, and Hurston herself distinguishes the living use from the souvenir trade. In the Motif Atlas the record stands at the junction of the culture-hero cluster and the charm-and-conjure cluster, and supplies the Afro-Atlantic case in which the hero's power survives not in temples or scriptures but in a root you can hold in a closed hand \u2014 the smallest relic in the corpus, and the one closest to its people's pockets."
    }
