    narrative: {
      en: "The second part of Hurston's Mules and Men is hoodoo fieldwork in New Orleans, and its climax is an initiation. Hurston, who had gone to New Orleans to be taught, apprenticed herself to a famous doctor and paid for her training; alongside her, another apprentice called Luke was passing through the ordered cycles of ordeal that made a practitioner. She watched and recorded his progress: the rites of sleeplessness and fasting that break the ordinary body-clock of the candidate; the graveyard work, in which the powers of the ancestors are approached on their own ground and the candidate sleeps among the graves; the ceremonies of the water and the crossroads; and the paroxysms of possession in which the candidate learns both to ride and to be ridden \u2014 the double grammar of every Afro-Atlantic priesthood. Hurston's account is the fullest published witness of New Orleans hoodoo initiation of her generation, and she wrote it from inside: she was not watching from the doorway but standing in the circle, paying her fees, running her own errands of candles and\u7C89, and describing the whole economy of the practice \u2014 the fees, the apprenticeship, the succession of a dying doctor's powers \u2014 with the participant's ear. The account matters to the corpus because it establishes the grammar: hoodoo initiation, like Vodou kanzo and Candombl\xE9's seclusion cycle, is an ordered passage through death-and-rebirth imagery, graveyard work and possession, ending in an anointing that makes the candidate a channel for the powers rather than a petitioner to them. The record here deliberately stops at the level of summary. The procedural detail of a living initiation is not reproduced beyond what Hurston published as general description; no working instructions are given; and the copyrighted text is paraphrased, not quoted. What the record preserves is the shape of the rite and its comparative position: the Afro-Atlantic initiated priesthood, in its New Orleans form, initiating by the same logic that governs the Haitian mountain peristyle and the Bahian terreiro \u2014 the candidate must die to the ordinary world in order to carry the powers of the other one. The comparative position of the New Orleans rite in the Afro-Atlantic grid is instructive. In Haiti the candidate's passage is marked by the kanzo cycles of the fire and the whip; in Bahia by the seclusion of the camarinha and the shaving of the head; in New Orleans by the graveyard sleep and the nine-night economy of fees. All three dramatize the same passage: the candidate is symbolically killed as an ordinary person, spends time among the powers of the dead, and is returned to the community as someone through whom the powers can now speak. Hurston's witness therefore links the northern diaspora's practice to the southern one at exactly the level where the comparison is legitimate \u2014 the structure of initiation, not the details of any one house's liturgy. For the Motif Atlas the record anchors the cluster of initiation-and-possession records and marks the northern limit of the initiated-priesthood geography: New Orleans, Havana, Bahia, Port-au-Prince, in a single arc of the Caribbean trade."
    }
