    narrative: {
      en: "The Kongo cosmogram is the master-diagram of Kongo religion: a diamond and a cross laid upon the quarters of a single field, with the sun's path marked around it, so that the whole diagram maps the cycle of birth, death and rebirth \u2014 the land of the living above the waterline of the horizontal bar, the land of the dead below it, the rising and setting sun as the doors between the two, and the vertical axis as the path of power that runs from the ancestors up through the living. Robert Farris Thompson's Flash of the Spirit is the classic map of what happened to this diagram when its makers crossed the Atlantic, and his Kongo-Atlantic chapter documents the continuity artifact by artifact and dance by dance. In Haiti the diagram becomes the ground-writing of the v\xE8v\xE8 \u2014 the emblem-script traced in flour on the peristyle floor before every service, the signature by which the lwa are invited \u2014 and the paired serpents about the center-pole of the Rada rites; in the Georgia Sea Islands it becomes the ring-shout, the counterclockwise circle of the praise-house, the oldest dance of the Gullah community, danced on feet that must never cross; in South Carolina and Georgia it becomes the east-west burial orientation of the graves, the dead laid to face the rising sun of rebirth; in Bahia it becomes the ground-signs and body-signs of Candombl\xE9 and Macumba practice, and in the Kongo-derived rites of Cuba the same four-quarter field under the palo altar. Thompson's larger argument is that the cosmogram made survival thinkable: it is a single portable diagram that maps the Middle Passage itself \u2014 the crossing from the land of the living to the land of the dead and back \u2014 and it gave every diaspora community a grammar of renewal that needed no buildings, no books, and no permission. The record anchors, for the Motif Atlas, the cluster of world-renewal and sacred-place records for the Afro-Atlantic tradition, and supplies the case in which a cosmology is carried not in texts but in diagrams, dances and grave-dirt \u2014 the most portable sacred technology of the whole diaspora, and the one whose continuity can be documented on four continents. Thompson's method is worth summarizing for the corpus because it is the model the Afro-Atlantic records as a set follow: continuity is demonstrated, not asserted, by pairing a source artifact in Africa with a diaspora artifact and documenting the transatlantic crossing between them \u2014 the Kongo tomb-sign against the Haitian v\xE8v\xE8, the Kongo circuit dance against the ring-shout, the Kongo east-facing burial against the Carolina grave. His account is also deliberately an art-historical one: the cosmogram lives in objects, drawings, dances and grave-architecture rather than in texts, which is why it survived the legal and educational regimes that suppressed texts. The comparative yield for the Motif Atlas is a documented chain of transmission at the level of form \u2014 the only one of its kind for any African tradition in the Americas \u2014 and it makes the Kongo-Atlantic material the evidential backbone of any claim about African continuities in the New World. The sensitivity note is that the sign-system is living practice; Thompson's published synthesis is summarized here, not reproduced, and no operational detail beyond the published level is given."
    }
